ing. `labs/box/` is the worked example. Use `node x new <kind> labs/game/<dir>/<name>` for props, bodies, builds, actions, emitters, sfx, songs, looks and cameras: each comes with its test. Grow from the starter in `labs/game/`, and remove its gem game once your scenes replace it. `AGENTS.md` says to ask the owner about anything outward or costly: there is nobody to ask in this run, and nothing in this plan needs it, so take the safer choice and note it.\"},{\"line_number\":932,\"content\":\"| `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` cites `labs/playground/…` | Those files are not in your workspace | Use `labs/box/`; or read the engine's GitHub repository if one is truly worth it |\"},{\"line_number\":942,\"content\":\"| `git` in the dev folder | It belongs to the larger repository around it | Do not `git init`, commit or restore there, despite `AGENTS.md`'s \\\"commit as you go\\\", unless your TASK.md asks |\"},{\"line_number\":1025,\"content\":\"Answers already decided: Plan: the name this file has in your folder. Genre and Perspective as in section 1. Tech: my-3dge at its pinned commit, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21, Vite, TypeScript. Art: the engine's native toon puppets and generators with new retreat assets. Animation: the engine's clip layer and humanoid with a new readable clip set scrubbed from the sim. Audio: tracker songs, `defineSfx` effects, gibberish syllable voices, all code, music at 10%. Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost; same-screen Group Class on its own. Environment and Model as your TASK.md gives them. Usage: \\\"added on import\\\".\"},{\"line_number\":1029,\"content\":\"**Credits must include:** my-3dge (MIT), three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), @stdlib (Apache-2.0); the AI Hub session client. Animation clips by what you actually copied: Mesh2Motion and Quaternius Universal Animation Library (CC0; credit anyway); **100STYLE dataset, Mason, Starke and Komura, 2022, CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), converted to key poses: required credit with a link and a note of changes** if you use any `STYLE100*` clip; CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database if you use any `CMU` clip (terms in `data/anim/LICENSES/CMU.txt`).\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"c30e9318-6bd6-4da0-a8cf-d633241a7b92"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_3","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a massive game-building objective.\n\nI will read STAGE.md and TASK.md. The plan must cover all PLAN.md tasks that can be done on this computer. It must also cover every item TASK.md requires to be handed in.\n\nThe objective is a Wii Fit-inspired balance game built with the my-3dge engine. Progress will be tracked in PROGRESS.md.\n\nThe plan must specify OUTCOMES rather than architecture. It must avoid prescribing module layouts, class names, or exact signatures. Acceptance criteria should be small and satisficing. Related mechanics will be grouped together. Visual and interactive objectives will anchor on static structural fallbacks, unit tests, and browser script loads.\n\nThe plan must verify that deliverables are runnable entry points. PLAN.md already defines the game as an original inspired by Wii Fit, so no external research is needed.\n\nPLAN.md is the authoritative specification. The deliverable is defined by its own rules, not by the named Wii Fit source. Defining mechanics are the primary behaviors without which the game would not be recognizable.\n\nA brief search will satisfy the research requirement before I draft the plan. TASK.md requires a titled dev subfolder, a root HTML file, a playful title chosen by real RNG from fifty brainstorm ideas, PROGRESS.md, and an exit-interview.json.\n\nThe exit-interview.json must contain Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback. These entries must appear in the chat reply and also inside the game’s About or Credits screen.\n\nThe dev subfolder name follows the literal placeholder pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656]`. The title will be chosen by real RNG from fifty brainstorm ideas, with \"Wii Fit\" as the source label.\n\nPLAN.md forbids using the word \"Wii\" anywhere in the game except in the About source line and the hub source field.\n\nThe root HTML filename must embed the literal placeholder `SOURCE` as \"Wii Fit\" with a space, since TASK.md requires that exact template. The game content itself must never use the word \"Wii\".\n\nThe criteria will be capped at three to five gating, outcome-based items. Defining mechanics will be grouped rather than silently omitted.\n\nThe first criterion covers deliverables and identity. It requires the RNG-chosen title, a single self-contained HTML file in the root, the dev folder workspace, PROGRESS.md, and a valid exit-interview.json. The About or Credits screen must display Source Information and all three feedback sections exactly as recorded in the interview. The game content must never include the word \"Wii\" outside those source fields, and it must omit weight, BMI, calorie, and body-size numbers.\n\nThe second criterion covers the balance simulation and activities. The wobble model governs every activity through its center-of-balance disc, gain, instability, damping, and noise parameters. Required activities are Morning Check, Pose Class with the poses, Still Pond, The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, and the rest of the plan’s activities. Scoring ranks Present to Unsettling. Bots meet the section 3 balance targets. No softlocks occur because time boxes cap each activity. The five-day programme includes Wobble Age, pebbles or cairn, island roam or Go, story beats, and a Great Tilt finale.\n\nThe third criterion covers presentation. The game is a third-person 3D retreat island with the cast in an engine-native toon style and retreat assets. Pose animations are scrubbed and all audio is generated in code with Animalese voices. Music defaults to ten percent. The UI targets ten-foot controller-first with PS glyphs and an Xbox option, plus native keyboard and mouse support. Prompts follow the last device used. Touch-only devices show a required message. WebGPU is excluded. A dev mode opens from pause with live sliders and reset. An attract mode activates after ten seconds of idle time.\n\nThe fourth criterion covers multiplayer. Same-screen Group Class supports up to four players. The AI Hub ghost session protocol requires meta tags, a hub client SHA, and harness passes for two, three, and four players. Host leave must leave the engine ready with zero errors. The game works alone outside any session.\n\nThe fifth criterion is the verification gate. `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` must report zero failures. `npm run check` and `npm test` must pass. The engine must be read-only or declared patch. The single HTML file must open from `file://` without fetching external resources. A scripted playthrough must reach the credits.\n\nEach criterion must be atomic and independently checkable from its own start state. No single holistic end-to-end gate is allowed. Visual and interactive outcomes must not require playing the game; they must be verified through unit tests of pure logic, browser script loads, and launch checks. This creates tension with PLAN.md, which explicitly requires browser tests, a hub harness, verify.mjs, and a scripted playthrough.\n\nThe objective overrides PLAN.md, so the required headless scripts and hub harness must be gating criteria. Headless Chromium with WebGPU SwiftShader is available in this environment, making launch checks feasible. PLAN.md also prescribes module layouts, which violates the rule against prescribing file structure.\n\nThe plan must not freeze PLAN.md's architecture as acceptance criteria. Freezing the file layout would pin one solution and let verifiers reject correct divergent work. The implementer follows PLAN.md, so the plan should state only observable outcomes.\n\nTASK.md filenames are deliverable outcomes. PLAN.md module layout is implementation guidance, not a criterion. The adversarial verifier uses this plan as the single source of truth. Omitting PLAN.md features would let an implementer skip them and still pass. Copying every PLAN.md requirement into criteria would make the goal unfinishable.\n\nA single criterion may group several closely-related mechanics into one checkable outcome, but it must never become a whole-system end-to-end gate. The second criterion is too holistic; it will be split into smaller atomic checks.\n\nThe second criterion is now split into two atomic checks. One validates the wobble model and activity scoring: the four bots must hit published score bands across every shipped activity, topple must not end the game, activities must self-end, ranks and pebble awards must appear, and the never-cut set must be fully present with no leftover traces. The other validates the activity roster itself, confirming that optional cut-line activities are either fully present or absent with days reflowed.\n\nThe third criterion is now a single atomic check covering the world, body, and shell. It requires a third-person island retreat with cast and stations, scrubbed pose clips so the body follows lean, code-only audio with music at ten percent and gibberish voices, a ten-foot UI with PS and Xbox glyphs, native keyboard and mouse support, prompts that follow the last device used, a touch-only message, no WebGPU, a five-day flow to credits and free play, and saves.\n\nThe fifth criterion is now split into two atomic checks. One validates hub multiplayer: same-screen Group Class up to four players, the ghost protocol with meta tags and client hash, harness passes for two, three, and four players, host leave leaving the engine ready with zero errors, and a session that omits title, dev, and pause-of-sim screens. The other validates launch and automated verification: verify.mjs reports zero failures, npm run check and npm test pass, engine files are unchanged or patched in ENGINE-FEEDBACK, the built HTML boot shows ready and a non-blank WebGPU view of the title or island, and a scripted auto playthrough reaches credits.\n\nThe verification plan is separate from the acceptance criteria. Criteria describe outcomes, and verification describes how to check them.\n\nThe entry-point launch check must be a gating step in the verification plan. It requires serving the page, loading it, confirming zero page errors, verifying render surface dimensions, checking that the view is substantially filled, and confirming that driven input produces visible change. An honest fallback is required if the launcher cannot run.\n\nHeadless Chromium with WebGPU can be used in this environment. PLAN.md warns that headless WebGPU canvases may appear blank in screenshots, so verification should inspect game state directly rather than relying on screenshots.\n\nThe plan writer's rule that a successful WebGPU readback must not return blank or partial is in direct conflict with TASK.md and PLAN.md, which explicitly allow blank headless screenshots. Because the OBJECTIVE overrides all other documents, the plan must follow the environment's stated limitation rather than demanding a non-blank buffer.\n\nThe launch check must assert engine readiness, zero errors, and the active game screen. A blank WebGPU canvas is acceptable when the engine is running correctly; only a failed start counts as a failure. This distinction belongs in the Risks section.\n\nWebGPU works on `file://` here, so the built HTML can open directly from disk without a server.\n\nThe plan should prefer plain `<script>` tags over ES modules for direct-disk loading. PLAN.md's onefile build uses inline `<script type=\"module\">`, which may conflict with this preference.\n\nThe built HTML must open from `file://` and run correctly. If ES modules are genuinely needed, the page should detect `file:` and display a serving instruction instead of failing silently. PLAN.md already confirmed that inlined module scripts work on `file://`, so a file: detection fallback is unnecessary.\n\nVerification steps must not hardcode the game title. They should reference the dev subfolder by its TASK.md naming pattern and the HTML in the work root. STAGE.md requires PROGRESS.md.\n\nThe plan must require exit-interview.json and the in-game About screen as durable artifacts. The chat reply is implementer finish evidence, not a harness-gated deliverable. The criterion will verify that exit-interview.json parses correctly and its fields match the About screen content.\n\nThe title must be chosen from a real fifty-title pool via RNG. Verification should check PROGRESS.md or README for the title list and the RNG result, without prescribing the selection method.\n\nForbidden terms include \"Wii\" outside the About and hub source fields, plus \"Fit Age\", \"Fit Credits\", \"weight\", \"BMI\", \"calorie\", and \"body-size\".\n\nFor hub multiplayer, the client SHA must match the expected hash `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` for client 1.4.0.\n\nThe hub client must be byte-identical to the fetched session.js. Its SHA-256 is recorded. If the hub moved and the hash differs from the pinned value, the README notes the new revision.\n\nEngine files must remain read-only; npm run check compares them against engine-lock.json. No new npm dependencies are allowed.\n\nThe workspace is empty except for the three markdown files. The implementer creates the game.\n\nDesign guidance should be provided as structure suggestions rather than criteria. The plan should recommend a pure wobble simulation separate from rendering, with data tables and bots serving as the test harness.\n\nThe implementation approach will cite PLAN.md as the design reference, but it must not freeze file names or layout details so a verifier cannot wrongly claim divergence. The criteria remain strictly outcome-based. The task checklist should be reduced to three to eight ordered steps rather than PLAN.md's full twelve build order.\n\nThe build is now organized into seven ordered steps: title selection and skeleton setup, wobble model and balance tests, body clips and pose classes, remaining activities and island flow, finale and hub multiplayer, shell screens and controls, and final polish with exit-interview.\n\nThe verification plan must cover every criterion with gating steps and evidence. For code changes, the implementer should add or update tests asserting the new behavior, using verify.mjs, vitest, npm run check, and playwright as PLAN.md specifies.\n\nThe launch check must run multiple times to confirm consistent success, not merely a non-empty result. The primary observable checks are engine readiness, zero errors, visible title state, driven input changing center of balance, music volume at 0.1, and the built HTML file existing in the root.\n\nThe launch step will assert game state directly rather than relying on screenshots. A blank WebGPU readback is treated as the known SwiftShader limitation when state shows a filled scene and no errors, with evidence captured instead of failing. This conflict is recorded in Risks.\n\nThe hub multiplayer harness is a local system fetched from GitHub and must be gated as part of PLAN’s done criteria. It is not an external oracle.\n\nFPS optimization is explicitly out of scope. Physical controllers, real iOS devices, and live audio listening are excluded. Recreating Wii Fit names, Mii avatars, Wuhu, Balance Board mechanics, or BMI/weight metrics is forbidden. Cut-line features are not non-goals; they must be cleanly removed per the plan’s ordered cut line with days reflowed and no leftover mentions. The dev folder must not contain a git repository, and review subagents are disallowed.\n\nThe deliverables must live in the work root: the built HTML, exit-interview.json, and PROGRESS.md. The AI Hub session client is fetched rather than cloned. Engine files remain read-only.\n\nRisks include earlyoom killing browsers, shared machine ports, and the 45-minute playthrough fitting within step speed.\n\nPerformance is unlimited except for the hub readiness deadline of twenty seconds. Hand-playtesting is forbidden; bots judge feel. The filename must include SOURCE per TASK, but \"Wii\" is never used inside the game itself. I will do a quick research pass on named canon to confirm which mechanics PLAN intends to borrow and which it wants excluded.\n\nThe plan already excludes Balance Board mechanics, Body Test metrics, and Fit Bank credits. A quick web search will verify whether any defining Wii Fit mechanics were missed before the plan is finalized.\n\nThe hub README and session protocol requirements from PLAN section 9 are already known. The plan will state only the observable harness pass with the engine running, omitting the full protocol details. Acceptance criteria will be kept short and concrete for small-model readability.\n\nThe plan is being drafted now. It will be a short, concrete code-change goal with numbered acceptance criteria.\n\nThe hand-in package must contain exactly one self-contained HTML file. Its name follows the TASK template: `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656.html`, where Title is the RNG-chosen game title and the literal phrase \"Wii Fit\" retains its space.\n\nA dev subfolder with the same basename will hold the my-3dge workspace and the game. PROGRESS.md lives in the work root. exit-interview.json must be a valid JSON object matching TASK’s exact shape, with every value filled and usage set to \"added on import\". The in-game About screen must display the same Source Information and Testing, Engine, and Prompt Feedback answers as that JSON file. The fifty-title pool and the RNG result are recorded in the dev folder.\n\nThe chosen title string must appear on the title screen, attract banner, hub card, and aiHub.name.\n\nThe game is a third-person 3D balance experience. The only balance verb is lean via stick, WASD, arrows, or pointer-locked mouse. Left foot, right foot, and both feet apply to every activity. Centre-of-balance is a point in a unit disc governed by the plan’s gain, instability, damping, slow noise, breath-calm, and topple mechanics. Stumble and recovery occur; there is no game over.\n\nThe five-day island residency includes Morning Check, Pose Class, Still Pond, The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, and The Great Tilt. Tilt follows the centre-of-balance. Dawn and credits close the cycle. Free play follows.\n\nReps Hall, Step Class, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, Island Jog, and expert variants are either fully playable or removed per the cut order with the day table reflowed and no leftover mentions. Each activity ends on a time box and scores 0–1000 with ranks Present, Adequate, Composed, Serene, or Unsettling. It awards 1–5 pebbles. The perfect bot scores at least 750 on 10 seeds. The human-like delayed thumb scores at least 550. The sloppy thumb scores at least 300. Idle scores no more than 500 and below the human-like bot. Weight, BMI, calorie, and body-size numbers are forbidden.\n\nThe acceptance criteria are too large. One criterion cannot cover the entire game as a single checkable outcome. I will split it into five independently startable checks.\n\nThe plan is being split into three independently startable checks. The first covers the hand-in package, title, reports, and forbidden words on static screens and the About screen. The second validates the wobble model and per-activity bot score bands. The third checks the day loop, story, finale steps, saves, and prevents softlocks to credits. A whole-game playthrough is decomposed into smaller scripted gates such as Morning Check producing an age in range.\n\nThe scripted full playthrough is now a single verification criterion. It runs a Node simulation from a fresh save at step speed, visiting every shipped day and activity and ending on credits across three seeds without throwing. This satisfies PLAN section 16 as the required softlock finder.\n\nThe Node softlock finder is kept as a standalone criterion because it is independently automatable and satisfies the objective. The plan now has five checks: hand-in package, wobble model with per-activity bot score bands, a 10-foot UX shell covering glyphs, dev mode, attract, touch, audio, and settings, and multiplayer hub readiness.\n\nThe island residency criterion now includes programme and noticeboard displays, scripted auto-credit exits on three seeds, and cast-and-retreat presentation with third-person scrubbed poses, code audio at ten percent music, and gibberish voices.\n\nThe controller-first shell criterion specifies prompts by last device, a PS default with an Xbox override, native keyboard and mouse controls, dev-mode live sliders with reset, an attract screen that exits on any input without touching save, a touch-only message, a no-WebGPU message, and pause.\n\nThe multiplayer criterion covers local group classes for one to four players and the AI Hub ghost session harness.\n\nCriterion 3 is being split to isolate concrete checks. The island world and story will be separated from audio and presentation.\n\nConcrete criteria now include a distinct camera per activity, pose progress scrubbing clips by slot time, non-silent songs with music at ten percent volume on fresh save, no embedded audio payloads, at least two hundred deadpan voice lines, and visible plaques and notices.\n\nPresentation is folded into criterion 3 as concrete observables rather than a subjective \"feel\" check. Cut line handling will be explicit so verifiers do not reject legitimate cuts. Every numeric game.* setting in the plan becomes a live dev-mode slider with reset. The attract screen must idle for ten seconds on the title or menu before exiting. Group class and hub readiness remain criterion 5. Verification plan steps are now being drafted.\n\nThe wobble and activity checks will run from a fresh simulation state. Tests assert bot score bands, self-termination, age formulas, still pond penalties, step windows, and no NaN. A log at `{SCRATCH}/verify.log` must show zero failures and a count line. `npm test` and `npm run check` logs also go in scratch. The engine lock must be clean or the patch must be declared in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK`.\n\nThe scripted whole-game auto playthrough is folded into `verify.mjs` as its sole gate. The implementer must keep all checks behind one command. The output must print only failures and a count line. PLAN names the exact path.\n\nA third gating check launches the built HTML in Chromium via file:// with WebGPU flags and CHROMIUM_PATH. It asserts __engine.ready is true, __engine.errors is empty, the title screen shows the chosen title, music volume is 0.1, and no console WebGPU downgrade appears. Screenshots are captured to {SCRATCH} as evidence.\n\nA blank canvas readback is treated as the known SwiftShader limit and will not fail the engine check on its own. A failed engine start remains a failure. Driven input tests use a stub gamepad or keyboard to move focus or change centre-of-balance during an active activity, then assert that state changes identically across both runs.\n\nA final gating check validates PLAN section 16 browser stages: controller, keyboard, mouse, touch, attract, dev mode, and about screens. It captures output under {SCRATCH}. The 1920x1080 viewport must render text at least 28px and menus in no more than six rows. The attract screen must last between ten and eleven seconds, exit on any input, and leave save data untouched.\n\nThe About screen must contain every source field and feedback answer. The hub harness must pass for two, three, and four players with host-leave, showing __hubCheck.pass, data-complete, per-frame engine readiness, a ghost session at two players, no [AIHub] console lines, and matching client bytes against the fetched file. WebKit or browsers without WebGPU must display the friendly WebGPU-required page instead of a blank canvas. Screenshots are required for the title, island, pose HUD, still pond lure, line, great tilt, results, pause, dev mode, and about screens.\n\nThe hand-in package must parse exit-interview.json and match about-data fields. The built HTML must live in the work root with no external script sources. The game source must contain no todo, placeholder, or stub markers.\n\nBecause the engine supports headless simulation and Playwright, full behavioral checks are appropriate rather than a static fallback. The hub harness and browser tests satisfy PLAN requirements and were validated on this machine.\n\nThe browser launch must run more than once. If Chromium or WebGPU cannot start for environmental reasons, capture the failure and fall back to Node simulation tests plus static checks; a successful launch that still fails is a hard failure.\n\nImplementation guidance should be short and focused on testability, not a second contract. PLAN.md serves as the design spec the implementer follows. Simulations, bots, and data tables are implementation details, not frozen judged names.\n\nA seven-item task checklist compresses the build order. Non-goals include pixel-perfect Wii Fit recreation, weight and BMI metrics, FPS optimization, real hardware controllers, iOS Safari, cloning the five-gigabyte ai-hub repository, new npm dependencies, git commits in the dev folder, features past the cut line, multiplayer beyond four players or ghost modes, and a second desktop UI layout. Risks include blank headless WebGPU screenshots, earlyoom, and hub client hash changes.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"PLAN.md is a full original-game spec on top of Wii Fit. I’ll confirm the source’s defining mechanics, then lock outcomes to what this machine can actually finish."},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_64a9b6bb-b0e1-9fc5-9a21-a8cc564b2933_call-586667c7-10ec-418a-895f-675c66669d86-6","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"Wii Fit defining gameplay mechanics balance board body test yoga strength aerobics minigames Wii Fit Age"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_64a9b6bb-b0e1-9fc5-9a21-a8cc564b2933_call-586667c7-10ec-418a-895f-675c66669d86-6","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/wii-fit-users-guide/1100-6191333/","title":"https://www.gamespot.com/articles/wii-fit-users-guide/1100-6191333/"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://w.atwiki.jp/gcmatome/pages/7748.html","title":"https://w.atwiki.jp/gcmatome/pages/7748.html"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wii_Fit","title":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wii_Fit"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/942009-wii-fit/faqs/52634","title":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/942009-wii-fit/faqs/52634"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/942009-wii-fit/faqs/53123","title":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/942009-wii-fit/faqs/53123"}]},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_64a9b6bb-b0e1-9fc5-9a21-a8cc564b2933_call-586667c7-10ec-418a-895f-675c66669d86-7","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"AI Hub session protocol v1 ghost mode michaelcrosato ai-hub lib/hub README session.js"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_64a9b6bb-b0e1-9fc5-9a21-a8cc564b2933_call-586667c7-10ec-418a-895f-675c66669d86-7","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md","title":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub","title":"https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js","title":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js"}]},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-586667c7-10ec-418a-895f-675c66669d86-8","name":"web_fetch","input":{"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":16066,"output_tokens":8920,"cache_read_input_tokens":83840,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"5b90b0d9-93ce-43d1-b92f-2a7e3a506702"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-586667c7-10ec-418a-895f-675c66669d86-8","content":"{\"type\":\"WebFetch\",\"Content\":{\"url\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\",\"content\":\"/*! aihub-session 1.4.0 — AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client. Paste it into your game unmodified. */\\n(function (root) {\\n  'use strict';\\n  if (root.AIHub) return;\\n\\n  var VERSION = '1.4.0';\\n  var HELLO_EVERY_MS = 500;\\n  var HELLO_TIMEOUT_MS = 10000;\\n  var PROGRESS_EVERY_MS = 1000;\\n  /** The hub's welcome may change these; a newer hub never needs a newer copy of this file for it. */\\n  var limits = { maxBytes: 16384, ratePerS: 20, burst: 40 };\\n\\n  var framed = false;\\n  try {\\n    framed = root.parent && root.parent !== root;\\n  } catch (e) {\\n    framed = true;\\n  }\\n  var search = String(root.location && root.location.search);\\n  var flagged = /[?&]aihub=/.test(search);\\n  var seedArg = /[?&]aihub-seed=(\\\\d+)/.exec(search);\\n\\n  var hub = {\\n    version: VERSION,\\n    protocol: 1,\\n    /** True when the hub launched this page for a session (?aihub=1 inside a frame). */\\n    active: !!(flagged && framed),\\n    me: null,\\n    host: null,\\n    isHost: false,\\n    players: [],\\n    mode: null,\\n    seed: 0,\\n    limitMs: 0,\\n    round: 0,\\n    /** True when the hub shows the round's splash and collects every player's ready: call ready() once loaded, with no prompt. */\\n    splash: false,\\n    /** True when the hub seeded Math.random for this round (side by side): every copy draws the same numbers. */\\n    seeded: false,\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A storage that lives and dies with the page: the Storage methods, and items as properties too. */\\n  function memoryStorage() {\\n    var data = Object.create(null);\\n    var api = {\\n      getItem: function (k) {\\n        k = String(k);\\n        return k in data ? data[k] : null;\\n      },\\n      setItem: function (k, v) {\\n        data[String(k)] = String(v);\\n      },\\n      removeItem: function (k) {\\n        delete data[String(k)];\\n      },\\n      clear: function () {\\n        for (var k in data) delete data[k];\\n      },\\n      key: function (i) {\\n        var keys = Object.keys(data);\\n        return i >= 0 && i < keys.length ? keys[i] : null;\\n      },\\n    };\\n    if (typeof Proxy !== 'function') return api;\\n    return new Proxy(api, {\\n      get: function (t, k) {\\n        if (k === 'length') return Object.keys(data).length;\\n        if (typeof k === 'symbol' || k in t) return t[k];\\n        return k in data ? data[k] : undefined;\\n      },\\n      set: function (t, k, v) {\\n        if (typeof k !== 'symbol') data[k] = String(v);\\n        return true;\\n      },\\n      has: function (t, k) {\\n        return k in data || k in t;\\n      },\\n      deleteProperty: function (t, k) {\\n        delete data[k];\\n        return true;\\n      },\\n      ownKeys: function () {\\n        return Object.keys(data);\\n      },\\n      getOwnPropertyDescriptor: function (t, k) {\\n        return k in data ? { value: data[k], writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: true } : undefined;\\n      },\\n    });\\n  }\\n\\n  /** mulberry32: numbers in [0, 1), the same sequence everywhere for the same 32-bit start. */\\n  function generator(a) {\\n    return function () {\\n      a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) >>> 0;\\n      var t = a;\\n      t = Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 15), t | 1);\\n      t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61);\\n      return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;\\n    };\\n  }\\n\\n  // A fair start: in a session the game gets an empty storage of its own, so\\n  // every round starts as a first launch (no saved unlocks, upgrades or runs),\\n  // and nothing the round saves touches the player's own saves.\\n  if (hub.active) {\\n    for (var s = 0, stores = ['localStorage', 'sessionStorage']; s < stores.length; s++) {\\n      try {\\n        Object.defineProperty(root, stores[s], { configurable: true, value: memoryStorage() });\\n      } catch (e) {\\n        warn('could not give the game a fresh ' + stores[s]);\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  // Side by side: before any of the game's own scripts run, Math.random becomes\\n  // a generator seeded by the hub, so every copy builds the same start.\\n  var realRandom = Math.random;\\n  var seededRandom = null;\\n  if (hub.active && seedArg) {\\n    Math.random = seededRandom = generator(Number(seedArg[1]) >>> 0);\\n    hub.seeded = true;\\n  }\\n\\n  // In a session the hub owns fullscreen: a game that asks for it gets the\\n  // hub's whole page, so the scoreboard and the hub's cards stay on top. The\\n  // hub says when its page goes in and out, so the game's own toggle and its\\n  // fullscreenchange listeners work as they always did.\\n  var fullscreenOn = false;\\n  if (hub.active && root.Element && root.document) {\\n    var asks = function (on) {\\n      return function () {\\n        post('fullscreen', { on: on });\\n        return Promise.resolve();\\n      };\\n    };\\n    var proto = root.Element.prototype;\\n    proto.requestFullscreen = proto.webkitRequestFullscreen = asks(true);\\n    root.document.exitFullscreen = root.document.webkitExitFullscreen = asks(false);\\n    var shown = function () {\\n      return fullscreenOn ? root.document.documentElement || null : null;\\n    };\\n    try {\\n      Object.defineProperty(root.document, 'fullscreenElement', { configurable: true, get: shown });\\n      Object.defineProperty(root.document, 'webkitFullscreenElement', { configurable: true, get: shown });\\n    } catch (e) {}\\n  }\\n\\n  /**\\n   * No hub after all: play on, with Math.random the page's own again. The\\n   * empty storage stays: the game has read it already, and what it writes\\n   * must never land on the player's real saves.\\n   */\\n  function standAlone() {\\n    hub.active = false;\\n    if (seededRandom && Math.random === seededRandom) Math.random = realRandom;\\n    hub.seeded = false;\\n  }\\n\\n  var origin = '*';\\n  var welcomed = false;\\n  var ended = false;\\n  var connecting = null;\\n  var goAt = null;\\n  var sentReady = false;\\n  var reported = false;\\n  var handlers = {};\\n  var pending = {};\\n  var tokens = limits.burst;\\n  var refilled = now();\\n  var probeFn = null;\\n  var lastProgress = -Infinity;\\n  var nextProgress = null;\\n  var progressTimer = null;\\n\\n  function now() {\\n    return root.performance && root.performance.now ? root.performance.now() : Date.now();\\n  }\\n\\n  function warn(text) {\\n    if (root.console) root.console.warn('[AIHub] ' + text);\\n  }\\n\\n  function own(o, k) {\\n    return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(o, k);\\n  }\\n\\n  /** This client's own fields, which no welcome may overwrite. */\\n  var KEEP = { aihub: 1, type: 1, version: 1, protocol: 1, active: 1, seeded: 1 };\\n\\n  /** A plain JSON copy, or undefined when it is not JSON. */\\n  function json(value) {\\n    try {\\n      var text = JSON.stringify(value);\\n      return text === undefined ? undefined : JSON.parse(text);\\n    } catch (e) {\\n      return undefined;\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function post(type, fields) {\\n    var msg = { aihub: 1, type: type };\\n    for (var key in fields) if (own(fields, key)) msg[key] = fields[key];\\n    try {\\n      root.parent.postMessage(msg, origin);\\n    } catch (e) {\\n      warn('could not reach the hub: ' + e.message);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function emit(type, args) {\\n    var list = handlers[type];\\n    if (!list || !list.length || pending[type]) {\\n      (pending[type] = pending[type] || []).push(args);\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    for (var i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {\\n      try {\\n        list[i].apply(null, args);\\n      } catch (e) {\\n        setTimeout(function () {\\n          throw e;\\n        });\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function onMessage(event) {\\n    if (event.source !== root.parent) return;\\n    var m = event.data;\\n    if (!m || m.aihub !== 1 || typeof m.type !== 'string') return;\\n    if (m.type === 'welcome') {\\n      if (welcomed) return;\\n      welcomed = true;\\n      origin = event.origin && event.origin !== 'null' ? event.origin : '*';\\n      // Every field the hub sends is kept, so a newer hub can add one without a newer client.\\n      for (var key in m) {\\n        if (!own(m, key) || KEEP[key] || typeof hub[key] === 'function') continue;\\n        hub[key] = m[key];\\n      }\\n      hub.players = (m.players || []).slice();\\n      hub.seed = m.seed >>> 0;\\n      hub.isHost = m.me === m.host;\\n      hub.splash = m.splash === true;\\n      if (m.limits) {\\n        for (var l in limits) if (own(limits, l) && Number(m.limits[l]) > 0) limits[l] = Number(m.limits[l]);\\n        tokens = limits.burst;\\n      }\\n      if (connecting) connecting.resolve(info());\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (m.type === 'fullscreen') {\\n      fullscreenOn = m.on === true;\\n      try {\\n        root.document.dispatchEvent(new root.Event('fullscreenchange'));\\n      } catch (e) {}\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    if (!welcomed || ended) return;\\n    if (m.type === 'start') {\\n      goAt = now() + Number(m.in || 0);\\n      emit('start', [{ in: Number(m.in || 0) }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'message') {\\n      emit('message', [m.data, m.from]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'leave') {\\n      hub.players = hub.players.filter(function (p) {\\n        return p.id !== m.id;\\n      });\\n      emit('leave', [{ id: m.id, reason: m.reason }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'time-up') {\\n      emit('time-up', [{ graceMs: m.graceMs }]);\\n    } else if (m.type === 'end') {\\n      ended = true;\\n      if (progressTimer) clearTimeout(progressTimer);\\n      emit('end', [{ reason: m.reason, standings: m.standings || null }]);\\n    } else {\\n      // An event this client has never heard of: hand it on as it came.\\n      var detail = {};\\n      for (var k in m) if (own(m, k) && k !== 'aihub' && k !== 'type') detail[k] = m[k];\\n      emit(m.type, [detail]);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  function info() {\\n    var out = {};\\n    for (var key in hub) if (own(hub, key) && typeof hub[key] !== 'function' && !KEEP[key]) out[key] = hub[key];\\n    out.players = hub.players.slice();\\n    return out;\\n  }\\n\\n  /** Join the session. Resolves with the round's info, or null when there is no hub: play normally. */\\n  hub.connect = function (opts) {\\n    if (connecting) return connecting.promise;\\n    var done;\\n    var promise = new Promise(function (resolve) {\\n      done = resolve;\\n    });\\n    connecting = { promise: promise, resolve: done };\\n    if (!hub.active) {\\n      done(null);\\n      return promise;\\n    }\\n    opts = opts || {};\\n    root.addEventListener('message', onMessage);\\n    var started = now();\\n    var hello = function () {\\n      if (welcomed) return;\\n      if (now() - started > HELLO_TIMEOUT_MS) {\\n        standAlone();\\n        warn('no hub answered; playing normally');\\n        done(null);\\n        return;\\n      }\\n      post('hello', { v: 1, client: VERSION, mode: opts.mode || null, seats: opts.seats || null, card: opts.card === true });\\n      setTimeout(hello, HELLO_EVERY_MS);\\n    };\\n    hello();\\n    return promise;\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Everything is loaded: at once when info.splash, otherwise from the player's ready gesture. */\\n  hub.ready = function () {\\n    if (!welcomed || sentReady || ended) return;\\n    sentReady = true;\\n    post('ready', {});\\n  };\\n\\n  /** On the game's own card (connect({ card: true })): this player is 'ready', or asks for 'more' time (again takes it back). */\\n  hub.answer = function (what) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || goAt !== null) return;\\n    if (what !== 'ready' && what !== 'more') return warn('answer is ready or more');\\n    post('answer', { answer: what });\\n  };\\n\\n  hub.on = function (type, fn) {\\n    (handlers[type] = handlers[type] || []).push(fn);\\n    if (pending[type] && !pending[type].flushing) {\\n      pending[type].flushing = true;\\n      setTimeout(function () {\\n        var queued = pending[type];\\n        delete pending[type];\\n        for (var i = 0; i < queued.length; i++) emit(type, queued[i]);\\n      });\\n    }\\n    return function () {\\n      var list = handlers[type] || [];\\n      var at = list.indexOf(fn);\\n      if (at !== -1) list.splice(at, 1);\\n    };\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Send JSON data to every other player, or to one with {to: id}. {reliable: false} may drop or reorder. */\\n  hub.send = function (data, opts) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended) return false;\\n    opts = opts || {};\\n    var text;\\n    try {\\n      text = JSON.stringify(data);\\n    } catch (e) {\\n      warn('send: data is not JSON');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    if (text === undefined) return false;\\n    if (text.length > limits.maxBytes) {\\n      warn('send: message over ' + limits.maxBytes + ' bytes dropped');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    var t = now();\\n    tokens = Math.min(limits.burst, tokens + ((t - refilled) / 1000) * limits.ratePerS);\\n    refilled = t;\\n    if (tokens < 1) {\\n      warn('send: over ' + limits.ratePerS + ' messages a second, dropped');\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n    tokens -= 1;\\n    var fields = { data: JSON.parse(text), reliable: opts.reliable !== false };\\n    if (opts.to !== undefined && opts.to !== null) fields.to = opts.to;\\n    post('send', fields);\\n    return true;\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Milliseconds since the synchronized start: negative during the countdown, -Infinity before start. */\\n  hub.time = function () {\\n    return goAt === null ? -Infinity : now() - goAt;\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A seeded random number generator (0 ≤ n < 1), the same sequence on every device for the same label. */\\n  hub.rng = function (label) {\\n    var h = 2166136261;\\n    var s = String(label === undefined ? '' : label);\\n    for (var i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {\\n      h ^= s.charCodeAt(i);\\n      h = Math.imul(h, 16777619);\\n    }\\n    return generator((hub.seed ^ h) >>> 0);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** A number the hub can rank: finite, given as a number or as text. Not null, '' or true, which JSON or Number would make 0. */\\n  function finite(v) {\\n    return (typeof v === 'number' || (typeof v === 'string' && v.trim() !== '')) && isFinite(v);\\n  }\\n\\n  /**\\n   * The whole outcome as plain JSON, field by field: the hub checks and trims\\n   * it, so new optional fields need no new client. A field that is not JSON\\n   * (a cycle, a BigInt) is left out, not the whole outcome.\\n   */\\n  function outcomeOf(outcome) {\\n    if (!outcome || typeof outcome !== 'object' || Array.isArray(outcome)) {\\n      warn('the outcome is not a plain object');\\n      return null;\\n    }\\n    var copy = {};\\n    for (var key in outcome) {\\n      if (!own(outcome, key)) continue;\\n      var value = json(outcome[key]);\\n      if (value !== undefined) copy[key] = value;\\n      else if (outcome[key] !== undefined) warn('the outcome\\\\'s ' + key + ' is not JSON: left out');\\n    }\\n    return copy;\\n  }\\n\\n  /** Race and side by side: report your own outcome once, {time: ms} (lower wins) or {score: n} (higher wins), with optional stats and awards. */\\n  hub.result = function (outcome) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || reported) return;\\n    var fields = outcomeOf(outcome);\\n    if (!fields) return;\\n    var given = outcome || {};\\n    delete fields.time;\\n    delete fields.score;\\n    if (finite(given.time)) fields.time = Number(given.time);\\n    if (finite(given.score)) fields.score = Number(given.score);\\n    if (!own(fields, 'time') && !own(fields, 'score')) return warn('result needs a finite time or score');\\n    reported = true;\\n    if (progressTimer) clearTimeout(progressTimer);\\n    post('result', fields);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** Versus and co-op: the host reports the standings once, best first, ties grouped: [[2], [0, 3], [1]]. */\\n  hub.finish = function (outcome) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || reported) return;\\n    if (!hub.isHost) return warn('finish is for the host only');\\n    var fields = outcomeOf(outcome);\\n    if (!fields) return;\\n    if (!Array.isArray(fields.standings)) return warn('finish needs standings');\\n    reported = true;\\n    post('finish', fields);\\n  };\\n\\n  function sendProgress() {\\n    progressTimer = null;\\n    if (!nextProgress || ended || reported) return;\\n    lastProgress = now();\\n    post('progress', nextProgress);\\n    nextProgress = null;\\n  }\\n\\n  /** Your standing during play, for the hub's scoreboard: {score, label}. Call it as often as you like; the hub hears it about once a second. */\\n  hub.progress = function (p) {\\n    if (!welcomed || ended || reported || !p || !finite(p.score)) return;\\n    nextProgress = { score: Number(p.score) };\\n    if (p.label != null) nextProgress.label = String(p.label).slice(0, 24);\\n    var wait = lastProgress + PROGRESS_EVERY_MS - now();\\n    if (wait <= 0) sendProgress();\\n    else if (!progressTimer) progressTimer = setTimeout(sendProgress, wait);\\n  };\\n\\n  /** For the harness, in or out of a session: fn() → { scene, menu, world, input }, read with inspect(). */\\n  hub.probe = function (fn) {\\n    probeFn = typeof fn === 'function' ? fn : null;\\n  };\\n\\n  hub.inspect = function () {\\n    if (!probeFn) return null;\\n    try {\\n      return JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(probeFn()));\\n    } catch (e) {\\n      return { error: String((e && e.message) || e) };\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  /** The game cannot run here (no WebGL, failed load): the hub skips it. */\\n  hub.fail = function (message) {\\n    if (!hub.active) return;\\n    post('error', { message: String(message || 'error').slice(0, 500), fatal: true });\\n  };\\n\\n  root.AIHub = hub;\\n})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis);\\n\",\"content_type\":\"text/plain; charset=utf-8\",\"status_code\":200,\"bytes\":16707}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"1847608e-0bef-4d6c-9714-85364bbab210"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_4","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Client 1.4.0 is available on raw GitHub.\n\nThe GitHub HTML page was stale at 1.3.0, but PLAN's pinned hash is current. The implementer should verify the fetched file matches that hash and record any new hash if the hub moves. The Balance Board center-of-balance input from research already maps to PLAN's single verb.\n\nBody Test daily balance tests map to PLAN's Morning Check and Wobble Age. Yoga poses and breathing map to Pose Class plus breath. Strength reps map to Reps Hall. Aerobics like hula hoop, step, and jogging map to Hoop, Step, and Island Jog. Balance minigames such as ski, table tilt, and tightrope map to Line, Sand Garden, and paddle. Trainer demonstrations map to Plumb. Fit Bank unlocks use pebbles rather than Fit Credits.\n\nThe original game was single-player. PLAN adds multiplayer as required.\n\nMii avatars are forbidden. The tone must be deadpan kindness rather than encouraging failure.\n\nThe deliverable follows PLAN.md criteria, not Wikipedia. BMI is explicitly excluded.\n\nRhythm step is cuttable. Dive is a balance minigame.\n\nThe deliverable also requires Island meta and a story tilt finale. Multiplayer needs a local group plus a hub ghost. Attract mode, dev mode, 10-foot mode, audio code, single HTML, and reports are all required.\n\nThese will be grouped into five independently checkable criteria: hand-in and identity with reports; the wobble verb and activity score contract for all shipped activities; residency, body, presentation, and shell UX; multiplayer local plus hub; and possibly splitting presentation from the third criterion.\n\nAC1 requires the work root to contain a single HTML file named with the chosen title, a matching dev subfolder, PROGRESS.md, and an exit-interview.json that parses and matches TASK’s schema. The title was generated by executing a real RNG over fifty playful riffs; the list and pick are saved in the dev folder. The same title appears on the title screen. About/Credits displays Source Information and the three feedback lists using the exact words from the JSON. The aiHub fields must be filled with genre Sports, source Wii Fit, players 1–4, and type Game.\n\nAC1 also requires the built HTML to be fully self-contained with no network fetches and no embedded audio file payloads.\n\nAC2 demands a fresh simulation where the shipped wobble integration matches the plan exactly. It uses slew, gain, instability, damping, and slow multi-sine noise from a named RNG rather than Math.random. Breathing calms the wobble. Tipping occurs after a random value exceeds one for six steps before recovery. No game-over flag is ever set.\n\nAC2 also requires every shipped activity to score 0–1000 with the five rank names. It must end itself when input stops and tear down without leftover entities. On ten seeds it needs perfect≥750, human-like (250ms, gain 1.2)≥550, sloppy (350ms, gain 0.8)≥300, and idle≤500.\n\nAC2 also requires every never-cut activity to be playable. Morning Check keeps its ring, reach, one-foot, and age formula. Pose Class includes eight poses with enter, hold, and release states plus clip scrubbing. Still Pond uses move penalties, a non-pause lure, and no-input Adequate scoring. The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, and Great Tilt steps 1, 2, 4, and 5 are all present. Cut-line items such as Tilt step 3, expert mode, Hoop, Reps, Jog race, Cliff, and Step are fully absent or removed with days reflowed and no dangling mentions. Weight, BMI, calorie, and body-size judgements are forbidden. The word \"Wii\" appears only in the About source and hub source fields.\n\nAC3 requires a scripted director to walk every shipped day, activity, and finale from a fresh save on three seeds without throwing. The Island is third-person and walkable with a Go shortcut that does not require walking. The Programme and noticeboard replay are present. Pebbles stack on a cairn rather than being spent. Save and load include erase confirmation handled in-game instead of via `confirm()`. The cast includes Tare, Plumb, Gerald, Bex, Nan, Gull, and Frog. Pose clips scrub with the center-of-balance input.\n\nAC3 also requires the music bus to default to 0.1 on a fresh save and persist as the settings value. Voices are code syllables rendered non-silently.\n\nThe UI must support 10-foot mode with body text at least 28px at 1920×1080 and no more than six menu rows. Last-device prompts use PS glyphs by default, with an Xbox toggle and key or mouse fallback. Keyboard and mouse flows include pointer-lock lean.\n\nPause reaches Dev Mode with live sliders for the plan’s numeric `game.*` settings and a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract mode starts after ten seconds of idle on the title screen, loops through multiple activities, exits on any key, click, or pad input, and leaves the save unchanged.\n\nAC3 also requires a touch-only profile to show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a tappable Settings link, with no touch controls present and attract mode still running. A missing WebGPU browser must display a plain message rather than a blank page.\n\nThe deliverable must be at least 200 lines, never exceed two short sentences, use deadpan second person, and omit any exclamation from Plumb.\n\nAC4 requires same-screen Group Class with up to four seats, joinable via Cross or Space, empty seats filled by CPU unless disabled, three short classes, and a podium. The hub meta tags must specify session one, players one through four, mode ghost, three minutes, a goal of sixty characters or fewer, a howto of eighty characters or fewer, webgpu required, type Game, and source Wii Fit.\n\nAC4 also requires the session client to be pasted unmodified, with its SHA-256 matching the fetched file and the plan pin unless the banner version differs. The harness must pass for N=2, 3, and 4 players. Host-side checks include `__hubCheck.pass`, `data-complete=true`, every frame `__engine.ready`, zero engine errors, a ghost at two players, and no console line starting `[AIHub]`. Inside a session, title, attract, and dev mode are hidden; pause does not stop the simulation; and `?aihub` keys are stripped so the engine remains alive. Outside a session, the full game still runs.\n\nAC5 requires the built HTML to open twice from file:// or 127.0.0.1. Both runs must show the engine ready, zero errors, the chosen title on the title screen, the intended canvas size, and consistent game state changes from lean or menu input. npm run check and npm test must pass. Engine files must match the lockfile, or any diff must be declared in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md. The game source must contain no TODO, FIXME, placeholder, or stub comments. The README must list the game and its judgment calls. A verify command must print only failures, a one-line count, and exit with code zero.\n\nAC5 is a valid gating criterion because it defines the primary observable outcome of a successful boot. AC2 is one family of criteria: the wobble verb must work in every activity to the published bar, and verifiers can check this via the test suite rather than manual playthrough. AC3 is also a single family covering the shell and meta, though its full playthrough is flagged as a single end-to-end gate that should be avoided.\n\nThe full playthrough is kept inside AC3 because PLAN uses it as the softlock finder. Verification runs this scripted director path in Node at step speed rather than relying on a flaky browser end-to-end test. Criteria will describe the shipped director or auto path instead of requiring a human to play for forty-five minutes.\n\nFile names such as `wobble.ts` are not prescribed in criteria. Verifiers judge behavior, not symbols. The plan itself names the component `wobble`, but the criteria only require that the balance step exists.\n\nThe deliverable folder and its HTML file must share the exact basename `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656`. Unsafe characters in the title are sanitized only to OS-safe equivalents, and the mapping is recorded in PROGRESS.md.\n\nPROGRESS.md will be placed in the work root folder so it survives and is findable. The \"Wii\" in the filename is acceptable because TASK requires it as the SOURCE field, and filenames are not part of the game content.\n\nThe exit-interview.json must be placed in the work root beside the game HTML. The implementer's chat reply is treated as the final feedback message, and AC1 will require that the runner's finishing notes match the JSON contents.\n\nThe chat reply is not a file artifact, so it cannot be a gating criterion. The About screen and exit-interview.json are the only checkable deliverables in the folder. The implementer's final reply will include the four feedback blocks, but this is a task checklist item rather than a verifier gate.\n\nThe implementation will keep the simulation pure, let the page read it, and have bots share the player input path. Data tables and verify.mjs will be the only command. PLAN.md supplies the design details.\n\nThe task checklist is organized into six ordered phases. Phase one records fifty titles, selects one at random, and scaffolds the dev folder with a new game project, dependencies, and PROGRESS.md. Phase two builds a single-file HTML skeleton that includes the hub client, meta tags, a tiny round scene, and a verify.mjs skeleton, then passes the harness with the engine ready. Phase three adds the wobble model, action maps, balance disc, Still Ring and Morning Check activities, four bots, dev sliders, and score-band tests. Phase four adds pose clips and scrubbing, the full cast, Pose Class, voices, island flow, saves, and results. Phase five completes the remaining activities, Still Pond, Great Tilt, and the day-to-credits flow. Phase six implements Group Class and the hub ghost round, then validates the harness across two, three, and four players plus host-leave.\n\nThe checklist now includes a shell audit: attract mode, settings, controls, about data, touch-only profile, no-WebGPU fallback, audio, and the 200-line limit. The final step marks PROGRESS.md done.\n\nTwo risks are noted. A headless WebGPU framebuffer may render blank; a ready engine with zero errors and real published state is acceptable, but an empty or error state is a failure. earlyoom may kill browsers, so the harness should not retry in a loop and should use one or two browsers.\n\nThe hub client SHA must match PLAN’s pinned version; if the fetched bytes differ, record the actual SHA and still require an unmodified paste plus a passing harness. The filename contains a space because SOURCE is “Wii Fit”. Cuts are allowed only in PLAN’s order and only when the rest cannot be finished; a never-cut list is mandatory. Engine AGENTS.md’s “commit as you go” guidance conflicts with PLAN’s no-git-init rule, so PLAN prevails. The `npm run dev` port 5173 is shared, so verification should prefer file:// or Node shot over a live server. DISPLAY is unset.\n\nPlaywright headless Chromium will run with the given flags. WebKit is reserved only for the no-WebGPU page.\n\nNo human may listen or play by hand. Bots and OfflineAudioContext/analyser are the bars.\n\nSwiftShader boot can take 8–16 seconds per copy. The hub watchdog is 14 seconds and the harness timeout is 20 seconds. The round scene must stay small.\n\nPLAN’s `node x new` may print spurious NEW_DOCS failures. Do not fix engine docs.\n\nTests must live in the game workspace and import the shipped simulation directly. They will exercise the wobble step, each activity’s bot bands across ten seeds, Still Pond, step timing, the age formula, teardown, and random input safety. The harness is `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` run from the dev folder. Its stdout must be captured to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`, contain no failure lines, and end with a zero count and exit code zero. The `--quick` flag is not a substitute for the full command on the final run. `{SCRATCH}/npm-test.log` and `{SCRATCH}/npm-check.log` must also capture passing results.\n\nThe auto/director playthrough must be executed from a fresh save on three seeds, and the log must record that credits were reached. Observation must be in the log, not prose.\n\nBoth built HTML runs from file:// via Playwright Chromium must show `__engine.ready === true`, zero errors, published state containing the screen title and chosen title string, and music volume 0.1.\n\nThe drawing buffer must match the page canvas size. A stubbed gamepad or key press should change focus or balance centre once an activity starts, and this must be consistent across both runs. Screenshots go to `{SCRATCH}/boot-1.png` and `boot-2.png`. If pixel readback is blank while state assertions hold, write `{SCRATCH}/webgpu-readback.txt` and do not fail the pixel fill; if state says the screen is up but ready is false, fail.\n\nIf Playwright or Chromium fails to start, the error is captured to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.txt` and the node gates plus static checks become the bar.\n\nThe primary observable is game state, not pixels. A blank SwiftShader canvas is acceptable when state assertions hold, and this conflict is recorded in Risks.\n\nCanvas width and height must match the target DOM attributes. Pixel fill is evidence only, not a gating criterion. If a non-blank readback shows an empty frame while state claims a scene is playing, that is a failure. An entirely blank readback on software WebGPU counts as unreadable.\n\nBrowser checks must run inside verify or a sibling script. They assert default PS glyphs, Xbox mapping after settings, prompt toggling on key and click, hover focus movement, 10–11 second attract mode with unchanged exit and save, dev slider and reset, touch profile messaging, and about text containing every source field and feedback answer. The layout requires 1920×1080 minimum, font 28px, and at most six menu rows. Results go to `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\n\nThe hub harness must serve on 127.0.0.1 with an OS-picked port, and only that PID may be stopped. It must pass the two-, three-, and four-player harness runs plus host-leave.\n\nHub harness logs must record pass, complete, per-frame ready, zero errors, ghost at two players, and no `[AIHub]` console lines. The client SHA goes to `{SCRATCH}/hub-client.sha` and the full log to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\n\nStatic checks require the HTML path and folder name, a parseable `exit-interview.json` with keys matching about content, no TODO or FIXME or placeholder or stub in game sources (test-word exceptions allowed), no audio files, a clean or declared engine lock, and the presence of README and ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md.\n\nThe built page must evaluate as a browser bundle under `window` without Node `module` or `require`. The ten screenshots from PLAN go to `{SCRATCH}/shots/` at 1920×1080. A standalone hub check runs Enter, Space, Enter, Space; it confirms the client is present and inactive, `Math.random` is untouched, the screen draws, and there are no errors. Goldens and replays are verified inside the main verify gating so they do not become a separate fragile bar.\n\nThe verify command must include reproducibility. If goldens are not yet recorded, the run fails. This is an observation in the verification step. The internal path `labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` is required because PLAN names it and the engine workspace convention places it there.\n\nThe goal-plan writer must not freeze file layout. PLAN prescribes the exact command path `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` from the dev folder, and that command is an observable deliverable listed under Done. A verifier looking for that specific path is correct.\n\nThe page must expose a read-only `window.__game` state handle. Tests need this unambiguous observable to inspect screen, attract, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, and event.\n\nThe `window.__game` handle must also expose focus. The scope is limited to work-root files. A temporary clone of my-3dge is created only to run `new-game.mjs`, then deleted. `npm ci` runs inside the dev folder with no new npm dependencies. Hub files are fetched by raw URL into the game workspace rather than cloned from ai-hub. The engine remains read-only.\n\nThe plan will be five short criteria: package and records, balance verb and activities, residency presentation and shell, multiplayer, and boot and engine integrity. Criterion five is kept because boot is an outcome that can be forgotten if only buried in verification.\n\nThe criteria must not become an exhaustive conjunction. The never-cut list is the mandatory core, and cut-line items are optional with clean conditions. This prevents inflating the contract into an unfinishable goal.\n\nGame filenames may contain spaces, matching the TASK pattern `GameTitle-SOURCE-...` and valid on Linux. The hub genre must be one of the enum values, specifically Sports as PLAN specifies. Research on Wii Fit's defining loop is noted: daily body and balance tests, COB dot, yoga holds with breathing, strength reps, aerobics, balance minigames, trainer, and time-bank unlocks.\n\nNo extra Wii Fit activities are added beyond PLAN. The scope assumes no research gap; web sources are confirmed. Implementation guidance is kept to a minimum and does not restate every required file path. PROGRESS.md must be placed at the work root so compaction can locate it next to STAGE.md.\n\nThe work root is the single contract file. A copy may also live in the dev folder. TASK.md asks for a subfolder, an HTML page, exit-interview.json, the About screen inside the game, and PROGRESS.md. Chat replies are not files. The plan will be written now with concise wording for small models.\n\nTests must not mock the unit under test. They must drive shipped functions and capture output under `{SCRATCH}`.\n\nThe browser launch step gates on canvas width and height matching the requested viewport and being non-zero. Screenshot evidence is recorded there.\n\nA fully transparent or black screenshot is recorded as a SwiftShader blank-canvas limit in `{SCRATCH}/webgpu-readback.txt` and does not fail launch. A partial UI readback that does not fill the frame while state claims the title or island is active is treated as an application bug.\n\nThe verifier must follow TASK.md's computer note because the OBJECTIVE includes it. The primary observable is engine state, not the framebuffer; pixel fill is evidence only. This resolves any contradiction inside the verification plan. For driven input, visible change means published state changes such as focus index or CoB coordinates.\n\nVerification must run twice. Grep cleanliness is added as a gating step per PLAN section 18. ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md and labs/game/README.md are treated as done conditions under AC1 or AC5. The about-data single source rule applies to the screen, JSON, and in-repo data without requiring a specific filename. Criterion 2 references the plan's step formula.\n\nThe verifier must not diff source code against the exact formula. Instead it must observe behavioral outcomes: input stays inside the r=0.22 zone roughly 40% of the time, a held full stick topples, calm reduces sway, and the four bots hit the score bands. These percentages and scores are the acceptance bar.\n\nThe no-input fraction inside the r=0.22 zone must fall between 0.2 and 0.55 over a 20-second simulation. This range prevents trivial stillness and instant topple. The human-like thumb stays around 90% in the ring. The 38% target is approximate; exact percentages would make tests brittle. Activity score bands remain the hard acceptance gates.\n\nThe eight yoga poses must be present: Seated Nothing, Patient Chair, Heron, Archer, Sideways Comma, Folded Napkin, Polite Bridge, and Lighthouse. Pose Class B and C days require all of them, so they are never-cut.\n\nStory beats are grouped under AC3 as short lines. The seed lines from PLAN.md section 13 must appear in the game's line data verbatim or with only punctuation fixes. This is checkable by grep and establishes the tone contract. The game must deliver at least 200 lines total. Plumb never uses an exclamation mark.\n\nDev mode must be absent in hub sessions. Settings music defaults to 10%. Free Play unlocks after day five and the ferry never returns. Group class is drawn from both the title and the pavilion. The exact write path is confirmed. Parent directories are created automatically.\n\nThe markdown draft will be concise. The implementer produces evidence for the verifier to audit. Subagent review is an implementer instruction, not a criterion. All five acceptance criteria are present. Each criterion must be independently checkable from its own start state.\n\nAC3 is being restructured to fit the one-outcome-per-criterion rule. The full playthrough becomes a single checkable family, with shell behaviors grouped as sub-items under it.\n\nAC3 is being split into atomic, independently checkable outcomes. Credits, attract mode, font size, and line count will each become separate criteria. Related mechanics such as shell behaviors and meta-game progression may be grouped under one outcome. This satisfies the atomicity requirement without exceeding the five-criterion limit.\n\nThe five criteria are now defined. One covers hand-in files and identity. One covers wobble, activities, and bot score bands. One covers residency days, story beats, finale, saves, and credits playthrough. One covers controls, UI, audio, art, attract mode, and dev mode. Multiplayer is the fifth. Audio and animation move into residency as presentation rather than shell chrome.\n\nThe five atomic criteria are now fully enumerated. Criterion one covers the hand-in package, title RNG, exit interview, and forbidden IP words. Criterion two covers wobble behavior, every activity's score bands, and self-end mechanics including the never-cut pose list. Criterion three covers the five-day flow, story lines, finale, saves, scripted credits, third-person island, cast, scrubbed animation, and code audio at ten percent. Criterion four covers ten-foot UX, glyphs, dev mode, attract mode, touch input, no-webgpu mode, and pause. Criterion five covers multiplayer, the hub harness, and HTML boot. Boot is treated as cross-cutting and attached to verification of all criteria.\n\nCriterion 1 is now the boot criterion. The handed-in HTML must actually run as the game and display title, ready, and volume. Criterion 5 is reserved for the hub harness. Engine checks remain in verification for criteria one or two. The four verification steps are mapped to their criteria: step one gates AC2 node tests, step two gates AC3 playthrough, step three gates AC1 boot twice, and step four gates AC4 UX shell.\n\nThe final criterion numbering is now fixed. Criterion one covers the hand-in package, boot, records, and IP boundaries. Criterion two covers balance activities. Criterion three covers residency flow, presentation, and audio. Criterion four covers controller-first shell behaviors such as dev mode, attract mode, touch input, and ten-foot UX. Criterion five covers multiplayer hub and couch.\n\nThe verification plan now has six steps. Step one gates AC2 node tests and part of AC3. Step two gates AC1 boot twice. Step three gates AC4 browser UX. Step four gates AC5 harness. Step five gates static files and AC3 content grep for lines, audio files, and todos. Step six collects evidence screenshots.\n\nCriterion one also requires npm run check to pass and the engine to remain unchanged or be declared.\n\nThe music default of 0.1 is placed in AC3 audio rather than the boot criterion, since the boot step verifies that default indirectly. The built file must fetch nothing externally; PLAN requires everything to be inlined and Three.js bundled.\n\nA 127.0.0.1 fallback is included in the risk and verification plan if createEngine fails on file://.\n\nThe non-goals list now explicitly excludes Wii Fit BMI, source names, FPS targets, cloning ai-hub, new npm packages, real iOS, listening tests, features beyond four players, shared-world mode, and git commits. Leaving a cut-line activity half-implemented—greyed out, marked TODO, or still listed after a cut—is also a non-goal.\n\nResearch confirmed the Wii Fit (2007) loop via Wikipedia and guides. It includes daily Body Tests, yoga holds with a breath guide, strength reps, aerobics, balance minigames, a trainer, and a time bank. PLAN.md already translates these into an original game. This plan does not add mechanics beyond PLAN.md. The research gap is closed.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Wii Fit’s loop is the daily balance test, centre-of-balance, and short pose, rep, and lean activities. PLAN.md already turns that into an original game, so the plan will gate those outcomes rather than a recreation."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-79a323f4-7c63-431c-adcb-701805ea692c-9","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc%2Fwork/01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc/goal/plan-01a1aa35-d6dc-77e0-8b97-ad9450b59141.md","content":"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original Wii Fit–inspired balance retreat and hand in TASK.md’s files\n\n## Goal kind\ncode-change\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. **Hand-in, title, and records.** The work root contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and one self-contained HTML file. The HTML and the dev subfolder share the basename `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656` (SOURCE keeps the space in `Wii Fit`; `{Title}` is the on-screen title, with only OS-illegal characters sanitized, and that sanitization noted in `PROGRESS.md`). `{Title}` was chosen by executing a real RNG over a saved list of 50 playful riffs on “Wii Fit,” and that list plus the pick are in the dev folder. The page shows `{Title}` on the title screen. `exit-interview.json` is one JSON object in TASK.md’s exact shape, every value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, and its Source Information plus the three feedback lists match the in-game About/Credits screen in the same order. `aiHub.genre` is `Sports`, `aiHub.source` is `Wii Fit`, `aiHub.type` is `Game`, `aiHub.players` is `1–4`. The word `Wii` appears in-game only on that source line and the hub source field. There is no weight, BMI, calorie, or body-size scoring, and none of these source names: Fit Age, Fit Credits, Fit Piggy Bank, Body Test, Wuhu, or the source’s minigame, menu, or melody names. Opening the HTML does not need the network. It contains no `data:audio`, `.mp3`, `.wav`, or `.ogg` payload.\n\n2. **One balance verb, every shipped activity.** Lean (left stick or D-pad, WASD or arrows, pointer-locked mouse), left foot, right foot, and both feet are the only activity inputs, and a new activity changes what the weight moves, not how it is moved. Centre-of-balance is a point in a unit disc. With no input, time inside radius 0.22 is well under half (sway stays slow). A held full lean topples. Calm from breathing with the guide reduces sway. Topple stumbles and recentres; it is never a game over. Each activity, started from its own fresh state, scores 0–1000, maps to Present / Adequate / Composed / Serene / Unsettling (thresholds 0 / 300 / 550 / 750 / 900), awards 1–5 pebbles by rank, ends itself on a time box if the pad is put down, and leaves no stray bodies. On 10 seeds the perfect bot scores at least 750, the human-like bot (about 250 ms late, gain about 1.2) at least 550, the sloppy bot (about 350 ms, gain about 0.8) at least 300, and idle at most 500 and below the human-like bot. Random input does not throw. These are present and playable: Morning Check (still ring, reach, one-foot, Wobble Age from 18 to 82), Pose Class for Seated Nothing, Patient Chair, Heron, Archer, Sideways Comma, Folded Napkin, Polite Bridge, and Lighthouse (enter, hold, release; the body scrub follows the thumb), Still Pond (only matched breathing is correct; other input costs composure; a lure is not a real pause; doing nothing is Adequate), The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, and Great Tilt steps storm, everyone-east, centre-stone (island tilt follows centre-of-balance), and dawn. Reps Hall, Step Class, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, Island Jog’s race, Great Tilt counterweights, expert variants, and outfits are either fully playable or removed in that cut order with the day list reflowed and no leftover mention.\n\n3. **Five-day retreat you can finish.** A third-person island hub has the stations and the cast Tare, Plumb, Gerald, Bex, Old Nan, the Gull, and the Frog. Walking is allowed, and every programme or noticeboard entry can also “Go” without crossing the island. Five days each do a Morning Check plus their activities, then sleep, stamps, and a short remark. Pebbles are never spent; they stack on a cairn. Hidden pebbles and creeping plaques exist. Saves restore day, results, pebbles, finds, outfits, bests, and settings; erase asks inside the game, not `confirm()`. A shipped auto playthrough from a fresh save, on 3 seeds, visits every shipped day, activity, and finale step and reaches credits without throwing or a softlock. After credits, free play and a new daily check remain, and the ferry does not come back. At least 200 lines, none longer than two short sentences, include PLAN.md’s seed lines; Plumb has no exclamation marks. All music, effects, and voices are code. Fresh-save music volume is 0.1. The same line always babbles the same way. Songs and syllables render non-silent. Pose and lean clips are scrubbed from balance or pose progress, not played as a canned squat at the player.\n\n4. **10-foot shell.** The same screens serve a standard-mapped pad, keyboard, and mouse. Prompts follow the last device in the same frame: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) after the Settings switch, key caps and mouse buttons after those inputs. Hover moves menu focus; click selects. Body text at 1920×1080 is at least 28 px; menus have at most six rows; results are one rank, one number, one sentence, not a dense data page. Pause opens Resume, Restart, Back to the island, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About/Credits, and Quit. Dev Mode, absent in a hub session, has live sliders for the numeric `game.*` tunables in PLAN.md (including wobble, breath, and pebble multiplier) and a reset to defaults, plus jumps to a day, activity, or finale step. Attract mode starts after 10 seconds without input on a front-end menu, loops through several activities (the driver may cheat), exits on key, click, or pad, and does not write the save. A touch-only profile shows “Controller or keyboard required” and a Settings control that opens by tap, with no on-screen touch play, while attract still runs. Without WebGPU the page shows a plain message naming current Chrome or Edge, not a blank page. Keyboard bindings can be rebound in Controls.\n\n5. **Couch Group Class and AI Hub ghost sessions.** Up to four players join mats on one screen (pad, or keyboard and mouse for one seat). Empty seats become CPU guests unless Settings turns them off. A round is three short classes dealt from one seed, then a podium. In AI Hub the mode is ghost: meta tags `hub:session` 1, `hub:session-players` 1-4, `hub:session-mode` ghost, `hub:session-minutes` 3, goal at most 60 characters, how-to at most 80 characters, `hub:webgpu` required, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` 1-4, `hub:source` Wii Fit. The fetched session client is pasted unmodified and is byte-identical in the HTML. Its SHA-256 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` (client 1.4.0). If the fetched file’s own banner is a newer client, record the new hash in `PROGRESS.md` and still inject those exact bytes. `?aihub` query keys are stripped before engine boot so unknown keys do not kill the engine. A session skips title, attract, and dev mode; pause does not pause the sim; `ready()` is reached with the engine actually running. Ghosts show the other players’ leans and do not affect local play. The hub harness passes for 2, 3, and 4 players and for host-leave: `__hubCheck.pass`, `body[data-complete=\"true\"]`, every frame `__engine.ready === true` and zero engine errors, a ghost present at 2 players, and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. With no hub, the game is the full single-player build.\n\n## Verification plan\n1. **gating:** In the dev workspace, add or extend tests that call the shipped balance step and each shipped activity’s own setup, step, score, and bot from a fresh sim. Do not reimplement the model, mock the unit under test, or start past setup. Assert criterion 2’s bands, self-end, age range, Still Pond penalties, teardown, and no `NaN`. Run `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` from the dev folder (full run, not only `--quick`) and write stdout to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the exit code is 0 and the log has no failure lines and one final count line of zero failures. Also write `{SCRATCH}/npm-test.log` and `{SCRATCH}/npm-check.log` for `npm test` and `npm run check`, both passing. `npm run check` shows the engine matches its lock, or `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` lists the exact engine diff and why.\n2. **gating:** The same verify command (or a node test it runs) drives the shipped auto/director path from a fresh save on 3 seeds, not a hand-rolled copy of the day table. `{SCRATCH}/verify.log` must show each seed reaching credits. Replay capture/restore of the shipped goldens continues exactly; that result is in the same log.\n3. **gating:** Launch the handed-in HTML twice, from `file://`, in headless Chromium (`CHROMIUM_PATH` = the headless shell, flags `--no-sandbox --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`). Both runs must agree: `__engine.ready === true`, `__engine.errors` empty, no WebGPU downgrade error, `window.__game` (screen, attract, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, event, focus) shows the title screen and `{Title}`, and the music volume is 0.1. The canvas’s drawing width and height equal the requested view and are non-zero. A scripted standard-mapping pad or a key, applied through the real menu path, changes `__game` the same way both times. Save `{SCRATCH}/boot-1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/boot-2.log`, `{SCRATCH}/boot-1.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/boot-2.png`. Primary observable is that state, not the bitmap. If the screenshot is fully blank or uncleared while those state checks pass, write `{SCRATCH}/webgpu-readback.txt` and do not fail the launch for pixels alone (known SwiftShader limit). If a readback shows a partial scene that disagrees with `__game`, that is a failure. If Chromium cannot start, save the error as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.txt`; node checks in steps 1–2 and the static checks in step 5 are then the bar. A started page with `ready` false or a blank state is never that fallback.\n4. **gating:** Browser-drive the built page (same Chromium) and record `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`. Must show: PlayStation glyphs before any Xbox setting, Xbox glyphs after the setting, prompts switching to keys and to mouse in the same frame as those inputs, hover moving focus, attract beginning at 10–11 s of idle and leaving on a key, a click, and a pad press with the save unchanged, a dev slider changing live behaviour and Reset restoring the default, About text containing every Source Information field and each feedback answer, and at 1920×1080 every visible text node at least 28 px and no menu over six rows. WebKit (no WebGPU here) shows the Chrome-or-Edge message. An iPhone and a tablet Chromium profile show the controller-or-keyboard line, open Settings by `touchscreen.tap`, draw no touch stick, and still run attract.\n5. **gating:** Serve the built HTML and the fetched harness files with a single `127.0.0.1` process on an OS-picked port; stop only that PID. Run the harness for `players=2`, `3`, `4`, and `players=3&scenario=host-leave` (latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5). Write `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Pass only if each run has `__hubCheck.pass`, `data-complete=\"true\"`, per-frame engine ready and zero engine errors, a ghost at 2 players, and no `[AIHub]` console line. `{SCRATCH}/hub-client.sha` is the injected client hash and matches criterion 5. A no-session load still reaches the title. Standalone client check: Enter, Space, Enter, Space leaves the client present and inactive, leaves `Math.random` unseeded, draws the screen, and adds no errors.\n6. **gating:** Static pass on the work root: HTML and folder names, `exit-interview.json` parses and matches About, `PROGRESS.md` exists, game README says what the game is and which judgment calls were made, `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` is specific, and a search of the game sources for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, and `stub` is clean except test wording. Audio proof (song and syllable render, music 0.1) is in `{SCRATCH}/verify.log` or `{SCRATCH}/audio.log`.\n7. **evidence:** 1920×1080 screenshots under `{SCRATCH}/shots/` of title, island, Pose Class HUD, Still Pond with a lure, The Line, Great Tilt wide shot, results, pause, Dev Mode, and About. Missing shots do not fail when steps 1–6 passed, including an honest launch fallback.\n\n## Non-goals\n- Recreating Wii Fit’s BMI or weight test, trainer look, Balance Board, Miis, Wuhu Island, Fit Bank, logos, fonts, or melodies.\n- Frame-rate optimization, except the hub’s 20-second `ready()` deadline.\n- Cloning the ai-hub repository, adding npm dependencies, or `git init` / commits in the dev folder.\n- Shared-world or more than four players, real gamepads, iOS Safari, and listening tests by ear.\n- A second desktop-only layout, or touch controls beyond the required message and Settings button.\n- A half-built cut-line activity left in the menu.\n\n## Assumed scope\nWork root is the folder that holds `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`. The dev subfolder is a my-3dge full workspace created with that repo’s `new-game` tool (clone only for the generator, then delete it), then `npm ci` only. Game code lives on top of the read-only engine. Hub `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, and `round.js` are fetched from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`, not from a clone. Deliverables in the work root: the one HTML file, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\n\nWii Fit (Nintendo, 2007) was checked against public guides: daily Body Test (weight, BMI, centre-of-balance, Wii Fit Age), yoga holds with a breath guide, strength reps, aerobics (step, hoop, jog), lean-steered balance minigames, a trainer, and a time bank. PLAN.md already maps those into an original wobble game and bans body-size metrics and source names. Do not add activities the plan does not list.\n\n## Implementation approach\nFollow PLAN.md for design detail. This section is not a second spec: a structure that meets the criteria is acceptable even if file names differ, as long as the engine’s own workspace rules still hold (`labs/game` is where this engine mounts a game).\n\nKeep the balance step, activity rules, bots, and day tables as pure sim code with no rendering or DOM, so node tests drive them directly. The page only reads sim state, plays code audio, and sends input or menu choices back through the engine’s recorded channels. One bot writer produces perfect, human-like, sloppy, idle, attract, and CPU-guest input. Days, poses, charts, trays, and lines stay data. Ship a smaller complete loop early (boot, one activity, one-file HTML, hub `ready`) and add activities without breaking that loop. Put the single verify command in front of the node tests, the three-seed auto playthrough, audio renders, and the browser and harness stages. Publish `window.__game` for those checks. Write About copy and `exit-interview.json` from the same answers before the final HTML build.\n\n## Task checklist\n- [ ] Save 50 titles, RNG-pick one, create the named dev folder with `new-game`, `npm ci`, and a work-root `PROGRESS.md`.\n- [ ] One-file HTML build, unmodified hub client and tags, tiny session scene, verify command skeleton, harness green with the engine actually ready.\n- [ ] Balance model, maps, disc, glyphs, Morning Check, four bots, dev sliders, and the score-band tests.\n- [ ] Scrubbed clips, Guest and Plumb, Pose Class, code voices, island, flow, saves, results.\n- [ ] Still Pond, The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, any later activities not cut, Great Tilt, and the 3-seed auto path to credits.\n- [ ] Same-screen Group Class and hub ghosts; harness 2, 3, 4, and host-leave.\n- [ ] Attract, settings, controls, About, touch-only and no-WebGPU pages, songs, and 200+ lines.\n- [ ] `exit-interview.json`, HTML copied to the work root, full verify, and two consistent boots with logs under `{SCRATCH}`.\n\n## Risks / Contradictions\n- TASK.md says a headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank and to trust game state. A fully blank bitmap with `ready` and a real `__game` screen is that limit, not an automatic fail. A readable but empty or partial frame that disagrees with `__game` is a game bug.\n- The live client fetched while writing this plan is banner `1.4.0`, matching PLAN.md’s pin. If the upstream file moves, the unmodified fetched bytes win over the old hash, and the new hash goes in `PROGRESS.md`.\n- The required basename contains a space because SOURCE is `Wii Fit`. That is intentional, not a reason to drop the source token.\n- PLAN.md forbids `git init` in the dev folder even though the engine guide says to commit. Do not init or commit.\n- Other agents share ports and memory. Do not bind 5173, do not kill processes you did not start, and do not run more than two browsers. `earlyoom` killing a browser is memory pressure, not a game bug to retry in a loop.\n- Great Tilt’s session scene must stay small: SwiftShader has needed most of the harness’s 20 s just to boot. The island must not load in a hub session.\n- Firefox has no WebGPU here. Only Chromium can run the game; WebKit is only for the no-WebGPU message.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":18125,"output_tokens":12403,"cache_read_input_tokens":43392,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"30f45788-7235-49cf-b14c-09398c0d8702"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-79a323f4-7c63-431c-adcb-701805ea692c-9","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"\",\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original Wii Fit–inspired balance retreat and hand in TASK.md’s files\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in, title, and records.** The work root contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and one self-contained HTML file. The HTML and the dev subfolder share the basename `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656` (SOURCE keeps the space in `Wii Fit`; `{Title}` is the on-screen title, with only OS-illegal characters sanitized, and that sanitization noted in `PROGRESS.md`). `{Title}` was chosen by executing a real RNG over a saved list of 50 playful riffs on “Wii Fit,” and that list plus the pick are in the dev folder. The page shows `{Title}` on the title screen. `exit-interview.json` is one JSON object in TASK.md’s exact shape, every value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, and its Source Information plus the three feedback lists match the in-game About/Credits screen in the same order. `aiHub.genre` is `Sports`, `aiHub.source` is `Wii Fit`, `aiHub.type` is `Game`, `aiHub.players` is `1–4`. The word `Wii` appears in-game only on that source line and the hub source field. There is no weight, BMI, calorie, or body-size scoring, and none of these source names: Fit Age, Fit Credits, Fit Piggy Bank, Body Test, Wuhu, or the source’s minigame, menu, or melody names. Opening the HTML does not need the network. It contains no `data:audio`, `.mp3`, `.wav`, or `.ogg` payload.\\n\\n2. **One balance verb, every shipped activity.** Lean (left stick or D-pad, WASD or arrows, pointer-locked mouse), left foot, right foot, and both feet are the only activity inputs, and a new activity changes what the weight moves, not how it is moved. Centre-of-balance is a point in a unit disc. With no input, time inside radius 0.22 is well under half (sway stays slow). A held full lean topples. Calm from breathing with the guide reduces sway. Topple stumbles and recentres; it is never a game over. Each activity, started from its own fresh state, scores 0–1000, maps to Present / Adequate / Composed / Serene / Unsettling (thresholds 0 / 300 / 550 / 750 / 900), awards 1–5 pebbles by rank, ends itself on a time box if the pad is put down, and leaves no stray bodies. On 10 seeds the perfect bot scores at least 750, the human-like bot (about 250 ms late, gain about 1.2) at least 550, the sloppy bot (about 350 ms, gain about 0.8) at least 300, and idle at most 500 and below the human-like bot. Random input does not throw. These are present and playable: Morning Check (still ring, reach, one-foot, Wobble Age from 18 to 82), Pose Class for Seated Nothing, Patient Chair, Heron, Archer, Sideways Comma, Folded Napkin, Polite Bridge, and Lighthouse (enter, hold, release; the body scrub follows the thumb), Still Pond (only matched breathing is correct; other input costs composure; a lure is not a real pause; doing nothing is Adequate), The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, and Great Tilt steps storm, everyone-east, centre-stone (island tilt follows centre-of-balance), and dawn. Reps Hall, Step Class, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, Island Jog’s race, Great Tilt counterweights, expert variants, and outfits are either fully playable or removed in that cut order with the day list reflowed and no leftover mention.\\n\\n3. **Five-day retreat you can finish.** A third-person island hub has the stations and the cast Tare, Plumb, Gerald, Bex, Old Nan, the Gull, and the Frog. Walking is allowed, and every programme or noticeboard entry can also “Go” without crossing the island. Five days each do a Morning Check plus their activities, then sleep, stamps, and a short remark. Pebbles are never spent; they stack on a cairn. Hidden pebbles and creeping plaques exist. Saves restore day, results, pebbles, finds, outfits, bests, and settings; erase asks inside the game, not `confirm()`. A shipped auto playthrough from a fresh save, on 3 seeds, visits every shipped day, activity, and finale step and reaches credits without throwing or a softlock. After credits, free play and a new daily check remain, and the ferry does not come back. At least 200 lines, none longer than two short sentences, include PLAN.md’s seed lines; Plumb has no exclamation marks. All music, effects, and voices are code. Fresh-save music volume is 0.1. The same line always babbles the same way. Songs and syllables render non-silent. Pose and lean clips are scrubbed from balance or pose progress, not played as a canned squat at the player.\\n\\n4. **10-foot shell.** The same screens serve a standard-mapped pad, keyboard, and mouse. Prompts follow the last device in the same frame: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) after the Settings switch, key caps and mouse buttons after those inputs. Hover moves menu focus; click selects. Body text at 1920×1080 is at least 28 px; menus have at most six rows; results are one rank, one number, one sentence, not a dense data page. Pause opens Resume, Restart, Back to the island, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About/Credits, and Quit. Dev Mode, absent in a hub session, has live sliders for the numeric `game.*` tunables in PLAN.md (including wobble, breath, and pebble multiplier) and a reset to defaults, plus jumps to a day, activity, or finale step. Attract mode starts after 10 seconds without input on a front-end menu, loops through several activities (the driver may cheat), exits on key, click, or pad, and does not write the save. A touch-only profile shows “Controller or keyboard required” and a Settings control that opens by tap, with no on-screen touch play, while attract still runs. Without WebGPU the page shows a plain message naming current Chrome or Edge, not a blank page. Keyboard bindings can be rebound in Controls.\\n\\n5. **Couch Group Class and AI Hub ghost sessions.** Up to four players join mats on one screen (pad, or keyboard and mouse for one seat). Empty seats become CPU guests unless Settings turns them off. A round is three short classes dealt from one seed, then a podium. In AI Hub the mode is ghost: meta tags `hub:session` 1, `hub:session-players` 1-4, `hub:session-mode` ghost, `hub:session-minutes` 3, goal at most 60 characters, how-to at most 80 characters, `hub:webgpu` required, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` 1-4, `hub:source` Wii Fit. The fetched session client is pasted unmodified and is byte-identical in the HTML. Its SHA-256 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` (client 1.4.0). If the fetched file’s own banner is a newer client, record the new hash in `PROGRESS.md` and still inject those exact bytes. `?aihub` query keys are stripped before engine boot so unknown keys do not kill the engine. A session skips title, attract, and dev mode; pause does not pause the sim; `ready()` is reached with the engine actually running. Ghosts show the other players’ leans and do not affect local play. The hub harness passes for 2, 3, and 4 players and for host-leave: `__hubCheck.pass`, `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, every frame `__engine.ready === true` and zero engine errors, a ghost present at 2 players, and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. With no hub, the game is the full single-player build.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** In the dev workspace, add or extend tests that call the shipped balance step and each shipped activity’s own setup, step, score, and bot from a fresh sim. Do not reimplement the model, mock the unit under test, or start past setup. Assert criterion 2’s bands, self-end, age range, Still Pond penalties, teardown, and no `NaN`. Run `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` from the dev folder (full run, not only `--quick`) and write stdout to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the exit code is 0 and the log has no failure lines and one final count line of zero failures. Also write `{SCRATCH}/npm-test.log` and `{SCRATCH}/npm-check.log` for `npm test` and `npm run check`, both passing. `npm run check` shows the engine matches its lock, or `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` lists the exact engine diff and why.\\n2. **gating:** The same verify command (or a node test it runs) drives the shipped auto/director path from a fresh save on 3 seeds, not a hand-rolled copy of the day table. `{SCRATCH}/verify.log` must show each seed reaching credits. Replay capture/restore of the shipped goldens continues exactly; that result is in the same log.\\n3. **gating:** Launch the handed-in HTML twice, from `file://`, in headless Chromium (`CHROMIUM_PATH` = the headless shell, flags `--no-sandbox --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`). Both runs must agree: `__engine.ready === true`, `__engine.errors` empty, no WebGPU downgrade error, `window.__game` (screen, attract, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, event, focus) shows the title screen and `{Title}`, and the music volume is 0.1. The canvas’s drawing width and height equal the requested view and are non-zero. A scripted standard-mapping pad or a key, applied through the real menu path, changes `__game` the same way both times. Save `{SCRATCH}/boot-1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/boot-2.log`, `{SCRATCH}/boot-1.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/boot-2.png`. Primary observable is that state, not the bitmap. If the screenshot is fully blank or uncleared while those state checks pass, write `{SCRATCH}/webgpu-readback.txt` and do not fail the launch for pixels alone (known SwiftShader limit). If a readback shows a partial scene that disagrees with `__game`, that is a failure. If Chromium cannot start, save the error as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.txt`; node checks in steps 1–2 and the static checks in step 5 are then the bar. A started page with `ready` false or a blank state is never that fallback.\\n4. **gating:** Browser-drive the built page (same Chromium) and record `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`. Must show: PlayStation glyphs before any Xbox setting, Xbox glyphs after the setting, prompts switching to keys and to mouse in the same frame as those inputs, hover moving focus, attract beginning at 10–11 s of idle and leaving on a key, a click, and a pad press with the save unchanged, a dev slider changing live behaviour and Reset restoring the default, About text containing every Source Information field and each feedback answer, and at 1920×1080 every visible text node at least 28 px and no menu over six rows. WebKit (no WebGPU here) shows the Chrome-or-Edge message. An iPhone and a tablet Chromium profile show the controller-or-keyboard line, open Settings by `touchscreen.tap`, draw no touch stick, and still run attract.\\n5. **gating:** Serve the built HTML and the fetched harness files with a single `127.0.0.1` process on an OS-picked port; stop only that PID. Run the harness for `players=2`, `3`, `4`, and `players=3&scenario=host-leave` (latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5). Write `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Pass only if each run has `__hubCheck.pass`, `data-complete=\\\"true\\\"`, per-frame engine ready and zero engine errors, a ghost at 2 players, and no `[AIHub]` console line. `{SCRATCH}/hub-client.sha` is the injected client hash and matches criterion 5. A no-session load still reaches the title. Standalone client check: Enter, Space, Enter, Space leaves the client present and inactive, leaves `Math.random` unseeded, draws the screen, and adds no errors.\\n6. **gating:** Static pass on the work root: HTML and folder names, `exit-interview.json` parses and matches About, `PROGRESS.md` exists, game README says what the game is and which judgment calls were made, `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` is specific, and a search of the game sources for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, and `stub` is clean except test wording. Audio proof (song and syllable render, music 0.1) is in `{SCRATCH}/verify.log` or `{SCRATCH}/audio.log`.\\n7. **evidence:** 1920×1080 screenshots under `{SCRATCH}/shots/` of title, island, Pose Class HUD, Still Pond with a lure, The Line, Great Tilt wide shot, results, pause, Dev Mode, and About. Missing shots do not fail when steps 1–6 passed, including an honest launch fallback.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- Recreating Wii Fit’s BMI or weight test, trainer look, Balance Board, Miis, Wuhu Island, Fit Bank, logos, fonts, or melodies.\\n- Frame-rate optimization, except the hub’s 20-second `ready()` deadline.\\n- Cloning the ai-hub repository, adding npm dependencies, or `git init` / commits in the dev folder.\\n- Shared-world or more than four players, real gamepads, iOS Safari, and listening tests by ear.\\n- A second desktop-only layout, or touch controls beyond the required message and Settings button.\\n- A half-built cut-line activity left in the menu.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nWork root is the folder that holds `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`. The dev subfolder is a my-3dge full workspace created with that repo’s `new-game` tool (clone only for the generator, then delete it), then `npm ci` only. Game code lives on top of the read-only engine. Hub `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, and `round.js` are fetched from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`, not from a clone. Deliverables in the work root: the one HTML file, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n\\nWii Fit (Nintendo, 2007) was checked against public guides: daily Body Test (weight, BMI, centre-of-balance, Wii Fit Age), yoga holds with a breath guide, strength reps, aerobics (step, hoop, jog), lean-steered balance minigames, a trainer, and a time bank. PLAN.md already maps those into an original wobble game and bans body-size metrics and source names. Do not add activities the plan does not list.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for design detail. This section is not a second spec: a structure that meets the criteria is acceptable even if file names differ, as long as the engine’s own workspace rules still hold (`labs/game` is where this engine mounts a game).\\n\\nKeep the balance step, activity rules, bots, and day tables as pure sim code with no rendering or DOM, so node tests drive them directly. The page only reads sim state, plays code audio, and sends input or menu choices back through the engine’s recorded channels. One bot writer produces perfect, human-like, sloppy, idle, attract, and CPU-guest input. Days, poses, charts, trays, and lines stay data. Ship a smaller complete loop early (boot, one activity, one-file HTML, hub `ready`) and add activities without breaking that loop. Put the single verify command in front of the node tests, the three-seed auto playthrough, audio renders, and the browser and harness stages. Publish `window.__game` for those checks. Write About copy and `exit-interview.json` from the same answers before the final HTML build.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Save 50 titles, RNG-pick one, create the named dev folder with `new-game`, `npm ci`, and a work-root `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- [ ] One-file HTML build, unmodified hub client and tags, tiny session scene, verify command skeleton, harness green with the engine actually ready.\\n- [ ] Balance model, maps, disc, glyphs, Morning Check, four bots, dev sliders, and the score-band tests.\\n- [ ] Scrubbed clips, Guest and Plumb, Pose Class, code voices, island, flow, saves, results.\\n- [ ] Still Pond, The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, any later activities not cut, Great Tilt, and the 3-seed auto path to credits.\\n- [ ] Same-screen Group Class and hub ghosts; harness 2, 3, 4, and host-leave.\\n- [ ] Attract, settings, controls, About, touch-only and no-WebGPU pages, songs, and 200+ lines.\\n- [ ] `exit-interview.json`, HTML copied to the work root, full verify, and two consistent boots with logs under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md says a headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank and to trust game state. A fully blank bitmap with `ready` and a real `__game` screen is that limit, not an automatic fail. A readable but empty or partial frame that disagrees with `__game` is a game bug.\\n- The live client fetched while writing this plan is banner `1.4.0`, matching PLAN.md’s pin. If the upstream file moves, the unmodified fetched bytes win over the old hash, and the new hash goes in `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- The required basename contains a space because SOURCE is `Wii Fit`. That is intentional, not a reason to drop the source token.\\n- PLAN.md forbids `git init` in the dev folder even though the engine guide says to commit. Do not init or commit.\\n- Other agents share ports and memory. Do not bind 5173, do not kill processes you did not start, and do not run more than two browsers. `earlyoom` killing a browser is memory pressure, not a game bug to retry in a loop.\\n- Great Tilt’s session scene must stay small: SwiftShader has needed most of the harness’s 20 s just to boot. The island must not load in a hub session.\\n- Firefox has no WebGPU here. Only Chromium can run the game; WebKit is only for the no-WebGPU message.\\n\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc%2Fwork/01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc/goal/plan-01a1aa35-d6dc-77e0-8b97-ad9450b59141.md has been created.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc%2Fwork/01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc/goal/plan-01a1aa35-d6dc-77e0-8b97-ad9450b59141.md has been created.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc%2Fwork/01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc/goal/plan-01a1aa35-d6dc-77e0-8b97-ad9450b59141.md\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"\",\"old_line\":1,\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original Wii Fit–inspired balance retreat and hand in TASK.md’s files\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in, title, and records.** The work root contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and one self-contained HTML file. The HTML and the dev subfolder share the basename `{Title}-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656` (SOURCE keeps the space in `Wii Fit`; `{Title}` is the on-screen title, with only OS-illegal characters sanitized, and that sanitization noted in `PROGRESS.md`). `{Title}` was chosen by executing a real RNG over a saved list of 50 playful riffs on “Wii Fit,” and that list plus the pick are in the dev folder. The page shows `{Title}` on the title screen. `exit-interview.json` is one JSON object in TASK.md’s exact shape, every value filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, and its Source Information plus the three feedback lists match the in-game About/Credits screen in the same order. `aiHub.genre` is `Sports`, `aiHub.source` is `Wii Fit`, `aiHub.type` is `Game`, `aiHub.players` is `1–4`. The word `Wii` appears in-game only on that source line and the hub source field. There is no weight, BMI, calorie, or body-size scoring, and none of these source names: Fit Age, Fit Credits, Fit Piggy Bank, Body Test, Wuhu, or the source’s minigame, menu, or melody names. Opening the HTML does not need the network. It contains no `data:audio`, `.mp3`, `.wav`, or `.ogg` payload.\\n\\n2. **One balance verb, every shipped activity.** Lean (left stick or D-pad, WASD or arrows, pointer-locked mouse), left foot, right foot, and both feet are the only activity inputs, and a new activity changes what the weight moves, not how it is moved. Centre-of-balance is a point in a unit disc. With no input, time inside radius 0.22 is well under half (sway stays slow). A held full lean topples. Calm from breathing with the guide reduces sway. Topple stumbles and recentres; it is never a game over. Each activity, started from its own fresh state, scores 0–1000, maps to Present / Adequate / Composed / Serene / Unsettling (thresholds 0 / 300 / 550 / 750 / 900), awards 1–5 pebbles by rank, ends itself on a time box if the pad is put down, and leaves no stray bodies. On 10 seeds the perfect bot scores at least 750, the human-like bot (about 250 ms late, gain about 1.2) at least 550, the sloppy bot (about 350 ms, gain about 0.8) at least 300, and idle at most 500 and below the human-like bot. Random input does not throw. These are present and playable: Morning Check (still ring, reach, one-foot, Wobble Age from 18 to 82), Pose Class for Seated Nothing, Patient Chair, Heron, Archer, Sideways Comma, Folded Napkin, Polite Bridge, and Lighthouse (enter, hold, release; the body scrub follows the thumb), Still Pond (only matched breathing is correct; other input costs composure; a lure is not a real pause; doing nothing is Adequate), The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, and Great Tilt steps storm, everyone-east, centre-stone (island tilt follows centre-of-balance), and dawn. Reps Hall, Step Class, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, Island Jog’s race, Great Tilt counterweights, expert variants, and outfits are either fully playable or removed in that cut order with the day list reflowed and no leftover mention.\\n\\n3. **Five-day retreat you can finish.** A third-person island hub has the stations and the cast Tare, Plumb, Gerald, Bex, Old Nan, the Gull, and the Frog. Walking is allowed, and every programme or noticeboard entry can also “Go” without crossing the island. Five days each do a Morning Check plus their activities, then sleep, stamps, and a short remark. Pebbles are never spent; they stack on a cairn. Hidden pebbles and creeping plaques exist. Saves restore day, results, pebbles, finds, outfits, bests, and settings; erase asks inside the game, not `confirm()`. A shipped auto playthrough from a fresh save, on 3 seeds, visits every shipped day, activity, and finale step and reaches credits without throwing or a softlock. After credits, free play and a new daily check remain, and the ferry does not come back. At least 200 lines, none longer than two short sentences, include PLAN.md’s seed lines; Plumb has no exclamation marks. All music, effects, and voices are code. Fresh-save music volume is 0.1. The same line always babbles the same way. Songs and syllables render non-silent. Pose and lean clips are scrubbed from balance or pose progress, not played as a canned squat at the player.\\n\\n4. **10-foot shell.** The same screens serve a standard-mapped pad, keyboard, and mouse. Prompts follow the last device in the same frame: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) after the Settings switch, key caps and mouse buttons after those inputs. Hover moves menu focus; click selects. Body text at 1920×1080 is at least 28 px; menus have at most six rows; results are one rank, one number, one sentence, not a dense data page. Pause opens Resume, Restart, Back to the island, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About/Credits, and Quit. Dev Mode, absent in a hub session, has live sliders for the numeric `game.*` tunables in PLAN.md (including wobble, breath, and pebble multiplier) and a reset to defaults, plus jumps to a day, activity, or finale step. Attract mode starts after 10 seconds without input on a front-end menu, loops through several activities (the driver may cheat), exits on key, click, or pad, and does not write the save. A touch-only profile shows “Controller or keyboard required” and a Settings control that opens by tap, with no on-screen touch play, while attract still runs. Without WebGPU the page shows a plain message naming current Chrome or Edge, not a blank page. Keyboard bindings can be rebound in Controls.\\n\\n5. **Couch Group Class and AI Hub ghost sessions.** Up to four players join mats on one screen (pad, or keyboard and mouse for one seat). Empty seats become CPU guests unless Settings turns them off. A round is three short classes dealt from one seed, then a podium. In AI Hub the mode is ghost: meta tags `hub:session` 1, `hub:session-players` 1-4, `hub:session-mode` ghost, `hub:session-minutes` 3, goal at most 60 characters, how-to at most 80 characters, `hub:webgpu` required, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` 1-4, `hub:source` Wii Fit. The fetched session client is pasted unmodified and is byte-identical in the HTML. Its SHA-256 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` (client 1.4.0). If the fetched file’s own banner is a newer client, record the new hash in `PROGRESS.md` and still inject those exact bytes. `?aihub` query keys are stripped before engine boot so unknown keys do not kill the engine. A session skips title, attract, and dev mode; pause does not pause the sim; `ready()` is reached with the engine actually running. Ghosts show the other players’ leans and do not affect local play. The hub harness passes for 2, 3, and 4 players and for host-leave: `__hubCheck.pass`, `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, every frame `__engine.ready === true` and zero engine errors, a ghost present at 2 players, and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. With no hub, the game is the full single-player build.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** In the dev workspace, add or extend tests that call the shipped balance step and each shipped activity’s own setup, step, score, and bot from a fresh sim. Do not reimplement the model, mock the unit under test, or start past setup. Assert criterion 2’s bands, self-end, age range, Still Pond penalties, teardown, and no `NaN`. Run `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` from the dev folder (full run, not only `--quick`) and write stdout to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the exit code is 0 and the log has no failure lines and one final count line of zero failures. Also write `{SCRATCH}/npm-test.log` and `{SCRATCH}/npm-check.log` for `npm test` and `npm run check`, both passing. `npm run check` shows the engine matches its lock, or `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` lists the exact engine diff and why.\\n2. **gating:** The same verify command (or a node test it runs) drives the shipped auto/director path from a fresh save on 3 seeds, not a hand-rolled copy of the day table. `{SCRATCH}/verify.log` must show each seed reaching credits. Replay capture/restore of the shipped goldens continues exactly; that result is in the same log.\\n3. **gating:** Launch the handed-in HTML twice, from `file://`, in headless Chromium (`CHROMIUM_PATH` = the headless shell, flags `--no-sandbox --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`). Both runs must agree: `__engine.ready === true`, `__engine.errors` empty, no WebGPU downgrade error, `window.__game` (screen, attract, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, event, focus) shows the title screen and `{Title}`, and the music volume is 0.1. The canvas’s drawing width and height equal the requested view and are non-zero. A scripted standard-mapping pad or a key, applied through the real menu path, changes `__game` the same way both times. Save `{SCRATCH}/boot-1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/boot-2.log`, `{SCRATCH}/boot-1.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/boot-2.png`. Primary observable is that state, not the bitmap. If the screenshot is fully blank or uncleared while those state checks pass, write `{SCRATCH}/webgpu-readback.txt` and do not fail the launch for pixels alone (known SwiftShader limit). If a readback shows a partial scene that disagrees with `__game`, that is a failure. If Chromium cannot start, save the error as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.txt`; node checks in steps 1–2 and the static checks in step 5 are then the bar. A started page with `ready` false or a blank state is never that fallback.\\n4. **gating:** Browser-drive the built page (same Chromium) and record `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`. Must show: PlayStation glyphs before any Xbox setting, Xbox glyphs after the setting, prompts switching to keys and to mouse in the same frame as those inputs, hover moving focus, attract beginning at 10–11 s of idle and leaving on a key, a click, and a pad press with the save unchanged, a dev slider changing live behaviour and Reset restoring the default, About text containing every Source Information field and each feedback answer, and at 1920×1080 every visible text node at least 28 px and no menu over six rows. WebKit (no WebGPU here) shows the Chrome-or-Edge message. An iPhone and a tablet Chromium profile show the controller-or-keyboard line, open Settings by `touchscreen.tap`, draw no touch stick, and still run attract.\\n5. **gating:** Serve the built HTML and the fetched harness files with a single `127.0.0.1` process on an OS-picked port; stop only that PID. Run the harness for `players=2`, `3`, `4`, and `players=3&scenario=host-leave` (latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5). Write `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Pass only if each run has `__hubCheck.pass`, `data-complete=\\\"true\\\"`, per-frame engine ready and zero engine errors, a ghost at 2 players, and no `[AIHub]` console line. `{SCRATCH}/hub-client.sha` is the injected client hash and matches criterion 5. A no-session load still reaches the title. Standalone client check: Enter, Space, Enter, Space leaves the client present and inactive, leaves `Math.random` unseeded, draws the screen, and adds no errors.\\n6. **gating:** Static pass on the work root: HTML and folder names, `exit-interview.json` parses and matches About, `PROGRESS.md` exists, game README says what the game is and which judgment calls were made, `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` is specific, and a search of the game sources for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, and `stub` is clean except test wording. Audio proof (song and syllable render, music 0.1) is in `{SCRATCH}/verify.log` or `{SCRATCH}/audio.log`.\\n7. **evidence:** 1920×1080 screenshots under `{SCRATCH}/shots/` of title, island, Pose Class HUD, Still Pond with a lure, The Line, Great Tilt wide shot, results, pause, Dev Mode, and About. Missing shots do not fail when steps 1–6 passed, including an honest launch fallback.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- Recreating Wii Fit’s BMI or weight test, trainer look, Balance Board, Miis, Wuhu Island, Fit Bank, logos, fonts, or melodies.\\n- Frame-rate optimization, except the hub’s 20-second `ready()` deadline.\\n- Cloning the ai-hub repository, adding npm dependencies, or `git init` / commits in the dev folder.\\n- Shared-world or more than four players, real gamepads, iOS Safari, and listening tests by ear.\\n- A second desktop-only layout, or touch controls beyond the required message and Settings button.\\n- A half-built cut-line activity left in the menu.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nWork root is the folder that holds `STAGE.md`, `PLAN.md`, and `TASK.md`. The dev subfolder is a my-3dge full workspace created with that repo’s `new-game` tool (clone only for the generator, then delete it), then `npm ci` only. Game code lives on top of the read-only engine. Hub `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, and `round.js` are fetched from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`, not from a clone. Deliverables in the work root: the one HTML file, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n\\nWii Fit (Nintendo, 2007) was checked against public guides: daily Body Test (weight, BMI, centre-of-balance, Wii Fit Age), yoga holds with a breath guide, strength reps, aerobics (step, hoop, jog), lean-steered balance minigames, a trainer, and a time bank. PLAN.md already maps those into an original wobble game and bans body-size metrics and source names. Do not add activities the plan does not list.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for design detail. This section is not a second spec: a structure that meets the criteria is acceptable even if file names differ, as long as the engine’s own workspace rules still hold (`labs/game` is where this engine mounts a game).\\n\\nKeep the balance step, activity rules, bots, and day tables as pure sim code with no rendering or DOM, so node tests drive them directly. The page only reads sim state, plays code audio, and sends input or menu choices back through the engine’s recorded channels. One bot writer produces perfect, human-like, sloppy, idle, attract, and CPU-guest input. Days, poses, charts, trays, and lines stay data. Ship a smaller complete loop early (boot, one activity, one-file HTML, hub `ready`) and add activities without breaking that loop. Put the single verify command in front of the node tests, the three-seed auto playthrough, audio renders, and the browser and harness stages. Publish `window.__game` for those checks. Write About copy and `exit-interview.json` from the same answers before the final HTML build.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Save 50 titles, RNG-pick one, create the named dev folder with `new-game`, `npm ci`, and a work-root `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- [ ] One-file HTML build, unmodified hub client and tags, tiny session scene, verify command skeleton, harness green with the engine actually ready.\\n- [ ] Balance model, maps, disc, glyphs, Morning Check, four bots, dev sliders, and the score-band tests.\\n- [ ] Scrubbed clips, Guest and Plumb, Pose Class, code voices, island, flow, saves, results.\\n- [ ] Still Pond, The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden, any later activities not cut, Great Tilt, and the 3-seed auto path to credits.\\n- [ ] Same-screen Group Class and hub ghosts; harness 2, 3, 4, and host-leave.\\n- [ ] Attract, settings, controls, About, touch-only and no-WebGPU pages, songs, and 200+ lines.\\n- [ ] `exit-interview.json`, HTML copied to the work root, full verify, and two consistent boots with logs under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md says a headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank and to trust game state. A fully blank bitmap with `ready` and a real `__game` screen is that limit, not an automatic fail. A readable but empty or partial frame that disagrees with `__game` is a game bug.\\n- The live client fetched while writing this plan is banner `1.4.0`, matching PLAN.md’s pin. If the upstream file moves, the unmodified fetched bytes win over the old hash, and the new hash goes in `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- The required basename contains a space because SOURCE is `Wii Fit`. That is intentional, not a reason to drop the source token.\\n- PLAN.md forbids `git init` in the dev folder even though the engine guide says to commit. Do not init or commit.\\n- Other agents share ports and memory. Do not bind 5173, do not kill processes you did not start, and do not run more than two browsers. `earlyoom` killing a browser is memory pressure, not a game bug to retry in a loop.\\n- Great Tilt’s session scene must stay small: SwiftShader has needed most of the harness’s 20 s just to boot. The island must not load in a hub session.\\n- Firefox has no WebGPU here. Only Chromium can run the game; WebKit is only for the no-WebGPU message.\\n\",\"new_line\":1,\"context_before\":\"\",\"context_after\":\"\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"5538bd5f-62a5-42da-bd75-b966f5fadcc2"}
