{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-870db9ee-a922-41af-aac6-dd7d5ced8481-84","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"140→  return [\\n    {\\n      name: 'sim: clocks, randomness, DOM, Web Audio',\\n      files: SIM_SIDE,\\n      ignores: ['**/*.test.ts'],\\n      plugins: { sim: simPlugin },\\n      rules: {\\n        'sim/no-restricted-globals': [\\n          'error',\\n          ...Object.entries(GLOBALS).map(([name, message]) => ({ name, message })),\\n150→        ],\\n        'sim/no-restricted-properties': ['error', { object: 'Math', property: 'random', message: RANDOM }],\\n        'sim/no-restricted-syntax': [\\n          'error',\\n          {\\n            selector: viaHost,\\n            message:\\n              \\\"a banned global reached through globalThis, self or global is still banned: sim-side code reads no clock, schedules nothing by wall time, touches no DOM or Web Audio, and draws randomness only from named streams, rng('<stream>') (PLAN.md §6.5)\\\",\\n          },\\n          { selector: 'ImportExpression', message: DYNAMIC },\\n160→          { selector: alias, message: ALIAS },\\n          {\\n            selector: \\\"BinaryExpression[operator='**'], AssignmentExpression[operator='**=']\\\",\\n            message:\\n              'write Math.pow in sim-side code: the sim swaps an fdlibm pow into Math while it steps, and ** escapes it (PLAN.md §6.5)',\\n          },\\n        ],\\n      },\\n    },\\n    imports('sim: three.js and Node built-ins', SIM_SIDE, [THREE_ENTRY, NODE_PREFIX]),\\n170→    imports('sim: Node built-ins in core/math.ts, which takes three.js', ['engine/core/math.ts'], [NODE_PREFIX]),\\n  ];\\n}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"140→  return [\\n    {\\n      name: 'sim: clocks, randomness, DOM, Web Audio',\\n      files: SIM_SIDE,\\n      ignores: ['**/*.test.ts'],\\n      plugins: { sim: simPlugin },\\n      rules: {\\n        'sim/no-restricted-globals': [\\n          'error',\\n          ...Object.entries(GLOBALS).map(([name, message]) => ({ name, message })),\\n150→        ],\\n        'sim/no-restricted-properties': ['error', { object: 'Math', property: 'random', message: RANDOM }],\\n        'sim/no-restricted-syntax': [\\n          'error',\\n          {\\n            selector: viaHost,\\n            message:\\n              \\\"a banned global reached through globalThis, self or global is still banned: sim-side code reads no clock, schedules nothing by wall time, touches no DOM or Web Audio, and draws randomness only from named streams, rng('<stream>') (PLAN.md §6.5)\\\",\\n          },\\n          { selector: 'ImportExpression', message: DYNAMIC },\\n160→          { selector: alias, message: ALIAS },\\n          {\\n            selector: \\\"BinaryExpression[operator='**'], AssignmentExpression[operator='**=']\\\",\\n            message:\\n              'write Math.pow in sim-side code: the sim swaps an fdlibm pow into Math while it steps, and ** escapes it (PLAN.md §6.5)',\\n          },\\n        ],\\n      },\\n    },\\n    imports('sim: three.js and Node built-ins', SIM_SIDE, [THREE_ENTRY, NODE_PREFIX]),\\n170→    imports('sim: Node built-ins in core/math.ts, which takes three.js', ['engine/core/math.ts'], [NODE_PREFIX]),\\n  ];\\n}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/tools/eslint/simSide.ts\",\"offset\":140,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"  return [\\n    {\\n      name: 'sim: clocks, randomness, DOM, Web Audio',\\n      files: SIM_SIDE,\\n      ignores: ['**/*.test.ts'],\\n      plugins: { sim: simPlugin },\\n      rules: {\\n        'sim/no-restricted-globals': [\\n          'error',\\n          ...Object.entries(GLOBALS).map(([name, message]) => ({ name, message })),\\n        ],\\n        'sim/no-restricted-properties': ['error', { object: 'Math', property: 'random', message: RANDOM }],\\n        'sim/no-restricted-syntax': [\\n          'error',\\n          {\\n            selector: viaHost,\\n            message:\\n              \\\"a banned global reached through globalThis, self or global is still banned: sim-side code reads no clock, schedules nothing by wall time, touches no DOM or Web Audio, and draws randomness only from named streams, rng('<stream>') (PLAN.md §6.5)\\\",\\n          },\\n          { selector: 'ImportExpression', message: DYNAMIC },\\n          { selector: alias, message: ALIAS },\\n          {\\n            selector: \\\"BinaryExpression[operator='**'], AssignmentExpression[operator='**=']\\\",\\n            message:\\n              'write Math.pow in sim-side code: the sim swaps an fdlibm pow into Math while it steps, and ** escapes it (PLAN.md §6.5)',\\n          },\\n        ],\\n      },\\n    },\\n    imports('sim: three.js and Node built-ins', SIM_SIDE, [THREE_ENTRY, NODE_PREFIX]),\\n    imports('sim: Node built-ins in core/math.ts, which takes three.js', ['engine/core/math.ts'], [NODE_PREFIX]),\\n  ];\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":172}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"0cbd7c28-5bfd-4429-8f46-f5ce6d76d924"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_49","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Tests are exempt from sim-side bans.\n\nSim code cannot use **. The game architecture is being planned out across settings, wobble physics, thumb bots, scoring, state components, activities, a registry, a scene driver, and island terrain.\n\nround.ts and main.ts are being added to the architecture. The flow state machine is being designed so the auto director and player share it. It tracks mode, day, event, phase, tick, score, rank, pebbles, age, visited locations, and credits.\n\nThe flow state now includes director flags, pose sets, finale, remarks, and an alive flag. A generated DAYS script will drive the director through morning, pose, and activity events per day.\n\nDay 5 is finalized with morning, group, and tilt. Pose C is now scheduled for day 4. After the final tilt, a last morning check runs before credits. The director will iterate through each day's events in sequence.\n\nThe director will insert an evening sleep between days 1 and 5. The final morning check is treated as part of the tilt finale, so the auto director must visit it before credits. Activities will end themselves by setting flow.alive to zero, which moves the driver into a results phase; the director auto-advances after three seconds, while the player can manually advance with \"Carry on\".\n\nTests will run a single activity until it self-ends or max steps are reached. Idle activities will time-box themselves even if the player does nothing. Activities spawn temporary entities marked with a `made` component, and teardown despawns them to prevent stray bodies. The driver calls each activity's teardown, and tests assert that query counts drop to zero.\n\nTests will use a seeded linear congruential generator for deterministic random inputs. They will verify that scores and wobble positions remain finite. The wobble physics interface is being defined with balance fields for position, velocity, spring offset, and mouse target.\n\nThe wobble physics interface now includes balance fields for calm, lungs, sync, over, recover, and topples. A BalanceCfg struct defines gain, instability, damping, noise, reach, and period parameters. Sway coefficients are stored on the component. A noiseAt helper is being drafted to generate per-frame noise values.\n\nThe noiseAt helper is now implemented as a sum of three sine waves with weighted amplitudes. The stepBalance function is being drafted to drive the breathing cycle: it computes a rising-or-falling phase from time, fills lungs when both sticks are pressed, and scales that fill by half the period.\n\nThe stepBalance function now drains lungs when breathing stops. It computes an instant sync from the lung level against the breathing guide, then blends that into a smoothed sync and a calm value over time. When recovery is active, it pulls the balance position back toward zero at a rate scaled by the remaining recovery duration.\n\nThe stepBalance function now resets velocity and clears the recovery flag when recovering. It decays the mouse target over 0.6 seconds using exponential smoothing. The raw stick input is clamped and slewed to a spring offset, then scaled by reach and normalized. A quietness factor is derived from the calm value.\n\nThe stepBalance function now computes noise vectors from the sway coefficients and applies them to the balance state. It blends target position, instability, noise, and input disturbance into acceleration terms, integrates velocity with exponential damping, and advances position. The distance from the origin is then measured.\n\nThe wobble physics now tracks overbalance: if the distance from the origin exceeds one, a counter increments; after six frames it triggers a topple and resets the counter. Recovery is fixed to a linear decay toward zero over sixty steps. Noise time is now tick divided by sixty instead of summed delta time. Sway coefficients are generated from a seeded random number generator.\n\nBot profiles are being defined with distinct delay, gain, and jitter values. Perfect bots respond instantly with high gain. Human bots add 15 ms delay and moderate jitter. Sloppy bots add 21 ms delay and higher jitter. Idle bots remain motionless. Attract bots cheat by matching perfect performance plus activity-specific overrides.\n\nA CPU bot profile is added with 18 ms delay, gain 1.0, and 0.08 jitter. The thumb bot function now computes a hold target from the desired position using gain, instability, and reach, then adds jitter sampled from the seeded random generator.\n\nBot thumb output is clamped to [-1, 1] after adding gain-weighted error from the delayed history sample and jitter. Jitter comes from a seeded world RNG rather than Math.random. A module-level WeakMap for bot history was rejected because it would break determinism and capture/restore.\n\nBot history will be stored inside the component itself. A single string field is one option, but a 24-element array of numbers is cleaner and more deterministic. The schema already supports numeric arrays, so this approach avoids module-level state.\n\nBot history will be stored as a fixed-length numeric array created at setup. The simulation likely detects mutations in component fields, so the array will be mutated in place and replaced if necessary to ensure capture and restore work correctly.\n\nBot thumb functions return stick input only. Activities add foot targets to the returned SeatInput. The morning check has five phases: ask, still, reach, foot, and reveal. Still lasts twelve seconds. Reach lasts up to twenty-four seconds. Foot lasts eight seconds. Reveal lasts three seconds before ending. A share occurs inside during the still phase.\n\nThe morning check's foot phase now tracks inside-share progress. While the named foot is held, a step counter and an inside counter advance. Releasing the foot pauses the timer and drains the inside count by one step per second. The share ratio is computed as inside steps divided by total foot steps.\n\nThe foot phase lasts exactly eight seconds of wall-clock time. Age is computed as `round(18 + 64 * (1-s)^1.3)`, yielding 82 at low score and 18 at high score.\n\nEach pose has three phases: enter, hold, and release. The enter phase requires staying within 0.18 of a moving guide dot that traces a curve from center to seat over roughly four seconds. Progress scrubs the pose. If the player fails to enter properly, the director advances after ten seconds with a low score.\n\nPose scoring weights ring share at 0.7 and sync at 0.3, minus 60 points per topple, clamped to 0–1000. The class mean is the result.\n\nThe chair pose replaces the sync term with a feet-hold share while both feet remain on the ground; sway noise scales upward with time. The heron pose uses a standing foot and a 1.5× instability multiplier. The comma pose requires two eight-second holds at (0.5, 0) and (-0.5, 0). The napkin pose adds a gull disturbance at halfway.\n\nBridge scores weight ring share at 0.3 and sync at 0.7. Nothing scores 0.4 ring plus 0.6 sync with half sway and 60% breath. Lighthouse uses a 0.14 ring and 1.2× sway. Per-pose instability and noise scales will be passed into stepBalance via new noiseScale and instabilityScale arguments. Still pond composure starts at 1000.\n\nStill pond composure drops by 80 for bad inputs: stick past 0.25, any button press, stray foot, or mouse past threshold. A real pause is page-side and does not cost; a fake pause lure does not pause the simulation, but pressing act during one costs composure. The final score is composure multiplied by (0.5 + 0.5 × sync), clamped to 0–1000. Nothing pose scores 500 with zero sync. A bot for the pond will hold both feet only when the breathing guide rises.\n\nThe pond bot delays its feet decision by 250 ms to match human timing. The Line activity tracks along-step distance, lateral position, and flags for reached, falls, gull, and gust. Stepping a foot advances the along position by 0.75 and adds lateral kick from a random or alternating sign. Stepping the same foot again causes a stumble and fall. Continuous lean x input reduces lateral drift by 0.08 per step. A gust hits every 6 to 10 seconds after a 1.5 second telegraph, adding lateral force.\n\nThe Line activity now has four flags at 0, 13.3, 26.6, and 40 meters. It ends at 120 seconds or when the player reaches 40 meters. A fall triggers if lateral drift exceeds one meter; the bot then resets to the last flag position with zero lateral velocity. The score is `(1000 - 150*falls - 8*max(0, seconds-50)) * (along/40)`, clamped to 0–1000. The bot alternates feet steadily and leans against lateral drift and gust telegraphs.\n\nPaddle physics use board position x, z, heading, and speed. Forward lean drives speed at six meters per second; side lean steers at 1.2 radians per second.\n\nPaddle feet alternate at 1.2 Hz with a small speed burst. Twelve gates sit on a circle radius 18 m with ±2 m seeded jitter. A 3 m radius marks each gate. Wake at gate 7 and turtle at gate 9 add a one-second cob disturbance. Toppling splashes the board and forces a three-second remount at zero speed. The race lasts 75 seconds. Score equals 60 times gates plus a time bonus up to 280 for the remaining time, capped at 1000. Idle score is zero.\n\nThe paddle bot steers toward the next gate and alternates feet. Forward lean drives speed, but full lean risks toppling. The bot will use a moderate forward lean of about 0.6 to stay stable. Speed is driven by lean angle rather than center-of-balance position, so the bot must resolve the conflict between forward momentum and balance.\n\nThe paddle bot will cruise with a desired center-of-balance at (0, 0.45) and steer by clamping side-lean to ±0.4. This keeps the board stable because the distance from center is only 0.6, well below the toppling radius of 1.\n\nSand garden wobble noise is disabled. Tilt from lean or pointer is capped at twelve degrees. Marbles roll down a tilted plane with gravity and friction, and holes catch them only if speed is below the sink threshold. Five trays hold the ninety-second time split; each tray lasts eighteen seconds or until all marbles sink. The score equals eight hundred times shares sunk plus a time-left bonus.\n\nThe sand garden time bonus is 200 times the remaining seconds out of ninety, but only if every marble sinks; otherwise it is zero. A perfect score nears 950.\n\nTray layout will use simple geometry: one marble starts left of a hole and tilts toward positive x so gravity rolls it right. Friction and gravity are tuned so marbles reach holes in a few seconds. Later trays may include moving rakes that the bot must wait for.\n\nPointer input for bots will use lean instead of a mouse pointer.\n\nThe Great Tilt activity has two phases. The first is a twenty-second storm with no input and no ring scoring; breathing is optional. The second is a seventy-five-second east terrace where the player starts at the plaza and moves guests toward the east sensor at x=60 by leaning. A downhill slope pushes them west, and a calm call—holding both feet for two seconds then releasing—waves guests within eight meters to follow. Twelve guests must be delivered for full points. If time runs out, undelivered guests may shuffle or count as partial.\n\nThe stone phase lasts sixty seconds. Tilt equals center-of-balance multiplied by a few degrees. The ring shrinks as the player holds it. Waves disturb the ring and breathing quiets them. Score is in-ring share.\n\nThe dawn phase lasts eight seconds and runs automatically.\n\nOverall score is one thousand times zero point fifteen for storm survival plus zero point thirty five for delivered guests plus zero point forty for stone hold plus zero point ten for breath sync.\n\nAn idle bot scores about two hundred ninety. That is below five hundred. It drifts west during the storm and fails to deliver guests or hold the stone.\n\nA human scoring 0.8 on guests, 0.85 on stone hold, and 0.6 on breath sync would reach 830 points. A sloppy human scoring 0.5 on guests, 0.7 on stone hold, and 0.4 on breath sync would reach 645 points. Both scores clear the idle baseline of 290.\n\nThe stone ring shrinks from 0.35 to 0.16 meters. Idle performance may rise slightly because the ring starts large, but it should still remain below the human threshold.\n\nA perfect idle score is roughly 270. The east delivery phase is designed to be reachable: the player moves at four meters per second toward the east sensor, and scattered guests within eight meters follow each calm call. A wave every four seconds should pull them in before the seventy-five-second limit expires.\n\nThe east delivery phase will cluster eleven guests near the player so one or two calm calls pull them all in before the seventy-five-second limit. Nan is pre-delivered.\n\nGroup class runs three forty-five-second shorts with a six-second lead-in. The middle short is always heron hold; the other two are still ring and still pond. Score equals the mean of the three zero-to-one-thousand scores. The deal seed is drawn from w.rng('deal'), but the middle slot is fixed to heron to keep the seed deterministic.\n\nCPU guests in group play run bots with extra sway, but only the local player's score is tested and ghosts do not affect local play. The activity test requires just one seat. The attract loop runs activities for about fifteen seconds each with a perfect bot; it exits on input and does not write a save. Dev mode exposes sliders that call inspector.set using paths from a table matching defineSettings. Saves live in page store and are passed to game.begin at the start, though the simulation itself does not read the store.\n\nLine IDs are emitted by the simulation and resolved to text in ui/lines/en.ts. Plumb lines omit exclamation marks. Audio songs live in sounds/songs.ts and syllables in sounds/voice.ts; the page say() function plays them. Tests render a song and a syllable via the audio API and verify RMS is above zero. Music volume is set to 0.1 on boot, checked by the browser test and a unit test. Node-side audio proof imports the page render functions through verify.mjs.\n\nverify.mjs will run vitest on the audio render tests and check the exit code. It will also perform its own node checks. The goal requires verify to print failures, a failure count, and confirmation that seeds reach credits.\n\nverify.mjs will run vitest directly and parse the JSON reporter to extract failures. It will also spawn a child process to import test runners via tsx.\n\nverify.mjs will shell out to a TypeScript file at labs/game/tools/node-verify.ts via tsx. That script runs the full verification matrix, prints the log, and exits with a non-zero code on failure. verify.mjs forwards stdout and the exit code. It imports activities and createHeadless to execute the test suite, and checks audio by importing renderSong.\n\ntsx is confirmed available as a devDependency. The hub client will fetch now and check the hash. The one-file build will be generated by onefile.mjs. The page structure splits into boot, shell menus, HUD disc, game state, about screen, and audio boot modules. Glyphs are SVG strings in ui/glyphs.ts. lastDevice tracking happens on the page from input events so same-frame device switches work; the engine also exposes input.lastDevice.\n\nInput glyphs will update synchronously inside event handlers so the page reflects device changes within the same frame. Menus are capped at six items and paginated when necessary. Text font size is set to `max(28px, 2.6vh)` to meet the minimum requirement.\n\ncreateMenu already handles mouseover focus, so custom menus must do the same. The attract loop will use real wall-clock time via `performance.now()` rather than simulation frame time, because the engine can pause and the title screen does not. Tests will wait ten to eleven seconds.\n\nThe attract loop will trigger at exactly ten seconds of idle wall-clock time. Touch devices are detected via CSS media queries or the playwright device flag. A settings button toggles touch off in query parameters, which disables the engine’s touch stick and runs the attract loop without adding a custom one.\n\nThe page will check for WebGPU support before creating the engine and display a message if it is missing. PlayStation glyphs are the default until settings or input change them. Xbox glyphs appear only after the user enables them in settings. Save erasure uses an in-game confirm screen instead of a browser dialog. Twenty pebble entities and twelve plaques creep across the island; each plaque shifts by half a meter per day. The simulation tracks pebble finds through a game.found event that the page listens for.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-bb564247-1978-4036-8643-17bb96021248-85","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"defineBuild\\('build:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine","glob":"*.ts"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-bb564247-1978-4036-8643-17bb96021248-86","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"export function renderSong|export function render\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/audio","glob":"*.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":504,"output_tokens":8186,"cache_read_input_tokens":136192,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"9803016b-4c2d-4107-8f05-1e9f80430ff6"}
