{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_66","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Materials might be defined by games.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d248c3bb-d6c8-47d5-ad42-9a6eeb6501fd-110","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/gfx/materials/material.ts","offset":1,"limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d248c3bb-d6c8-47d5-ad42-9a6eeb6501fd-111","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"defineMaterial\\('material:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/box","glob":"*.ts","head_limit":5}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":769,"output_tokens":257,"cache_read_input_tokens":168960,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"c6af17be-7813-4c94-bed2-38cf4389fd7c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-d248c3bb-d6c8-47d5-ad42-9a6eeb6501fd-110","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The material registry (PLAN.md WP 2.3, §6.6, §6.7, I-49): the registry kind `material`, one entry per\\n * material, as data with the parameters agents know from three.js's classic materials: `color`, `map`, `roughness`,\\n * `metalness`, `emissive`, `emissiveIntensity`, `normalScale`, `opacity`, `transparent`, `alphaTest`, `side`,\\n * `flatShading`, `vertexColors`, `fog`, `depthWrite`, plus `unlit` (glTF's word: drawn without lights). How a\\n * material is drawn is the engine's business (engine/gfx/materials/compile.ts): v1 compiles the same data to\\n * `MeshLambertMaterial` or `MeshBasicMaterial`, later WPs to better node materials and styles (WP 7.2), without the\\n * data changing (doctrine: Common ground outside, Quality under the hood inside).\\n *\\n10→ * `map` names a texture generator (engine/gfx/textures/texture.ts): an id (`'texture:brick'`) or\\n * `{ texture, seed, params, size, look, repeat }`. One generator fills every slot: its colour is the `map`, its\\n * height the normal map (scaled by `normalScale`), its roughness the roughness map (used by styles that shade with\\n * roughness) and its emissive the emissive map.\\n *\\n * `defineMaterial(id, spec)` adds one (a game's own materials live with the game); `materialKind(registry)` declares\\n * the kind with the engine's built-in materials (engine/gfx/materials/builtins.ts), and `material:default` stands in,\\n * with a warning, for a missing id.\\n *\\n * Invariants: colours are hex strings; a map's texture exists when the material is defined (define textures\\n20→ * first), and its parameters, size, look and repeat are checked then, so a bad map fails at `defineMaterial`, never\\n * at the first frame. Problems raise `CORE_BAD_SPEC` naming each, as every registry kind does.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineMaterial('material:mossyBrick', { color: '#c8d8b0', map: { texture: 'texture:brick', seed: 4 } }, reg);\\n * reg.get('material', 'material:mossyBrick').roughness; // 1, the default\\n * textureRefOf(reg.get('material', 'material:mossyBrick').map); // { texture: 'texture:brick', seed: 4, … }\\n * @see engine/gfx/materials/material.test.ts\\n30→ */\\nimport { hex } from '../../core/color';\\nimport { galleryField } from '../../core/gallery';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../../core/registry';\\nimport { isPlainObject, type Schema, type SpecOf } from '../../core/schema';\\nimport { textureSampler, type TextureRef } from '../textures/bake';\\nimport { textureKind } from '../textures/texture';\\nimport type { TextureLook } from '../textures/types';\\nimport { BUILT_IN_MATERIALS } from './builtins';\\n\\n40→/** The fields of a `material` entry: three.js's classic material parameters, as data. */\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The material registry (PLAN.md WP 2.3, §6.6, §6.7, I-49): the registry kind `material`, one entry per\\n * material, as data with the parameters agents know from three.js's classic materials: `color`, `map`, `roughness`,\\n * `metalness`, `emissive`, `emissiveIntensity`, `normalScale`, `opacity`, `transparent`, `alphaTest`, `side`,\\n * `flatShading`, `vertexColors`, `fog`, `depthWrite`, plus `unlit` (glTF's word: drawn without lights). How a\\n * material is drawn is the engine's business (engine/gfx/materials/compile.ts): v1 compiles the same data to\\n * `MeshLambertMaterial` or `MeshBasicMaterial`, later WPs to better node materials and styles (WP 7.2), without the\\n * data changing (doctrine: Common ground outside, Quality under the hood inside).\\n *\\n10→ * `map` names a texture generator (engine/gfx/textures/texture.ts): an id (`'texture:brick'`) or\\n * `{ texture, seed, params, size, look, repeat }`. One generator fills every slot: its colour is the `map`, its\\n * height the normal map (scaled by `normalScale`), its roughness the roughness map (used by styles that shade with\\n * roughness) and its emissive the emissive map.\\n *\\n * `defineMaterial(id, spec)` adds one (a game's own materials live with the game); `materialKind(registry)` declares\\n * the kind with the engine's built-in materials (engine/gfx/materials/builtins.ts), and `material:default` stands in,\\n * with a warning, for a missing id.\\n *\\n * Invariants: colours are hex strings; a map's texture exists when the material is defined (define textures\\n20→ * first), and its parameters, size, look and repeat are checked then, so a bad map fails at `defineMaterial`, never\\n * at the first frame. Problems raise `CORE_BAD_SPEC` naming each, as every registry kind does.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineMaterial('material:mossyBrick', { color: '#c8d8b0', map: { texture: 'texture:brick', seed: 4 } }, reg);\\n * reg.get('material', 'material:mossyBrick').roughness; // 1, the default\\n * textureRefOf(reg.get('material', 'material:mossyBrick').map); // { texture: 'texture:brick', seed: 4, … }\\n * @see engine/gfx/materials/material.test.ts\\n30→ */\\nimport { hex } from '../../core/color';\\nimport { galleryField } from '../../core/gallery';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../../core/registry';\\nimport { isPlainObject, type Schema, type SpecOf } from '../../core/schema';\\nimport { textureSampler, type TextureRef } from '../textures/bake';\\nimport { textureKind } from '../textures/texture';\\nimport type { TextureLook } from '../textures/types';\\nimport { BUILT_IN_MATERIALS } from './builtins';\\n\\n40→/** The fields of a `material` entry: three.js's classic material parameters, as data. */\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/gfx/materials/material.ts\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The material registry (PLAN.md WP 2.3, §6.6, §6.7, I-49): the registry kind `material`, one entry per\\n * material, as data with the parameters agents know from three.js's classic materials: `color`, `map`, `roughness`,\\n * `metalness`, `emissive`, `emissiveIntensity`, `normalScale`, `opacity`, `transparent`, `alphaTest`, `side`,\\n * `flatShading`, `vertexColors`, `fog`, `depthWrite`, plus `unlit` (glTF's word: drawn without lights). How a\\n * material is drawn is the engine's business (engine/gfx/materials/compile.ts): v1 compiles the same data to\\n * `MeshLambertMaterial` or `MeshBasicMaterial`, later WPs to better node materials and styles (WP 7.2), without the\\n * data changing (doctrine: Common ground outside, Quality under the hood inside).\\n *\\n * `map` names a texture generator (engine/gfx/textures/texture.ts): an id (`'texture:brick'`) or\\n * `{ texture, seed, params, size, look, repeat }`. One generator fills every slot: its colour is the `map`, its\\n * height the normal map (scaled by `normalScale`), its roughness the roughness map (used by styles that shade with\\n * roughness) and its emissive the emissive map.\\n *\\n * `defineMaterial(id, spec)` adds one (a game's own materials live with the game); `materialKind(registry)` declares\\n * the kind with the engine's built-in materials (engine/gfx/materials/builtins.ts), and `material:default` stands in,\\n * with a warning, for a missing id.\\n *\\n * Invariants: colours are hex strings; a map's texture exists when the material is defined (define textures\\n * first), and its parameters, size, look and repeat are checked then, so a bad map fails at `defineMaterial`, never\\n * at the first frame. Problems raise `CORE_BAD_SPEC` naming each, as every registry kind does.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineMaterial('material:mossyBrick', { color: '#c8d8b0', map: { texture: 'texture:brick', seed: 4 } }, reg);\\n * reg.get('material', 'material:mossyBrick').roughness; // 1, the default\\n * textureRefOf(reg.get('material', 'material:mossyBrick').map); // { texture: 'texture:brick', seed: 4, … }\\n * @see engine/gfx/materials/material.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { hex } from '../../core/color';\\nimport { galleryField } from '../../core/gallery';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../../core/registry';\\nimport { isPlainObject, type Schema, type SpecOf } from '../../core/schema';\\nimport { textureSampler, type TextureRef } from '../textures/bake';\\nimport { textureKind } from '../textures/texture';\\nimport type { TextureLook } from '../textures/types';\\nimport { BUILT_IN_MATERIALS } from './builtins';\\n\\n/** The fields of a `material` entry: three.js's classic material parameters, as data. */\\n\",\"total_lines\":239}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-d248c3bb-d6c8-47d5-ad42-9a6eeb6501fd-111","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,95,95,120,53,52,102,97,99,99,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"59f1d9db-c0c8-431a-a12f-27286b8fac8d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_67","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll define materials using only colors.\n\nI'll define all materials to avoid warnings that could become errors. I'm writing the simulation in a batch of files with careful line counts. I'll add clamp and lerp helpers without exponentiation. The director visit log will accumulate comma-separated IDs in flow.seen.\n\nA `game.pace` setting will default to 1 so director timing stays accurate. The results screen auto-advances after one second in automated playthroughs while still marking the mode as visited. Sleep and evening nodes are marked as visited.\n\nPond lures will appear randomly every 6–10 seconds. Fake pauses show a line but keep the phase active and flag the extra state. Pressing the action button during a fake pause costs composure. Real pauses are page-side and do not trigger actions.\n\nThe pond test will assert that the activity continues stepping while a fake pause lure is displayed. A no-input pond run must score exactly 500, keeping sync at zero and composure at 1000. The bot's breath behavior will be correct: both feet and no act button pressed. Human players will delay their breath signal by 15 steps.\n\nLate breath signals only lower sync, not composure. Perfect breathing matches the guide's slope exactly: lungs fill at rate 2/period while holding and empty at the same rate on release. This yields perfect sync of 1 and a score of 1000.\n\nA 15-step human delay yields roughly 950 points, and a 21-step delay yields about 900. Idle playthroughs score exactly 500 with no penalties, keeping them above the bot baseline. Sway noise in the pond does not affect scoring.\n\nBot stick and mouse decay are confirmed safe. Morning check scores are estimated: perfect stays near 100%, human near 90%, sloppy near 72%, idle near 38%. The reach activity requires six targets at radius 0.5 held for 0.5 seconds each within a 24-second budget, with feedforward positioning and movement time factored in.\n\nThe reach activity bonus is now defined as `B = 1 − time/24`, where time is measured from the start until the activity ends. A perfect run finishing in about ten seconds yields roughly 0.58, a human-like run in fourteen seconds yields about 0.42, and an idle run scores zero.\n\nThe balance activity requires holding one foot for eight seconds. Idle playthroughs score zero because the composure drain prevents any inside-time count. The scoring formula is 0.45A + 0.3B + 0.25C, where A is reach accuracy, B is reach bonus, and C is balance stability. A perfect run scores about 859 points, and a human-like run scores about 703.\n\nSloppy morning check scores about 468 points, which is borderline if the reach activity fails. Idle scores only 135 points, safely below the human baseline.\n\nThe reach radius is fixed at 0.5. If the sloppy bot cannot hold that distance, I will tune its feedforward or reduce jitter rather than lowering the target.\n\nThe stick needs roughly 0.5 seconds to slew into position for a 0.437 hold command. The target zone is a 0.16 radius around the point at radius 0.5, so equilibrium must sit exactly on that spot. Noise and controller gain should keep residual wobble within about 0.1 for a perfect run; sloppy play may exceed the 0.16 limit.\n\nIf the sloppy bot fails reach, its score drops to about 410 points, which still clears the 300 threshold. If a human player fails reach, the score lands near 583 points, barely above the 550 baseline; I will tune the controller after testing.\n\nFor the pose activity, the score is the mean of all poses attempted. Entering a pose counts as progress, but the main score comes from staying inside the ring during the hold. The easy pose uses no breath and minimal sway, while the chair pose requires both feet and no breath with a slightly raised sway.\n\nPose difficulty is tiered by seat position. The central chair pose uses a 0.2 ring and moderate instability, while the harder archer and comma seats use smaller rings and higher instability. If comma poses prove too difficult for bots, the class mean should still clear the threshold because the central poses are easier to hold.\n\nPose B pairs the archer and comma seats, with comma being the main risk. Pose C uses the bridge and lighthouse poses; the lighthouse ring is 0.14 and sway is 1.2, so it should be manageable for a perfect run but tight for sloppy play.\n\nThe line bot will use gentle lateral dynamics so proportional leaning holds the course, and gusts will be telegraphed so the bot can pre-lean. Falls cost 150 points. A perfect run with zero falls and a full cross under 50 seconds scores 1000. Idle scores 0. A human player with a couple of falls scores about 700, which clears the 550 baseline. Sloppy play may reach roughly 400.\n\nGust strength will be tuned so perfect runs never fall and sloppy runs fall only once or twice. Each activity run takes about 13 seconds, which is acceptable. The engine steps one frame at a time because the bot reads state each step. When an activity starts, wobble resets to zero and sway draws from a seeded random generator so results stay deterministic.\n\nI will replace the old starter files rather than write a Python script. The deleted files include play.ts, bot.ts, best.ts, main.ts, and several test or replay files. I must also update main.ts to remove its import of best.ts. npm test may fail until the new tests pass, which is acceptable.\n\nI will delete the stale replay file so the replay tool does not fail on a missing golden record. The dev slider settings are now fixed: wobble gain at 14 (range 4–30), instability at 3 (0–10), and damping at 5 (1–12).\n\nThe remaining dev slider values are now fixed. Wobble noise is 3.5, noise rate is 0.15, and reach is 0.9. Breath period is 8, calm effect is 0.5, age k is 1.3, pose hold is 12, and pose ring is 0.2. Pond penalty is 80, line step kick is 0.35, paddle speed is 6, garden tilt is 12, and pebbles multiplier is 1. Activities that are cut will not appear in the dev mode settings.\n\nMouse sensitivity is a page-level setting, not a simulation parameter. The dev-mode slider list will include every numeric game.* setting in the plan except for reps, step, and hoop, since those activities are removed and must leave no trace.\n\nThe plan's instruction to include every numeric game.* setting is read as a minimum requirement, so sliders will cover only shipped activities. Cut-activity tunables will be omitted entirely and documented in the README and deviations file. String settings such as game.auto do not need dev-mode sliders.\n\nThe auto-play setting will be a string enum with values off, director, perfect, human, sloppy, idle, and attract. It defaults to off. The director mode will trigger an immediate seed restart via inspector.set when activated from the page.\n\nThe director mode reads the auto setting each step, so a page-side reseed will be visible to setup. Attract mode must not write to the save store; the page sets the flag and reseeds, then exits by reseeding again to return to the title screen without altering localStorage. Fresh saves default music volume to 0.1, while existing saves retain their stored value. The game state object is being shaped around a title field.\n\nThe game state object now defines a `screen` union type with every UI mode, plus fields for attract mode, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, event, focus, and ready. The title screen will display the HTML heading \"Thumb Yoga\". About data will live in a single file, `about-data.ts`, that both the about screen and the exit interview read.\n\nI will generate the exit interview from the same about data and keep the about screen and JSON in sync. Testing feedback answers will be filled honestly after runs, using real sentences as placeholders until the numbers are known. PROGRESS.md will be started now with title pick notes, and I will add a deviations bullet for each cut activity while updating the plan checklist.\n\nThe plan file will be updated as items are completed. The cast of characters—Tare, Plumb, Gerald, Bex, Old Nan, the Gull, and the Frog—will spawn on the island. Walking will use WASD in both the balance and roam action maps; the page switches between them. On the island, the engine reads the lean difference between right and left gamepad axes to drive movement.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-514cef44-b3f8-40ab-9e36-d12160e7f71a-112","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/input/actionMaps.ts","offset":1,"limit":100}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1066,"output_tokens":4441,"cache_read_input_tokens":169728,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"7e9b1cad-a33a-4bd9-9415-3e67acc83310"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-514cef44-b3f8-40ab-9e36-d12160e7f71a-112","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Action maps and presets (PLAN.md WP 3.3, I-24; doctrine: Common ground): the registry kind `actionMap`, a set\\n * of named actions with the physical codes bound to each (engine/input/codes.ts), as Unity's action maps and Godot's\\n * InputMap hold them; the presets; and the system layer, which no player rebinds.\\n *\\n * An action is a button the sim reads: held actions arrive as the intents' `b`, pressed ones as `p`\\n * (engine/input/intents.ts). The four actions a map names in `move` (forward, back, left, right) are not buttons:\\n * their strengths make the move axes `[right − left, forward − back]`, so keys walk at full speed and a half-pushed\\n * stick at half, and `fromCamera` turns the axes into a world direction. `values` writes an action's analog strength\\n10→ * (a trigger) as a custom intent. `look: true` (first person, the shooter) adds the camera's view direction as `look`,\\n * `aimWith` names the rule that gives the intents' `aim` its point (engine/input/aim.ts: the screen centre's ray, the\\n * pointer's onto the floor, or none; the action `aim`, where a map has one, raises the sights), and `lock: true` takes\\n * the pointer lock on a click so the mouse turns the view (with the cursor free, it points and aims). The actions a\\n * map names in `view` (a camera switch, a view's turn) are the page's: bound and rebound like the others, never\\n * buttons in the intents (`createEngine({ onView })`). A game with several camera modes gives each its own map\\n * (`createEngine({ actionMap })`, WP 13.9).\\n *\\n * The system layer (`SYSTEM_ACTIONS`): pause, and the menu's accept, cancel and directions. It is always active\\n * beside the map, never rebound, and never part of the intents: the app and the UI read it (`input.pressed('pause')`),\\n20→ * so remapping movement never breaks a menu (the lesson of `my-3d2dge:docs/CONTROLS-AUDIT.md`). `RESERVED_CODES` (the\\n * pause buttons, and keys the browser or system keeps) and a map's own `reserved` codes are refused by rebinding.\\n *\\n * Invariants (`CORE_BAD_SPEC` when broken): every code is valid (`isCode`), action names are lower-case words\\n * (`jump`, `skill2`) and none is a system action, `move` names four actions of the map and `view` others, `values` keys\\n * are namespaced custom intents naming actions of the map, and no action binds a reserved code. The presets are registered on the\\n * shared registry when this module loads.\\n *\\n * Carried from `my-3d2dge:engine/my-3d2dge.js:670-693` (`Input.DEFAULT`, `PLATFORMER`, `SHMUP`, `MENU_KEYS`) with 3D\\n * movement, and shardfall's layer that is never rebound (`shardfall:crates/pav_app/src/input.rs:131-161`).\\n30→ *\\n * @example\\n * const map = getActionMap('actionMap:thirdPerson');\\n * map.actions.jump; // ['Space', 'PadSouth']\\n * map.move; // ['forward', 'back', 'left', 'right']\\n * SYSTEM_ACTIONS.pause; // ['Escape', 'PadStart']\\n * listActionMaps().map((entry) => entry.id); // ['actionMap:firstPerson', 'actionMap:platformer', …]\\n * @see engine/input/bindings.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\\n40→import { show, type Schema } from '../core/schema';\\nimport { AIM_RULES, type AimRule } from './aim';\\nimport { isCode } from './codes';\\n\\n/** The system layer: action → codes. Always active, never rebound, never in the intents. */\\nexport const SYSTEM_ACTIONS: Readonly<Record<string, readonly string[]>> = Object.freeze({\\n  pause: ['Escape', 'PadStart'],\\n  uiAccept: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Space', 'PadSouth'],\\n  uiCancel: ['Escape', 'Backspace', 'PadEast'],\\n  uiUp: ['ArrowUp', 'PadUp', 'PadLeftStickUp'],\\n50→  uiDown: ['ArrowDown', 'PadDown', 'PadLeftStickDown'],\\n  uiLeft: ['ArrowLeft', 'PadLeft', 'PadLeftStickLeft'],\\n  uiRight: ['ArrowRight', 'PadRight', 'PadLeftStickRight'],\\n});\\n\\n/** Codes no action map binds: the pause buttons, and keys the browser or the system keeps. */\\nexport const RESERVED_CODES: readonly string[] = Object.freeze([\\n  'Escape',\\n  'PadStart',\\n  'PadHome',\\n60→  'F5',\\n  'F11',\\n  'F12',\\n  'MetaLeft',\\n  'MetaRight',\\n]);\\n\\n/** The fields of an `actionMap` entry. */\\nconst ACTION_MAP_FIELDS = {\\n  description: { type: 'string', default: '', description: 'What the map is for, in plain words.' },\\n70→  actions: {\\n    type: 'object',\\n    required: true,\\n    description:\\n      'Action name → the codes bound to it (KeyboardEvent.code, MouseLeft…, PadSouth…, PadLeftStickUp…): held actions are the intents b, pressed ones p.',\\n  },\\n  move: {\\n    type: 'array',\\n    items: { type: 'string' },\\n    default: ['forward', 'back', 'left', 'right'],\\n80→    description: 'The four actions that walk, as forward, back, left, right: their strengths make the move axes.',\\n  },\\n  values: {\\n    type: 'object',\\n    default: {},\\n    description:\\n      'Custom intent key (\\\"game:throttle\\\") → action: its analog strength (0 to 1, a trigger) is that intent.',\\n  },\\n  look: {\\n    type: 'boolean',\\n90→    default: false,\\n    description: \\\"True for first person and the shooter: the intents carry look = [the camera's yaw, its pitch].\\\",\\n  },\\n  aimWith: {\\n    type: 'string',\\n    enum: AIM_RULES,\\n    default: 'none',\\n    description:\\n      \\\"What the intents' aim holds: none; centre, the point the screen centre's ray meets (first person, the shooter); pointer, the pointer's ray onto the player's floor or a right stick's push (isometric, top-down).\\\",\\n  },\\n100→  lock: {\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Action maps and presets (PLAN.md WP 3.3, I-24; doctrine: Common ground): the registry kind `actionMap`, a set\\n * of named actions with the physical codes bound to each (engine/input/codes.ts), as Unity's action maps and Godot's\\n * InputMap hold them; the presets; and the system layer, which no player rebinds.\\n *\\n * An action is a button the sim reads: held actions arrive as the intents' `b`, pressed ones as `p`\\n * (engine/input/intents.ts). The four actions a map names in `move` (forward, back, left, right) are not buttons:\\n * their strengths make the move axes `[right − left, forward − back]`, so keys walk at full speed and a half-pushed\\n * stick at half, and `fromCamera` turns the axes into a world direction. `values` writes an action's analog strength\\n10→ * (a trigger) as a custom intent. `look: true` (first person, the shooter) adds the camera's view direction as `look`,\\n * `aimWith` names the rule that gives the intents' `aim` its point (engine/input/aim.ts: the screen centre's ray, the\\n * pointer's onto the floor, or none; the action `aim`, where a map has one, raises the sights), and `lock: true` takes\\n * the pointer lock on a click so the mouse turns the view (with the cursor free, it points and aims). The actions a\\n * map names in `view` (a camera switch, a view's turn) are the page's: bound and rebound like the others, never\\n * buttons in the intents (`createEngine({ onView })`). A game with several camera modes gives each its own map\\n * (`createEngine({ actionMap })`, WP 13.9).\\n *\\n * The system layer (`SYSTEM_ACTIONS`): pause, and the menu's accept, cancel and directions. It is always active\\n * beside the map, never rebound, and never part of the intents: the app and the UI read it (`input.pressed('pause')`),\\n20→ * so remapping movement never breaks a menu (the lesson of `my-3d2dge:docs/CONTROLS-AUDIT.md`). `RESERVED_CODES` (the\\n * pause buttons, and keys the browser or system keeps) and a map's own `reserved` codes are refused by rebinding.\\n *\\n * Invariants (`CORE_BAD_SPEC` when broken): every code is valid (`isCode`), action names are lower-case words\\n * (`jump`, `skill2`) and none is a system action, `move` names four actions of the map and `view` others, `values` keys\\n * are namespaced custom intents naming actions of the map, and no action binds a reserved code. The presets are registered on the\\n * shared registry when this module loads.\\n *\\n * Carried from `my-3d2dge:engine/my-3d2dge.js:670-693` (`Input.DEFAULT`, `PLATFORMER`, `SHMUP`, `MENU_KEYS`) with 3D\\n * movement, and shardfall's layer that is never rebound (`shardfall:crates/pav_app/src/input.rs:131-161`).\\n30→ *\\n * @example\\n * const map = getActionMap('actionMap:thirdPerson');\\n * map.actions.jump; // ['Space', 'PadSouth']\\n * map.move; // ['forward', 'back', 'left', 'right']\\n * SYSTEM_ACTIONS.pause; // ['Escape', 'PadStart']\\n * listActionMaps().map((entry) => entry.id); // ['actionMap:firstPerson', 'actionMap:platformer', …]\\n * @see engine/input/bindings.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\\n40→import { show, type Schema } from '../core/schema';\\nimport { AIM_RULES, type AimRule } from './aim';\\nimport { isCode } from './codes';\\n\\n/** The system layer: action → codes. Always active, never rebound, never in the intents. */\\nexport const SYSTEM_ACTIONS: Readonly<Record<string, readonly string[]>> = Object.freeze({\\n  pause: ['Escape', 'PadStart'],\\n  uiAccept: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Space', 'PadSouth'],\\n  uiCancel: ['Escape', 'Backspace', 'PadEast'],\\n  uiUp: ['ArrowUp', 'PadUp', 'PadLeftStickUp'],\\n50→  uiDown: ['ArrowDown', 'PadDown', 'PadLeftStickDown'],\\n  uiLeft: ['ArrowLeft', 'PadLeft', 'PadLeftStickLeft'],\\n  uiRight: ['ArrowRight', 'PadRight', 'PadLeftStickRight'],\\n});\\n\\n/** Codes no action map binds: the pause buttons, and keys the browser or the system keeps. */\\nexport const RESERVED_CODES: readonly string[] = Object.freeze([\\n  'Escape',\\n  'PadStart',\\n  'PadHome',\\n60→  'F5',\\n  'F11',\\n  'F12',\\n  'MetaLeft',\\n  'MetaRight',\\n]);\\n\\n/** The fields of an `actionMap` entry. */\\nconst ACTION_MAP_FIELDS = {\\n  description: { type: 'string', default: '', description: 'What the map is for, in plain words.' },\\n70→  actions: {\\n    type: 'object',\\n    required: true,\\n    description:\\n      'Action name → the codes bound to it (KeyboardEvent.code, MouseLeft…, PadSouth…, PadLeftStickUp…): held actions are the intents b, pressed ones p.',\\n  },\\n  move: {\\n    type: 'array',\\n    items: { type: 'string' },\\n    default: ['forward', 'back', 'left', 'right'],\\n80→    description: 'The four actions that walk, as forward, back, left, right: their strengths make the move axes.',\\n  },\\n  values: {\\n    type: 'object',\\n    default: {},\\n    description:\\n      'Custom intent key (\\\"game:throttle\\\") → action: its analog strength (0 to 1, a trigger) is that intent.',\\n  },\\n  look: {\\n    type: 'boolean',\\n90→    default: false,\\n    description: \\\"True for first person and the shooter: the intents carry look = [the camera's yaw, its pitch].\\\",\\n  },\\n  aimWith: {\\n    type: 'string',\\n    enum: AIM_RULES,\\n    default: 'none',\\n    description:\\n      \\\"What the intents' aim holds: none; centre, the point the screen centre's ray meets (first person, the shooter); pointer, the pointer's ray onto the player's floor or a right stick's push (isometric, top-down).\\\",\\n  },\\n100→  lock: {\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/input/actionMaps.ts\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":100,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file Action maps and presets (PLAN.md WP 3.3, I-24; doctrine: Common ground): the registry kind `actionMap`, a set\\n * of named actions with the physical codes bound to each (engine/input/codes.ts), as Unity's action maps and Godot's\\n * InputMap hold them; the presets; and the system layer, which no player rebinds.\\n *\\n * An action is a button the sim