{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_112","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed that sim-api exports defineClipSet, placeActor, defineTerrain, and spawnActor.\n\nPages import from engine, which re-exports input/api for defineActionMap. Action maps are page state per the plan. Now designing compact activity logic to hit score bands and beginning implementation.\n\nReviewing ECS patterns for component queries and settings access. Finalizing the game state schema with mode, event, day, cursor, seen, and credits fields.\n\nExpanding the game state schema with band, phase, tick, and line fields. Designing an activity blackboard to track score parts, counts, goal positions, hold duration, falls, distance, flags, text IDs, sync accuracy, samples, topples, and marble physics.\n\nAdding total, gates, calm, penWait, guests, stone, breath, heading, foot, poseI, enter, and done to the activity blackboard. The made component tracks spawned entities for teardown. Pad inputs will be decoded into a component each step so activities read decoded values instead of raw intents.\n\nPad decoding will live inside `activity.step` via a shared `readStick` helper so direct test calls remain valid. Island and ghost components are being added for tilt and ghost state. Score parts will be stored on the activity blackboard to let `score()` compute without re-simulating. A shared `holdSeek` bot will guide bots toward target score bands. The `Band` type is being defined.\n\nImplementing `bandTune` to map each score band to delay, gain, and jitter parameters. Starting the `seek` function that returns zero movement for idle mode and otherwise pulls from the tuned values.\n\nThe `seek` function is now fully implemented. It blends a delayed history sample with a tuned proportional term and per-axis jitter, then clamps the result to ±1. The equilibrium formula will also scale with an activity-set instability value.\n\nThe equilibrium formula is being refined so the bot receives the activity's instability scale directly. This lets it compute the correct stick value from the target position. Activities will not use gentle mode for bots. For chair poses, desired is seat and feet are held separately as lean. For heron standing foot, desired is zero and the bot must pass a scale of 1.5 into the hold term; otherwise equilibrium drifts because the actual instability becomes 4.5.\n\nA numerical check confirms the heron seat is stable at zero. For the archer pose, a wrong instability scale would push the centre of balance about six percent past the ring; passing the correct scale keeps it inside the target. The lighthouse ring sits at the centre.\n\nScoring math for the lighthouse and bridge poses is being checked against score bands. A pure lighthouse pose scores about 560, which is tight for the human band. The combined bridge-plus-lighthouse mean reaches roughly 712, satisfying the 550 target. A sloppy lighthouse alone scores only about 395.\n\nSloppy bridge pose scores about 600. Combined with a sloppy lighthouse, the mean is roughly 497, which clears the 300 target.\n\nA perfect lighthouse at ring 0.14 should hit near 100% because gain is high and there is no delay.\n\nAn idle lighthouse yields about 140 points. Adding an idle bridge brings the mean to roughly 130, which sits below the human band.\n\nNothing pose scores about 152 points. Chair pose only counts ring position while both feet are held; idle chair scores zero. Comma pose averages two seat holds over eight seconds each. Enter phase lasts ten seconds but does not count toward score; the bot follows a moving guide toward the seat.\n\nEnter progress will advance based on closeness to the seat and auto-complete at ten seconds. Perfect tracking fills it quickly.\n\nThe gull napkin activity adds a disturbance halfway through; the bot must correct, though idle poses risk topples that deduct sixty points each.\n\nPond composure starts at one thousand. Disturbances trigger when stick magnitude exceeds zero point two five, mouse magnitude exceeds zero point zero two, act exceeds zero point five, or exactly one foot is held. Breathing with both feet counts as normal. Final score equals composure multiplied by half plus half of sync.\n\nAveraging sync across the whole activity is fairer than using the final value, since a short hold could otherwise skew the score. Idle lungs and sync stay at zero, so pond idle scores exactly five hundred. The breath bot will hold both feet when the guide rises or lungs lag behind it, releasing only when lungs exceed the guide; the delay applies to this decision based on the delayed guide reading.\n\nA `lag` component will store a small ring buffer of recent breath decisions. The bot writes each decision immediately into intents for direct testing, but the activity reads the value from this buffer delayed by `delay` steps to simulate timing error.\n\nThe bot will store its delayed breath decisions in a 32-bit integer bitmask on the activity component. Each step shifts the bits left and appends the current decision. The activity reads the value from this buffer to simulate timing error.\n\nThe delayed breath logic is being simplified. The bot will no longer mutate component state directly. Instead, the activity step records each commanded breath decision into a ring buffer, and the bot reads that delayed value to output its actual press. The first delay steps will simply output zero until enough history exists.\n\nThe bot must retain its delayed decision in component state between steps. It will write the new intended breath into a lag queue slot, and the step function will commit that value so the delayed command is preserved.\n\nThe director and test paths must not call the bot twice. The director invokes the bot inside `activity.step` to produce intents, while tests pass intents from their own `bot()` call. This prevents double writes to the lag queue.\n\nA `resolveIntents` helper will return the bot output when auto mode is director or attract, and raw intents otherwise. Tests keep auto off and supply their own intents, so the scene step never calls the bot again. This prevents double writes to the lag queue that would corrupt the delayed breath history.\n\nThe delayed breath history will be stored in the existing `hist` ring buffer as a 24-step array of 0 or 1 values. A `delayedBreath` helper reads the value from `delay` steps ago and appends the new decision, dropping the oldest entry when the buffer overflows.\n\nThe delayed-breath helper now returns the current decision immediately when delay is zero or negative, avoiding a stale history read. For pond, perfect timing yields a score near one thousand; a fifteen-step human delay drops it to about nine hundred seventy. Idle pond scores five hundred. Lures appear every six to ten seconds and trigger a fake pause text without pausing the activity tick.\n\nPressing act during a lure costs composure. The test verifies the tick keeps advancing while the text shows pause, and Escape is excluded from the balance map.\n\nRandom finite game inputs for a fixed number of steps will yield a finite score.\n\nMorning has two phases. The still phase lasts twelve seconds with ring positions of 0.22 for the first eight seconds and 0.16 for the last four. The reach phase lasts twenty-four seconds across six targets at radius 0.5. Each target requires a half-second hold inside a 0.16 window. If the bot fails to complete all six holds within twenty-four seconds, the second term becomes zero.\n\nThe reach phase score is one minus the phase duration divided by twenty-four. The one-foot hold lasts eight seconds with a randomly chosen foot, ring radius 0.3, and instability scale 1.6; steps inside the ring while holding count toward the score, but releasing the foot pauses counting and may drain composure. The final score is multiplied by one thousand. Age derives from the pose score using the formula round(18 + 64 * (1-s)^k), storing the result on the activity component. For each pose, the enter phase lasts up to ten seconds with the guide moving from zero to the seat; progress increases when the guide is within 0.18 of the seat, and the bot advances to the hold phase once progress reaches one or the timer expires.\n\nPose holds last for the pose-specific duration, with comma pose repeating twice. The bot tracks ring position, sync, and topples during each hold; releasing a foot triggers a one-second release before the next pose begins. Final score is the mean of per-pose scores, defined in `data/poses.ts`.\n\nLine activity covers forty meters on alternating feet. Lateral position integrates step kicks with alternating signs, plus a small correction from cob.x; leaning fights lateral drift.\n\nLine drift is damped by a 0.98 lateral multiplier and an 0.08 cob.x correction each step; falling triggers when |lateral| exceeds one. The bot will alternate feet every twenty-five steps, yielding roughly one point eight meters per second. Gusts strike every six to ten seconds after a one point five second telegraph, adding a 0.55 lateral impulse over half a second. Flags sit at zero, thirteen point three, twenty six point six, and forty meters; falling returns the bot to the last flag.\n\nLine score is one thousand minus one hundred fifty per fall and eight per second over fifty, multiplied by distance over forty, clamped to zero to one thousand. Idle line scores zero and ends at one hundred twenty seconds.\n\nThe bot will fight lateral drift by clamping desired lean to twice the lateral position, capped at ±0.8, while alternating feet. For sloppy mode, a twenty-one-step lean delay means late corrections; a 0.55 gust can push lateral past one before the bot reacts, so the lateral dynamics need tuning.\n\nThe line activity is being tuned to specific performance targets. Perfect play should finish in about forty seconds with zero falls and a score near one thousand. Human delay of fifteen steps should still clear the course with no falls and a score above five hundred fifty. Sloppy mode may fall once but must still cross, yielding a score above three hundred. Idle line scores zero.\n\nA new lateral velocity model is being considered: each step adds the accumulated kick impulse minus a correction scaled by cob.x, with kick accumulation decaying between steps.\n\nThe line activity lean model is being refined: desired cob.x will be clamped to ±0.9, scaled by 1.2 times lateral drift plus accumulated kick impulse. This equilibrium fight must be tuned through tests.\n\nPaddle activity will use a 2D board with yaw and speed derived from cob.y and cob.x. Feet alternate at roughly 1.2 Hz to add +30% speed when aligned. Twelve gates will sit along a circular loop at thirty-degree intervals, starting behind gate zero.\n\nPaddle gates are spaced at a 40-meter radius, making the course about 42 seconds long. Each gate must be passed within three meters in order. Wake and turtle at gates seven and nine add lateral disturbance to balance. A topple triggers a splash and a three-second remount with zero speed. The score is sixty points per gate plus two hundred eighty points for a full twelve-gate run, scaled by time; finishing at forty-two seconds yields roughly eight hundred forty-three, while sloppy play still scores above three hundred. Idle paddling scores zero. The activity ends after seventy-five seconds.\n\nGarden activity uses zero wobble noise. Tilt derives from lean angle via garden.tilt degrees. Marbles roll in two dimensions under gravity scaled by sin(tilt). Five trays each contain a starting marble and a hole; tilting the board toward +x rolls the ball toward its hole. A sink requires low speed and proximity to the hole. Each tray allows eighteen seconds or until all marbles sink. The score is eight hundred times the fraction of marbles sunk, plus two hundred times the leftover time if every tray completes.\n\nGarden idle scores zero because marbles remain motionless without tilt. The bot will lean toward the centroid offset to the nearest unsunk hole.\n\nTilt activity splits into two phases. A twenty-second automatic storm phase ends with storm=1 and gives idle a bonus. The east phase lasts seventy-five seconds. Twelve guests drift around; the bot holds both feet for two seconds then releases to call them over. Guests within eight meters follow, and each delivered guest adds one point while reducing the tilt by half a degree. The nan guest is already present, so that delivery counts automatically. The final score is the delivered share out of twelve.\n\nThe Tilt east phase will use calm waves to deliver guests. Guests are modeled as numbers, with each completed breath cycle releasing a batch; perfect delivery brings all twelve, a human about nine, sloppy five, and idle only the Nan guest. The stone phase lasts sixty seconds: tilt follows the center of balance, the ring shrinks from 0.3 to 0.16, and stoneHold equals the share of guests inside the ring. Breathing quiets waves by reducing disturbance.\n\nTilt activity scores are now fully weighted. The final score is one thousand times a weighted sum of four components: storm at fifteen percent, delivered guests at thirty-five percent, stone hold at forty percent, and breath sync at ten percent. Idle scores about two hundred seventy-nine, human about eight hundred twenty-seven, perfect about nine hundred ninety, and sloppy about five hundred forty-five. This confirms the skill gap and meets the minimum score targets.\n\nGroup activity uses three randomly drawn classes, but the middle class is always heron. Each forty-five-second segment scores one thousand times the fraction of guests inside the ring. The still ring segment uses a ring radius of zero point two two. The heron segment requires holding one foot with instability set to 1.5.\n\nThe pond segment uses the same scoring formula as the other two group segments. The final group score is the mean of the three forty-five-second segments.\n\nIdle scores are roughly two hundred ninety-three, human about eight hundred thirty-three, sloppy about six hundred thirty-three, and perfect around nine hundred seventy-seven.\n\nThe CPU bots in the other seats do not affect the local player's score; only seat zero counts. For testing, one seat is sufficient, but four wobbles will be spawned for the presentation.\n\nThe Director activity will run a five-day schedule. Day one covers morning, pose-a, and pond. Day two covers morning, pose-b, and garden. Day three covers morning, paddle, and line. Day four covers morning and pose-c. Day five covers morning, group, and tilt. The final two events are morning and credits.\n\nThe Director activity runs in auto mode. Scene setup begins at the first programme item and skips the title. When an activity ends, the director carries on immediately with brief results. A few steps of sleep separate the days. After tilt, the final morning and credits follow. The flow tracker records each event ID and the tilt phases. Morning alone requires about 2640 simulation steps.\n\nThe worst-case Director timeline is roughly 1,100 seconds per seed. That is about 200,000 simulation steps, or 40 seconds at 200 microseconds per step. Band tests will run ten iterations across four activities.\n\nBand tests will run about 1.7 million simulation steps, taking roughly 134 to 252 seconds depending on step timing. The Vitest timeout will be set to ten minutes.\n\nTo speed up testing, the plan is to reuse one headless engine across all seeds of an activity instead of creating a new one each time. The cost of spawning forty headless engines could add around twenty seconds, so reusing one engine avoids that overhead.\n\nA single engine will be reused per activity, with `engine.seed(n)` between seeds to restart the scene. Only one random seed is needed per activity; ten is unnecessary.\n\nTeardown will count entities with `made` set to zero and compare total entity counts against the boot baseline.\n\nThe `game.auto` setting is a string defaulting to `'off'`, not a numeric slider. The `game.assist` flag halves wobble noise when enabled.\n\nI will add `game.cpu` as a numeric default of 1. The plan is to write simulation files first, run model tests, then activities, band tests, and finally the page. Each activity file should stay under 250 lines. I will enforce ESLint rules against `any`, enums, `@file`, and line limits, plus Prettier with single quotes and width 120. In simulation code, I will use `Math.pow` instead of the `**` operator.\n\nThe simulation will use no Math.random. Intents custom keys are typed as game:* strings, so a helper function will read numeric values safely. The activity step reads intents before calling stepBalance, letting the bot see its previous input.\n\nThe bot will recall its previous position from history before each step. The first step has no prior history, so recall defaults to zero.\n\nSway coefficients are drawn once during boot and reset at the start of each activity. For group activities, three additional wobbles are spawned with their own sway values. Morning uses only seat zero.\n\nBalance configuration is read from world settings on every step so slider changes take effect immediately.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-35b622f1-7ba0-4ed2-a9e3-d1047f7b605b-38","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"virtual\\.axis|axis\\(name","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/input","glob":"*.ts","head_limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-35b622f1-7ba0-4ed2-a9e3-d1047f7b605b-39","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/input/intents.ts","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":722,"output_tokens":8192,"cache_read_input_tokens":100352,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"a1de7823-598a-4ef4-8e54-831a208f4b16"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-35b622f1-7ba0-4ed2-a9e3-d1047f7b605b-38","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,95,95,120,53,52,102,97,99,99,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,52,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,95,95,120,53,52,102,97,99,99,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,104,117,109,98,32,89,111,103,97,45,87,105,105,32,70,105,116,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,105,110,112,117,116,47,100,101,118,105,99,101,115,47,118,105,114,116,117,97,108,46,116,115,10,54,49,58,32,32,97,120,105,115,40,110,97,109,101,58,32,115,116,114,105,110,103,44,32,118,97,108,117,101,58,32,117,110,107,110,111,119,110,41,58,32,118,111,105,100,59,10,57,53,58,32,32,32,32,97,120,105,115,40,110,97,109,101,44,32,118,97,108,117,101,41,32,123,10,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,95,95,120,53,52,102,97,99,99,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,104,117,109,98,32,89,111,103,97,45,87,105,105,32,70,105,116,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,105,110,112,117,116,47,100,101,118,105,99,101,115,47,105,110,115,112,101,99,116,111,114,46,116,115,10,54,58,32,42,32,45,32,96,105,110,112,117,116,46,112,114,101,115,115,40,97,99,116,105,111,110,41,96,44,32,96,105,110,112,117,116,46,104,111,108,100,40,97,99,116,105,111,110,44,32,100,111,119,110,41,96,44,32,96,105,110,112,117,116,46,97,120,105,115,40,110,97,109,101,44,32,118,97,108,117,101,41,96,58,32,99,104,97,110,103,101,32,119,104,97,116,32,116,104,101,32,118,105,114,116,117,97,108,32,100,101,118,105,99,101,32,104,111,108,100,115,46,10,56,51,58,32,32,32,32,118,105,114,116,117,97,108,68,101,118,105,99,101,79,102,40,104,111,115,116,41,46,97,120,105,115,40,110,97,109,101,44,32,118,97,108,117,101,41,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":4,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/input/devices/virtual.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":61,\"content\":\"  axis(name: string, value: unknown): void;\"},{\"line_number\":95,\"content\":\"    axis(name, value) {\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/input/devices/inspector.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":6,\"content\":\" * - `input.press(action)`, `input.hold(action, down)`, `input.axis(name, value)`: change what the virtual device holds.\"},{\"line_number\":83,\"content\":\"    virtualDeviceOf(host).axis(name, value);\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-35b622f1-7ba0-4ed2-a9e3-d1047f7b605b-39","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The intents vocabulary (PLAN.md §6.4, §6.5 item 3, §8.4, WP 1.5; doctrine: Reproducible): everything the sim\\n * reads from outside arrives once per step as plain data, recorded by replays. `move` is a world-space direction\\n * `[x, z]` (`fromCamera` turns a stick or keys into one, so W walks away from the camera), `cam` the camera heading,\\n * `aim` a world-space aim point, `look` a view direction, `b` the buttons held and `p` the buttons pressed this step,\\n * and game code adds its own keys, namespaced (`'game:charge'`). Devices (WP 3.3) make intents; the sim only reads them.\\n *\\n * Invariants: `normalizeIntents` checks every key and value (an unknown key names the closest; numbers are finite;\\n * custom values are plain data) and returns a frozen object with keys in a fixed order, buttons sorted and unique, and\\n10→ * absent or empty entries left out. `p` is the union of the presses given and the edges of `b` (held now, not held the\\n * step before), so a press is never lost and a hold presses once; when one frame runs several steps, the frame loop\\n * passes its taps to the first step only (§6.4). `diffIntents` and `applyIntents` are the replay encoding (§8.4): held\\n * state repeats until a change-point changes it (`null` clears a key), `p` belongs to its step and is written only for\\n * presses the edges of `b` do not explain, so `applyIntents(prev, diffIntents(prev, next))` is `next` again. `-0` is\\n * kept (`Object.is`), since replays must give the sim the very bits live play gave it.\\n *\\n * `cam` is the yaw of the camera's horizontal view direction, forward(ψ) = (sin ψ, 0, cos ψ) (§6.3), not three.js's\\n * `camera.rotation.y`, which is ψ − π since a camera looks down its −Z.\\n *\\n20→ * Edges, buffering and `consume` in sim time (WP 3.3): an `InputBuffer` is what a body remembers of its buttons across\\n * steps, plain data in a component field, so the world hashes, captures and restores it. `updateInputBuffer(buffer,\\n * intents, w.tick)` runs once per step (a system in the phase `intents`); then `released` says a button went up this\\n * step (a tap shorter than a step is pressed and released on the same one), `buffered` that it was pressed within the\\n * last `BUFFER_STEPS` steps and not yet used up by `consume` (a jump pressed just before landing still jumps), and\\n * `repeated` that a held button repeats, as a menu cursor does. Carried from `my-3d2dge:engine/my-3d2dge.js:564-583`\\n * (`released`, `repeat`, `buffered`, `consume`, `consumeAll`), counted in steps instead of seconds.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * const step1 = normalizeIntents({ move: fromCamera(Math.PI / 2, [0, 1]), b: ['jump'] }); // move ≈ [1, 0]: +X\\n30→ * pressed(step1, 'jump'); // true: an edge of b\\n * const step2 = normalizeIntents({ move: step1.move, b: ['jump'] }, step1);\\n * [held(step2, 'jump'), pressed(step2, 'jump')]; // [true, false]\\n * diffIntents(step1, normalizeIntents({}, step1)); // { b: null, move: null }\\n * const buffer = updateInputBuffer(createInputBuffer(), step1, 1);\\n * buffered(buffer, 'jump'); // true for 9 steps, or until consume(buffer, 'jump')\\n * @see engine/input/intents.test.ts\\n * @see engine/input/buffer.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { codeError, defineCodes, didYouMean } from '../core/log';\\n40→import { isPlainObject, show } from '../core/schema';\\n\\n/** The codes this module raises, with their fixes. */\\nexport const INTENT_CODES = defineCodes('input', {\\n  INPUT_BAD_INTENTS: {\\n    template: '{where}: {problems}',\\n    fix: 'give intents as { move: [x, z], cam, aim: [x, y, z], look: [yaw, pitch], b: [held buttons], p: [pressed buttons] } with finite numbers, plus custom keys namespaced \\\"game:key\\\" holding plain data (engine/input/intents.ts); null or a missing key means not given',\\n    doc: 'Raised by `normalizeIntents`, `applyIntents` and the replay checks (engine/input/intents.ts, engine/sim/replay.ts) for an unknown key (named with the closest), a non-finite or misshapen number, a button that is not a non-empty string, or a custom value that is not plain data. Every problem is listed at once.',\\n  },\\n});\\n50→\\n/** A custom intent's value: plain data, as a replay file holds it. */\\nexport type IntentValue = number | string | boolean | readonly IntentValue[] | { readonly [key: string]: IntentValue };\\n\\n/** A namespaced key for game code's own intents: `'game:charge'`. */\\nexport type CustomIntentKey = `${string}:${string}`;\\n\\n/** One step's intents, as the sim reads them (systems get them as their second argument). */\\nexport type Intents = {\\n  /** The direction to move in, world space `[x, z]`, length 1 at most from `fromCamera`. */\\n60→  readonly move?: readonly [number, number];\\n  /** The camera heading: the yaw of its horizontal view direction, rad (see the file comment). */\\n  readonly cam?: number;\\n  /** The point aimed at, world space `[x, y, z]`, m. */\\n  readonly aim?: readonly [number, number, number];\\n  /** The view direction `[yaw, pitch]`, rad (first-person look). */\\n  readonly look?: readonly [number, number];\\n  /** The buttons held this step, sorted. */\\n  readonly b?: readonly string[];\\n  /** The buttons pressed this step (edges of `b` plus taps released within the step), sorted. */\\n70→  readonly p?: readonly string[];\\n} & { readonly [key: CustomIntentKey]: IntentValue | undefined };\\n\\n/** A replay change-point's intent part: the keys that changed, `null` for a key cleared, `p` for unexplained presses. */\\nexport type IntentChanges = { -readonly [K in keyof Intents]?: Intents[K] | null };\\n\\n/** The built-in keys, in the order a normalized object holds them, each with what it means. */\\nexport const INTENT_KEYS = {\\n  move: 'the direction to move in, world space [x, z]',\\n  cam: 'the camera heading (yaw of its view direction), rad',\\n80→  aim: 'the point aimed at, world space [x, y, z], m',\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The intents vocabulary (PLAN.md §6.4, §6.5 item 3, §8.4, WP 1.5; doctrine: Reproducible): everything the sim\\n * reads from outside arrives once per step as plain data, recorded by replays. `move` is a world-space direction\\n * `[x, z]` (`fromCamera` turns a stick or keys into one, so W walks away from the camera), `cam` the camera heading,\\n * `aim` a world-space aim point, `look` a view direction, `b` the buttons held and `p` the buttons pressed this step,\\n * and game code adds its own keys, namespaced (`'game:charge'`). Devices (WP 3.3) make intents; the sim only reads them.\\n *\\n * Invariants: `normalizeIntents` checks every key and value (an unknown key names the closest; numbers are finite;\\n * custom values are plain data) and returns a frozen object with keys in a fixed order, buttons sorted and unique, and\\n10→ * absent or empty entries left out. `p` is the union of the presses given and the edges of `b` (held now, not held the\\n * step before), so a press is never lost and a hold presses once; when one frame runs several steps, the frame loop\\n * passes its taps to the first step only (§6.4). `diffIntents` and `applyIntents` are the replay encoding (§8.4): held\\n * state repeats until a change-point changes it (`null` clears a key), `p` belongs to its step and is written only for\\n * presses the edges of `b` do not explain, so `applyIntents(prev, diffIntents(prev, next))` is `next` again. `-0` is\\n * kept (`Object.is`), since replays must give the sim the very bits live play gave it.\\n *\\n * `cam` is the yaw of the camera's horizontal view direction, forward(ψ) = (sin ψ, 0, cos ψ) (§6.3), not three.js's\\n * `camera.rotation.y`, which is ψ − π since a camera looks down its −Z.\\n *\\n20→ * Edges, buffering and `consume` in sim time (WP 3.3): an `InputBuffer` is what a body remembers of its buttons across\\n * steps, plain data in a component field, so the world hashes, captures and restores it. `updateInputBuffer(buffer,\\n * intents, w.tick)` runs once per step (a system in the phase `intents`); then `released` says a button went up this\\n * step (a tap shorter than a step is pressed and released on the same one), `buffered` that it was pressed within the\\n * last `BUFFER_STEPS` steps and not yet used up by `consume` (a jump pressed just before landing still jumps), and\\n * `repeated` that a held button repeats, as a menu cursor does. Carried from `my-3d2dge:engine/my-3d2dge.js:564-583`\\n * (`released`, `repeat`, `buffered`, `consume`, `consumeAll`), counted in steps instead of seconds.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * const step1 = normalizeIntents({ move: fromCamera(Math.PI / 2, [0, 1]), b: ['jump'] }); // move ≈ [1, 0]: +X\\n30→ * pressed(step1, 'jump'); // true: an edge of b\\n * const step2 = normalizeIntents({ move: step1.move, b: ['jump'] }, step1);\\n * [held(step2, 'jump'), pressed(step2, 'jump')]; // [true, false]\\n * diffIntents(step1, normalizeIntents({}, step1)); // { b: null, move: null }\\n * const buffer = updateInputBuffer(createInputBuffer(), step1, 1);\\n * buffered(buffer, 'jump'); // true for 9 steps, or until consume(buffer, 'jump')\\n * @see engine/input/intents.test.ts\\n * @see engine/input/buffer.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { codeError, defineCodes, didYouMean } from '../core/log';\\n40→import { isPlainObject, show } from '../core/schema';\\n\\n/** The codes this module raises, with their fixes. */\\nexport const INTENT_CODES = defineCodes('input', {\\n  INPUT_BAD_INTENTS: {\\n    template: '{where}: {problems}',\\n    fix: 'give intents as { move: [x, z], cam, aim: [x, y, z], look: [yaw, pitch], b: [held buttons], p: [pressed buttons] } with finite numbers, plus custom keys namespaced \\\"game:key\\\" holding plain data (engine/input/intents.ts); null or a missing key means not given',\\n    doc: 'Raised by `normalizeIntents`, `applyIntents` and the replay checks (engine/input/intents.ts, engine/sim/replay.ts) for an unknown key (named with the closest), a non-finite or misshapen number, a button that is not a non-empty string, or a custom value that is not plain data. Every problem is listed at once.',\\n  },\\n});\\n50→\\n/** A custom intent's value: plain data, as a replay file holds it. */\\nexport type IntentValue = number | string | boolean | readonly IntentValue[] | { readonly [key: string]: IntentValue };\\n\\n/** A namespaced key for game code's own intents: `'game:charge'`. */\\nexport type CustomIntentKey = `${string}:${string}`;\\n\\n/** One step's intents, as the sim reads them (systems get them as their second argument). */\\nexport type Intents = {\\n  /** The direction to move in, world space `[x, z]`, length 1 at most from `fromCamera`. */\\n60→  readonly move?: readonly [number, number];\\n  /** The camera heading: the yaw of its horizontal view direction, rad (see the file comment). */\\n  readonly cam?: number;\\n  /** The point aimed at, world space `[x, y, z]`, m. */\\n  readonly aim?: readonly [number, number, number];\\n  /** The view direction `[yaw, pitch]`, rad (first-person look). */\\n  readonly look?: readonly [number, number];\\n  /** The buttons held this step, sorted. */\\n  readonly b?: readonly string[];\\n  /** The buttons pressed this step (edges of `b` plus taps released within the step), sorted. */\\n70→  readonly p?: readonly string[];\\n} & { readonly [key: CustomIntentKey]: IntentValue | undefined };\\n\\n/** A replay change-point's intent part: the keys that changed, `null` for a key cleared, `p` for unexplained presses. */\\nexport type IntentChanges = { -readonly [K in keyof Intents]?: Intents[K] | null };\\n\\n/** The built-in keys, in the order a normalized object holds them, each with what it means. */\\nexport const INTENT_KEYS = {\\n  move: 'the direction to move in, world space [x, z]',\\n  cam: 'the camera heading (yaw of its view direction), rad',\\n80→  aim: 'the point aimed at, world space [x, y, z], m',\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/input/intents.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The intents vocabulary (PLAN.md §6.4, §6.5 item 3, §8.4, WP 1.5; doctrine: Reproducible