 places it: `x`, `y` (the box's bottom, 0 by\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/gfx/stageGround.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":29,\"content\":\"/** The stage's ground round an area of `size` (m, along x and z: see the file comment). */\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/gfx/cameras/codes.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":12,\"content\":\" *   the target's height: above the followed point, or the fixed target's y when x and z are given.\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"57e3e5fc-0cca-442e-bf81-1708b02913e2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_120","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Components with x and z coordinates will use stand-in boxes.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-86169b4c-3503-4e01-93b6-5dd03bcc1dea-52","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/world/props/prop.ts","limit":60}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-86169b4c-3503-4e01-93b6-5dd03bcc1dea-53","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/gfx/post/looks.ts","limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-86169b4c-3503-4e01-93b6-5dd03bcc1dea-54","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"export function defineLook|export interface LookSpec|sky:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/gfx/sky.ts","head_limit":15}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":985,"output_tokens":388,"cache_read_input_tokens":123648,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"5996e3b5-e8dd-4973-ae22-5e33873d55b1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-86169b4c-3503-4e01-93b6-5dd03bcc1dea-52","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The prop registry (PLAN.md WP 4.5, §6.6): the registry kind `prop`, one entry per kind of thing that stands\\n * in a level, as data: the meshes it is drawn with (geometry and material by id, §6.1: `world` names them, `gfx`\\n * builds them, engine/gfx/props.ts), its body (a shape and a material for the physics world), an optional light\\n * spot, where a level places it, and its interaction hooks. The engine's props are the crate, the barrel, the\\n * brazier's fire and the pillar (engine/world/props/catalog.ts), the library's sconce, banner, chest, door, table,\\n * bench, statue, lantern and chair (library.ts, WP 10.3, the chair WP 13.16's) and the house's fittings, the pane,\\n * the switch, the lamp and the garage door (fittings.ts, WP 13.16); a game adds its own with `defineProp`\\n * (`x new prop`). WP 13.16's fields: a `kinematic` body the sim drives, `health` and what breaking leaves (`breaks`),\\n10→ * a `prompt` for its own `use`, and parts `lit` only while the prop is on; a hook's touch also names the world, the\\n * entity and its user.\\n *\\n * A prop's frame: its base, where it stands, is the origin, +Y up, and its parts stand on it as the geometry kit's\\n * shapes stand on the ground. Its body's shape stands on the base too, so the body's origin, the prop's centre, is\\n * `propCentre(prop)` above it: what a body's pose (`readBody`) and `propPose` give, and what the meshes are drawn\\n * at. A part with `flame.tongues` above 0 is drawn as that many flame tongues dancing round its origin (its geometry\\n * a cone one metre high, stretched each frame), the 2D hall's flames. The hooks: `hit` (a swing, a blast) knocks a\\n * dynamic prop away and leaves the others; `use` does nothing; shared code calls them and never compares ids.\\n *\\n20→ * Carried: the idea of my-3d2dge's 2D prop catalog (`src/starter/*`, `src/emberdeep/51-themes.js`): kinds of props\\n * as data with their light metadata (a colour, a reach and a flicker beside the drawing).\\n *\\n * Invariants: an entry is plain data plus its two hooks, every field filled with its default; a part's name is\\n * unique in its prop; colours are hex; vectors have three numbers; a body's shape has a size above 0; spawn names\\n * and light tags are words. Geometry and material ids are gfx's to resolve (a missing one draws its kind's fallback,\\n * and `node x qa geo tex --only prop:` reports it). Problems raise `CORE_BAD_SPEC` naming each; a missing id gets\\n * `prop:crate` after a warning (`CORE_UNKNOWN_ID`).\\n *\\n * @example\\n30→ * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineProp('prop:stool', { parts: [{ geometry: 'geometry:frustum', params: { radiusBottom: 0.2, radiusTop: 0.2, height: 0.45 }, material: 'material:planks' }], body: { type: 'dynamic', shape: 'cylinder', radius: 0.2, height: 0.45 } }, reg);\\n * propCentre(getProp('prop:stool', reg)); // 0.225: half its height\\n * getProp('prop:crate', reg).body.type; // 'dynamic': the engine's props come with the kind\\n * @see engine/world/props/prop.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { galleryField } from '../../core/gallery';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../../core/registry';\\nimport type { EntryOf, Schema } from '../../core/schema';\\n40→import type { Point3 } from '../level/types';\\nimport { BUILT_IN_PROPS } from './catalog';\\nimport { FITTING_PROPS } from './fittings';\\nimport { LIBRARY_PROPS } from './library';\\n\\n/** A vector field in metres (or `unit`), three numbers. */\\nconst vec3 = (value: readonly number[], description: string, unit = 'm') =>\\n  ({ type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, default: value, unit, description }) as const;\\n\\n/** A length in metres, at least 0. */\\n50→const metres = (value: number, description: string) =>\\n  ({ type: 'number', default: value, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description }) as const;\\n\\n/** The object of `properties`' defaults: a nested field's default, so a spec leaving it out gets every key. */\\nconst defaultsOf = (properties: Schema): Record<string, unknown> =>\\n  Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(properties).map(([key, field]) => [key, field.default]));\\n\\n/** The fields of a part's flame. */\\nconst FLAME_FIELDS = {\\n  tongues: { type: 'integer', default: 0, minimum: 0, maximum: 8, description: 'How many tongues; 0: still.' },\\n60→  ring: metres(0.1, 'How far from its origin the tongues stand.'),\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The prop registry (PLAN.md WP 4.5, §6.6): the registry kind `prop`, one entry per kind of thing that stands\\n * in a level, as data: the meshes it is drawn with (geometry and material by id, §6.1: `world` names them, `gfx`\\n * builds them, engine/gfx/props.ts), its body (a shape and a material for the physics world), an optional light\\n * spot, where a level places it, and its interaction hooks. The engine's props are the crate, the barrel, the\\n * brazier's fire and the pillar (engine/world/props/catalog.ts), the library's sconce, banner, chest, door, table,\\n * bench, statue, lantern and chair (library.ts, WP 10.3, the chair WP 13.16's) and the house's fittings, the pane,\\n * the switch, the lamp and the garage door (fittings.ts, WP 13.16); a game adds its own with `defineProp`\\n * (`x new prop`). WP 13.16's fields: a `kinematic` body the sim drives, `health` and what breaking leaves (`breaks`),\\n10→ * a `prompt` for its own `use`, and parts `lit` only while the prop is on; a hook's touch also names the world, the\\n * entity and its user.\\n *\\n * A prop's frame: its base, where it stands, is the origin, +Y up, and its parts stand on it as the geometry kit's\\n * shapes stand on the ground. Its body's shape stands on the base too, so the body's origin, the prop's centre, is\\n * `propCentre(prop)` above it: what a body's pose (`readBody`) and `propPose` give, and what the meshes are drawn\\n * at. A part with `flame.tongues` above 0 is drawn as that many flame tongues dancing round its origin (its geometry\\n * a cone one metre high, stretched each frame), the 2D hall's flames. The hooks: `hit` (a swing, a blast) knocks a\\n * dynamic prop away and leaves the others; `use` does nothing; shared code calls them and never compares ids.\\n *\\n20→ * Carried: the idea of my-3d2dge's 2D prop catalog (`src/starter/*`, `src/emberdeep/51-themes.js`): kinds of props\\n * as data with their light metadata (a colour, a reach and a flicker beside the drawing).\\n *\\n * Invariants: an entry is plain data plus its two hooks, every field filled with its default; a part's name is\\n * unique in its prop; colours are hex; vectors have three numbers; a body's shape has a size above 0; spawn names\\n * and light tags are words. Geometry and material ids are gfx's to resolve (a missing one draws its kind's fallback,\\n * and `node x qa geo tex --only prop:` reports it). Problems raise `CORE_BAD_SPEC` naming each; a missing id gets\\n * `prop:crate` after a warning (`CORE_UNKNOWN_ID`).\\n *\\n * @example\\n30→ * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineProp('prop:stool', { parts: [{ geometry: 'geometry:frustum', params: { radiusBottom: 0.2, radiusTop: 0.2, height: 0.45 }, material: 'material:planks' }], body: { type: 'dynamic', shape: 'cylinder', radius: 0.2, height: 0.45 } }, reg);\\n * propCentre(getProp('prop:stool', reg)); // 0.225: half its height\\n * getProp('prop:crate', reg).body.type; // 'dynamic': the engine's props come with the kind\\n * @see engine/world/props/prop.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { galleryField } from '../../core/gallery';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../../core/registry';\\nimport type { EntryOf, Schema } from '../../core/schema';\\n40→import type { Point3 } from '../level/types';\\nimport { BUILT_IN_PROPS } from './catalog';\\nimport { FITTING_PROPS } from './fittings';\\nimport { LIBRARY_PROPS } from './library';\\n\\n/** A vector field in metres (or `unit`), three numbers. */\\nconst vec3 = (value: readonly number[], description: string, unit = 'm') =>\\n  ({ type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, default: value, unit, description }) as const;\\n\\n/** A length in metres, at least 0. */\\n50→const metres = (value: number, description: string) =>\\n  ({ type: 'number', default: value, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description }) as const;\\n\\n/** The object of `properties`' defaults: a nested field's default, so a spec leaving it out gets every key. */\\nconst defaultsOf = (properties: Schema): Record<string, unknown> =>\\n  Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(properties).map(([key, field]) => [key, field.default]));\\n\\n/** The fields of a part's flame. */\\nconst FLAME_FIELDS = {\\n  tongues: { type: 'integer', default: 0, minimum: 0, maximum: 8, description: 'How many tongues; 0: still.' },\\n60→  ring: metres(0.1, 'How far from its origin the tongues stand.'),\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/world/props/prop.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":60,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The prop registry (PLAN.md WP 4.5, §6.6): the registry kind `prop`, one entry per kind of thing that stands\\n * in a level, as data: the meshes it is drawn with (geometry and material by id, §6.1: `world` names them, `gfx`\\n * builds them, engine/gfx/props.ts), its body (a shape and a material for the physics world), an optional light\\n * spot, where a level places it, and its interaction hooks. The engine's props are the crate, the barrel, the\\n * brazier's fire and the pillar (engine/world/props/catalog.ts), the library's sconce, banner, chest, door, table,\\n * bench, statue, lantern and chair (library.ts, WP 10.3, the chair WP 13.16's) and the house's fittings, the pane,\\n * the switch, the lamp and the garage door (fittings.ts, WP 13.16); a game adds its own with `defineProp`\\n * (`x new prop`). WP 13.16's fields: a `kinematic` body the sim drives, `health` and what breaking leaves (`breaks`),\\n * a `prompt` for its own `use`, and parts `lit` only while the prop is on; a hook's touch also names the world, the\\n * entity and its user.\\n *\\n * A prop's frame: its base, where it stands, is the origin, +Y up, and its parts stand on it as the geometry kit's\\n * shapes stand on the ground. Its body's shape stands on the base too, so the body's origin, the prop's centre, is\\n * `propCentre(prop)` above it: what a body's pose (`readBody`) and `propPose` give, and what the meshes are drawn\\n * at. A part with `flame.tongues` above 0 is drawn as that many flame tongues dancing round its origin (its geometry\\n * a cone one metre high, stretched each frame), the 2D hall's flames. The hooks: `hit` (a swing, a blast) knocks a\\n * dynamic prop away and leaves the others; `use` does nothing; shared code calls them and never compares ids.\\n *\\n * Carried: the idea of my-3d2dge's 2D prop catalog (`src/starter/*`, `src/emberdeep/51-themes.js`): kinds of props\\n * as data with their light metadata (a colour, a reach and a flicker beside the drawing).\\n *\\n * Invariants: an entry is plain data plus its two hooks, every field filled with its default; a part's name is\\n * unique in its prop; colours are hex; vectors have three numbers; a body's shape has a size above 0; spawn names\\n * and light tags are words. Geometry and material ids are gfx's to resolve (a missing one draws its kind's fallback,\\n * and `node x qa geo tex --only prop:` reports it). Problems raise `CORE_BAD_SPEC` naming each; a missing id gets\\n * `prop:crate` after a warning (`CORE_UNKNOWN_ID`).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineProp('prop:stool', { parts: [{ geometry: 'geometry:frustum', params: { radiusBottom: 0.2, radiusTop: 0.2, height: 0.45 }, material: 'material:planks' }], body: { type: 'dynamic', shape: 'cylinder', radius: 0.2, height: 0.45 } }, reg);\\n * propCentre(getProp('prop:stool', reg)); // 0.225: half its height\\n * getProp('prop:crate', reg).body.type; // 'dynamic': the engine's props come with the kind\\n * @see engine/world/props/prop.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { galleryField } from '../../core/gallery';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../../core/registry';\\nimport type { EntryOf, Schema } from '../../core/schema';\\nimport type { Point3 } from '../level/types';\\nimport { BUILT_IN_PROPS } from './catalog';\\nimport { FITTING_PROPS } from './fittings';\\nimport { LIBRARY_PROPS } from './library';\\n\\n/** A vector field in metres (or `unit`), three numbers. */\\nconst vec3 = (value: readonly number[], description: string, unit = 'm') =>\\n  ({ type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, default: value, unit, description }) as const;\\n\\n/** A length in metres, at least 0. */\\nconst metres = (value: number, description: string) =>\\n  ({ type: 'number', default: value, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description }) as const;\\n\\n/** The object of `properties`' defaults: a nested field's default, so a spec leaving it out gets every key. */\\nconst defaultsOf = (properties: Schema): Record<string, unknown> =>\\n  Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(properties).map(([key, field]) => [key, field.default]));\\n\\n/** The fields of a part's flame. */\\nconst FLAME_FIELDS = {\\n  tongues: { type: 'integer', default: 0, minimum: 0, maximum: 8, description: 'How many tongues; 0: still.' },\\n  ring: metres(0.1, 'How far from its origin the tongues stand.'),\\n\",\"total_lines\":373}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-86169b4c-3503-4e01-93b6-5dd03bcc1dea-53","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Looks (PLAN.md WP 7.4, §6.6 kind `look`): a material style (WP 7.2, engine/gfx/materials/styles.ts) plus a\\n * filter chain (engine/gfx/post/filters.ts), as data, and the sky that lights the scene (WP 7.8, a `sky` id,\\n * engine/gfx/sky.ts). A look names what it changes; every filter it leaves out is off, and a style or sky of `''`\\n * keeps the scene's own. The chain draws the look the setting `post.look` names (engine/gfx/post/chain.ts); switching\\n * looks writes uniforms and re-points materials to styles the warm-up has already compiled, so it builds no\\n * pipeline. A look's sky is shown by the pages `createEngine` starts (engine/app/look.ts): the stage's lights give way\\n * to it (engine/gfx/stageSky.ts), which changes the shader key, so a look with another sky warms up before it draws.\\n * WP 13.24 adds the toon style's `shade` and `dither`, what a look asks of the stage (`shells`, outline shells round\\n10→ * its props and bodies, engine/gfx/stageShells.ts; `skyGradient`, the dome as a clean gradient) and its variant for\\n * perspective cameras (`perspective`, a look id whose filters and toon numbers the chain draws there). WP 13.30 adds\\n * what it asks of the whole picture: `resolution`, the mode drawn while `gfx.resolution` is `auto`\\n * (engine/gfx/resolution.ts), and `textures`, every material's maps filtered `smooth` or `pixel`\\n * (engine/gfx/materials/library.ts `filter`); the page's look applies both (engine/app/look.ts), building nothing.\\n *\\n * The entries are seeded from shardfall's tuned presets (`shardfall:crates/pav_view/src/looks.toml`, with\\n * `look.rs:100-353`), keys renamed: its whole looks are here (`look:hd-2d`, `look:game-boy`…), and its one-section\\n * presets are looks of their own, grouped by the filter they set (`look:palette/game-boy`, `look:stylize/oil-paint`,\\n * engine/gfx/post/presets.ts). Its \\\"objects\\\" (characters and objects) is the scope `characters+objects`. Its air and\\n20→ * bounce light came with WP 11.5 (the `air` and `gi` sections, engine/gfx/post/shading.ts): the looks Sunbeams and\\n * Lamplit fog, and their presets. Left out: styles that differ by part and the knobs WP 7.2's styles lack\\n * (`flat_shadow`, `specular`, `exposure`), so four looks keep the scene's style where shardfall styled the parts\\n * apart. One rename for the lesson: \\\"64 colours\\\" posterized to 4 levels, which shifts the hue of dull colours, so it\\n * is `look:palette/512-colours`, at 8. WP 13.31 and 13.32 add Style Lab's presets and looks made by combining them\\n * (engine/gfx/post/presetsMore.ts), and every look's `combine` and `strength` (engine/gfx/post/combine.ts): the looks\\n * it lays on, how much of each and which of its filters, and how much of the whole look shows.\\n *\\n * Invariants: every look's levels are 0 or 8 and more (the kind's check); a missing id falls back to `look:clean`\\n * with a warning naming the closest ids; looks read their fields, never their ids. A game's own look is scaffolded by\\n30→ * `node x new look labs/<lab>/looks/<name>` (tools/templates/look/).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const registry = createRegistry();\\n * defineLooks(registry);\\n * registry.get('look', 'look:game-boy').filters.palette.palette; // 'gameboy'\\n * registry.list('look').length; // 137\\n * @see engine/gfx/post/looks.test.ts\\n * @see tests/e2e/post.spec.ts\\n40→ */\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Looks (PLAN.md WP 7.4, §6.6 kind `look`): a material style (WP 7.2, engine/gfx/materials/styles.ts) plus a\\n * filter chain (engine/gfx/post/filters.ts), as data, and the sky that lights the scene (WP 7.8, a `sky` id,\\n * engine/gfx/sky.ts). A look names what it changes; every filter it leaves out is off, and a style or sky of `''`\\n * keeps the scene's own. The chain draws the look the setting `post.look` names (engine/gfx/post/chain.ts); switching\\n * looks writes uniforms and re-points materials to styles the warm-up has already compiled, so it builds no\\n * pipeline. A look's sky is shown by the pages `createEngine` starts (engine/app/look.ts): the stage's lights give way\\n * to it (engine/gfx/stageSky.ts), which changes the shader key, so a look with another sky warms up before it draws.\\n * WP 13.24 adds the toon style's `shade` and `dither`, what a look asks of the stage (`shells`, outline shells round\\n10→ * its props and bodies, engine/gfx/stageShells.ts; `skyGradient`, the dome as a clean gradient) and its variant for\\n * perspective cameras (`perspective`, a look id whose filters and toon numbers the chain draws there). WP 13.30 adds\\n * what it asks of the whole picture: `resolution`, the mode drawn while `gfx.resolution` is `auto`\\n * (engine/gfx/resolution.ts), and `textures`, every material's maps filtered `smooth` or `pixel`\\n * (engine/gfx/materials/library.ts `filter`); the page's look applies both (engine/app/look.ts), building nothing.\\n *\\n * The entries are seeded from shardfall's tuned presets (`shardfall:crates/pav_view/src/looks.toml`, with\\n * `look.rs:100-353`), keys renamed: its whole looks are here (`look:hd-2d`, `look:game-boy`…), and its one-section\\n * presets are looks of their own, grouped by the filter they set (`look:palette/game-boy`, `look:stylize/oil-paint`,\\n * engine/gfx/post/presets.ts). Its \\\"objects\\\" (characters and objects) is the scope `characters+objects`. Its air and\\n20→ * bounce light came with WP 11.5 (the `air` and `gi` sections, engine/gfx/post/shading.ts): the looks Sunbeams and\\n * Lamplit fog, and their presets. Left out: styles that differ by part and the knobs WP 7.2's styles lack\\n * (`flat_shadow`, `specular`, `exposure`), so four looks keep the scene's style where shardfall styled the parts\\n * apart. One rename for the lesson: \\\"64 colours\\\" posterized to 4 levels, which shifts the hue of dull colours, so it\\n * is `look:palette/512-colours`, at 8. WP 13.31 and 13.32 add Style Lab's presets and looks made by combining them\\n * (engine/gfx/post/presetsMore.ts), and every look's `combine` and `strength` (engine/gfx/post/combine.ts): the looks\\n * it lays on, how much of each and which of its filters, and how much of the whole look shows.\\n *\\n * Invariants: every look's levels are 0 or 8 and more (the kind's check); a missing id falls back to `look:clean`\\n * with a warning naming the closest ids; looks read their fields, never their ids. A game's own look is scaffolded by\\n30→ * `node x new look labs/<lab>/looks/<name>` (tools/templates/look/).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const registry = createRegistry();\\n * defineLooks(registry);\\n * registry.get('look', 'look:game-boy').filters.palette.palette; // 'gameboy'\\n * registry.list('look').length; // 137\\n * @see engine/gfx/post/looks.test.ts\\n * @see tests/e2e/post.spec.ts\\n40→ */\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/gfx/post/looks.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file Looks (PLAN.md WP 7.4, §6.6 kind `look`): a material style (WP 7.2, engine/gfx/materials/styles.ts) plus a\\n * filter chain (engine/gfx/post/filters.ts), as data, and the sky that lights the scene (WP 7.8, a `sky` id,\\n * engine/gfx/sky.ts). A look names what it changes; every filter it leaves out is off, and a style or sky of `''`\\n * keeps the scene's own. The chain draws the look the setting `post.look` names (engine/gfx/post/chain.ts); switching\\n * looks writes uniforms and re-points materials to styles the warm-up has already compiled, so it builds no\\n * pipeline. A look's sky is shown by the pages `createEngine` starts (engine/app/look.ts): the stage's lights give way\\n * to it (engine/gfx/stageSky.ts), which changes the shader key, so a look with another sky warms up before it draws.\\n * WP 13.24 adds the toon style's `shade` and `dither`, what a look asks of the stage (`shells`, outline shells round\\n * its props and bodies, engine/gfx/stageShells.ts; `skyGradient`, the dome as a clean gradient) and its variant for\\n * perspective cameras (`perspective`, a look id whose filters and toon numbers the chain draws there). WP 13.30 adds\\n * what it asks of the whole picture: `resolution`, the mode drawn while `gfx.resolution` is `auto`\\n * (engine/gfx/resolution.ts), and `textures`, every material's maps filtered `smooth` or `pixel`\\n * (engine/gfx/materials/library.ts `filter`); the page's look applies both (engine/app/look.ts), building nothing.\\n *\\n * The entries are seeded from shardfall's tuned presets (`shardfall:crates/pav_view/src/looks.toml`, with\\n * `look.rs:100-353`), keys renamed: its whole looks are here (`look:hd-2d`, `look:game-boy`…), and its one-section\\n * presets are looks of their own, grouped by the filter they set (`look:palette/game-boy`, `look:stylize/oil-paint`,\\n * engine/gfx/post/presets.ts). Its \\\"objects\\\" (characters and objects) is the scope `characters+objects`. Its air and\\n * bounce light came with WP 11.5 (the `air` and `gi` sections, engine/gfx/post/shading.ts): the looks Sunbeams and\\n * Lamplit fog, and their presets. Left out: styles that differ by part and the knobs WP 7.2's styles lack\\n * (`flat_shadow`, `specular`, `exposure`), so four looks keep the scene's style where shardfall styled the parts\\n * apart. One rename for the lesson: \\\"64 colours\\\" posterized to 4 levels, which shifts the hue of dull colours, so it\\n * is `look:palette/512-colours`, at 8. WP 13.31 and 13.32 add Style Lab's presets and looks made by combining them\\n * (engine/gfx/post/presetsMore.ts), and every look's `combine` and `strength` (engine/gfx/post/combine.ts): the looks\\n * it lays on, how much of each and which of its filters, and how much of the whole look shows.\\n *\\n * Invariants: every look's levels are 0 or 8 and more (the kind's check); a missing id falls back to `look:clean`\\n * with a warning naming the closest ids; looks read their fields, never their ids. A game's own look is scaffolded by\\n * `node x new look labs/<lab>/looks/<name>` (tools/templates/look/).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../../core/registry';\\n * const registry = createRegistry();\\n * defineLooks(registry);\\n * registry.get('look', 'look:game-boy').filters.palette.palette; // 'gameboy'\\n * registry.list('look').length; // 137\\n * @see engine/gfx/post/looks.test.ts\\n * @see tests/e2e/post.spec.ts\\n */\\n\",\"total_lines\":363}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-86169b4c-3503-4e01-93b6-5dd03bcc1dea-54","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,95,95,120,53,52,102,97,99,99,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,53,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,95,95,120,53,52,102,97,99,99,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,104,117,109,98,32,89,111,103,97,45,87,105,105,32,70,105,116,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,103,102,120,47,115,107,121,46,116,115,10,55,58,32,42,32,102,105,101,108,100,115,32,97,110,100,32,105,116,115,32,112,114,101,115,101,116,115,32,40,96,115,107,121,58,104,97,108,108,96,44,32,96,115,107,121,58,100,97,121,108,105,103,104,116,96,41,32,97,114,101,32,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,103,102,120,47,115,107,121,75,105,110,100,46,116,115,39,115,46,10,51,54,58,32,42,32,47,47,32,115,107,121,46,115,101,116,40,39,115,107,121,58,104,97,108,108,39,41,59,10,49,54,54,58,32,42,32,116,104,101,32,100,111,109,101,44,32,116,104,101,32,98,97,99,107,100,114,111,112,32,97,110,100,32,116,104,101,32,97,116,109,111,115,112,104,101,114,101,59,32,115,104,111,119,115,32,96,115,107,121,58,100,97,121,108,105,103,104,116,96,32,117,110,116,105,108,32,96,115,101,116,96,32,110,97,109,101,115,32,97,110,111,116,104,101,114,32,112,114,101,115,101,116,46,10,50,50,56,58,32,32,108,101,116,32,112,114,101,115,101,116,32,61,32,114,101,103,105,115,116,114,121,46,103,101,116,40,39,115,107,121,39,44,32,39,115,107,121,58,100,97,121,108,105,103,104,116,39,41,32,97,115,32,83,107,121,69,110,116,114,121,59,10,50,51,57,58,32,32,99,111,110,115,116,32,115,107,121,58,32,83,107,121,32,61,32,123,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":5,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/gfx/sky.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":7,\"content\":\" * fields and its presets (`sky:hall`, `sky:daylight`) are engine/gfx/skyKind.ts's.\"},{\"line_number\":36,\"content\":\" * // sky.set('sky:hall');\"},{\"line_number\":166,\"content\":\" * the dome, the backdrop and the atmosphere; shows `sky:daylight` until `set` names another preset.\"},{\"line_number\":228,\"content\":\"  let preset = registry.get('sky', 'sky:daylight') as SkyEntry;\"},{\"line_number\":239,\"content\":\"  const sky: Sky = {\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"8be74e2e-b853-48d5-8b3a-6a2f0da65054"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_121","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll use look:clean or define a simple look.\n\nI'll define look:retreat from the engine import, falling back to an existing bright look if typechecking fails. I'm focusing on sim correctness and will write all simulation files in a batch before testing. I've started drafting the Activity interface.\n\nThe Activity interface now includes step, teardown, score, and bot methods. Pose sets will be created through a poseClass factory. Short forms and group activities will reuse shared scoring logic with durations stored on the activity object.\n\nGroup will use a `makeMorning` factory with an optional short flag. It will call shared step helpers directly instead of invoking morning.setup. The shipped activities are morning, the pose classes, pond, line, paddle, garden, group, and tilt.\n\nThe director seen list now includes all activities, including the tilt variants and evening. Pose-a will be recorded as both pose and its specific ID. game.start will accept plain JSON with an event and optional pose. game.carry routes results to next or roam, while game.sleep ends the day. game.begin loads saved state or starts fresh at day one. When auto is director, setup begins the programme immediately.\n\nAttract mode will loop short slices of about fifteen seconds using perfect bots with slight noise. It will skip activities that were cut from the plan, such as step, hoop, cliff, and jog. The attract sequence will cycle through pose-a, pond, line, paddle, garden, and tilt, jumping directly to the stone phase for tilt. A `{ event, phase }` argument will be added to game.start to support developer jumps.\n\nA `game.jump` action will handle developer jumps with `{ day, event, phase }`. `game.finish` will set the score to the rank threshold and end the session. The simulation will keep its save state minimal, passing only `{ day, cursor, pebbles, seen, ages }` into `game.begin`. The page will own the bests store and persist results. On the title screen, the flow runs but the step does nothing until `game.begin` or `game.start`.\n\nClicking Begin will start the day's first activity, morning, so a sofa player enters the ritual immediately. After each result, Carry on advances to the next programme item. The day ends with evening, sleep, and the next day begins.\n\nAfter credits, the game enters free play roam. The programme menu also includes a Go option. A noticeboard appears after day one for free play. During the story, Carry on advances the narrative; otherwise the player roams freely with the noticeboard. I am now implementing the `startEvent` driver transition to switch between activities.\n\n`startEvent` now clears the flow line, resets the active activity, sets its phase, records the visit, and calls setup. `finishEvent` computes the score, clamps it, assigns a rank, awards pebbles based on that rank and a multiplier setting, and switches to results mode. The `carry` function is being drafted to advance the programme in auto mode or handle manual continuation otherwise.\n\nActivities signal completion by setting `act.done` when their time box ends. The driver checks this flag after each step and calls `finishEvent` to avoid finishing inside the same step as setup. In `playStep`, the director feeds auto intents into the active activity, then advances to results mode once the activity is done. During results, brief auto mode counts a few steps before carrying on.\n\nAuto mode now waits thirty steps before advancing from results or evening. carryOn starts the next programme event if one remains, otherwise it triggers evening. sleep advances to the next day, or to credits once the final morning is complete. The programme cursor lives in flow.cursor and bootDirector initializes the first day and begins the full programme.\n\nThe programme walker now advances the cursor after each activity. It checks whether the next event belongs to a new day; if so, it inserts an evening transition before continuing.\n\nThe final morning and credits both belong to day five, so no evening separates them. The attract loop runs each activity for up to fifteen seconds or until it finishes, then advances without entering results mode.\n\nThe attract loop index will live on flow.cursor. Activities signal completion by setting done when their time box expires, and tests assert this happens within seconds plus a buffer before the mode shifts to results. In band tests game.auto is off, so done triggers results directly. Score must be computed before teardown clears the activity state, and finishEvent stores flow.result before tearing down.\n\nTeardown will only despawn `made` entities, leaving activity score fields intact so `score()` remains valid after the event ends. A shared `balance(w, stick, scales)` wrapper will handle all balance logic, including topples which reset at setup. Mouse decay lives inside `stepBalance`, and the simulation accepts per-step deltas via `game:mouse`. Garden uses `game:pointer` with coordinates in -1..1.\n\nTilt will use a finite cob value instead of the default when one is provided, and the bot will lean rather than point. Non-finite numbers resolve to zero. A `visit` helper appends event IDs to a comma-separated seen list. Scores are ranked into Unsettling, Serene, Composed, or Adequate tiers.\n\nThe `pebblesOf` function awards pebbles based on rank: five for Unsettling, four for Serene, three for Composed, two for Adequate, and one for Present. The multiplier setting scales these base amounts. `clampScore` clamps scores to zero or one thousand and returns zero for non-finite values. `wobbleAge` maps a normalized age value to a rounded age between eighteen and eighty-two.\n\nThe line bot model is being tuned for quick node-script testing. It tracks distance, lateral offset, kick residual, foot timer, last foot, gust state, and gull bias. The simulation runs a step loop that ends at 120 seconds. Gusts are scheduled from setup fields rather than recomputed from tick and seed.\n\nThe line bot step loop now includes gust kicks, gull bias, and lateral drift. When the gull flicks at fifteen metres or the flip is fast, the bias drops to zero. Lateral offset accumulates from kick plus bias minus cob drag, with kick decaying by 0.35 seconds per second. If lateral drift exceeds 1.15 metres, the bot falls back to the last flag, resets offset and kick, and recovers for thirty steps without input. Foot edges trigger tryStep: stepping on the same foot as last causes a stumble with a sideways nudge, while stepping on the opposite foot advances distance and adds a directional kick.\n\nThe line bot will alternate feet every eighteen steps to reach forty metres in about twenty-one seconds. Its desired lateral correction is clamped to ±0.85, and the equilibrium cob.x equals that value divided by 1.6. The fight drag subtracts cob.x multiplied by 1.6 per step. The stepKick impulse and kick residual are treated as a velocity-like quantity.\n\nThe line bot's step impulse and kick decay integrate to roughly 0.14 metres of lateral drift per second. This is small enough that idle play scores zero while human players easily correct it, so all finishers should clear the score thresholds.\n\nThe line activity must not be too easy. If idle play scores zero while sloppy finishes with one fall, the score still clears the threshold. Perfect runs should finish under fifty seconds; otherwise the time penalty drops them below the required seventy-five hundred points. At a speed of one point five metres per second, forty metres takes about twenty-seven seconds.\n\nThe line bot will step every 22 frames, yielding a speed of roughly 2.04 metres per second and a 20-second finish with zero falls. A constant gull bias of 0.4 would drift the lateral offset by 0.4 metres per second; without correction, that would trigger a fall in about 2.9 seconds. The fight rate is tuned so a full lean corrects 1.2 metres per second, meaning a bias of 0.4 requires only a cob.x of 0.27 to stay balanced. A gust impulse adds 0.8 to lateral offset instantly, but the bot can recover over half a second before the 1.15 metre fall threshold is reached.\n\nThe paddle bot will drive forward at 5.1 metres per second, steering toward each gate in a 30-metre ring with a 4-metre acceptance radius. The gates are spaced about 15.7 metres apart, so the full lap should take roughly 38 seconds and earn a time bonus. Heading is corrected by yawing toward the next gate using atan2, then adjusted by cob.x scaled by 1.2 per step.\n\nThe paddle bot drives forward at 5.1 metres per second, scaling by 1.3 when paddling. Its desired lateral correction is clamped to ±1 and its desired vertical speed is fixed at 0.75. Feet alternate every 25 steps for a bonus, though this is not required to pass.\n\nIn the garden activity, the tray measures one metre across and the hole has a radius of 0.12 metres. Gravity is set to nine and tilt to twelve degrees. At full cob, the marble accelerates at roughly 1.7 metres per second squared and crosses the tray in about one second. Friction of 1.5 metres per second squared brings it to rest, while sink speed is 1.2; damping must be added so marbles settle cleanly inside the hole rather than overshooting.\n\nThe garden physics now include exponential friction on velocity and a sink threshold when the marble nears the hole. The bot steers toward the hole with clamped desired tilt, easing to zero as it approaches so the marble can settle cleanly. Five test trays are planned: one straight marble, one diagonal, two marbles in the same hole, one around a ridge, and two marbles in separate holes. Each tray runs for sixteen seconds or until the marbles sink, capped at ninety seconds total; any time left is added as a bonus.\n\nThe ridge tray will keep the proportional bot simple. Morning reach targets are six at radius 0.5, held for thirty steps within 0.16. A feedforward bot should sit on the target; perfect gain without delay is very tight. A human with 250 ms delay can still hold because the target is static and the hold term keeps them there once they arrive. Stick jitter is 0.04 and cob sway noise amplitude is 3.5. At equilibrium that noise acts as acceleration, so position jitter scales with acceleration divided by damping.\n\nThe morning reach target holds must be continuous, not just average. A human who settles at seventy percent of the ring radius and never holds for half a second will score zero on that target. The combined score formula is 0.45A + 0.3B + 0.25C, so a zero B value leaves only about five hundred seventy points from A and C. That is barely above the five hundred fifty threshold.\n\nThe slow noise correlation means humans can hold the morning reach targets for half a second once settled, so the threshold is achievable. The one-foot instability test uses effective gain 4.8 and a centre hold with a generous 0.3 ring radius; the human score should stay high.\n\nA three-second question phase will appear at the start of day one without affecting the score, keeping the total duration within specification. Day one lines will be emitted as flow.line changes for the page to display; the simulation only sets line identifiers. I will write a model test after the wobble is wired, and add auto and assist settings to the end of GAME_SETTINGS.\n\nAssist will be a numeric page toggle rather than a dev slider, since it is not listed in the plan’s numeric game settings. It will be added as a plain string setting with values 'off', 'director', and 'attract', and will not appear in TUNABLES.\n\nThe auto setting will use 'now' so the page can change it live. The director mode must be set before seed() because seed() preserves settings. Multiple defineSettings calls merge automatically, so I will update settings.ts directly.\n\nFiles will be written in parallel batches using the Write tool.\n\nFor the pose activity, the guide position equals seat multiplied by progress. The bot seeks this guide. If it stays within 0.18 of the guide for at least 1.5 seconds, progress advances; otherwise it resets. Progress caps at one after ten seconds.\n\nPose hold scoring counts a sample only when both the ring and foot conditions are satisfied.\n\nPose hold scoring will weight sync and foot stability at 0.4 and 0.6, bridge balance at 0.3 and 0.7, and chair balance by shared feet. Heron ring entries count only when the standing foot is held; otherwise that step is a miss without pausing the timer. Sway noise scales with stick.noiseScale.\n\nInstability scales with stick.instabilityScale. Chair sway noise climbs from 0.4 to 1.2 as the hold progresses. The napkin gull phase disturbs lateral position by 0.8 for two seconds at the halfway point. The lighthouse pose overrides the ring radius to 0.14 and sets sway to 1.2. Pose definitions will live in data/poses.ts.\n\nPose definitions now include a feet field and a gull flag. Nothing uses no feet and noise 0.5. Heron uses left feet. Bridge uses both feet with weights 0.3 and 0.7. Chair uses both feet with weights 0.7 and 0.3. The second weight is foot share, not sync. Napkin sits at (0, 0.5) with gull enabled. Comma uses two seats at (±0.5, 0) for eight seconds each. Archer uses instability 1.2 and seat (0.35, 0.35). During the pose activity the bot tracks the guide position during entry and holds the current seat during the hold.\n\nBreath poses require both feet, while Heron requires only the standing foot. A perfect Heron score would be 700 points, which falls short of the 750 target. The bot may need to breathe during Heron to raise sync and reach the threshold.\n\nHolding both feet counts as breathing, so the Heron pose can be completed without dropping sync. The chair pose requires both feet held throughout, which prevents breathing and scores through foot-share instead of sync.\n\nThe one-foot morning pose counts the named foot as held even if both feet are down, and breathing is optional. The score uses foot-share inside rather than sync.\n\nThe seek() function will accept an instability scale parameter so the morning one-foot pose can use 1.6 while holding the target at zero. The pose bot will read act.phase, act.enter, act.pose, and the pose data to drive its behaviour. Each activity's bot function will close over its own data.\n\nThe director mode will use a human-like bot so the scripted playthrough can finish every activity without needing high scores. The attract mode will instead use a perfect bot with reduced noise scale to cheat slightly. Idle director also reaches credits through time boxes.\n\nThe director bot must finish line, paddle, and garden without getting stuck. Time boxes guarantee completion even with low scores. Every seed visits the same fixed programme, and flow.seen will include all activities. The simulation will spawn twenty hidden pebbles and twelve plaques on the island; walking near them collects them, though the Go action does not require walking.\n\nOutfits will offer three pier looks: mint, sand, and sea. They are character-select options rather than expert unlocks.\n\nThe cairn will stack pebbles visually, with lean angle driven by age. The simulation will manage a cairn component tracking count and yaw.\n\nPlaques will creep upward slightly each day.\n\nIsland content will live in island.ts as components. The simulation emits line identifiers while the page supplies the text. I will generate at least two hundred short lines, each under two sentences, and ensure Plumb has no exclamation marks. Voice audio will use eighty sixteen-syllable samples across five speakers, defined in a loop at module initialization. Songs will include lobby, stillness, water, tilt, dawn, and stamp; the garden activity can reuse lobby or a rake-specific track.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c1e353d9-d1fd-4a84-9deb-22be09373469-55","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"export function render|export function songTiming|export async function renderSong","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/audio","glob":"*.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2901,"output_tokens":7366,"cache_read_input_tokens":124544,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"a8c85534-60ec-43e5-9976-63198c0c6c84"}
