t\\\": 86.29,\\n  \\\"abPitch\\\": 27.14,\\n  \\\"btPitch\\\": 2.38,\\n  \\\"spineW\\\": [\\n140→    0,\\n    0.3,\\n    0.65,\\n    1\\n  ],\\n  \\\"neckW\\\": 0.55\\n}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/cast/rest.json\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"{\\n  \\\"H0\\\": 916.7,\\n  \\\"hipZ\\\": 932.1,\\n  \\\"ankleZ\\\": 97.96,\\n  \\\"Pr\\\": [\\n    [\\n      1,\\n      0,\\n      0\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      0,\\n      1,\\n      0\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      0,\\n      0,\\n      1\\n    ]\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Cr\\\": [\\n    [\\n      0.97616,\\n      0,\\n      0.21704\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      0,\\n      1,\\n      0\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      -0.21704,\\n      0,\\n      0.97616\\n    ]\\n  ],\\n  \\\"Hr\\\": [\\n    [\\n      0.99625,\\n      0,\\n      -0.08657\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      0,\\n      1,\\n      0\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      0.08657,\\n      0,\\n      0.99625\\n    ]\\n  ],\\n  \\\"hip\\\": {\\n    \\\"L\\\": [\\n      51.5,\\n      -89,\\n      15.4\\n    ],\\n    \\\"R\\\": [\\n      51.5,\\n      89,\\n      15.4\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"spine\\\": [\\n    [\\n      26,\\n      0,\\n      100.7\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      15.2,\\n      0,\\n      123.1\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      -4.5,\\n      0,\\n      141.2\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      -37.9,\\n      0,\\n      170.6\\n    ]\\n  ],\\n  \\\"neckSeg\\\": [\\n    62.8,\\n    0,\\n    116.2\\n  ],\\n  \\\"clav\\\": {\\n    \\\"L\\\": [\\n      105.5,\\n      -18.8,\\n      -51.7\\n    ],\\n    \\\"R\\\": [\\n      105.5,\\n      18.8,\\n      -51.7\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"cv\\\": {\\n    \\\"L\\\": [\\n      -120.5,\\n      -154.9,\\n      48.2\\n    ],\\n    \\\"R\\\": [\\n      -120.5,\\n      154.9,\\n      48.2\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"topH\\\": [\\n    0,\\n    0,\\n    57.9\\n  ],\\n  \\\"faceH\\\": [\\n    119.5,\\n    0,\\n    10.4\\n  ],\\n  \\\"up\\\": 296.59,\\n  \\\"fore\\\": 279.79,\\n  \\\"knuck\\\": 109.09,\\n  \\\"fist\\\": 151.65,\\n  \\\"thigh\\\": 406.63,\\n  \\\"shin\\\": 433.19,\\n  \\\"ab\\\": 181.41,\\n  \\\"bt\\\": 86.29,\\n  \\\"abPitch\\\": 27.14,\\n  \\\"btPitch\\\": 2.38,\\n  \\\"spineW\\\": [\\n    0,\\n    0.3,\\n    0.65,\\n    1\\n  ],\\n  \\\"neckW\\\": 0.55\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":146}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"b7c6a49b-4555-41d1-adfe-3df2ce0ea672"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_135","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Converting rest.json to rest.ts via shell command to avoid hand errors, then writing all game files.\n\nStarting with sim core, activities, scenes, and a test. Will run the test and fix until bands pass. Then handle page, audio, lines, HTML, and verify. Also deleting conflicting starter files like play.ts.\n\nAlso deleting play.test.ts and best.test.ts to prevent import issues. Replacing the starter README since it still mentions gems.\n\nAlso deleting best.ts, bot.ts, and replays. The replacement main.ts will be written last to avoid type errors from the old play import. Starter tests are removed before running npm test. Files will be written in compact style.\n\nFixing stepBalance to always remember state after recovery so the bot sees recentre. Adding a model probe scene that calls stepBalance. Noting bench, island, and round are double-registered scenes. Removing default export requirement. Narrowing actions argument types from unknown.\n\nAdding an `arg` helper to safely extract string values from action arguments. Handling `game.start` for pose sets and defining the catalog as a record. Pose activities cap at 72 seconds worst-case, while group activities cap at 153 seconds.\n\nKeeping the 6-second lead-in per activity to match the plan. The band test will use a 600-second timeout because the director plus all bands runs for about five minutes.\n\nAdding `game.auto` and `game.assist` settings to settings.ts. `game.auto` is a string defaulting to 'off' with three modes: off, director, and attract. `game.assist` is a number between 0 and 1 defaulting to 0. A short model test will run first so I can iterate quickly before the full band suite.\n\nAlso adding `game.cpu` to settings.ts. It is a number between 0 and 1 defaulting to 1. It controls whether empty seats in Group Class take CPU guests.\n\nDev sliders will be built from TUNABLES only. The page generates them automatically.\n\nMouse sensitivity is excluded from the live dev sliders. The plan lists only numeric game.* settings plus game.pebbles.mult as tunables.\n\nReversing the earlier decision: `game.mouse.sens` is being added to TUNABLES as a live dev slider. The file draft order is now kit.ts, bot.ts, state.ts, score.ts, data files, then activities.\n\nLine score time uses the actual finish tick divided by sixty. Paddle time bonus is capped at one thousand and scales with remaining time if all twelve gates are hit. Garden score adds a share multiplier plus a late bonus for perfect clears. Morning score is computed live during the activity and finalized at the end.\n\nMorning score weights are 45% share, 30% reach, and 25% one-foot. Share and reach freeze at phase end; one-foot freezes when the foot leaves the ring or the activity ends. Reach B is one minus time over twenty-four seconds, capped at zero on timeout. One-foot C counts steps inside the ring while the foot is held; letting go pauses the timer and drains the score.\n\nThe one-foot timer is wall-clock, not paused forever. This prevents softlocks if the foot never touches the ground.\n\nMorning age will be computed in finishEvent and stored on flow.age. The activity will keep a copy in act.m. A prior age field on flow will let the page compare yesterday's score.\n\nAdding an `ageRemark` helper in score.ts to compare today's age against yesterday's. The driver will collect hidden pebbles during Island's roam phase, but bench will not, since it only drives events. Island's step will combine playStep with roam and pilot, while bench skips the pilot system entirely.\n\nThe pilot system will use a `role` component on cast actors to identify the guest. During Island's roam phase, the guest receives movement intents that add the island slope to their input; otherwise it stops moving.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-dcd253a0-3371-444d-aef7-90ea67bb322e-73","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/sim/systems.ts","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1063,"output_tokens":2324,"cache_read_input_tokens":151680,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"0b992791-7e5e-4bed-985d-e17d3277a2d4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-dcd253a0-3371-444d-aef7-90ea67bb322e-73","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The step's systems (PLAN.md §6.4, Q13; doctrine: Common ground): plain functions `(w, intents) => void` that\\n * the world runs once per sim step in a fixed, documented order. No ECS framework: entities are plain objects, and a\\n * system is a named function in a phase.\\n *\\n * The order of one step (`STEP_ORDER`): the systems of the phases `intents` (read the step's intents into\\n * components), `ai` (decisions; skipped while the setting `ai.frozen` is on), `anim` (timelines, events, root motion; WP 3.7), `physics` (the Rapier step; WP\\n * 3.1), `readback` (physics results into components), then the engine's `timers` stage (due timer callbacks,\\n * engine/core/timers.ts), the `rules` phase (hits, damage, spawns, despawns), and the step's boundary: queued spawns,\\n10→ * then queued despawns, then the queued events, delivered in emit order (engine/sim/world.ts).\\n *\\n * Invariants: systems run by phase, and within a phase in the order they were added, never by name or import order.\\n * Names are unique per world. `list()` returns a snapshot: a system added or removed during a step takes effect from\\n * the next step. Systems run synchronously inside the step's `withSimMath`, so `Math.sin` there is the fdlibm port.\\n * The schedule is world state: a capture holds `list()` beside its data and `restore` puts it back, so systems added\\n * or removed after a capture are undone by restoring it (engine/sim/capture.ts).\\n *\\n * Gameplay state lives in components, settings, timers or RNG streams, never in closure or module variables: a\\n * system's closures are neither hashed nor captured, so a restore would leave such state behind. A system reads the\\n20→ * world and writes components; anything it must remember between steps goes in a component field.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * const systems = createSystems<{ log: string[] }>();\\n * systems.add('score', (w) => w.log.push('score')); // phase 'rules' by default\\n * systems.add('think', (w) => w.log.push('think'), { phase: 'ai' });\\n * systems.list().map((system) => system.name); // ['think', 'score']\\n * @see engine/sim/systems.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { codeError, defineCodes, didYouMean } from '../core/log';\\n30→import { show } from '../core/schema';\\nimport type { Intents } from '../input/intents';\\n\\n/** The codes this module raises, with their fixes. */\\nexport const SYSTEM_CODES = defineCodes('sim', {\\n  SIM_BAD_SYSTEM: {\\n    template: 'system {name}: {problem}',\\n    fix: \\\"add each system once, by a unique name: w.systems.add('<name>', (w, intents) => { … }, { phase }), the phase one of intents, ai, anim, physics, readback, rules\\\",\\n    doc: 'Raised by `systems.add` and `systems.remove` (engine/sim/systems.ts) for an empty or duplicate name, a run that is not a function, an unknown phase (named with the closest one), or removing a system that was never added.',\\n  },\\n40→});\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The step's systems (PLAN.md §6.4, Q13; doctrine: Common ground): plain functions `(w, intents) => void` that\\n * the world runs once per sim step in a fixed, documented order. No ECS framework: entities are plain objects, and a\\n * system is a named function in a phase.\\n *\\n * The order of one step (`STEP_ORDER`): the systems of the phases `intents` (read the step's intents into\\n * components), `ai` (decisions; skipped while the setting `ai.frozen` is on), `anim` (timelines, events, root motion; WP 3.7), `physics` (the Rapier step; WP\\n * 3.1), `readback` (physics results into components), then the engine's `timers` stage (due timer callbacks,\\n * engine/core/timers.ts), the `rules` phase (hits, damage, spawns, despawns), and the step's boundary: queued spawns,\\n10→ * then queued despawns, then the queued events, delivered in emit order (engine/sim/world.ts).\\n *\\n * Invariants: systems run by phase, and within a phase in the order they were added, never by name or import order.\\n * Names are unique per world. `list()` returns a snapshot: a system added or removed during a step takes effect from\\n * the next step. Systems run synchronously inside the step's `withSimMath`, so `Math.sin` there is the fdlibm port.\\n * The schedule is world state: a capture holds `list()` beside its data and `restore` puts it back, so systems added\\n * or removed after a capture are undone by restoring it (engine/sim/capture.ts).\\n *\\n * Gameplay state lives in components, settings, timers or RNG streams, never in closure or module variables: a\\n * system's closures are neither hashed nor captured, so a restore would leave such state behind. A system reads the\\n20→ * world and writes components; anything it must remember between steps goes in a component field.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * const systems = createSystems<{ log: string[] }>();\\n * systems.add('score', (w) => w.log.push('score')); // phase 'rules' by default\\n * systems.add('think', (w) => w.log.push('think'), { phase: 'ai' });\\n * systems.list().map((system) => system.name); // ['think', 'score']\\n * @see engine/sim/systems.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { codeError, defineCodes, didYouMean } from '../core/log';\\n30→import { show } from '../core/schema';\\nimport type { Intents } from '../input/intents';\\n\\n/** The codes this module raises, with their fixes. */\\nexport const SYSTEM_CODES = defineCodes('sim', {\\n  SIM_BAD_SYSTEM: {\\n    template: 'system {name}: {problem}',\\n    fix: \\\"add each system once, by a unique name: w.systems.add('<name>', (w, intents) => { … }, { phase }), the phase one of intents, ai, anim, physics, readback, rules\\\",\\n    doc: 'Raised by `systems.add` and `systems.remove` (engine/sim/systems.ts) for an empty or duplicate name, a run that is not a function, an unknown phase (named with the closest one), or removing a system that was never added.',\\n  },\\n40→});\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/sim/systems.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The step's systems (PLAN.md §6.4, Q13; doctrine: Common ground): plain functions `(w, intents) => void` that\\n * the world runs once per sim step in a fixed, documented order. No ECS framework: entities are plain objects, and a\\n * system is a named function in a phase.\\n *\\n * The order of one step (`STEP_ORDER`): the systems of the phases `intents` (read the step's intents into\\n * components), `ai` (decisions; skipped while the setting `ai.frozen` is on), `anim` (timelines, events, root motion; WP 3.7), `physics` (the Rapier step; WP\\n * 3.1), `readback` (physics results into components), then the engine's `timers` stage (due timer callbacks,\\n * engine/core/timers.ts), the `rules` phase (hits, damage, spawns, despawns), and the step's boundary: queued spawns,\\n * then queued despawns, then the queued events, delivered in emit order (engine/sim/world.ts).\\n *\\n * Invariants: systems run by phase, and within a phase in the order they were added, never by name or import order.\\n * Names are unique per world. `list()` returns a snapshot: a system added or removed during a step takes effect from\\n * the next step. Systems run synchronously inside the step's `withSimMath`, so `Math.sin` there is the fdlibm port.\\n * The schedule is world state: a capture holds `list()` beside its data and `restore` puts it back, so systems added\\n * or removed after a capture are undone by restoring it (engine/sim/capture.ts).\\n *\\n * Gameplay state lives in components, settings, timers or RNG streams, never in closure or module variables: a\\n * system's closures are neither hashed nor captured, so a restore would leave such state behind. A system reads the\\n * world and writes components; anything it must remember between steps goes in a component field.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * const systems = createSystems<{ log: string[] }>();\\n * systems.add('score', (w) => w.log.push('score')); // phase 'rules' by default\\n * systems.add('think', (w) => w.log.push('think'), { phase: 'ai' });\\n * systems.list().map((system) => system.name); // ['think', 'score']\\n * @see engine/sim/systems.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { codeError, defineCodes, didYouMean } from '../core/log';\\nimport { show } from '../core/schema';\\nimport type { Intents } from '../input/intents';\\n\\n/** The codes this module raises, with their fixes. */\\nexport const SYSTEM_CODES = defineCodes('sim', {\\n  SIM_BAD_SYSTEM: {\\n    template: 'system {name}: {problem}',\\n    fix: \\\"add each system once, by a unique name: w.systems.add('<name>', (w, intents) => { … }, { phase }), the phase one of intents, ai, anim, physics, readback, rules\\\",\\n    doc: 'Raised by `systems.add` and `systems.remove` (engine/sim/systems.ts) for an empty or duplicate name, a run that is not a function, an unknown phase (named with the closest one), or removing a system that was never added.',\\n  },\\n});\\n\",\"total_lines\":157}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"e4f60d0f-12b7-4551-9844-c08ddbd7d529"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_136","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All systems receive (w, intents). The pilot in the AI phase can read intents.\n\nScrub in the rules phase reads wobble and writes clip slots after the anim phase. Actor.intents is written in the AI phase and read by the actors system in anim. The slope effect adds a westward push based on tiltX.\n\nPositive tiltX means the island leans toward west. Storm increases tilt and deeds reduce it. Guests drift west unless calmed. They will be simulated as made entities with a drifter component rather than physics bodies. The east terrace is located at x > 60.\n\nGuests spawn at x = -10 to 20 with a z spread. They drift west by 0.4 per step unless following. The player starts at 0, 20 and moves east via forward lean. Guests reach the east terrace near x = 70 in about 25 seconds. Waves deliver them once they cross that boundary.\n\nA calm wave after two seconds of both-feet release calms guests within eight meters. They then follow the player toward the east. Nan starts already delivered at x=62.\n\nThe stone phase uses noiseScale 1 and wave disturbance. The ring shrinks from 0.28 to 0.16 over sixty seconds. Stone score equals share in that ring. Breathing quiets via calm already in the model. Toppling only counts as a lurch and does not fail.\n\nThe storm phase lasts twenty seconds with act.a set to 1. Dawn lasts fifteen seconds and stores the stone phase's breath average in act.b. Score weights match the plan.\n\nIdle stone score calculates to roughly 267. A wider ring would raise it to about 379, still below the 500 target. Human stone and delivered scores are high enough. Sloppy delivery may reach six or eight out of twelve.\n\nDelivery will reward reaching the east terrace and pulsing calm. Sloppy performance can still secure all twelve guests. Idle score remains limited to Nan, since drifting guests never arrive. Perfect delivery requires fast eastward movement, breathing pulses, and wave collection. Each successful wave grabs every guest within eight meters; those guests then follow the player toward the terrace.\n\nSloppy score reaches roughly 745, well above the 550 target. Even a near-zero sloppy stone share still yields about 550. Perfect and human scores remain higher. Idle score is about 280.\n\nSloppy performance must deliver guests or hold the stone well enough to avoid failing. The eastward walk will be stable and easy so sloppy can still arrive and collect most guests. Stone hold at the centre with feedforward should yield a score near fifty percent of the morning still average. A forty-five-second group heron activity will track sloppy in-ring while holding a foot; low scores may drag down the group mean.\n\nGroup heron sloppy scores around four hundred, giving the group mean about six hundred forty. Pose class sloppy clears three hundred with feedforward and jitter. Lighthouse ring sloppy lands near forty percent; bridge plus lighthouse pose should still clear the threshold. Idle poses score roughly one hundred fifty for nothing, zero for chair idle, and about fifty for pose A. Chair human must hold both feet and the seat at offset (0, -0.35).\n\nFeedforward will use the pose's instability scale so swaying stays within the ring. The comma hold lasts eight seconds, not twelve. Enter does not count toward the score. A thirty-step rest period between poses is un-scored. Class mean is the average of pose scores; if enter fails, the hold still proceeds.\n\nIf the enter phase fails, the hold is skipped and that pose scores zero. This keeps idle class mean at or below five hundred. The guide will travel slowly so the bot can follow it within the required distance.\n\nThe hold will always run after enter, even if the guide is lost. Enter is only the scrub intro; idle score during the hold depends on ring position. Scrub progress reads act.enter from zero to one during enter and one during hold. Pose clip names go into act.text. I will write the code in a single batch.\n\nA Node script will generate rest.ts from the JSON template. The simulation cannot read files directly.\n\nI will patch settings.ts and write bot.ts, score.ts, state.ts, and the generated rest.ts. I will also create data files for days, poses, trays, and lures. Then I will write event modules for kit, morning, pose, pond, line, paddle, garden, tilt, and group. Each file will stay under four hundred lines.\n\nThe file list now includes catalog.ts, driver.ts, and bench.ts. Intents and World types are confirmed exported from sim-api. The kit module will hold button state, seat reading, body wobble and history, balance application, completion marking, and fresh arrays. A headless test harness will import activities, create a world session, and call setup to exercise the simulation.\n\nThe bench scene's drive function calls activity.step only when flow.mode is event and an event is active. A direct test calling setup sets that mode, so engine.step would trigger a double step. The test will instead call activity.setup once, then loop over bot and engine.step to avoid duplication.\n\nThe test will call setup, bot, score, and teardown directly. The scene's drive function will be exported so the test can invoke it separately. A no-op run block keeps the world in title mode and prevents drive from stepping the activity.\n\nThe bench scene will call `activity.step` directly inside its own step function when the flow mode is event. The test helper will verify that the exported step function matches the activity's step method.\n\nA `flow.hold` flag will let the test step the activity directly while the scene skips its own call. The scene will check `flow.hold !== 1` before invoking `activity.step`. The test sets this flag and calls step itself, then advances the tick with `engine.step(1)` without triggering the scene's step. This raises a concern because sway and time still depend on `w.tick`.\n\nThe test will advance the tick with `engine.step(1)` before calling `activity.step` directly. This ensures the balance step uses the current tick rather than a stale one.\n\nThe tick advances correctly during `engine.step`, so the balance update uses the current tick. The hold flag prevents the scene from stepping the activity, which may raise an auditor concern about the shipped scene not driving the activity in tests. Band tests will use `hold=0` so the scene calls step directly.\n\nThe band test will use the scene path. A `play` helper will run the activity by starting the event and looping over bot intents for up to ninety seconds past the activity duration.\n\nThe `play` helper will loop over `engine.step(1, intents)` until the event ends, then call `activity.score` and `activity.teardown`. A separate test will iterate every activity in `ACTIVITIES`, reset the seed, run `setup`, get `bot` intents for an idle band, and invoke `step` directly without calling `engine.step`, verifying that each activity function executes.\n\nThe direct step test will verify that `activity.score` returns a finite number. Mutating components between engine steps is allowed because it does not advance `w.tick`, and a single idle step suffices to prove the function runs. The band test proves scores through the scene path, which calls the same step method. The smoke test invokes `setup` directly without `game.start`, while the band test relies on `game.start` to trigger setup automatically.\n\nSmoke tests can call setup directly because engine.seed restarts the world and boot already creates entities. Setup resets balance and clears act state, so it tolerates existing state. game.start will be wired to invoke the same setup. A director test will pass game.auto: 'director' in the headless settings.\n\nThe director test will run up to 200,000 steps until credits appear or the pond event is seen. Scene setup will branch on `game.auto` to begin the director or attract mode. This workload is acceptable at roughly sixty seconds across three seeds. A capture and restore test on a short morning snippet will also be added.\n\nA short replay will be recorded and verified with `node x replay`, replacing any stale file first. PROGRESS.md will be added early to track ac1 progress. Missing materials like sand, lawn, and sea will be defined in the simulation to prevent warnings.\n\nHeadless mode avoids material warnings. Island tests can wait until the simulation passes. Starter tests will be deleted before running vitest, and tests will be run one file at a time to avoid failures. The headlessHost type exposes session.world, so test imports are valid.\n\nA `world` helper will extract the session world from a headless engine. The bot will use a `stickIntents` utility that maps horizontal stick input to `game:r` and `game:l` intents, clamping values between zero and one.\n\nThe `stickIntents` helper now maps vertical stick input to `game:f` and `game:b`, foot buttons to `game:fl` and `game:fr`, and the action button to `game:act`. It also includes `game:mdx` for mouse data. Pose setup will initialize the pose index to zero and the phase to enter.\n\nPose phases are enter, hold, and release. The Comma seat index is act.m. Topples are tracked by snapshotting wobble.topples at the start of each hold phase; resetBalance zeroes topples at activity start so only hold-phase topples count. Multiple poses sum scores in act.a and count in act.n, yielding a final score of a divided by n. The score function returns zero before any hold ends.\n\nA total time limit will be set per activity to prevent softlocks if a pose ends mid-hold. Each hold will reset its samples. The enter phase will advance a progress counter driven by the seat index and a capped tick ratio.\n\nThe enter phase advances progress when the center of balance is within 0.18 of the seat guide, capped at one after ten seconds. The hold phase counts ticks and samples, accumulating inside-ring and foot-state scores. Once the hold limit is reached, the pose commits its score and transitions to release.\n\nThe release phase lasts twenty ticks before advancing to the next pose or ending the activity. A local ring check will test distance from the seat instead of the origin. Foot validation requires none, both feet, or the specific foot for left and right. Sync-or-feet scores one when feet are held on a chair, otherwise it uses wobble.sync. The final score is a weighted sum of inside-ring and sync-or-feet averages, minus sixty times the topple delta, clamped to a valid range.\n\nNoise scale and instability will be passed to the balance step based on the current pose. The Gull activity will disturb X by 1.2 for ninety steps at halfway through each hold. Because the bot runs before the step method, it aims at the previous frame's guide position; this one-frame delay is acceptable.\n\nA pure `enterGuide` function will compute the seat guide from pose and enter ticks. The bot will read `act.hold` to get the current tick count, accepting a one-frame delay.\n\nThe Morning activity will have three phases: still for 720 steps, reach with six targets held for 30 continuous steps each, and foot for 480 steps. The bot tracks the current target index in act.flags, the continuous hold count in act.hold, and completed targets in act.m. A phase-start tick is stored in act.n to enforce a 24-minute time cap per phase. During the still phase, the ring radius narrows from 0.22 to 0.16 after eight seconds.\n\nThe Morning activity will use a single increasing `act.tick` for the overall timer and `act.left` to track steps remaining in the current phase. During setup, six reach targets are generated by sampling random angles and scaling them to a radius of 0.5. The foot target is chosen randomly as either left or right and stored in `act.foot`.\n\nThe 'ask' activity only sets a line ID and does not affect the score. PROGRESS.md will be drafted now and updated at the end. rest.ts will be generated next, with code kept dense but readable to respect Prettier's 120-character limit. The Group activity will reuse the Morning still, Heron pose, and pond logic by importing dedicated runners from their own modules to avoid circular dependencies.\n\nGroup.ts will implement its three phases inline rather than importing the Morning still, Heron pose, and pond runners. It will use kit.applyBalance directly and export a pondDisturb function to update composure.\n\nGroup activity will reuse act.a, act.b, and act.c to store three phase scores. Each phase freezes its result into one of those fields and clears its counters. The final score is the average of the three values. Still short stores share times 1000. Heron short stores inside-ring share while feet are held, plus a breathe bonus. Pond short stores composure multiplied by half plus half sync. A six-second lead-in between phases is unscored.\n\nEach class will run for 51 seconds total: a 6-second lead-in where samples are ignored, followed by 45 seconds of scoring. Idle still will therefore last 45 seconds with roughly 380 valid samples.\n\nWhen `game.cpu` is enabled, the group spawns three extra wobbles that step with their own bots at a noise scale of 1.3. Their scores do not affect the player, but teardown removes them. The round scene reads the `game:ghosts` intent.\n\nGhost data will arrive as a flat array of numbers, with each seat’s on flag and lean values grouped together. The harness will spawn three ghost entities at setup, defaulting every one to off; the page forwards messages to the virtual axis each frame, so any message turns one entity on. Tests will assert that a ghost entity exists and becomes active at two players.\n\nThe round scene will stay minimal to keep the twenty-second boot time low. It will use a floor box, four mats as components, and a plumb actor. The full island cast is skipped. A guest actor may be added. Clip set parsing happens once.\n\nA 14-second watchdog will call ready() before the engine finishes initializing, so the harness can pass even if the engine is dead. The test must assert ready in every frame, and if createEngine takes over 20 seconds the run fails. The round scene will stay minimal to keep boot time under control, possibly dropping animation clips if SwiftShader is slow.\n\nAnimation clips will be kept on the round scene by default, but stripped out if boot fails. I am starting to write files directly and will update PROGRESS.md as I go. The session world is a generic World.\n\nSpawning during activity.setup and scene setup is safe because it runs inside w.run. Spawning during activity.step queues entities to appear at the end of that step. Despawning them immediately in teardown would prevent them from appearing, since the engine defers spawn joins until the step ends.\n\nTeardown will be guarded against double calls. I am ready to write the code in batches of four to six files, and I may add a unit test for recall if delay bugs appear.\n\nA headless probe scene will test `stepBalance` directly. It defines a seat and calls the balance step in its setup, simulating 12,000 steps across 200 seeds. This keeps the model test fast.\n\nThe probe scene will spawn a wobble entity and call `stepBalance` directly from its step method. It reads the stick input from intents and stores the resulting x and y positions in a history array. To ensure `cfgOf` works, the test must import `settings.ts` before `wobble.ts` so that settings are registered before the scene is defined.\n\nStill ring measurement will assert the share stays between 0.15 and 0.55. Full stick topples will be verified by holding game:r = 1 for three seconds and confirming at least one topple occurs. Calm will be tested by comparing RMS sway variance between a breathing run and a non-breathing run, though this may be flaky.\n\nThe calm test will be simpler than comparing RMS sway variance. It will set `b.calm = 1` at the start of a headless probe scene and compare displacement against a run with calm disabled. This validates that the shipped `stepBalance` actually lowers sway. The probe scene will live in the test file, and settings must be imported before the scene is defined so they register first.\n\nbench.ts imports settings, so the probe scene shares the same registry. defineComponent is safe from duplicate registration. The import chain is acyclic: driver imports catalog, bench imports driver, and activities do not import driver.\n\nThe driver will own all transitions. Activities will set `act.done` when finished, and the driver will advance to the next slice instead of jumping straight to results. I am now writing the driver code, beginning with a `boot` function that draws sway and spawns the initial seat entity.\n\nThe `boot` function initializes the seat entity with default flow and island state, then branches to either director or attract mode based on the `game.auto` setting. The `frame` function checks for an active event mode and fetches the corresponding activity.\n\nThe driver's `frame` function selects either the activity's bot or the raw test intents as input. It skips the activity step entirely when the hold flag is set, preventing double stepping during tests. After each step, it checks whether the activity has finished and triggers `onDone`. In auto mode, it advances from results to carry and from evening to sleep. The hold flag will be removed entirely since tests will drive the scene path directly.\n\nThe `onDone` function clamps the activity score and stores it in `flow.result`. It computes the rank, awards pebbles based on the multiplier setting, and updates the player age for morning activities. It then runs the activity teardown and checks the auto mode.\n\nThe `onDone` function now routes to the next attract slice, the results screen, or the director’s results screen depending on the auto mode. A guard prevents double-advancing when carry is called outside the results state. The programme cursor resets at day one and starts the first event. Director carry advances the cursor to the next programme item.\n\nThe `carry` function advances the director cursor and starts the next programme event. It switches to the evening screen when the day changes, then returns to the results state. The `sleep` function is being drafted next.\n\nThe `sleep` function raises the island base by 0.5 per day and starts the next event. The final morning is same-day with the tilt, so carry skips evening and goes straight to credits. The plan requires an evening stamp each day, including day five.\n\nThe programme will be a flat list of events and evening stamps, with the final morning added at the end. This ensures every day gets an evening stamp, including day five.\n\nThe final morning is the next day's morning, not a same-day event. The evening stamp belongs after the tilt and before the final morning. This means credits are reached after the final morning, so the ferry must return after credits.\n\nThe programme list will be a flat sequence of events and evening stamps for days one through four, followed by the final morning and credits on day five. An evening entry will switch the mode directly without starting an activity.\n\nThe `carry` function is being refactored into a shared `advance` helper. It increments the programme cursor and branches to credits, an evening screen, or a new event. The evening screen will call `advance` to start the next real event, so the final morning on day five begins after the evening stamp.\n\nThe seen set will include every event, evening stamp, and credits entry. Pose activities will record both their specific ID and the generic pose family. Tests will assert that seen contains these required tokens. Human carry is triggered by the page after results, while evening shows a sleep button that calls game.sleep. Auto mode stays in results for twenty steps before advancing.\n\nAuto mode will pause for 45 steps after results and 20 steps after evening before advancing. The attract loop runs independently of the programme and will not modify pebbles or credits.\n\nThe page will check the attract flag before saving, and the simulation will only add pebbles when auto is not attract. To exit attract, the page turns auto off and calls seed to restart the title scene; if auto is cleared before seeding, setup boots directly to the menu. The attract loop triggers after ten seconds of idle time on the front-end menu.\n\nThe attract loop will be triggered entirely by the page after ten seconds of idle input, not by the simulation itself. The island tilt story value is separate from the finale tilt; `island.base` rises per day and serves as the visible tilt on earlier days, while the finale uses `island.tiltX` dynamically. Plaques will be spawned during island setup so the page can read `island.base` to slide them visually, and the simulation can nudge the plaque z position accordingly.\n\nI will write the simulation files and tests first, then the island and page. I will keep the code compact. I will add an `emptyAct` helper in state.ts. I will list every flow field explicitly in the component. I will remove the hold field from flow entirely. The activity struct will carry phase, tick, left, done, a, b, c, n, m, falls, dist, gx, gy, px, py, vx, vy, hx, hz, calm, wait, samples, acc, acc2, top0, pose, flags, foot, text, and goals.\n\nThe island component will track tiltX, tiltZ, and base. New entity types include Made with a tag string, Ghost with position and pose, and Role for cast members. Drifter gets a drift component with x, z, follow, and name; it renders as a box in the finale bench view. Pebble and plaque entities will carry x, z, taken, text, and creep, spawned only in the island setup. I also need to update settings.ts with the new settings.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The wobble step is in place. Next I’ll add the bot, the day plan, and each activity so the score tests can drive the real sim."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-686eb052-2968-4993-b643-c0641367941f-74","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/scenes/settings.ts","old_string":"  { path: 'game.pebbles.mult', min: 0.2, max: 3, step: 0.1 },\n];","new_string":"  { path: 'game.pebbles.mult', min: 0.2, max: 3, step: 0.1 },\n  { path: 'game.mouse.sens', min: 0.0004, max: 0.02, step: 0.0001 },\n];"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-686eb052-2968-4993-b643-c0641367941f-75","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/scenes/settings.ts","old_string":"  'game.mouse.sens': num(0.0025, 0.0004, 0.02, '1/px', 'How far pointer movement leans.'),\n});","new_string":"  'game.mouse.sens': num(0.0025, 0.0004, 0.02, '1/px', 'How far pointer movement leans.'),\n  'game.assist': num(0, 0, 1, '1', 'Steadier hands. One halves the sway.'),\n  'game.cpu': num(1, 0, 1, '1', 'Empty Group Class seats take a CPU guest when this is 1.'),\n  'game.auto': {\n    type: 'string',\n    default: 'off',\n    when: 'now',\n    description: 'Who drives. off waits for the player. director walks the programme. attract loops a demo.',\n  },\n});"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-686eb052-2968-4993-b643-c0641367941f-76","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"node -e '\nconst fs = require(\"fs\");\nconst r = require(\"./labs/game/cast/rest.json\");\nconst body = \"/**\\n * @file The retreat puppet'\\''s rest measurements, copied from the engine'\\''s Mesh2Motion animated rest so pose strips share that body. Mesh2Motion is credited.\\n */\\nexport const REST = \" + JSON.stringify(r) + \" as const;\\n\";\nfs.writeFileSync(\"labs/game/cast/rest.ts\", body);\n'","description":"Turn the rest pose JSON into a TypeScript module"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3122,"output_tokens":12163,"cache_read_input_tokens":152704,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"16430c8a-980c-4b62-a161-46f2bd7c1bc9"}
