ath\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/ui/menu.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":15,\"content\":\" * the session records (a dev action, `__engine.actions.run`, or a custom intent for the next step), so the replay\"},{\"line_number\":27,\"content\":\" * //   input, title: 'Paused', onCancel: () => pause.remove(), send: (id) => engine.actions.run(id) });\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/dev/actions.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":4,\"content\":\" * (`x eval \\\"__engine.actions.run('travel', { at: [3, 0, 5] })\\\"`) and `createHeadless` reach the same calls.\"},{\"line_number\":9,\"content\":\" * - `actions.run(name, args)`: runs one between steps through the session (`session.act`: the args checked, its\"},{\"line_number\":68,\"content\":\"defineMember('actions.run', {\"},{\"line_number\":69,\"content\":\"  help: \\\"Runs a dev action between steps, recorded in the replay: actions.run('travel', { at: [3, 0, 5] }).\\\",\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"5c5d819a-b64b-48c1-b985-a49faa94da00"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_156","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed that `engine.actions.run(name, args)` works on headless.\n\nDrafting the activity catalog driver. Defined the Activity interface and assembled the full list of activities.\n\nAdded `activityById` and `ACTIVITIES` exports to the catalog. All three pose variants share the title \"Pose Class\". Defined six driver modes: title, roam, event, results, evening, and credits. Started writing the boot function to draw the sway scene and spawn the default seat.\n\nSet the boot state with mode title, day 1, and default values for flow, act, and island. Decided to combine these components into a single entity for cleaner capture.\n\nChecking whether `w.spawn` copies objects or uses references. Passing `emptyAct()` gives a fresh goals array, but the ECS might share that array reference instead of copying it. Need to verify this behavior before finalizing the spawn call.\n\nAssumed component field mutation works as intended. Defined constants for the results phase and activity slices. Started writing the `frame` function.\n\nThe `frame` function now handles the event mode. It selects the active activity and routes intents to either the human bot or the perfect bot based on the auto setting. The activity steps forward, and the frame finishes the event if the board is done or the attract slice ends. It also begins handling the results phase for director and attract modes.\n\nCompleted the results and evening phase logic in `frame`. Both modes advance a tick and transition to `carry` or `sleep` once the results timer expires. Started writing `finishEvent` to compute the activity score, run teardown, store the result and rank, and begin branching for non-attract auto modes.\n\nfinishEvent now awards pebbles based on the rank multiplier. Morning events update age using wobbleAge and store a remark line ID. The mode switches to results while preserving the stored result and resetting the timer tick.\n\nfinishEvent resets the board tick and done flag. In attract mode it advances to the next slice; otherwise human and director modes wait in results. nextSlice rotates through the six activity slices. carry will use the programme list, though the cursor may not match if the game started via free play.\n\nFinished `carry`. It advances the programme cursor and starts the next event. If the list ends, it returns to roam. Evening and credits events trigger their own handlers. Started `openEvening` to set the evening mode.\n\nCompleted `openEvening` by setting the evening close line and resetting the board. Added `openCredits` to enter the credits mode, set the credits flag, visited the credits page, and assigned the roll line. Started `sleep` to advance the programme cursor past evening and prepare for the next day.\n\nCompleted `sleep`. The island base and tilt rise by 0.5 degrees each day starting from 0.4 on day one. After sleep it advances the programme cursor, sets the new day, and updates the island entity.\n\nRealized the final morning after credits is the last event in the programme. The full schedule runs five days of activities followed by a final morning and credits.\n\nClarified the programme flow: after tilt finishes, carry advances to evening; evening sleep moves past evening to the final morning, which then proceeds to credits. Credits mode stays open and director auto-leave is disabled there. The ferry does not return automatically, so replaying from day one is not supported, but free play remains available. Started sketching the `game.begin` signature to accept save state arguments such as day, cursor, and pebbles.\n\nCompleted the `game.begin` signature with save fields for age, prior, seen, credits, bests, and finds. Without a save it starts day one morning so the pad reaches credits without walking. With a save it restores state and resumes at the stored cursor; if credits are already complete it enters free play, otherwise it begins the current programme item. The page stores the cursor.\n\n`game.start` launches a single activity immediately without requiring the programme cursor to match. After results, carry must check whether the finished event matches the current programme item at the cursor. If they differ, free play should return to roam instead of advancing the story.\n\nDefined `game.jump` for developer testing. It accepts day, event, step, and rank arguments. Day jumps to the first programme item for that day. Event jumps directly to that activity. Finale steps like tilt:storm or tilt:dawn start the tilt activity then set the phase and visit flags manually.\n\nCompleted the `game.jump` implementation. Finale steps like `tilt:east` start the tilt activity and set the phase and visit flags manually. Day jumps locate the first non-evening programme item for that day, update the cursor and day, and begin that event. Event jumps launch the specified activity directly.\n\nDrafted `game.finish` to end the current activity immediately. It accepts an optional rank argument to override the computed score. The function sets the board done flag and stores the desired rank in the activity component.\n\nCompleted `game.finish` by calling `finishEvent` with the auto mode setting. When a rank override is provided, it maps that rank to a fixed score and adjusts the pebble reward so the override does not conflict with the pebbles already added for the real score.\n\nRefined `game.finish` to replace the result and rank after `finishEvent`, then correct the pebble delta by subtracting the old reward and adding the new one, except in attract mode. Defined `game.leave` to return to the island without scoring pebbles. Confirmed that `game.sleep` is only reachable from evening. Noted that director auto-mode is set via inspector on boot, which defers the first activity until `game.begin`.\n\nTests will call `game.begin` after booting with director auto-mode, then step the engine until credits. The director bot runs inside `frame`, so tests only need to step without intents. A seed test must verify that every required remark ID is in `flow.seen` and that `flow.credits` equals one.\n\nThe required remark IDs are morning, pose, pose-a, pose-b, pose-c, pond, garden, paddle, line, group, tilt:storm, tilt:east, tilt:stone, tilt:dawn, evening, and credits. The 3-seed test uses director auto-mode, so the full 170-second tilt sequence completes and visits every phase including dawn.\n\nCalculated the simulation workload. The full programme totals roughly 1,183 seconds of activity. Three seeds require about 213,000 steps. Even at a slow 0.5 ms per step, the run should finish in under a minute.\n\nAt 2 ms per step the full run takes about seven minutes, which fits comfortably within the 600,000 ms test timeout. The band tests will use ten seeds across four bands and activities. Step caps were estimated for each activity: morning at 2,640, pose-a at roughly 3,240, pond at 4,500, garden at 5,400, and paddle at 4,500.\n\nRevised the band test workload. The worst-case scenario across four bands and ten seeds totals roughly 2.3 million steps. At one millisecond per step that run would take about thirty-eight minutes, which is too slow. The plan is to run all bands in a single engine with seeded resets between them, possibly starting with a single activity to tune the step rate. Batching steps is also under consideration.\n\nBots require per-step intents, so `engine.step(1)` is the only viable approach. A tight loop of single-step calls should be fast enough because the simulation step itself is the real bottleneck. The first task is a small smoke test on one seed for morning to gauge performance before scaling up. Attract mode will also receive a 0.25 noise multiplier.\n\nBands tests will not use director auto-mode. They will call the bot manually, pass intents to `engine.step(1)`, and let the scene handle activity stepping directly. A `play` helper is being drafted: it seeds the engine, starts an activity, and loops up to a step cap while feeding per-step intents from the band bot.\n\nConfirmed the band test loop must not call `activity.step` directly. The scene handles that internally during `engine.step(1)`, so the test only needs to pass intents from `activity.bot` each frame.\n\nfinishEvent computes the score before teardown, so the test can read flow.result directly. Calling activity.score(w) afterward would be redundant and potentially unsafe if teardown clears the activity fields.\n\nTeardown only despawns entities and must not clear score fields. However, the driver zeroes the activity tick during teardown, which breaks line scoring because that activity reads the tick field. Morning and pond use different fields, so they remain safe. The test loop should call activity.score before the driver finishes.\n\nThe band test loop will call `activity.score` immediately after the step that sets `board.done`, before the next step triggers `finishEvent`. This ensures the score is read from the final activity state while `tick` is still intact, since `finishEvent` only zeroes `tick` during teardown.\n\nConfirmed that calling `activity.score` after `engine.step(1)` is unsafe because `finishEvent` zeroes the activity tick during teardown, which breaks line scoring. The smoke test must invoke `setup`, `step`, `score`, and `bot` directly. The band test path uses scene stepping instead, so it cannot safely call `score` afterward.\n\nThe smoke test will directly invoke setup, step, score, and bot for the verify plan. Band tests will read flow.result from the scene path instead of calling score afterward. The driver defers scoring to the next frame after setting a done flag. Another option is to stop clearing the fields that activity.score requires.\n\nThe results timer will use `act.wait` instead of `tick`, so `tick` stays intact for line scoring. Other activity fields are safe because teardown only sweeps made entities, allowing `activity.score()` to run after `finishEvent`.\n\nThe results timer will use `act.left` as a countdown instead of `tick`, since pond scoring depends on `act.calm`. No module-level variables or extra fields are allowed; the simulation must live entirely in components.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a17f1e37-23cd-430a-bb25-13f93eeabf9b-39","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"export function score","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/scenes/events","-A":12}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-a17f1e37-23cd-430a-bb25-13f93eeabf9b-40","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/sim/world.ts","offset":1,"limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":595,"output_tokens":5988,"cache_read_input_tokens":86144,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"6e5fd1e8-1b10-4ce9-bea8-914ae8f1bb7d"}
