b\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/TASK.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":200,\"raw_output\":\"SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:36:56\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-c069415a-e8e5-4ff5-b0a8-e8b108e702cb-21","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"90→    fields: CLIPSET_FIELDS,\\n    defineWith:\\n      \\\"defineClipSet({ set: 'NAME', sources: { … }, clips: { … } }) with a set's _set.json and its clip files\\\",\\n  });\\n}\\nclipSetKind(sharedRegistry);\\n\\n/** The id a set registers under: `clipset:` and its name in lower case (`clipset:style100_back`). */\\nexport function clipSetId(name: string): string {\\n  return `clipset:${name.toLowerCase()}`;\\n100→}\\n\\n/**\\n * Parses `input` (a set's text or object, `parseClipSet`) and registers it on `registry` as `clipSetId(set)`; a set\\n * registered already is returned when it is the same, and refused (`CORE_DUPLICATE_ID`) when it differs.\\n */\\nexport function defineClipSet(input: ClipSet | string | object, options: { registry?: Registry } = {}): ClipSetEntry {\\n  const registry = options.registry ?? sharedRegistry;\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  const set = parseClipSet(input);\\n110→  const id = clipSetId(set.set);\\n  if (registry.has('clipset', id)) {\\n    const kept = registry.get('clipset', id) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n    if (Object.entries(set).every(([key, value]) => sameValue(value, kept[key as keyof ClipSetEntry]))) return kept;\\n  }\\n  return registry.def('clipset', id, set as unknown as Record<string, unknown>) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * A set directory's files (`_set.json` and one `.json` file a clip, by file name; others ignored) as one set\\n120→ * (`parseClipSet`), its clips in its `fit`'s order, then by file name, as tools/anim/repack.ts `joinSet` gives it:\\n * what a page registers from the text `import.meta.glob` loads. `where` names the directory in errors.\\n */\\nexport function clipSetFromFiles(files: Readonly<Record<string, string>>, where = 'the set'): ClipSet {\\n  const { [SET_FILE]: head, ...rest } = files;\\n  if (head === undefined) throw codeError('ANIM_CLIP_BAD_HEADER', { where, field: SET_FILE, value: 'missing', shape: 'the set’s header' }); // prettier-ignore\\n  let raw: object;\\n  try {\\n    raw = JSON.parse(head) as object;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n130→    throw codeError('ANIM_CLIP_NOT_JSON', { where: `${where}/${SET_FILE}`, problem: (error as Error).message }, error);\\n  }\\n  const header = parseClipSet({ ...raw, clips: {} });\\n  const clips = new Map<string, Clip>();\\n  const names = Object.keys(rest).filter((name) => name.endsWith('.json'));\\n  for (const file of names.sort()) {\\n    const clip = parseClip(rest[file], `${where}/${file}`);\\n    clips.set(clip.clip ?? file.replace(/\\\\.json$/, ''), clip);\\n  }\\n  const fit = header.fit ?? {};\\n140→  const order = [...Object.keys(fit).filter((name) => clips.has(name)), ...[...clips.keys()].filter((name) => !Object.hasOwn(fit, name))]; // prettier-ignore\\n  return { ...header, clips: Object.fromEntries(order.map((name) => [name, clips.get(name)!])) };\\n}\\n\\n/** The registered set `id` (`clipset:quaternius`); a missing one throws `CORE_NO_ENTRY`, naming the closest ids. */\\nexport function getClipSet(id: string, registry: Registry = sharedRegistry): ClipSetEntry {\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  return registry.get('clipset', id) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n}\\n\\n150→/** The ids of the sets registered on `registry`, sorted (`clipset:cmu`, `clipset:mesh2motion`…). */\\nexport function clipSetIds(registry: Registry = sharedRegistry): string[] {\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  return registry.list('clipset').map((entry) => entry.id);\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * Registers build `id` with the proportions clip set `setId`'s clips were captured on (retarget.ts `restBuild` of its\\n * first source's rest body, or `source`'s), once, and returns it: `build:mannequin` from `clipset:quaternius` is the\\n * build engine/gfx/puppets/bodies/mannequin.ts draws, which the sim's animator needs before the view's body exists.\\n160→ */\\nexport function defineSetBuild(\\n  id: string,\\n  setId: string,\\n  options: { source?: string; registry?: Registry } = {},\\n): Build {\\n  const registry = options.registry ?? sharedRegistry;\\n  buildKind(registry);\\n  if (registry.has('build', id)) return getBuild(id, registry);\\n  const { set, sources = {} } = getClipSet(setId, registry);\",\"content_concise\":\"90→    fields: CLIPSET_FIELDS,\\n    defineWith:\\n      \\\"defineClipSet({ set: 'NAME', sources: { … }, clips: { … } }) with a set's _set.json and its clip files\\\",\\n  });\\n}\\nclipSetKind(sharedRegistry);\\n\\n/** The id a set registers under: `clipset:` and its name in lower case (`clipset:style100_back`). */\\nexport function clipSetId(name: string): string {\\n  return `clipset:${name.toLowerCase()}`;\\n100→}\\n\\n/**\\n * Parses `input` (a set's text or object, `parseClipSet`) and registers it on `registry` as `clipSetId(set)`; a set\\n * registered already is returned when it is the same, and refused (`CORE_DUPLICATE_ID`) when it differs.\\n */\\nexport function defineClipSet(input: ClipSet | string | object, options: { registry?: Registry } = {}): ClipSetEntry {\\n  const registry = options.registry ?? sharedRegistry;\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  const set = parseClipSet(input);\\n110→  const id = clipSetId(set.set);\\n  if (registry.has('clipset', id)) {\\n    const kept = registry.get('clipset', id) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n    if (Object.entries(set).every(([key, value]) => sameValue(value, kept[key as keyof ClipSetEntry]))) return kept;\\n  }\\n  return registry.def('clipset', id, set as unknown as Record<string, unknown>) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * A set directory's files (`_set.json` and one `.json` file a clip, by file name; others ignored) as one set\\n120→ * (`parseClipSet`), its clips in its `fit`'s order, then by file name, as tools/anim/repack.ts `joinSet` gives it:\\n * what a page registers from the text `import.meta.glob` loads. `where` names the directory in errors.\\n */\\nexport function clipSetFromFiles(files: Readonly<Record<string, string>>, where = 'the set'): ClipSet {\\n  const { [SET_FILE]: head, ...rest } = files;\\n  if (head === undefined) throw codeError('ANIM_CLIP_BAD_HEADER', { where, field: SET_FILE, value: 'missing', shape: 'the set’s header' }); // prettier-ignore\\n  let raw: object;\\n  try {\\n    raw = JSON.parse(head) as object;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n130→    throw codeError('ANIM_CLIP_NOT_JSON', { where: `${where}/${SET_FILE}`, problem: (error as Error).message }, error);\\n  }\\n  const header = parseClipSet({ ...raw, clips: {} });\\n  const clips = new Map<string, Clip>();\\n  const names = Object.keys(rest).filter((name) => name.endsWith('.json'));\\n  for (const file of names.sort()) {\\n    const clip = parseClip(rest[file], `${where}/${file}`);\\n    clips.set(clip.clip ?? file.replace(/\\\\.json$/, ''), clip);\\n  }\\n  const fit = header.fit ?? {};\\n140→  const order = [...Object.keys(fit).filter((name) => clips.has(name)), ...[...clips.keys()].filter((name) => !Object.hasOwn(fit, name))]; // prettier-ignore\\n  return { ...header, clips: Object.fromEntries(order.map((name) => [name, clips.get(name)!])) };\\n}\\n\\n/** The registered set `id` (`clipset:quaternius`); a missing one throws `CORE_NO_ENTRY`, naming the closest ids. */\\nexport function getClipSet(id: string, registry: Registry = sharedRegistry): ClipSetEntry {\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  return registry.get('clipset', id) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n}\\n\\n150→/** The ids of the sets registered on `registry`, sorted (`clipset:cmu`, `clipset:mesh2motion`…). */\\nexport function clipSetIds(registry: Registry = sharedRegistry): string[] {\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  return registry.list('clipset').map((entry) => entry.id);\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * Registers build `id` with the proportions clip set `setId`'s clips were captured on (retarget.ts `restBuild` of its\\n * first source's rest body, or `source`'s), once, and returns it: `build:mannequin` from `clipset:quaternius` is the\\n * build engine/gfx/puppets/bodies/mannequin.ts draws, which the sim's animator needs before the view's body exists.\\n160→ */\\nexport function defineSetBuild(\\n  id: string,\\n  setId: string,\\n  options: { source?: string; registry?: Registry } = {},\\n): Build {\\n  const registry = options.registry ?? sharedRegistry;\\n  buildKind(registry);\\n  if (registry.has('build', id)) return getBuild(id, registry);\\n  const { set, sources = {} } = getClipSet(setId, registry);\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/anim/clip/clipsets.ts\",\"offset\":90,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"    fields: CLIPSET_FIELDS,\\n    defineWith:\\n      \\\"defineClipSet({ set: 'NAME', sources: { … }, clips: { … } }) with a set's _set.json and its clip files\\\",\\n  });\\n}\\nclipSetKind(sharedRegistry);\\n\\n/** The id a set registers under: `clipset:` and its name in lower case (`clipset:style100_back`). */\\nexport function clipSetId(name: string): string {\\n  return `clipset:${name.toLowerCase()}`;\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * Parses `input` (a set's text or object, `parseClipSet`) and registers it on `registry` as `clipSetId(set)`; a set\\n * registered already is returned when it is the same, and refused (`CORE_DUPLICATE_ID`) when it differs.\\n */\\nexport function defineClipSet(input: ClipSet | string | object, options: { registry?: Registry } = {}): ClipSetEntry {\\n  const registry = options.registry ?? sharedRegistry;\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  const set = parseClipSet(input);\\n  const id = clipSetId(set.set);\\n  if (registry.has('clipset', id)) {\\n    const kept = registry.get('clipset', id) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n    if (Object.entries(set).every(([key, value]) => sameValue(value, kept[key as keyof ClipSetEntry]))) return kept;\\n  }\\n  return registry.def('clipset', id, set as unknown as Record<string, unknown>) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * A set directory's files (`_set.json` and one `.json` file a clip, by file name; others ignored) as one set\\n * (`parseClipSet`), its clips in its `fit`'s order, then by file name, as tools/anim/repack.ts `joinSet` gives it:\\n * what a page registers from the text `import.meta.glob` loads. `where` names the directory in errors.\\n */\\nexport function clipSetFromFiles(files: Readonly<Record<string, string>>, where = 'the set'): ClipSet {\\n  const { [SET_FILE]: head, ...rest } = files;\\n  if (head === undefined) throw codeError('ANIM_CLIP_BAD_HEADER', { where, field: SET_FILE, value: 'missing', shape: 'the set’s header' }); // prettier-ignore\\n  let raw: object;\\n  try {\\n    raw = JSON.parse(head) as object;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    throw codeError('ANIM_CLIP_NOT_JSON', { where: `${where}/${SET_FILE}`, problem: (error as Error).message }, error);\\n  }\\n  const header = parseClipSet({ ...raw, clips: {} });\\n  const clips = new Map<string, Clip>();\\n  const names = Object.keys(rest).filter((name) => name.endsWith('.json'));\\n  for (const file of names.sort()) {\\n    const clip = parseClip(rest[file], `${where}/${file}`);\\n    clips.set(clip.clip ?? file.replace(/\\\\.json$/, ''), clip);\\n  }\\n  const fit = header.fit ?? {};\\n  const order = [...Object.keys(fit).filter((name) => clips.has(name)), ...[...clips.keys()].filter((name) => !Object.hasOwn(fit, name))]; // prettier-ignore\\n  return { ...header, clips: Object.fromEntries(order.map((name) => [name, clips.get(name)!])) };\\n}\\n\\n/** The registered set `id` (`clipset:quaternius`); a missing one throws `CORE_NO_ENTRY`, naming the closest ids. */\\nexport function getClipSet(id: string, registry: Registry = sharedRegistry): ClipSetEntry {\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  return registry.get('clipset', id) as unknown as ClipSetEntry;\\n}\\n\\n/** The ids of the sets registered on `registry`, sorted (`clipset:cmu`, `clipset:mesh2motion`…). */\\nexport function clipSetIds(registry: Registry = sharedRegistry): string[] {\\n  clipSetKind(registry);\\n  return registry.list('clipset').map((entry) => entry.id);\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * Registers build `id` with the proportions clip set `setId`'s clips were captured on (retarget.ts `restBuild` of its\\n * first source's rest body, or `source`'s), once, and returns it: `build:mannequin` from `clipset:quaternius` is the\\n * build engine/gfx/puppets/bodies/mannequin.ts draws, which the sim's animator needs before the view's body exists.\\n */\\nexport function defineSetBuild(\\n  id: string,\\n  setId: string,\\n  options: { source?: string; registry?: Registry } = {},\\n): Build {\\n  const registry = options.registry ?? sharedRegistry;\\n  buildKind(registry);\\n  if (registry.has('build', id)) return getBuild(id, registry);\\n  const { set, sources = {} } = getClipSet(setId, registry);\\n\",\"total_lines\":269}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-c069415a-e8e5-4ff5-b0a8-e8b108e702cb-22","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Terrain (PLAN.md WP 10.5, §5.8; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §2.2, §6.3): outdoor ground as plain data, a heightfield\\n * made from a seeded spec, and the queries on it. `defineTerrain(id, spec)` registers a spec (the registry kind\\n * `terrain`); `getTerrain(id)` makes it (`makeTerrain`): a grid of `cols` × `rows` cells `cell` metres wide from its\\n * north-west corner `origin`, a height at every cell corner. The physics stands it as a heightfield\\n * (engine/sim/levelBodies.ts's `addTerrainBody`, engine/physics/heightfield.ts), the view draws it in chunks\\n * (engine/gfx/terrain.ts), navigation walks it (engine/world/terrainNav.ts).\\n *\\n * A height is shardfall's: fbm value noise of `height` metres' range at the `scale` of its features, plus a ridged\\n10→ * fbm (`ridges` of it), then the `hills` (smooth domes, each `height` high and `radius` across from `at`) added, then\\n * rounded to `terrace` steps when that is set, then pulled to each of the `flats` (a footprint flattened to its\\n * `height`, blending back over `margin` metres round it), all over `origin`'s height. Cells split as Rapier splits a\\n * heightfield's: along the diagonal from the north-east corner to the south-west one, so `terrainHeight` is the\\n * collider's surface, the triangle under the point interpolated, and `terrainNormal` that triangle's normal.\\n *\\n * `surface` is the tag underfoot, `steepSurface` where the ground rises `steep` metres a metre or more (0.7: 35°);\\n * `materials` (presentation only, as a level's) are the drawing's layers: the ground, the steep and, from `high`\\n * metres over the origin up (0: none), the high one. `scatterTerrain` places trees and rocks by hashes of the cells\\n * (shardfall's stateless placement).\\n20→ *\\n * Invariants: pure: equal specs give equal terrains in Node and Chromium (int32 hashing, polynomial blends, no trig);\\n * heights are float32, as the collider keeps them; outside the grid nothing stands (`terrainHeight` is null).\\n *\\n * Carried from shardfall@fa2dab6 `crates/pav_core/src/terrain.rs:93-153` (`raw_height`: fbm of 5 octaves at\\n * lacunarity 2.03, the ridge term, the flattened footprints' smoothstep, `cell`'s terraces; `decor`'s hashed cells),\\n * as a concept: on a heightfield's corners rather than in terraced tiles.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../core/registry';\\n30→ * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineTerrain('terrain:knoll', { size: [16, 16], hills: [{ at: [8, 8], radius: 8, height: 2 }] }, reg);\\n * const knoll = getTerrain('terrain:knoll', reg);\\n * [knoll.cols, terrainHeight(knoll, 8, 8), terrainHeight(knoll, 0, 0), terrainHeight(knoll, -1, 0)]; // [16, 2, 0, null]\\n * @see engine/world/terrain.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { mix32 } from '../core/hash';\\nimport { codeError } from '../core/log';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\\nimport { createNoise2D } from '../core/noise';\\n40→import { Vector3 } from '../core/math';\\nimport type { Schema, SpecOf } from '../core/schema';\\n\\n/** A two-number field (m). */\\nconst pair = (description: string, required = false) =>\\n  ({ type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, ...(required ? { required } : { default: [0, 0] }), unit: 'm', description }) as const; // prettier-ignore\\n\\n/** The fields of a `terrain` entry (see the file comment). */\\nexport const TERRAIN_FIELDS = {\\n  size: pair('Its extent along x and along z, each a whole number of cells: [24, 96].', true),\\n50→  cell: { type: 'number', default: 1, minimum: 0.25, maximum: 8, unit: 'm', description: 'The grid’s spacing: a height stands at every cell’s corners.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  origin: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, default: [0, 0, 0], unit: 'm', description: 'Its north-west corner, at the height everything else is added to: [x, y, z].' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  seed: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'The noise’s seed.' },\\n  height: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'The noise’s range: rolling ground about this high (0: none).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  scale: { type: 'number', default: 40, minimum: 1, unit: 'm', description: 'The size of the noise’s features.' },\\n  octaves: { type: 'integer', default: 5, minimum: 1, maximum: 8, description: 'The noise’s octaves.' },\\n  ridges: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, minimum: 0, maximum: 1, description: 'How much ridged noise is added: shardfall’s half.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  hills: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object' }, default: [], description: 'Smooth domes added: { at: [x, z], radius, height } (m; a negative height digs a hollow).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  terrace: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'Rounds heights to steps this high (0: smooth).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  flats: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object' }, default: [], description: 'Footprints flattened: { rect: [x0, z0, x1, z1], height, margin } (m), the ground blending back over margin round each.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n60→  surface: { type: 'string', default: 'grass', description: 'The surface tag underfoot (footsteps, particles).' },\\n  steep: { type: 'number', default: 0.7, minimum: 0, unit: 'm/m', description: 'Ground rising this much a metre or more is steep (0.7: 35°).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  steepSurface: { type: 'string', default: 'rock', description: 'The surface tag on steep ground.' },\\n  high: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'The high layer shows from this height over the origin up (0: none).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  materials: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, default: ['material:default'], description: 'Drawing only: the layers’ materials, the ground, then the steep and the high (a game names its own).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n} as const satisfies Schema;\\n\\n/** A `terrain` entry: its spec. */\\nexport type TerrainEntry = Entry<typeof TERRAIN_FIELDS>;\\n/** What `defineTerrain` and `makeTerrain` take: each field of `TERRAIN_FIELDS` (`size` required). */\\n70→export type TerrainSpec = SpecOf<typeof TERRAIN_FIELDS>;\\n\\n/** A dome added to a terrain. */\\nexport interface TerrainHill {\\n  readonly at: readonly [number, number];\\n  readonly radius: number;\\n  readonly height: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** A footprint a terrain flattens. */\\n80→export interface TerrainFlat {\\n  readonly rect: readonly [x0: number, z0: number, x1: number, z1: number];\\n  readonly height: number;\\n  readonly margin: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** A made terrain: its grid, its heights and what the queries and the drawing read (see the file comment). */\\nexport interface Terrain {\\n  /** Its id without `terrain:`. */\\n  readonly name: string;\\n90→  /** Cells along x and along z, and a cell's side (m). */\\n  readonly cols: number;\\n  readonly rows: number;\\n  readonly cell: number;\\n  /** Its north-west corner (m). */\\n  readonly origin: readonly [number, number, number];\\n  /** The height (m) at every corner, row by row from the north: corner (col, row) at `row * (cols + 1) + col`. */\\n  readonly heights: Float32Array;\\n  /** Its box (m). */\\n  readonly bounds: { readonly min: readonly [number, number, number]; readonly max: readonly [number, number, number] }; // prettier-ignore\\n100→  readonly surface: string;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Terrain (PLAN.md WP 10.5, §5.8; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §2.2, §6.3): outdoor ground as plain data, a heightfield\\n * made from a seeded spec, and the queries on it. `defineTerrain(id, spec)` registers a spec (the registry kind\\n * `terrain`); `getTerrain(id)` makes it (`makeTerrain`): a grid of `cols` × `rows` cells `cell` metres wide from its\\n * north-west corner `origin`, a height at every cell corner. The physics stands it as a heightfield\\n * (engine/sim/levelBodies.ts's `addTerrainBody`, engine/physics/heightfield.ts), the view draws it in chunks\\n * (engine/gfx/terrain.ts), navigation walks it (engine/world/terrainNav.ts).\\n *\\n * A height is shardfall's: fbm value noise of `height` metres' range at the `scale` of its features, plus a ridged\\n10→ * fbm (`ridges` of it), then the `hills` (smooth domes, each `height` high and `radius` across from `at`) added, then\\n * rounded to `terrace` steps when that is set, then pulled to each of the `flats` (a footprint flattened to its\\n * `height`, blending back over `margin` metres round it), all over `origin`'s height. Cells split as Rapier splits a\\n * heightfield's: along the diagonal from the north-east corner to the south-west one, so `terrainHeight` is the\\n * collider's surface, the triangle under the point interpolated, and `terrainNormal` that triangle's normal.\\n *\\n * `surface` is the tag underfoot, `steepSurface` where the ground rises `steep` metres a metre or more (0.7: 35°);\\n * `materials` (presentation only, as a level's) are the drawing's layers: the ground, the steep and, from `high`\\n * metres over the origin up (0: none), the high one. `scatterTerrain` places trees and rocks by hashes of the cells\\n * (shardfall's stateless placement).\\n20→ *\\n * Invariants: pure: equal specs give equal terrains in Node and Chromium (int32 hashing, polynomial blends, no trig);\\n * heights are float32, as the collider keeps them; outside the grid nothing stands (`terrainHeight` is null).\\n *\\n * Carried from shardfall@fa2dab6 `crates/pav_core/src/terrain.rs:93-153` (`raw_height`: fbm of 5 octaves at\\n * lacunarity 2.03, the ridge term, the flattened footprints' smoothstep, `cell`'s terraces; `decor`'s hashed cells),\\n * as a concept: on a heightfield's corners rather than in terraced tiles.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../core/registry';\\n30→ * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineTerrain('terrain:knoll', { size: [16, 16], hills: [{ at: [8, 8], radius: 8, height: 2 }] }, reg);\\n * const knoll = getTerrain('terrain:knoll', reg);\\n * [knoll.cols, terrainHeight(knoll, 8, 8), terrainHeight(knoll, 0, 0), terrainHeight(knoll, -1, 0)]; // [16, 2, 0, null]\\n * @see engine/world/terrain.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { mix32 } from '../core/hash';\\nimport { codeError } from '../core/log';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\\nimport { createNoise2D } from '../core/noise';\\n40→import { Vector3 } from '../core/math';\\nimport type { Schema, SpecOf } from '../core/schema';\\n\\n/** A two-number field (m). */\\nconst pair = (description: string, required = false) =>\\n  ({ type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, ...(required ? { required } : { default: [0, 0] }), unit: 'm', description }) as const; // prettier-ignore\\n\\n/** The fields of a `terrain` entry (see the file comment). */\\nexport const TERRAIN_FIELDS = {\\n  size: pair('Its extent along x and along z, each a whole number of cells: [24, 96].', true),\\n50→  cell: { type: 'number', default: 1, minimum: 0.25, maximum: 8, unit: 'm', description: 'The grid’s spacing: a height stands at every cell’s corners.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  origin: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, default: [0, 0, 0], unit: 'm', description: 'Its north-west corner, at the height everything else is added to: [x, y, z].' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  seed: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'The noise’s seed.' },\\n  height: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'The noise’s range: rolling ground about this high (0: none).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  scale: { type: 'number', default: 40, minimum: 1, unit: 'm', description: 'The size of the noise’s features.' },\\n  octaves: { type: 'integer', default: 5, minimum: 1, maximum: 8, description: 'The noise’s octaves.' },\\n  ridges: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, minimum: 0, maximum: 1, description: 'How much ridged noise is added: shardfall’s half.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  hills: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object' }, default: [], description: 'Smooth domes added: { at: [x, z], radius, height } (m; a negative height digs a hollow).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  terrace: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'Rounds heights to steps this high (0: smooth).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  flats: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object' }, default: [], description: 'Footprints flattened: { rect: [x0, z0, x1, z1], height, margin } (m), the ground blending back over margin round each.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n60→  surface: { type: 'string', default: 'grass', description: 'The surface tag underfoot (footsteps, particles).' },\\n  steep: { type: 'number', default: 0.7, minimum: 0, unit: 'm/m', description: 'Ground rising this much a metre or more is steep (0.7: 35°).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  steepSurface: { type: 'string', default: 'rock', description: 'The surface tag on steep ground.' },\\n  high: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'The high layer shows from this height over the origin up (0: none).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  materials: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, default: ['material:default'], description: 'Drawing only: the layers’ materials, the ground, then the steep and the high (a game names its own).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n} as const satisfies Schema;\\n\\n/** A `terrain` entry: its spec. */\\nexport type TerrainEntry = Entry<typeof TERRAIN_FIELDS>;\\n/** What `defineTerrain` and `makeTerrain` take: each field of `TERRAIN_FIELDS` (`size` required). */\\n70→export type TerrainSpec = SpecOf<typeof TERRAIN_FIELDS>;\\n\\n/** A dome added to a terrain. */\\nexport interface TerrainHill {\\n  readonly at: readonly [number, number];\\n  readonly radius: number;\\n  readonly height: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** A footprint a terrain flattens. */\\n80→export interface TerrainFlat {\\n  readonly rect: readonly [x0: number, z0: number, x1: number, z1: number];\\n  readonly height: number;\\n  readonly margin: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** A made terrain: its grid, its heights and what the queries and the drawing read (see the file comment). */\\nexport interface Terrain {\\n  /** Its id without `terrain:`. */\\n  readonly name: string;\\n90→  /** Cells along x and along z, and a cell's side (m). */\\n  readonly cols: number;\\n  readonly rows: number;\\n  readonly cell: number;\\n  /** Its north-west corner (m). */\\n  readonly origin: readonly [number, number, number];\\n  /** The height (m) at every corner, row by row from the north: corner (col, row) at `row * (cols + 1) + col`. */\\n  readonly heights: Float32Array;\\n  /** Its box (m). */\\n  readonly bounds: { readonly min: readonly [number, number, number]; readonly max: readonly [number, number, number] }; // prettier-ignore\\n100→  readonly surface: string;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/world/terrain.ts\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":100,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file Terrain (PLAN.md WP 10.5, §5.8; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §2.2, §6.3): outdoor ground as plain data, a heightfield\\n * made from a seeded spec, and the queries on it. `defineTerrain(id, spec)` registers a spec (the registry kind\\n * `terrain`); `getTerrain(id)` makes it (`makeTerrain`): a grid of `cols` × `rows` cells `cell` metres wide from its\\n * north-west corner `origin`, a height at every cell corner. The physics stands it as a heightfield\\n * (engine/sim/levelBodies.ts's `addTerrainBody`, engine/physics/heightfield.ts), the view draws it in chunks\\n * (engine/gfx/terrain.ts), navigation walks it (engine/world/terrainNav.ts).\\n *\\n * A height is shardfall's: fbm value noise of `height` metres' range at the `scale` of its features, plus a ridged\\n * fbm (`ridges` of it), then the `hills` (smooth domes, each `height` high and `radius` across from `at`) added, then\\n * rounded to `terrace` steps when that is set, then pulled to each of the `flats` (a footprint flattened to its\\n * `height`, blending back over `margin` metres round it), all over `origin`'s height. Cells split as Rapier splits a\\n * heightfield's: along the diagonal from the north-east corner to the south-west one, so `terrainHeight` is the\\n * collider's surface, the triangle under the point interpolated, and `terrainNormal` that triangle's normal.\\n *\\n * `surface` is the tag underfoot, `steepSurface` where the ground rises `steep` metres a metre or more (0.7: 35°);\\n * `materials` (presentation only, as a level's) are the drawing's layers: the ground, the steep and, from `high`\\n * metres over the origin up (0: none), the high one. `scatterTerrain` places trees and rocks by hashes of the cells\\n * (shardfall's stateless placement).\\n *\\n * Invariants: pure: equal specs give equal terrains in Node and Chromium (int32 hashing, polynomial blends, no trig);\\n * heights are float32, as the collider keeps them; outside the grid nothing stands (`terrainHeight` is null).\\n *\\n * Carried from shardfall@fa2dab6 `crates/pav_core/src/terrain.rs:93-153` (`raw_height`: fbm of 5 octaves at\\n * lacunarity 2.03, the ridge term, the flattened footprints' smoothstep, `cell`'s terraces; `decor`'s hashed cells),\\n * as a concept: on a heightfield's corners rather than in terraced tiles.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { createRegistry } from '../core/registry';\\n * const reg = createRegistry();\\n * defineTerrain('terrain:knoll', { size: [16, 16], hills: [{ at: [8, 8], radius: 8, height: 2 }] }, reg);\\n * const knoll = getTerrain('terrain:knoll', reg);\\n * [knoll.cols, terrainHeight(knoll, 8, 8), terrainHeight(knoll, 0, 0), terrainHeight(knoll, -1, 0)]; // [16, 2, 0, null]\\n * @see engine/world/terrain.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { mix32 } from '../core/hash';\\nimport { codeError } from '../core/log';\\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\\nimport { createNoise2D } from '../core/noise';\\nimport { Vector3 } from '../core/math';\\nimport type { Schema, SpecOf } from '../core/schema';\\n\\n/** A two-number field (m). */\\nconst pair = (description: string, required = false) =>\\n  ({ type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, ...(required ? { required } : { default: [0, 0] }), unit: 'm', description }) as const; // prettier-ignore\\n\\n/** The fields of a `terrain` entry (see the file comment). */\\nexport const TERRAIN_FIELDS = {\\n  size: pair('Its extent along x and along z, each a whole number of cells: [24, 96].', true),\\n  cell: { type: 'number', default: 1, minimum: 0.25, maximum: 8, unit: 'm', description: 'The grid’s spacing: a height stands at every cell’s corners.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  origin: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, default: [0, 0, 0], unit: 'm', description: 'Its north-west corner, at the height everything else is added to: [x, y, z].' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  seed: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'The noise’s seed.' },\\n  height: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'The noise’s range: rolling ground about this high (0: none).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  scale: { type: 'number', default: 40, minimum: 1, unit: 'm', description: 'The size of the noise’s features.' },\\n  octaves: { type: 'integer', default: 5, minimum: 1, maximum: 8, description: 'The noise’s octaves.' },\\n  ridges: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, minimum: 0, maximum: 1, description: 'How much ridged noise is added: shardfall’s half.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  hills: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object' }, default: [], description: 'Smooth domes added: { at: [x, z], radius, height } (m; a negative height digs a hollow).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  terrace: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'Rounds heights to steps this high (0: smooth).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  flats: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object' }, default: [], description: 'Footprints flattened: { rect: [x0, z0, x1, z1], height, margin } (m), the ground blending back over margin round each.' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  surface: { type: 'string', default: 'grass', description: 'The surface tag underfoot (footsteps, particles).' },\\n  steep: { type: 'number', default: 0.7, minimum: 0, unit: 'm/m', description: 'Ground rising this much a metre or more is steep (0.7: 35°).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  steepSurface: { type: 'string', default: 'rock', description: 'The surface tag on steep ground.' },\\n  high: { type: 'number', default: 0, minimum: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'The high layer shows from this height over the origin up (0: none).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n  materials: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, default: ['material:default'], description: 'Drawing only: the layers’ materials, the ground, then the steep and the high (a game names its own).' }, // prettier-ignore\\n} as const satisfies Schema;\\n\\n/** A `terrain` entry: its spec. */\\nexport type TerrainEntry = Entry<typeof TERRAIN_FIELDS>;\\n/** What `defineTerrain` and `makeTerrain` take: each field of `TERRAIN_FIELDS` (`size` required). */\\nexport type TerrainSpec = SpecOf<typeof TERRAIN_FIELDS>;\\n\\n/** A dome added to a terrain. */\\nexport interface TerrainHill {\\n  readonly at: readonly [number, number];\\n  readonly radius: number;\\n  readonly height: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** A footprint a terrain flattens. */\\nexport interface TerrainFlat {\\n  readonly rect: readonly [x0: number, z0: number, x1: number, z1: number];\\n  readonly height: number;\\n  readonly margin: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** A made terrain: its grid, its heights and what the queries and the drawing read (see the file comment). */\\nexport interface Terrain {\\n  /** Its id without `terrain:`. */\\n  readonly name: string;\\n  /** Cells along x and along z, and a cell's side (m). */\\n  readonly cols: number;\\n  readonly rows: number;\\n  readonly cell: number;\\n  /** Its north-west corner (m). */\\n  readonly origin: readonly [number, number, number];\\n  /** The height (m) at every corner, row by row from the north: corner (col, row) at `row * (cols + 1) + col`. */\\n  readonly heights: Float32Array;\\n  /** Its box (m). */\\n  readonly bounds: { readonly min: readonly [number, number, number]; readonly max: readonly [number, number, number] }; // prettier-ignore\\n  readonly surface: string;\\n\",\"total_lines\":338}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"d1209ce7-fd0f-4206-bf8a-c87f5e208039"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_214","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Reviewing PLAN.md sections 8, 9, and 16 to ensure the page matches the requirements.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3baea44a-9878-46d0-972a-4c818a5b7e31-23","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/PLAN.md","offset":480,"limit":160}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3baea44a-9878-46d0-972a-4c818a5b7e31-24","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/PLAN.md","offset":860,"limit":160}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3baea44a-9878-46d0-972a-4c818a5b7e31-25","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"terrain\\?:|scene: \\{|defineScene\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/engine/sim","glob":"*.ts","head_limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3baea44a-9878-46d0-972a-4c818a5b7e31-26","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/cast/rest.ts","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8012,"output_tokens":378,"cache_read_input_tokens":62208,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"dcc74b20-edb8-4be3-b073-92679e3bb48a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3baea44a-9878-46d0-972a-4c818a5b7e31-23","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"480→## 8. Screens and UX\\n\\n**10-foot rules (test them at 1920 × 1080):** body text at least 2.6vh (28 px), prompts and menus 3.2vh or more; one idea per\\nscreen; at most six menu rows; 5% safe margins; focus unmistakable from a sofa. **No dense web-style data screens:** results are\\none word, one number, one sentence, one small graph. The dev panel is the only busy screen, and only developers open it.\\n\\n**UI look:** pebble-shaped rounded panels in warm off-white with soft shadows, not the source's look. A rounded system stack\\n(`ui-rounded, \\\"SF Pro Rounded\\\", \\\"Segoe UI Rounded\\\", system-ui`); fonts are allowed but not needed. Screens are HTML/CSS under\\n`engine.ui.root`, with `createMenu` for lists (hover already moves its focus, click picks **[source]**) and a small\\ngrid of your own for the Noticeboard. `createDialog` is fine for speech: keep text on screen, always, with the voice.\\n490→Restyle the engine's widgets for 10 feet: their defaults are small.\\n\\n**The Balance Disc** is the HUD's heart, HTML/SVG, 30vh across at the lower centre: target ring, your dot with a short trail, a\\nguide ring swelling with the breath, a foot pill either side with analog fill. It reads the sim's `wobble` each frame. A\\nmatching ring also lies on the mat in the world (a component with `telegraph`, `position`, `yaw`, `u`, `size` is drawn on the\\nfloor). In Group Class each seat gets a smaller disc under its mat.\\n\\n**Flow:**\\n\\n```\\n500→Load → Title ─┬─ Begin/Continue → Pier or the day's morning → Island ⇄ Activity → Results → … → Evening → next day\\n              ├─ Free Play (Noticeboard, after Day 1)\\n              ├─ Group Class → join on mats → three classes → podium\\n              ├─ Settings\\n              └─ About / Credits\\n      (10 s idle on any front-end menu) ⇄ Attract\\n```\\n\\n- **Load:** HTML first, immediately (\\\"Calibrating…\\\"), so the screen is never blank.\\n- **Title:** the game's title over a slow orbit of the island, the menu, \\\"Press any button\\\" until a pad wakes.\\n510→- **Island:** a small prompt near things (\\\"✕ Talk\\\"), Triangle or Tab for today's Programme, nothing else on screen.\\n- **Programme:** today's list as up to four cards with stamps, each with \\\"Go\\\". **Noticeboard:** a 3 × 2 page of activity cards with\\n  best rank; L1, R1 or Q, E page through them.\\n- **Results:** rank word, score, pebbles falling onto a little cairn, a line from Plumb or Tare; \\\"Again\\\" or \\\"Carry on\\\".\\n- **Evening:** calendar stamp, the age graph, a closing remark, \\\"Sleep\\\".\\n- **Pause** (Options or Esc, in play and on the island; pauses the sim with `inspector.pause()`, `modal` keeps keys out of play):\\n  Resume, Restart Activity, Back to the Island, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About / Credits, Quit to Title.\\n- **Settings:** Music (**10% by default**), Effects, Voices, Button icons, Mouse sensitivity, Invert look, Rumble, Steadier Hands (an\\n  assist: halves the sway), Reduced Motion (no camera roll or shake), Fullscreen, Erase Save.\\n- **Controls:** shows the bindings per scheme; keyboard rebinding through `input.bindingsFor`, `rebind`, `listen`.\\n520→- **About / Credits:** paged, from one data file: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback, credits\\n  (section 19).\\n\\n**Dev Mode** (from the pause menu; absent in hub sessions; a clear panel that works with pad, keyboard and mouse):\\n\\n- **Live sliders with Reset**, per group and for all: every numeric `game.*` setting in this plan, plus `game.pebbles.mult`. They are\\n  sim settings with `when: 'now'`, changed by `inspector.set(path, value)`, so they apply at once and replays hold them. Defaults come\\n  from `inspector.describe('setting', path)` **[tested]**.\\n- **Jumps:** to any day, any activity, any finale step. Finish activity now at a chosen rank.\\n- **Cheats:** bot plays for me, no topple, unlock all, give pebbles, reset save, a throwaway save mode (`createStore` `sandbox`).\\n530→- **Tools:** time scale (0.25, 1, 4), pause and frame step, free camera (`cam3=fly`), state overlay (CoB, calm, sync, tilt, event,\\n  phase, tick, hash), seed and new seed, copy tuned settings as JSON.\\n\\n**Attract mode.** 10 s with no input on any front-end menu starts it; any key, mouse movement, click or pad input leaves it, back to\\nwhere you were. Set `game.auto` and restart the scene with `inspector.seed(n)` (restarts the sim, keeps settings **[tested]**).\\nThe sim's director runs a loop with the bot cheating freely: island flyby and jog, Pose Class, Still Pond with its lures, The Line,\\nPaddle Glide, Sand Garden, Step Class, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, then the Great Tilt's Centre Stone, about 15 s each, then round\\nagain. Banner: the game's title, \\\"DEMO\\\", \\\"Press any button\\\" with the current glyph. It never touches the save.\\n\\n**Touch-only devices** (`(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)`, no pad or key seen yet): no touch controls, no touch play. The title\\n540→shows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button that works by tap. Attract still starts and loops behind it; taps reach only\\nSettings and never start play. A key or pad input at any time returns to normal. Always pass `touch=off` in the engine's query, or it draws its own touch stick **[source; the setting is accepted: tested]**.\\n\\n**No WebGPU:** a plain, friendly page: \\\"This game needs a browser with WebGPU, such as current Chrome or Edge.\\\" Never a blank page.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Multiplayer\\n\\nThe game has two kinds, both up to four players. They share one module: `labs/game/scenes/round.ts`.\\n550→\\n**A Round** is three classes dealt from `w.rng('deal')` (same seed, same deal): Still Ring, then one of Heron, Hoop or Step, then Still\\nPond, each in its `short` 45 s form with a 6 s lead-in. About 2.7 minutes. Score: the sum, 0–3000. They all play on mats, so they need\\nnothing but the pavilion.\\n\\n### On its own: Group Class\\n\\nSame screen, up to four pads (keyboard and mouse can hold one seat). Walk up to the Pavilion or pick it from the title. Mats light in\\nseat colours as people press Cross or Space to join. Empty seats go to CPU guests (Settings can turn them off); Day 5 fields Gerald,\\nBex and Nan. CPU guests are the bot with extra sway per character. Podium at the end, and a sash reading \\\"Least Wobbly\\\".\\n560→\\nThe engine's gamepad device only follows one active pad, so the page reads `navigator.getGamepads()` itself in Group Class and sends\\neach seat's input with `engine.input.virtual.axis('game:seats', […])`.\\n\\n### In AI Hub: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, mode **ghost**\\n\\nRead the protocol once: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`. Fetch by raw URL, into\\n`labs/game/hub/`: `session.js` (save it as `session-client.txt`), and for tests `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` from the same\\nfolder. **Do not clone the ai-hub repository: it is 5.3 GB.**\\n\\n570→Why ghost: every copy runs its own seeded round, nothing depends on a host, and each player sees the others on the neighbouring mats,\\nwobbling in their seat colour. It is the source's solo ritual made social without any interference.\\n\\n**Tags** in the head, right after the client (the build injects them, section 14):\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"ghost\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"3\\\">\\n580→<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Stay the steadiest across three classes\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Lean: stick or WASD · Feet: L2 R2 or Q E · Breathe: both or Space\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:webgpu\\\" content=\\\"required\\\">\\n```\\n\\nGoal at most 60 characters, how-to at most 80. Also `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` 1-4, `hub:source` Wii Fit.\\n\\n**Startup** **[tested: this shape, on the starter's one scene, passed the harness at 2, 3 (host leaving) and 4 players with the engine running in every frame]**:\\n\\n```ts\\n590→const search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\\nfor (const key of [...search.keys()]) if (key.startsWith('aihub')) search.delete(key); // see the trap below\\nsearch.set('touch', 'off');\\nconst AIHub = window.AIHub;\\nconst info = AIHub?.active ? await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;\\nif (info) { search.set('scene', 'game:round'); search.set('seed', String(info.seed)); }\\nconst watchdog = info ? setTimeout(() => AIHub.ready(), 14000) : 0;   // the harness allows 20 s after hello\\nengine = await createEngine({ canvas, scenes: ['game:island', 'game:round'], search: `?${search}`, /* … */ });\\nif (info) { clearTimeout(watchdog); AIHub.ready(); }\\n```\\n600→\\n**The biggest trap in this whole plan [tested]:** the hub opens the page with `?aihub=1&aihub-seed=N`. `createEngine` treats every\\nunknown query key as a setting and fails with `CORE_BAD_SETTING`, **and the harness still reports PASS**, because the client keeps\\nanswering while the game is dead. Strip those keys, and make your test assert, inside every frame, `__engine.ready === true` and\\n`__engine.errors.length === 0`.\\n\\n**The round in a session:**\\n\\n- No title, menus or attract. Straight to the game's own card: the four mats in the pavilion, Plumb in front, each player standing on their\\n  mat. It shows the game's title, players in colour with who is ready, how to win, this player's controls, seconds left (from the `card` event).\\n610→  Keys: Enter or Cross answers `AIHub.answer('ready')`, Triangle or T answers `'more'`, a tap or click works too. Listen for Enter with your own\\n  `keydown` on `window`: the harness sends a synthetic one. The card has no menus or choices, and draws numbers only from `AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n  Make it HTML over whatever is drawn, because the watchdog may call `ready()` while the view is still warming up.\\n- `start` arrives; play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`. Send the sim `round.go` then (scene action). On `start` also send one\\n  `AIHub.progress({ score: 0, label: 'Breathing in' })` and one ghost message, so a slow copy still passes the harness.\\n- During play: `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` whenever it changes (label at most 24 characters, \\\"Class 2 · 640\\\"), and every 100 ms\\n  `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', e: classIndex, x, y, p: poseId, s: score }, { reliable: false })`. Never above 20 a second.\\n- Ghosts are sim entities, because only sim entities can be drawn: incoming messages go through\\n  `engine.input.virtual.axis('game:ghosts', […])` to actors on the other mats, tinted with `info.players[i].color`, leans scrubbed by their CoB,\\n  name labels from the hub as text (never as HTML), shown only while `e` matches. On `leave`, they walk off politely.\\n620→- Finished: `AIHub.result({ score, stats, awards })` once. Stats at most six, names at most 16 characters (\\\"Wobble Age\\\", \\\"Best hold\\\", \\\"Breath\\\"),\\n  values short; awards at most three, title at most 24, detail at most 40 (\\\"Unnervingly Still\\\").\\n- `time-up`: report the current score at once if you have not. `end`: `engine.stop()`, stop all sound, take no input, send nothing.\\n- If `engine.ready` is false (no WebGPU): `AIHub.fail('WebGPU required')`.\\n- **Rules in a session:** network only through the hub; no chat or custom names; no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; restart\\n  and quit do nothing. Pause is an overlay that never pauses the sim: Resume, volume, button icons. No dev mode. **Create + Options belongs to the hub:**\\n  while pad button 8 is held, ignore the engine's `pause`. Storage is a fresh memory copy, handled by the client, so write nothing special for it.\\n- Outside a session (`connect` gives `null`, or no client at all in the dev server) the game must behave exactly as it does alone.\\n\\n**Boot deadline, the one place performance matters.** The harness gives every copy 20 s to call `ready()`. **[tested on SwiftShader]** the starter\\n630→game alone needed 8–16 s per copy at four copies; the engine's playground needed 52–73 s. So `game:round` is a small separate scene with just the\\npavilion, the mats and the cast. The island's cost never reaches a session, and the watchdog covers the rest. Performance is out of scope everywhere else.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Art direction\\n\\nBase: **the engine's native style**: its puppet bodies, its toon material style, its generators. New assets are everything that makes it feel like a\\nwellness retreat; the engine's library is fine for crates and benches.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"480→## 8. Screens and UX\\n\\n**10-foot rules (test them at 1920 × 1080):** body text at least 2.6vh (28 px), prompts and menus 3.2vh or more; one idea per\\nscreen; at most six menu rows; 5% safe margins; focus unmistakable from a sofa. **No dense web-style data screens:** results are\\none word, one number, one sentence, one small graph. The dev panel is the only busy screen, and only developers open it.\\n\\n**UI look:** pebble-shaped rounded panels in warm off-white with soft shadows, not the source's look. A rounded system stack\\n(`ui-rounded, \\\"SF Pro Rounded\\\", \\\"Segoe UI Rounded\\\", system-ui`); fonts are allowed but not needed. Screens are HTML/CSS under\\n`engine.ui.root`, with `createMenu` for lists (hover already moves its focus, click picks **[source]**) and a small\\ngrid of your own for the Noticeboard. `createDialog` is fine for speech: keep text on screen, always, with the voice.\\n490→Restyle the engine's widgets for 10 feet: their defaults are small.\\n\\n**The Balance Disc** is the HUD's heart, HTML/SVG, 30vh across at the lower centre: target ring, your dot with a short trail, a\\nguide ring swelling with the breath, a foot pill either side with analog fill. It reads the sim's `wobble` each frame. A\\nmatching ring also lies on the mat in the world (a component with `telegraph`, `position`, `yaw`, `u`, `size` is drawn on the\\nfloor). In Group Class each seat gets a smaller disc under its mat.\\n\\n**Flow:**\\n\\n```\\n500→Load → Title ─┬─ Begin/Continue → Pier or the day's morning → Island ⇄ Activity → Results → … → Evening → next day\\n              ├─ Free Play (Noticeboard, after Day 1)\\n              ├─ Group Class → join on mats → three classes → podium\\n              ├─ Settings\\n              └─ About / Credits\\n      (10 s idle on any front-end menu) ⇄ Attract\\n```\\n\\n- **Load:** HTML first, immediately (\\\"Calibrating…\\\"), so the screen is never blank.\\n- **Title:** the game's title over a slow orbit of the island, the menu, \\\"Press any button\\\" until a pad wakes.\\n510→- **Island:** a small prompt near things (\\\"✕ Talk\\\"), Triangle or Tab for today's Programme, nothing else on screen.\\n- **Programme:** today's list as up to four cards with stamps, each with \\\"Go\\\". **Noticeboard:** a 3 × 2 page of activity cards with\\n  best rank; L1, R1 or Q, E page through them.\\n- **Results:** rank word, score, pebbles falling onto a little cairn, a line from Plumb or Tare; \\\"Again\\\" or \\\"Carry on\\\".\\n- **Evening:** calendar stamp, the age graph, a closing remark, \\\"Sleep\\\".\\n- **Pause** (Options or Esc, in play and on the island; pauses the sim with `inspector.pause()`, `modal` keeps keys out of play):\\n  Resume, Restart Activity, Back to the Island, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About / Credits, Quit to Title.\\n- **Settings:** Music (**10% by default**), Effects, Voices, Button icons, Mouse sensitivity, Invert look, Rumble, Steadier Hands (an\\n  assist: halves the sway), Reduced Motion (no camera roll or shake), Fullscreen, Erase Save.\\n- **Controls:** shows the bindings per scheme; keyboard rebinding through `input.bindingsFor`, `rebind`, `listen`.\\n520→- **About / Credits:** paged, from one data file: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback, credits\\n  (section 19).\\n\\n**Dev Mode** (from the pause menu; absent in hub sessions; a clear panel that works with pad, keyboard and mouse):\\n\\n- **Live sliders with Reset**, per group and for all: every numeric `game.*` setting in this plan, plus `game.pebbles.mult`. They are\\n  sim settings with `when: 'now'`, changed by `inspector.set(path, value)`, so they apply at once and replays hold them. Defaults come\\n  from `inspector.describe('setting', path)` **[tested]**.\\n- **Jumps:** to any day, any activity, any finale step. Finish activity now at a chosen rank.\\n- **Cheats:** bot plays for me, no topple, unlock all, give pebbles, reset save, a throwaway save mode (`createStore` `sandbox`).\\n530→- **Tools:** time scale (0.25, 1, 4), pause and frame step, free camera (`cam3=fly`), state overlay (CoB, calm, sync, tilt, event,\\n  phase, tick, hash), seed and new seed, copy tuned settings as JSON.\\n\\n**Attract mode.** 10 s with no input on any front-end menu starts it; any key, mouse movement, click or pad input leaves it, back to\\nwhere you were. Set `game.auto` and restart the scene with `inspector.seed(n)` (restarts the sim, keeps settings **[tested]**).\\nThe sim's director runs a loop with the bot cheating freely: island flyby and jog, Pose Class, Still Pond with its lures, The Line,\\nPaddle Glide, Sand Garden, Step Class, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, then the Great Tilt's Centre Stone, about 15 s each, then round\\nagain. Banner: the game's title, \\\"DEMO\\\", \\\"Press any button\\\" with the current glyph. It never touches the save.\\n\\n**Touch-only devices** (`(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)`, no pad or key seen yet): no touch controls, no touch play. The title\\n540→shows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button that works by tap. Attract still starts and loops behind it; taps reach only\\nSettings and never start play. A key or pad input at any time returns to normal. Always pass `touch=off` in the engine's query, or it draws its own touch stick **[source; the setting is accepted: tested]**.\\n\\n**No WebGPU:** a plain, friendly page: \\\"This game needs a browser with WebGPU, such as current Chrome or Edge.\\\" Never a blank page.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Multiplayer\\n\\nThe game has two kinds, both up to four players. They share one module: `labs/game/scenes/round.ts`.\\n550→\\n**A Round** is three classes dealt from `w.rng('deal')` (same seed, same deal): Still Ring, then one of Heron, Hoop or Step, then Still\\nPond, each in its `short` 45 s form with a 6 s lead-in. About 2.7 minutes. Score: the sum, 0–3000. They all play on mats, so they need\\nnothing but the pavilion.\\n\\n### On its own: Group Class\\n\\nSame screen, up to four pads (keyboard and mouse can hold one seat). Walk up to the Pavilion or pick it from the title. Mats light in\\nseat colours as people press Cross or Space to join. Empty seats go to CPU guests (Settings can turn them off); Day 5 fields Gerald,\\nBex and Nan. CPU guests are the bot with extra sway per character. Podium at the end, and a sash reading \\\"Least Wobbly\\\".\\n560→\\nThe engine's gamepad device only follows one active pad, so the page reads `navigator.getGamepads()` itself in Group Class and sends\\neach seat's input with `engine.input.virtual.axis('game:seats', […])`.\\n\\n### In AI Hub: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, mode **ghost**\\n\\nRead the protocol once: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`. Fetch by raw URL, into\\n`labs/game/hub/`: `session.js` (save it as `session-client.txt`), and for tests `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` from the same\\nfolder. **Do not clone the ai-hub repository: it is 5.3 GB.**\\n\\n570→Why ghost: every copy runs its own seeded round, nothing depends on a host, and each player sees the others on the neighbouring mats,\\nwobbling in their seat colour. It is the source's solo ritual made social without any interference.\\n\\n**Tags** in the head, right after the client (the build injects them, section 14):\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"ghost\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"3\\\">\\n580→<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Stay the steadiest across three classes\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Lean: stick or WASD · Feet: L2 R2 or Q E · Breathe: both or Space\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:webgpu\\\" content=\\\"required\\\">\\n```\\n\\nGoal at most 60 characters, how-to at most 80. Also `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` 1-4, `hub:source` Wii Fit.\\n\\n**Startup** **[tested: this shape, on the starter's one scene, passed the harness at 2, 3 (host leaving) and 4 players with the engine running in every frame]**:\\n\\n```ts\\n590→const search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\\nfor (const key of [...search.keys()]) if (key.startsWith('aihub')) search.delete(key); // see the trap below\\nsearch.set('touch', 'off');\\nconst AIHub = window.AIHub;\\nconst info = AIHub?.active ? await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;\\nif (info) { search.set('scene', 'game:round'); search.set('seed', String(info.seed)); }\\nconst watchdog = info ? setTimeout(() => AIHub.ready(), 14000) : 0;   // the harness allows 20 s after hello\\nengine = await createEngine({ canvas, scenes: ['game:island', 'game:round'], search: `?${search}`, /* … */ });\\nif (info) { clearTimeout(watchdog); AIHub.ready(); }\\n```\\n600→\\n**The biggest trap in this whole plan [tested]:** the hub opens the page with `?aihub=1&aihub-seed=N`. `createEngine` treats every\\nunknown query key as a setting and fails with `CORE_BAD_SETTING`, **and the harness still reports PASS**, because the client keeps\\nanswering while the game is dead. Strip those keys, and make your test assert, inside every frame, `__engine.ready === true` and\\n`__engine.errors.length === 0`.\\n\\n**The round in a session:**\\n\\n- No title, menus or attract. Straight to the game's own card: the four mats in the pavilion, Plumb in front, each player standing on their\\n  mat. It shows the game's title, players in colour with who is ready, how to win, this player's controls, seconds left (from the `card` event).\\n610→  Keys: Enter or Cross answers `AIHub.answer('ready')`, Triangle or T answers `'more'`, a tap or click works too. Listen for Enter with your own\\n  `keydown` on `window`: the harness sends a synthetic one. The card has no menus or choices, and draws numbers only from `AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n  Make it HTML over whatever is drawn, because the watchdog may call `ready()` while the view is still warming up.\\n- `start` arrives; play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`. Send the sim `round.go` then (scene action). On `start` also send one\\n  `AIHub.progress({ score: 0, label: 'Breathing in' })` and one ghost message, so a slow copy still passes the harness.\\n- During play: `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` whenever it changes (label at most 24 characters, \\\"Class 2 · 640\\\"), and every 100 ms\\n  `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', e: classIndex, x, y, p: poseId, s: score }, { reliable: false })`. Never above 20 a second.\\n- Ghosts are sim entities, because only sim entities can be drawn: incoming messages go through\\n  `engine.input.virtual.axis('game:ghosts', […])` to actors on the other mats, tinted with `info.players[i].color`, leans scrubbed by their CoB,\\n  name labels from the hub as text (never as HTML), shown only while `e` matches. On `leave`, they walk off politely.\\n620→- Finished: `AIHub.result({ score, stats, awards })` once. Stats at most six, names at most 16 characters (\\\"Wobble Age\\\", \\\"Best hold\\\", \\\"Breath\\\"),\\n  values short; awards at most three, title at most 24, detail at most 40 (\\\"Unnervingly Still\\\").\\n- `time-up`: report the current score at once if you have not. `end`: `engine.stop()`, stop all sound, take no input, send nothing.\\n- If `engine.ready` is false (no WebGPU): `AIHub.fail('WebGPU required')`.\\n- **Rules in a session:** network only through the hub; no chat or custom names; no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; restart\\n  and quit do nothing. Pause is an overlay that never pauses the sim: Resume, volume, button icons. No dev mode. **Create + Options belongs to the hub:**\\n  while pad button 8 is held, ignore the engine's `pause`. Storage is a fresh memory copy, handled by the client, so write nothing special for it.\\n- Outside a session (`connect` gives `null`, or no client at all in the dev server) the game must behave exactly as it does alone.\\n\\n**Boot deadline, the one place performance matters.** The harness gives every copy 20 s to call `ready()`. **[tested on SwiftShader]** the starter\\n630→game alone needed 8–16 s per copy at four copies; the engine's playground needed 52–73 s. So `game:round` is a small separate scene with just the\\npavilion, the mats and the cast. The island's cost never reaches a session, and the watchdog covers the rest. Performance is out of scope everywhere else.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Art direction\\n\\nBase: **the engine's native style**: its puppet bodies, its toon material style, its generators. New assets are everything that makes it feel like a\\nwellness retreat; the engine's library is fine for crates and benches.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":480,\"limit\":160,\"raw_output\":\"## 8. Screens and UX\\n\\n**10-foot rules (test them at 1920 × 1080):** body text at least 2.6vh (28 px), prompts and menus 3.2vh or more; one idea per\\nscreen; at most six menu rows; 5% safe margins; focus unmistakable from a sofa. **No dense web-style data screens:** results are\\none word, one number, one sentence, one small graph. The dev panel is the only busy screen, and only developers open it.\\n\\n**UI look:** pebble-shaped rounded panels in warm off-white with soft shadows, not the source's look. A rounded system stack\\n(`ui-rounded, \\\"SF Pro Rounded\\\", \\\"Segoe UI Rounded\\\", system-ui`); fonts are allowed but not needed. Screens are HTML/CSS under\\n`engine.ui.root`, with `createMenu` for lists (hover already moves its focus, click picks **[source]**) and a small\\ngrid of your own for the Noticeboard. `createDialog` is fine for speech: keep text on screen, always, with the voice.\\nRestyle the engine's widgets for 10 feet: their defaults are small.\\n\\n**The Balance Disc** is the HUD's heart, HTML/SVG, 30vh across at the lower centre: target ring, your dot with a short trail, a\\nguide ring swelling with the breath, a foot pill either side with analog fill. It reads the sim's `wobble` each frame. A\\nmatching ring also lies on the mat in the world (a component with `telegraph`, `position`, `yaw`, `u`, `size` is drawn on the\\nfloor). In Group Class each seat gets a smaller disc under its mat.\\n\\n**Flow:**\\n\\n```\\nLoad → Title ─┬─ Begin/Continue → Pier or the day's morning → Island ⇄ Activity → Results → … → Evening → next day\\n              ├─ Free Play (Noticeboard, after Day 1)\\n              ├─ Group Class → join on mats → three classes → podium\\n              ├─ Settings\\n              └─ About / Credits\\n      (10 s idle on any front-end menu) ⇄ Attract\\n```\\n\\n- **Load:** HTML first, immediately (\\\"Calibrating…\\\"), so the screen is never blank.\\n- **Title:** the game's title over a slow orbit of the island, the menu, \\\"Press any button\\\" until a pad wakes.\\n- **Island:** a small prompt near things (\\\"✕ Talk\\\"), Triangle or Tab for today's Programme, nothing else on screen.\\n- **Programme:** today's list as up to four cards with stamps, each with \\\"Go\\\". **Noticeboard:** a 3 × 2 page of activity cards with\\n  best rank; L1, R1 or Q, E page through them.\\n- **Results:** rank word, score, pebbles falling onto a little cairn, a line from Plumb or Tare; \\\"Again\\\" or \\\"Carry on\\\".\\n- **Evening:** calendar stamp, the age graph, a closing remark, \\\"Sleep\\\".\\n- **Pause** (Options or Esc, in play and on the island; pauses the sim with `inspector.pause()`, `modal` keeps keys out of play):\\n  Resume, Restart Activity, Back to the Island, Settings, Controls, Dev Mode, About / Credits, Quit to Title.\\n- **Settings:** Music (**10% by default**), Effects, Voices, Button icons, Mouse sensitivity, Invert look, Rumble, Steadier Hands (an\\n  assist: halves the sway), Reduced Motion (no camera roll or shake), Fullscreen, Erase Save.\\n- **Controls:** shows the bindings per scheme; keyboard rebinding through `input.bindingsFor`, `rebind`, `listen`.\\n- **About / Credits:** paged, from one data file: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback, credits\\n  (section 19).\\n\\n**Dev Mode** (from the pause menu; absent in hub sessions; a clear panel that works with pad, keyboard and mouse):\\n\\n- **Live sliders with Reset**, per group and for all: every numeric `game.*` setting in this plan, plus `game.pebbles.mult`. They are\\n  sim settings with `when: 'now'`, changed by `inspector.set(path, value)`, so they apply at once and replays hold them. Defaults come\\n  from `inspector.describe('setting', path)` **[tested]**.\\n- **Jumps:** to any day, any activity, any finale step. Finish activity now at a chosen rank.\\n- **Cheats:** bot plays for me, no topple, unlock all, give pebbles, reset save, a throwaway save mode (`createStore` `sandbox`).\\n- **Tools:** time scale (0.25, 1, 4), pause and frame step, free camera (`cam3=fly`), state overlay (CoB, calm, sync, tilt, event,\\n  phase, tick, hash), seed and new seed, copy tuned settings as JSON.\\n\\n**Attract mode.** 10 s with no input on any front-end menu starts it; any key, mouse movement, click or pad input leaves it, back to\\nwhere you were. Set `game.auto` and restart the scene with `inspector.seed(n)` (restarts the sim, keeps settings **[tested]**).\\nThe sim's director runs a loop with the bot cheating freely: island flyby and jog, Pose Class, Still Pond with its lures, The Line,\\nPaddle Glide, Sand Garden, Step Class, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, then the Great Tilt's Centre Stone, about 15 s each, then round\\nagain. Banner: the game's title, \\\"DEMO\\\", \\\"Press any button\\\" with the current glyph. It never touches the save.\\n\\n**Touch-only devices** (`(hover: none) and (pointer: coarse)`, no pad or key seen yet): no touch controls, no touch play. The title\\nshows \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and a Settings button that works by tap. Attract still starts and loops behind it; taps reach only\\nSettings and never start play. A key or pad input at any time returns to normal. Always pass `touch=off` in the engine's query, or it draws its own touch stick **[source; the setting is accepted: tested]**.\\n\\n**No WebGPU:** a plain, friendly page: \\\"This game needs a browser with WebGPU, such as current Chrome or Edge.\\\" Never a blank page.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Multiplayer\\n\\nThe game has two kinds, both up to four players. They share one module: `labs/game/scenes/round.ts`.\\n\\n**A Round** is three classes dealt from `w.rng('deal')` (same seed, same deal): Still Ring, then one of Heron, Hoop or Step, then Still\\nPond, each in its `short` 45 s form with a 6 s lead-in. About 2.7 minutes. Score: the sum, 0–3000. They all play on mats, so they need\\nnothing but the pavilion.\\n\\n### On its own: Group Class\\n\\nSame screen, up to four pads (keyboard and mouse can hold one seat). Walk up to the Pavilion or pick it from the title. Mats light in\\nseat colours as people press Cross or Space to join. Empty seats go to CPU guests (Settings can turn them off); Day 5 fields Gerald,\\nBex and Nan. CPU guests are the bot with extra sway per character. Podium at the end, and a sash reading \\\"Least Wobbly\\\".\\n\\nThe engine's gamepad device only follows one active pad, so the page reads `navigator.getGamepads()` itself in Group Class and sends\\neach seat's input with `engine.input.virtual.axis('game:seats', […])`.\\n\\n### In AI Hub: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, mode **ghost**\\n\\nRead the protocol once: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`. Fetch by raw URL, into\\n`labs/game/hub/`: `session.js` (save it as `session-client.txt`), and for tests `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` from the same\\nfolder. **Do not clone the ai-hub repository: it is 5.3 GB.**\\n\\nWhy ghost: every copy runs its own seeded round, nothing depends on a host, and each player sees the others on the neighbouring mats,\\nwobbling in their seat colour. It is the source's solo ritual made social without any interference.\\n\\n**Tags** in the head, right after the client (the build injects them, section 14):\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"1-4\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"ghost\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-minutes\\\" content=\\\"3\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"Stay the steadiest across three classes\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"Lean: stick or WASD · Feet: L2 R2 or Q E · Breathe: both or Space\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:webgpu\\\" content=\\\"required\\\">\\n```\\n\\nGoal at most 60 characters, how-to at most 80. Also `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` 1-4, `hub:source` Wii Fit.\\n\\n**Startup** **[tested: this shape, on the starter's one scene, passed the harness at 2, 3 (host leaving) and 4 players with the engine running in every frame]**:\\n\\n```ts\\nconst search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\\nfor (const key of [...search.keys()]) if (key.startsWith('aihub')) search.delete(key); // see the trap below\\nsearch.set('touch', 'off');\\nconst AIHub = window.AIHub;\\nconst info = AIHub?.active ? await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;\\nif (info) { search.set('scene', 'game:round'); search.set('seed', String(info.seed)); }\\nconst watchdog = info ? setTimeout(() => AIHub.ready(), 14000) : 0;   // the harness allows 20 s after hello\\nengine = await createEngine({ canvas, scenes: ['game:island', 'game:round'], search: `?${search}`, /* … */ });\\nif (info) { clearTimeout(watchdog); AIHub.ready(); }\\n```\\n\\n**The biggest trap in this whole plan [tested]:** the hub opens the page with `?aihub=1&aihub-seed=N`. `createEngine` treats every\\nunknown query key as a setting and fails with `CORE_BAD_SETTING`, **and the harness still reports PASS**, because the client keeps\\nanswering while the game is dead. Strip those keys, and make your test assert, inside every frame, `__engine.ready === true` and\\n`__engine.errors.length === 0`.\\n\\n**The round in a session:**\\n\\n- No title, menus or attract. Straight to the game's own card: the four mats in the pavilion, Plumb in front, each player standing on their\\n  mat. It shows the game's title, players in colour with who is ready, how to win, this player's controls, seconds left (from the `card` event).\\n  Keys: Enter or Cross answers `AIHub.answer('ready')`, Triangle or T answers `'more'`, a tap or click works too. Listen for Enter with your own\\n  `keydown` on `window`: the harness sends a synthetic one. The card has no menus or choices, and draws numbers only from `AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n  Make it HTML over whatever is drawn, because the watchdog may call `ready()` while the view is still warming up.\\n- `start` arrives; play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`. Send the sim `round.go` then (scene action). On `start` also send one\\n  `AIHub.progress({ score: 0, label: 'Breathing in' })` and one ghost message, so a slow copy still passes the harness.\\n- During play: `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` whenever it changes (label at most 24 characters, \\\"Class 2 · 640\\\"), and every 100 ms\\n  `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', e: classIndex, x, y, p: poseId, s: score }, { reliable: false })`. Never above 20 a second.\\n- Ghosts are sim entities, because only sim entities can be drawn: incoming messages go through\\n  `engine.input.virtual.axis('game:ghosts', […])` to actors on the other mats, tinted with `info.players[i].color`, leans scrubbed by their CoB,\\n  name labels from the hub as text (never as HTML), shown only while `e` matches. On `leave`, they walk off politely.\\n- Finished: `AIHub.result({ score, stats, awards })` once. Stats at most six, names at most 16 characters (\\\"Wobble Age\\\", \\\"Best hold\\\", \\\"Breath\\\"),\\n  values short; awards at most three, title at most 24, detail at most 40 (\\\"Unnervingly Still\\\").\\n- `time-up`: report the current score at once if you have not. `end`: `engine.stop()`, stop all sound, take no input, send nothing.\\n- If `engine.ready` is false (no WebGPU): `AIHub.fail('WebGPU required')`.\\n- **Rules in a session:** network only through the hub; no chat or custom names; no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; restart\\n  and quit do nothing. Pause is an overlay that never pauses the sim: Resume, volume, button icons. No dev mode. **Create + Options belongs to the hub:**\\n  while pad button 8 is held, ignore the engine's `pause`. Storage is a fresh memory copy, handled by the client, so write nothing special for it.\\n- Outside a session (`connect` gives `null`, or no client at all in the dev server) the game must behave exactly as it does alone.\\n\\n**Boot deadline, the one place performance matters.** The harness gives every copy 20 s to call `ready()`. **[tested on SwiftShader]** the starter\\ngame alone needed 8–16 s per copy at four copies; the engine's playground needed 52–73 s. So `game:round` is a small separate scene with just the\\npavilion, the mats and the cast. The island's cost never reaches a session, and the watchdog covers the rest. Performance is out of scope everywhere else.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Art direction\\n\\nBase: **the engine's native style**: its puppet bodies, its toon material style, its generators. New assets are everything that makes it feel like a\\nwellness retreat; the engine's library is fine for crates and benches.\\n\\n\",\"total_lines\":1059}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3baea44a-9878-46d0-972a-4c818a5b7e31-24","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"860→- **Page to sim** goes three ways, all recorded in replays:\\n  1. Scene `actions` **[tested]**: `defineScene(id, { actions: { 'game.start': (w, args) => … } })`, called with `inspector.actions.run('game.start', { event: 'pond' })`. Runs between steps, recorded in replays. **Use this for every menu pick:** `game.begin` (with the save's state, since the sim cannot read the store), `game.start`, `game.leave`, `game.sleep`, `round.go`.\\n  2. Custom intents: `input.virtual.axis('game:mouse', [dx, dy])`. Held until changed, and dropped while `modal()` is true.\\n  3. Settings: `inspector.set(path, value)`.\\n- **Sim to page:** components read in `onFrame`, and events from `frame.world.events.trace()`.\\n\\n**Suggested layout** (each file under 400 lines, 600 is a hard error, each opening with a `@file` comment; no enums; no `any`):\\n\\n```\\nlabs/game/\\n870→  index.html  main.ts  README.md\\n  scenes/   island.ts  round.ts  flow.ts  wobble.ts  breath.ts  pilot.ts  director.ts\\n            tilt.ts  events/*.ts  bot/*.ts  data/{days,poses,charts,trays,courses,lures}.ts\\n  cast/     clips.ts  rest.json  clipdata/*.json  guest.ts  plumb.ts  tare.ts  guests.ts  critters.ts\\n  props/ geometry/ materials/ textures/ bodies/ builds/ emitters/ looks/ cameras/ sounds/\\n  ui/       screens/*.ts  hud/*.ts  glyphs.ts  lines/*.ts  dev/*.ts  theme.css  about-data.ts\\n  audio/    sound.ts  voice.ts\\n  hub/      session.ts  session-client.txt  tags.html\\n  tools/    onefile.mjs  verify.mjs\\n  replays/\\n880→```\\n\\n**Key components** (plain data): `flow` (mode: front, roam, event, cutscene, results; event, phase, tick; day), `wobble` (per seat), `seat` (input per seat), `island` (tilt), `round`, `pebble`, `plaque`, `ghost`, plus one per activity. Time in whole steps, randomness from named streams, loops in `w.query` order, `Math.pow` not `**`.\\n\\n**The single HTML [tested: 6.7 MB, 4 s, opens from file://, client stays byte-identical].** `node x html` is engine-only, so the game carries this. The session client goes in as text, so no formatter ever touches it: the hub rejects an edited copy by SHA-256.\\n\\n```js\\n// labs/game/tools/onefile.mjs  (add its @file comment). Usage: node labs/game/tools/onefile.mjs <output.html>\\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { join, resolve } from 'node:path';\\n890→import { build } from 'vite';\\nconst root = process.cwd();\\nconst result = await build({\\n  root, configFile: false, logLevel: 'warn', base: './',\\n  resolve: { alias: [{ find: /^three$/, replacement: 'three/webgpu' }] },\\n  build: { write: false, modulePreload: false, cssCodeSplit: false, assetsInlineLimit: () => true, chunkSizeWarningLimit: Infinity,\\n    rollupOptions: { input: { game: join(root, 'labs/game/index.html') }, output: { inlineDynamicImports: true } } },\\n});\\nconst files = {}; let html = '';\\nfor (const item of (Array.isArray(result) ? result : [result]).flatMap((out) => out.output)) {\\n900→  const text = item.type === 'chunk' ? item.code : typeof item.source === 'string' ? item.source : new TextDecoder().decode(item.source);\\n  if (item.fileName.endsWith('.html')) html = text; else files[item.fileName] = text;\\n}\\nconst find = (ref) => files[Object.keys(files).find((name) => ref.endsWith(name))];\\nconst LOADED = /<script type=\\\"module\\\" crossorigin src=\\\"([^\\\"]+)\\\"><\\\\/script>|<link rel=\\\"stylesheet\\\" crossorigin href=\\\"([^\\\"]+)\\\">/g;\\nconst inlined = html.replace(LOADED, (_tag, script, style) => {\\n  if (style) return `<style>${find(style)}</style>`;\\n  const code = find(script).replaceAll('</script', '<\\\\\\\\/script');\\n  if (code.includes('<!--')) throw new Error(`${script} holds \\\"<!--\\\": write it apart in the source`);\\n  return `<script type=\\\"module\\\">${code}</script>`;\\n910→});\\nconst client = readFileSync('labs/game/hub/session-client.txt', 'utf8');\\nconst tags = readFileSync('labs/game/hub/tags.html', 'utf8');\\nwriteFileSync(resolve(process.argv[2]), inlined.replace('<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\" />', () => `<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\" />\\\\n<script>\\\\n${client}</script>\\\\n${tags}`));\\n```\\n\\nUse a function as `replace`'s second argument, as here: the bundle holds `$` patterns that a string replacement would corrupt. Everything is inlined, so no CDN is needed. Run from the dev folder; write the deliverable where your TASK.md says. `npm run format` will restyle `tags.html` and this script, harmlessly **[tested: the build still passes and the client stays pristine]**; Prettier leaves the `.txt` alone.\\n\\n**`window.__game`**, a small read-only state handle the page publishes for tests: screen, attract, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, event, focus.\\n\\n920→---\\n\\n## 15. Engine traps (hit on this machine unless marked **[source]**)\\n\\n| Trap | What happens | Do this |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Hub's `?aihub=…` in the query | `CORE_BAD_SETTING`, engine dead, harness still passes | Strip them; assert `__engine.ready` in every frame |\\n| No `CHROMIUM_PATH` | `x shot` and friends crash on `/opt/pw-browsers/chromium` | Export the headless shell path (section 14) |\\n| A crashed `x` browser launch | The process hangs after printing the crash | Wrap browser commands in `timeout` |\\n| Full `chrome` binary as `CHROMIUM_PATH` | `Socket path too long`, because this run's `TMPDIR` is a long path | Use the headless shell, which works with it |\\n930→| `scripts/setup.sh` | Downloads Node and Chromium outside your folder | `npm ci` only. Node 24.22.1 here is fine |\\n| `node x new <kind>` reports failure in a game | `NEW_DOCS` lines about `labs/playground/…`: the engine's docs check tripping over its own guide. The entry is written and its test passes | Ignore those lines and don't touch the docs; note it in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` |\\n| `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` cites `labs/playground/…` | Those files are not in your workspace | Use `labs/box/`; or read the engine's GitHub repository if one is truly worth it |\\n| `playClip` in `setup` | `SIM_BAD_ARGUMENT` | `clips.play` at spawn, or from the first step |\\n| `seekClip` **[source]** | Moves only the top clip | Write `clips.state.slots[i].t` |\\n| No public way into the three.js scene **[source]** | A page cannot add a mesh | Everything drawn is a sim entity: terrain, level, prop, actor, crowd body, telegraph, emitter, or a component with `x`, `z`. All else is HTML |\\n| `modal()` true **[source]** | Intents dropped, virtual device too | Menu picks go through scene actions |\\n| Importing `three` or `rapier` **[source]** | Lint error | Public API only |\\n| `input.prompt()` **[source]** | Xbox labels for unknown pads | Own glyph table (section 4) |\\n| Engine touch stick **[source]** | Appears on touch screens | `touch=off` in the query |\\n940→| Bus defaults **[source]** | Music 0.5 | `audio.volume('music', 0.1)` |\\n| Hub client through a formatter **[source]** | Hub rejects it | Keep it as `.txt`, inject at build |\\n| `git` in the dev folder | It belongs to the larger repository around it | Do not `git init`, commit or restore there, despite `AGENTS.md`'s \\\"commit as you go\\\", unless your TASK.md asks |\\n| `actionMap` reserved codes **[source]** | `Escape`, `PadStart`, `PadHome`, `F5`, `F11`, `F12` cannot bind | Leave them to the system layer |\\n| Sim reads files **[source]** | Not allowed | Level text and data are strings or static JSON imports |\\n| Actor `player: true` during an activity **[source]** | Stick walks it off the mat | The pilot rule (section 14) |\\n| `npm run dev` | Binds port 5173 strictly, on a machine other agents share; `PORT=0` falls back to 5173 **[source]** | Prefer the built file on `file://` and `node x shot`, which picks a free port. If you must, pick a free port for `PORT` |\\n| A map with `lock: true` on a menu **[source]** | A click behind the menu takes the pointer | `lock: false` on front-end screens |\\n| Fast sway | Doing nothing beats a human thumb **[tested]** | Keep `game.wobble.noiseRate` slow; judge with the delayed-thumb bot |\\n\\n950→---\\n\\n## 16. Testing\\n\\n**One command:** `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` prints only failures and one final count line. `--quick` stops after the Node stages. Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Prefer scripts: screenshots only for what a script cannot check. Do not use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n**Node stages (seconds to run):**\\n\\n- `npm run check`.\\n- **Model:** the Wobble Model's maths; no input stays in a 0.22 ring only part of the time (about 40%), a held full stick topples, `calm` lowers sway.\\n960→- **Four players stand in for a human, since nobody here can play by hand.** All are bots writing seat input: *perfect* (no delay, gain 3); *human-like* (reads the CoB 250 ms late, gain 1.2, jitter 0.04); *sloppy* (350 ms, gain 0.8, jitter 0.06); *idle* (no input). Delay is a small history buffer in the bot's state. In rhythm and timing activities, the delay and jitter become timing error.\\n- **Every activity on 10 seeds:** the four players meet the targets in section 3 (perfect ≥ 750, human-like ≥ 550, sloppy ≥ 300, idle ≤ 500 and below human-like), random input never throws, idle finishes by itself, score within 0–1000, no `NaN`, and activity teardown leaves no stray bodies or entities.\\n- **Still Pond:** pressing a fake prompt costs Composure; a no-input run earns exactly the capped result; real breath lifts the score.\\n- **Step Class:** hits land in windows at each BPM; misses break the combo.\\n- **Morning Check:** age formula bounds; yesterday comparison.\\n- **The whole game:** a scripted playthrough with `game.auto`: every day, every activity, the finale's steps, ending at credits, on 3 seeds. It is the softlock finder. Keep it running in Node at step speed.\\n- **Reproducibility:** golden replays (one short per activity, one for a whole day) with `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim`; capture and restore continue exactly. Record goldens once content settles (`--update`, with a reason); while tuning, keep few.\\n- **Audio data:** every song and syllable renders non-silent (`node x audio`, `node x qa audio`); no audio files in the build.\\n\\n**Browser stages (the built file, on `file://` where possible).** Use the workspace's `@playwright/test` with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and flags `--no-sandbox --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Or Python Playwright, already installed. One or two browsers at once; close them. \\\"Target closed\\\" means memory: run fewer, don't retry blindly. Firefox and WebKit have no WebGPU here, so the game can only run in Chromium; use WebKit only to check the \\\"no WebGPU\\\" page.\\n970→\\n- **Boot:** `__engine.ready`, zero errors, no WebGPU downgrade errors.\\n- **Controller:** replace `navigator.getGamepads` with a stub in an init script (or the engine's `createScriptedGamepad`). Menus work on D-pad and stick, Cross and Circle; glyphs are PlayStation by default; the Settings switch shows Xbox; prompts change to keys the frame a key is pressed, and to mouse on a click.\\n- **Keyboard and mouse:** hover moves focus, click picks; keys drive the same flows; pointer-lock lean moves the CoB.\\n- **10-foot:** every visible text node at 1920 × 1080 is at least 28 px; no menu over six rows.\\n- **Attract:** starts at 10–11 s idle on the title, shows several activities across a loop, exits on a key, a click and a pad press; the save is untouched.\\n- **Pause and dev:** pause menu reaches Dev Mode; a slider changes behaviour; Reset restores defaults; jumps work; not present in a session.\\n- **Settings:** music 0.1 on a fresh save; volumes persist across reload; Erase asks in-game.\\n- **Touch-only:** Playwright's iPhone and tablet profiles in Chromium: \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\", a Settings button that opens by `page.touchscreen.tap`, attract runs, no touch controls in the DOM.\\n- **About:** every Source Information field and every feedback answer is on the screen.\\n980→- **Standalone hub checks, as the hub's own test does:** press Enter, Space, Enter, Space; the client is there and inactive; `Math.random` is untouched; the screen is drawn; no errors.\\n- **Hub harness:** serve the built file beside `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` (Node `http` server or `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, confirm the page is your game, stop only that PID: never `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`). Run `harness.html?game=<file>&players=N&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000` for N = 2, 3, 4, then `players=3&…&scenario=host-leave`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass`, with `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]` at the end. **Also assert in each frame** `__engine.ready === true`, zero engine errors, a `ghost` entity at 2 players; no console line starting `[AIHub]`; and that the pasted client's SHA-256 from banner to last line matches `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` (client 1.4.0; compare what you fetched first, the hub may have moved on).\\n- **By hand in the harness:** run it once without `auto` at 2 players: its frames are your players on this computer, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard. New round, Time up, Remove player. Drive it by script and read the state to see the card, ghosts and results; one screenshot.\\n\\n**Screenshots, at the end, by eye, 1920 × 1080:** title, island, Pose Class HUD, Still Pond with a lure, The Line, Great Tilt wide shot, results, pause, Dev Mode, About. `node x shot labs/game --png` or a Playwright screenshot: both worked here.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 17. Build order\\n\\n990→Do the risky integration first. The game should be shippable, in a smaller form, at every milestone.\\n\\n1. **Skeleton (first hour's job).** Workspace, one-file build, hub client and tags, `game:round` with one mat, harness passing with the engine truly running, `verify.mjs` skeleton.\\n2. **The verb.** Wobble Model, action maps, mouse lean, Balance Disc, glyphs, one activity (Still Ring), its bot, tests, dev sliders. Tune until holding still is fun by itself. The bot tells you it is winnable; numbers tell you it isn't trivial.\\n3. **The body.** Clip set, scrubbing, Guest and Plumb, Pose Class. Voice system early: it carries the tone.\\n4. **The island.** Terrain, sea, look, stations, roam, flow, Programme, Noticeboard, saves, Morning Check, results, Tare.\\n5. **The joke.** Still Pond. Then Cairn, plaques, day cycle.\\n6. **Water and sand.** The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden.\\n7. **The rest.** Step Class, Reps Hall, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, Island Jog.\\n8. **The finale.** Great Tilt, ending, credits.\\n1000→9. **Together.** Group Class; hub ghosts, own card, full round.\\n10. **The shell.** Attract, full Dev Mode, Settings, Controls, About, touch-only, no-WebGPU page.\\n11. **Polish.** All 200+ lines, songs, ambience, effects, camera moves, rumble, Expert variants, outfits.\\n12. **Finish.** Section 18 and 19.\\n\\n**Cut line, if and only if you run out of budget.** Cut whole things and re-flow `days.ts`, leaving no trace (no greyed-out rows, no mentions), in this order: Great Tilt step 3 → Expert variants and outfits → Hoop Patrol → Reps Hall → Island Jog's race (keep the walk) → Cliff Plunge → Step Class. Never cut: Morning Check, Pose Class, Still Pond, The Line, Sand Garden, Paddle Glide, Great Tilt steps 1, 2, 4 and 5, Group Class, hub ghost round, attract, Dev Mode, About.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 18. Done\\n1010→\\n- Every feature in this plan is real: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. `grep -rEi \\\"todo|fixme|placeholder|stub\\\" labs/game` is clean (except test words).\\n- A new player can reach the credits from the title with a pad alone, and again with keyboard and mouse alone.\\n- `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` reports zero failures; `npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the engine is unchanged or its patch is declared.\\n- The single HTML is in the place your TASK.md names, opens from `file://`, and fetches nothing.\\n- `labs/game/README.md` says what the game is and the calls you made. `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` is honest and specific.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 19. Reports\",\"content_concise\":\"860→- **Page to sim** goes three ways, all recorded in replays:\\n  1. Scene `actions` **[tested]**: `defineScene(id, { actions: { 'game.start': (w, args) => … } })`, called with `inspector.actions.run('game.start', { event: 'pond' })`. Runs between steps, recorded in replays. **Use this for every menu pick:** `game.begin` (with the save's state, since the sim cannot read the store), `game.start`, `game.leave`, `game.sleep`, `round.go`.\\n  2. Custom intents: `input.virtual.axis('game:mouse', [dx, dy])`. Held until changed, and dropped while `modal()` is true.\\n  3. Settings: `inspector.set(path, value)`.\\n- **Sim to page:** components read in `onFrame`, and events from `frame.world.events.trace()`.\\n\\n**Suggested layout** (each file under 400 lines, 600 is a hard error, each opening with a `@file` comment; no enums; no `any`):\\n\\n```\\nlabs/game/\\n870→  index.html  main.ts  README.md\\n  scenes/   island.ts  round.ts  flow.ts  wobble.ts  breath.ts  pilot.ts  director.ts\\n            tilt.ts  events/*.ts  bot/*.ts  data/{days,poses,charts,trays,courses,lures}.ts\\n  cast/     clips.ts  rest.json  clipdata/*.json  guest.ts  plumb.ts  tare.ts  guests.ts  critters.ts\\n  props/ geometry/ materials/ textures/ bodies/ builds/ emitters/ looks/ cameras/ sounds/\\n  ui/       screens/*.ts  hud/*.ts  glyphs.ts  lines/*.ts  dev/*.ts  theme.css  about-data.ts\\n  audio/    sound.ts  voice.ts\\n  hub/      session.ts  session-client.txt  tags.html\\n  tools/    onefile.mjs  verify.mjs\\n  replays/\\n880→```\\n\\n**Key components** (plain data): `flow` (mode: front, roam, event, cutscene, results; event, phase, tick; day), `wobble` (per seat), `seat` (input per seat), `island` (tilt), `round`, `pebble`, `plaque`, `ghost`, plus one per activity. Time in whole steps, randomness from named streams, loops in `w.query` order, `Math.pow` not `**`.\\n\\n**The single HTML [tested: 6.7 MB, 4 s, opens from file://, client stays byte-identical].** `node x html` is engine-only, so the game carries this. The session client goes in as text, so no formatter ever touches it: the hub rejects an edited copy by SHA-256.\\n\\n```js\\n// labs/game/tools/onefile.mjs  (add its @file comment). Usage: node labs/game/tools/onefile.mjs <output.html>\\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { join, resolve } from 'node:path';\\n890→import { build } from 'vite';\\nconst root = process.cwd();\\nconst result = await build({\\n  root, configFile: false, logLevel: 'warn', base: './',\\n  resolve: { alias: [{ find: /^three$/, replacement: 'three/webgpu' }] },\\n  build: { write: false, modulePreload: false, cssCodeSplit: false, assetsInlineLimit: () => true, chunkSizeWarningLimit: Infinity,\\n    rollupOptions: { input: { game: join(root, 'labs/game/index.html') }, output: { inlineDynamicImports: true } } },\\n});\\nconst files = {}; let html = '';\\nfor (const item of (Array.isArray(result) ? result : [result]).flatMap((out) => out.output)) {\\n900→  const text = item.type === 'chunk' ? item.code : typeof item.source === 'string' ? item.source : new TextDecoder().decode(item.source);\\n  if (item.fileName.endsWith('.html')) html = text; else files[item.fileName] = text;\\n}\\nconst find = (ref) => files[Object.keys(files).find((name) => ref.endsWith(name))];\\nconst LOADED = /<script type=\\\"module\\\" crossorigin src=\\\"([^\\\"]+)\\\"><\\\\/script>|<link rel=\\\"stylesheet\\\" crossorigin href=\\\"([^\\\"]+)\\\">/g;\\nconst inlined = html.replace(LOADED, (_tag, script, style) => {\\n  if (style) return `<style>${find(style)}</style>`;\\n  const code = find(script).replaceAll('</script', '<\\\\\\\\/script');\\n  if (code.includes('<!--')) throw new Error(`${script} holds \\\"<!--\\\": write it apart in the source`);\\n  return `<script type=\\\"module\\\">${code}</script>`;\\n910→});\\nconst client = readFileSync('labs/game/hub/session-client.txt', 'utf8');\\nconst tags = readFileSync('labs/game/hub/tags.html', 'utf8');\\nwriteFileSync(resolve(process.argv[2]), inlined.replace('<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\" />', () => `<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\" />\\\\n<script>\\\\n${client}</script>\\\\n${tags}`));\\n```\\n\\nUse a function as `replace`'s second argument, as here: the bundle holds `$` patterns that a string replacement would corrupt. Everything is inlined, so no CDN is needed. Run from the dev folder; write the deliverable where your TASK.md says. `npm run format` will restyle `tags.html` and this script, harmlessly **[tested: the build still passes and the client stays pristine]**; Prettier leaves the `.txt` alone.\\n\\n**`window.__game`**, a small read-only state handle the page publishes for tests: screen, attract, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, event, focus.\\n\\n920→---\\n\\n## 15. Engine traps (hit on this machine unless marked **[source]**)\\n\\n| Trap | What happens | Do this |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Hub's `?aihub=…` in the query | `CORE_BAD_SETTING`, engine dead, harness still passes | Strip them; assert `__engine.ready` in every frame |\\n| No `CHROMIUM_PATH` | `x shot` and friends crash on `/opt/pw-browsers/chromium` | Export the headless shell path (section 14) |\\n| A crashed `x` browser launch | The process hangs after printing the crash | Wrap browser commands in `timeout` |\\n| Full `chrome` binary as `CHROMIUM_PATH` | `Socket path too long`, because this run's `TMPDIR` is a long path | Use the headless shell, which works with it |\\n930→| `scripts/setup.sh` | Downloads Node and Chromium outside your folder | `npm ci` only. Node 24.22.1 here is fine |\\n| `node x new <kind>` reports failure in a game | `NEW_DOCS` lines about `labs/playground/…`: the engine's docs check tripping over its own guide. The entry is written and its test passes | Ignore those lines and don't touch the docs; note it in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` |\\n| `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` cites `labs/playground/…` | Those files are not in your workspace | Use `labs/box/`; or read the engine's GitHub repository if one is truly worth it |\\n| `playClip` in `setup` | `SIM_BAD_ARGUMENT` | `clips.play` at spawn, or from the first step |\\n| `seekClip` **[source]** | Moves only the top clip | Write `clips.state.slots[i].t` |\\n| No public way into the three.js scene **[source]** | A page cannot add a mesh | Everything drawn is a sim entity: terrain, level, prop, actor, crowd body, telegraph, emitter, or a component with `x`, `z`. All else is HTML |\\n| `modal()` true **[source]** | Intents dropped, virtual device too | Menu picks go through scene actions |\\n| Importing `three` or `rapier` **[source]** | Lint error | Public API only |\\n| `input.prompt()` **[source]** | Xbox labels for unknown pads | Own glyph table (section 4) |\\n| Engine touch stick **[source]** | Appears on touch screens | `touch=off` in the query |\\n940→| Bus defaults **[source]** | Music 0.5 | `audio.volume('music', 0.1)` |\\n| Hub client through a formatter **[source]** | Hub rejects it | Keep it as `.txt`, inject at build |\\n| `git` in the dev folder | It belongs to the larger repository around it | Do not `git init`, commit or restore there, despite `AGENTS.md`'s \\\"commit as you go\\\", unless your TASK.md asks |\\n| `actionMap` reserved codes **[source]** | `Escape`, `PadStart`, `PadHome`, `F5`, `F11`, `F12` cannot bind | Leave them to the system layer |\\n| Sim reads files **[source]** | Not allowed | Level text and data are strings or static JSON imports |\\n| Actor `player: true` during an activity **[source]** | Stick walks it off the mat | The pilot rule (section 14) |\\n| `npm run dev` | Binds port 5173 strictly, on a machine other agents share; `PORT=0` falls back to 5173 **[source]** | Prefer the built file on `file://` and `node x shot`, which picks a free port. If you must, pick a free port for `PORT` |\\n| A map with `lock: true` on a menu **[source]** | A click behind the menu takes the pointer | `lock: false` on front-end screens |\\n| Fast sway | Doing nothing beats a human thumb **[tested]** | Keep `game.wobble.noiseRate` slow; judge with the delayed-thumb bot |\\n\\n950→---\\n\\n## 16. Testing\\n\\n**One command:** `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` prints only failures and one final count line. `--quick` stops after the Node stages. Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Prefer scripts: screenshots only for what a script cannot check. Do not use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n**Node stages (seconds to run):**\\n\\n- `npm run check`.\\n- **Model:** the Wobble Model's maths; no input stays in a 0.22 ring only part of the time (about 40%), a held full stick topples, `calm` lowers sway.\\n960→- **Four players stand in for a human, since nobody here can play by hand.** All are bots writing seat input: *perfect* (no delay, gain 3); *human-like* (reads the CoB 250 ms late, gain 1.2, jitter 0.04); *sloppy* (350 ms, gain 0.8, jitter 0.06); *idle* (no input). Delay is a small history buffer in the bot's state. In rhythm and timing activities, the delay and jitter become timing error.\\n- **Every activity on 10 seeds:** the four players meet the targets in section 3 (perfect ≥ 750, human-like ≥ 550, sloppy ≥ 300, idle ≤ 500 and below human-like), random input never throws, idle finishes by itself, score within 0–1000, no `NaN`, and activity teardown leaves no stray bodies or entities.\\n- **Still Pond:** pressing a fake prompt costs Composure; a no-input run earns exactly the capped result; real breath lifts the score.\\n- **Step Class:** hits land in windows at each BPM; misses break the combo.\\n- **Morning Check:** age formula bounds; yesterday comparison.\\n- **The whole game:** a scripted playthrough with `game.auto`: every day, every activity, the finale's steps, ending at credits, on 3 seeds. It is the softlock finder. Keep it running in Node at step speed.\\n- **Reproducibility:** golden replays (one short per activity, one for a whole day) with `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim`; capture and restore continue exactly. Record goldens once content settles (`--update`, with a reason); while tuning, keep few.\\n- **Audio data:** every song and syllable renders non-silent (`node x audio`, `node x qa audio`); no audio files in the build.\\n\\n**Browser stages (the built file, on `file://` where possible).** Use the workspace's `@playwright/test` with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and flags `--no-sandbox --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Or Python Playwright, already installed. One or two browsers at once; close them. \\\"Target closed\\\" means memory: run fewer, don't retry blindly. Firefox and WebKit have no WebGPU here, so the game can only run in Chromium; use WebKit only to check the \\\"no WebGPU\\\" page.\\n970→\\n- **Boot:** `__engine.ready`, zero errors, no WebGPU downgrade errors.\\n- **Controller:** replace `navigator.getGamepads` with a stub in an init script (or the engine's `createScriptedGamepad`). Menus work on D-pad and stick, Cross and Circle; glyphs are PlayStation by default; the Settings switch shows Xbox; prompts change to keys the frame a key is pressed, and to mouse on a click.\\n- **Keyboard and mouse:** hover moves focus, click picks; keys drive the same flows; pointer-lock lean moves the CoB.\\n- **10-foot:** every visible text node at 1920 × 1080 is at least 28 px; no menu over six rows.\\n- **Attract:** starts at 10–11 s idle on the title, shows several activities across a loop, exits on a key, a click and a pad press; the save is untouched.\\n- **Pause and dev:** pause menu reaches Dev Mode; a slider changes behaviour; Reset restores defaults; jumps work; not present in a session.\\n- **Settings:** music 0.1 on a fresh save; volumes persist across reload; Erase asks in-game.\\n- **Touch-only:** Playwright's iPhone and tablet profiles in Chromium: \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\", a Settings button that opens by `page.touchscreen.tap`, attract runs, no touch controls in the DOM.\\n- **About:** every Source Information field and every feedback answer is on the screen.\\n980→- **Standalone hub checks, as the hub's own test does:** press Enter, Space, Enter, Space; the client is there and inactive; `Math.random` is untouched; the screen is drawn; no errors.\\n- **Hub harness:** serve the built file beside `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` (Node `http` server or `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, confirm the page is your game, stop only that PID: never `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`). Run `harness.html?game=<file>&players=N&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000` for N = 2, 3, 4, then `players=3&…&scenario=host-leave`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass`, with `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]` at the end. **Also assert in each frame** `__engine.ready === true`, zero engine errors, a `ghost` entity at 2 players; no console line starting `[AIHub]`; and that the pasted client's SHA-256 from banner to last line matches `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` (client 1.4.0; compare what you fetched first, the hub may have moved on).\\n- **By hand in the harness:** run it once without `auto` at 2 players: its frames are your players on this computer, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard. New round, Time up, Remove player. Drive it by script and read the state to see the card, ghosts and results; one screenshot.\\n\\n**Screenshots, at the end, by eye, 1920 × 1080:** title, island, Pose Class HUD, Still Pond with a lure, The Line, Great Tilt wide shot, results, pause, Dev Mode, About. `node x shot labs/game --png` or a Playwright screenshot: both worked here.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 17. Build order\\n\\n990→Do the risky integration first. The game should be shippable, in a smaller form, at every milestone.\\n\\n1. **Skeleton (first hour's job).** Workspace, one-file build, hub client and tags, `game:round` with one mat, harness passing with the engine truly running, `verify.mjs` skeleton.\\n2. **The verb.** Wobble Model, action maps, mouse lean, Balance Disc, glyphs, one activity (Still Ring), its bot, tests, dev sliders. Tune until holding still is fun by itself. The bot tells you it is winnable; numbers tell you it isn't trivial.\\n3. **The body.** Clip set, scrubbing, Guest and Plumb, Pose Class. Voice system early: it carries the tone.\\n4. **The island.** Terrain, sea, look, stations, roam, flow, Programme, Noticeboard, saves, Morning Check, results, Tare.\\n5. **The joke.** Still Pond. Then Cairn, plaques, day cycle.\\n6. **Water and sand.** The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden.\\n7. **The rest.** Step Class, Reps Hall, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, Island Jog.\\n8. **The finale.** Great Tilt, ending, credits.\\n1000→9. **Together.** Group Class; hub ghosts, own card, full round.\\n10. **The shell.** Attract, full Dev Mode, Settings, Controls, About, touch-only, no-WebGPU page.\\n11. **Polish.** All 200+ lines, songs, ambience, effects, camera moves, rumble, Expert variants, outfits.\\n12. **Finish.** Section 18 and 19.\\n\\n**Cut line, if and only if you run out of budget.** Cut whole things and re-flow `days.ts`, leaving no trace (no greyed-out rows, no mentions), in this order: Great Tilt step 3 → Expert variants and outfits → Hoop Patrol → Reps Hall → Island Jog's race (keep the walk) → Cliff Plunge → Step Class. Never cut: Morning Check, Pose Class, Still Pond, The Line, Sand Garden, Paddle Glide, Great Tilt steps 1, 2, 4 and 5, Group Class, hub ghost round, attract, Dev Mode, About.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 18. Done\\n1010→\\n- Every feature in this plan is real: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. `grep -rEi \\\"todo|fixme|placeholder|stub\\\" labs/game` is clean (except test words).\\n- A new player can reach the credits from the title with a pad alone, and again with keyboard and mouse alone.\\n- `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` reports zero failures; `npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the engine is unchanged or its patch is declared.\\n- The single HTML is in the place your TASK.md names, opens from `file://`, and fetches nothing.\\n- `labs/game/README.md` says what the game is and the calls you made. `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` is honest and specific.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 19. Reports\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":860,\"limit\":160,\"raw_output\":\"- **Page to sim** goes three ways, all recorded in replays:\\n  1. Scene `actions` **[tested]**: `defineScene(id, { actions: { 'game.start': (w, args) => … } })`, called with `inspector.actions.run('game.start', { event: 'pond' })`. Runs between steps, recorded in replays. **Use this for every menu pick:** `game.begin` (with the save's state, since the sim cannot read the store), `game.start`, `game.leave`, `game.sleep`, `round.go`.\\n  2. Custom intents: `input.virtual.axis('game:mouse', [dx, dy])`. Held until changed, and dropped while `modal()` is true.\\n  3. Settings: `inspector.set(path, value)`.\\n- **Sim to page:** components read in `onFrame`, and events from `frame.world.events.trace()`.\\n\\n**Suggested layout** (each file under 400 lines, 600 is a hard error, each opening with a `@file` comment; no enums; no `any`):\\n\\n```\\nlabs/game/\\n  index.html  main.ts  README.md\\n  scenes/   island.ts  round.ts  flow.ts  wobble.ts  breath.ts  pilot.ts  director.ts\\n            tilt.ts  events/*.ts  bot/*.ts  data/{days,poses,charts,trays,courses,lures}.ts\\n  cast/     clips.ts  rest.json  clipdata/*.json  guest.ts  plumb.ts  tare.ts  guests.ts  critters.ts\\n  props/ geometry/ materials/ textures/ bodies/ builds/ emitters/ looks/ cameras/ sounds/\\n  ui/       screens/*.ts  hud/*.ts  glyphs.ts  lines/*.ts  dev/*.ts  theme.css  about-data.ts\\n  audio/    sound.ts  voice.ts\\n  hub/      session.ts  session-client.txt  tags.html\\n  tools/    onefile.mjs  verify.mjs\\n  replays/\\n```\\n\\n**Key components** (plain data): `flow` (mode: front, roam, event, cutscene, results; event, phase, tick; day), `wobble` (per seat), `seat` (input per seat), `island` (tilt), `round`, `pebble`, `plaque`, `ghost`, plus one per activity. Time in whole steps, randomness from named streams, loops in `w.query` order, `Math.pow` not `**`.\\n\\n**The single HTML [tested: 6.7 MB, 4 s, opens from file://, client stays byte-identical].** `node x html` is engine-only, so the game carries this. The session client goes in as text, so no formatter ever touches it: the hub rejects an edited copy by SHA-256.\\n\\n```js\\n// labs/game/tools/onefile.mjs  (add its @file comment). Usage: node labs/game/tools/onefile.mjs <output.html>\\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { join, resolve } from 'node:path';\\nimport { build } from 'vite';\\nconst root = process.cwd();\\nconst result = await build({\\n  root, configFile: false, logLevel: 'warn', base: './',\\n  resolve: { alias: [{ find: /^three$/, replacement: 'three/webgpu' }] },\\n  build: { write: false, modulePreload: false, cssCodeSplit: false, assetsInlineLimit: () => true, chunkSizeWarningLimit: Infinity,\\n    rollupOptions: { input: { game: join(root, 'labs/game/index.html') }, output: { inlineDynamicImports: true } } },\\n});\\nconst files = {}; let html = '';\\nfor (const item of (Array.isArray(result) ? result : [result]).flatMap((out) => out.output)) {\\n  const text = item.type === 'chunk' ? item.code : typeof item.source === 'string' ? item.source : new TextDecoder().decode(item.source);\\n  if (item.fileName.endsWith('.html')) html = text; else files[item.fileName] = text;\\n}\\nconst find = (ref) => files[Object.keys(files).find((name) => ref.endsWith(name))];\\nconst LOADED = /<script type=\\\"module\\\" crossorigin src=\\\"([^\\\"]+)\\\"><\\\\/script>|<link rel=\\\"stylesheet\\\" crossorigin href=\\\"([^\\\"]+)\\\">/g;\\nconst inlined = html.replace(LOADED, (_tag, script, style) => {\\n  if (style) return `<style>${find(style)}</style>`;\\n  const code = find(script).replaceAll('</script', '<\\\\\\\\/script');\\n  if (code.includes('<!--')) throw new Error(`${script} holds \\\"<!--\\\": write it apart in the source`);\\n  return `<script type=\\\"module\\\">${code}</script>`;\\n});\\nconst client = readFileSync('labs/game/hub/session-client.txt', 'utf8');\\nconst tags = readFileSync('labs/game/hub/tags.html', 'utf8');\\nwriteFileSync(resolve(process.argv[2]), inlined.replace('<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\" />', () => `<meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\" />\\\\n<script>\\\\n${client}</script>\\\\n${tags}`));\\n```\\n\\nUse a function as `replace`'s second argument, as here: the bundle holds `$` patterns that a string replacement would corrupt. Everything is inlined, so no CDN is needed. Run from the dev folder; write the deliverable where your TASK.md says. `npm run format` will restyle `tags.html` and this script, harmlessly **[tested: the build still passes and the client stays pristine]**; Prettier leaves the `.txt` alone.\\n\\n**`window.__game`**, a small read-only state handle the page publishes for tests: screen, attract, last device, glyph set, volumes, day, event, focus.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. Engine traps (hit on this machine unless marked **[source]**)\\n\\n| Trap | What happens | Do this |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Hub's `?aihub=…` in the query | `CORE_BAD_SETTING`, engine dead, harness still passes | Strip them; assert `__engine.ready` in every frame |\\n| No `CHROMIUM_PATH` | `x shot` and friends crash on `/opt/pw-browsers/chromium` | Export the headless shell path (section 14) |\\n| A crashed `x` browser launch | The process hangs after printing the crash | Wrap browser commands in `timeout` |\\n| Full `chrome` binary as `CHROMIUM_PATH` | `Socket path too long`, because this run's `TMPDIR` is a long path | Use the headless shell, which works with it |\\n| `scripts/setup.sh` | Downloads Node and Chromium outside your folder | `npm ci` only. Node 24.22.1 here is fine |\\n| `node x new <kind>` reports failure in a game | `NEW_DOCS` lines about `labs/playground/…`: the engine's docs check tripping over its own guide. The entry is written and its test passes | Ignore those lines and don't touch the docs; note it in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` |\\n| `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` cites `labs/playground/…` | Those files are not in your workspace | Use `labs/box/`; or read the engine's GitHub repository if one is truly worth it |\\n| `playClip` in `setup` | `SIM_BAD_ARGUMENT` | `clips.play` at spawn, or from the first step |\\n| `seekClip` **[source]** | Moves only the top clip | Write `clips.state.slots[i].t` |\\n| No public way into the three.js scene **[source]** | A page cannot add a mesh | Everything drawn is a sim entity: terrain, level, prop, actor, crowd body, telegraph, emitter, or a component with `x`, `z`. All else is HTML |\\n| `modal()` true **[source]** | Intents dropped, virtual device too | Menu picks go through scene actions |\\n| Importing `three` or `rapier` **[source]** | Lint error | Public API only |\\n| `input.prompt()` **[source]** | Xbox labels for unknown pads | Own glyph table (section 4) |\\n| Engine touch stick **[source]** | Appears on touch screens | `touch=off` in the query |\\n| Bus defaults **[source]** | Music 0.5 | `audio.volume('music', 0.1)` |\\n| Hub client through a formatter **[source]** | Hub rejects it | Keep it as `.txt`, inject at build |\\n| `git` in the dev folder | It belongs to the larger repository around it | Do not `git init`, commit or restore there, despite `AGENTS.md`'s \\\"commit as you go\\\", unless your TASK.md asks |\\n| `actionMap` reserved codes **[source]** | `Escape`, `PadStart`, `PadHome`, `F5`, `F11`, `F12` cannot bind | Leave them to the system layer |\\n| Sim reads files **[source]** | Not allowed | Level text and data are strings or static JSON imports |\\n| Actor `player: true` during an activity **[source]** | Stick walks it off the mat | The pilot rule (section 14) |\\n| `npm run dev` | Binds port 5173 strictly, on a machine other agents share; `PORT=0` falls back to 5173 **[source]** | Prefer the built file on `file://` and `node x shot`, which picks a free port. If you must, pick a free port for `PORT` |\\n| A map with `lock: true` on a menu **[source]** | A click behind the menu takes the pointer | `lock: false` on front-end screens |\\n| Fast sway | Doing nothing beats a human thumb **[tested]** | Keep `game.wobble.noiseRate` slow; judge with the delayed-thumb bot |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 16. Testing\\n\\n**One command:** `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` prints only failures and one final count line. `--quick` stops after the Node stages. Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Prefer scripts: screenshots only for what a script cannot check. Do not use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n**Node stages (seconds to run):**\\n\\n- `npm run check`.\\n- **Model:** the Wobble Model's maths; no input stays in a 0.22 ring only part of the time (about 40%), a held full stick topples, `calm` lowers sway.\\n- **Four players stand in for a human, since nobody here can play by hand.** All are bots writing seat input: *perfect* (no delay, gain 3); *human-like* (reads the CoB 250 ms late, gain 1.2, jitter 0.04); *sloppy* (350 ms, gain 0.8, jitter 0.06); *idle* (no input). Delay is a small history buffer in the bot's state. In rhythm and timing activities, the delay and jitter become timing error.\\n- **Every activity on 10 seeds:** the four players meet the targets in section 3 (perfect ≥ 750, human-like ≥ 550, sloppy ≥ 300, idle ≤ 500 and below human-like), random input never throws, idle finishes by itself, score within 0–1000, no `NaN`, and activity teardown leaves no stray bodies or entities.\\n- **Still Pond:** pressing a fake prompt costs Composure; a no-input run earns exactly the capped result; real breath lifts the score.\\n- **Step Class:** hits land in windows at each BPM; misses break the combo.\\n- **Morning Check:** age formula bounds; yesterday comparison.\\n- **The whole game:** a scripted playthrough with `game.auto`: every day, every activity, the finale's steps, ending at credits, on 3 seeds. It is the softlock finder. Keep it running in Node at step speed.\\n- **Reproducibility:** golden replays (one short per activity, one for a whole day) with `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim`; capture and restore continue exactly. Record goldens once content settles (`--update`, with a reason); while tuning, keep few.\\n- **Audio data:** every song and syllable renders non-silent (`node x audio`, `node x qa audio`); no audio files in the build.\\n\\n**Browser stages (the built file, on `file://` where possible).** Use the workspace's `@playwright/test` with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and flags `--no-sandbox --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Or Python Playwright, already installed. One or two browsers at once; close them. \\\"Target closed\\\" means memory: run fewer, don't retry blindly. Firefox and WebKit have no WebGPU here, so the game can only run in Chromium; use WebKit only to check the \\\"no WebGPU\\\" page.\\n\\n- **Boot:** `__engine.ready`, zero errors, no WebGPU downgrade errors.\\n- **Controller:** replace `navigator.getGamepads` with a stub in an init script (or the engine's `createScriptedGamepad`). Menus work on D-pad and stick, Cross and Circle; glyphs are PlayStation by default; the Settings switch shows Xbox; prompts change to keys the frame a key is pressed, and to mouse on a click.\\n- **Keyboard and mouse:** hover moves focus, click picks; keys drive the same flows; pointer-lock lean moves the CoB.\\n- **10-foot:** every visible text node at 1920 × 1080 is at least 28 px; no menu over six rows.\\n- **Attract:** starts at 10–11 s idle on the title, shows several activities across a loop, exits on a key, a click and a pad press; the save is untouched.\\n- **Pause and dev:** pause menu reaches Dev Mode; a slider changes behaviour; Reset restores defaults; jumps work; not present in a session.\\n- **Settings:** music 0.1 on a fresh save; volumes persist across reload; Erase asks in-game.\\n- **Touch-only:** Playwright's iPhone and tablet profiles in Chromium: \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\", a Settings button that opens by `page.touchscreen.tap`, attract runs, no touch controls in the DOM.\\n- **About:** every Source Information field and every feedback answer is on the screen.\\n- **Standalone hub checks, as the hub's own test does:** press Enter, Space, Enter, Space; the client is there and inactive; `Math.random` is untouched; the screen is drawn; no errors.\\n- **Hub harness:** serve the built file beside `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` (Node `http` server or `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, confirm the page is your game, stop only that PID: never `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`). Run `harness.html?game=<file>&players=N&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000` for N = 2, 3, 4, then `players=3&…&scenario=host-leave`. Pass means `window.__hubCheck.pass`, with `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]` at the end. **Also assert in each frame** `__engine.ready === true`, zero engine errors, a `ghost` entity at 2 players; no console line starting `[AIHub]`; and that the pasted client's SHA-256 from banner to last line matches `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` (client 1.4.0; compare what you fetched first, the hub may have moved on).\\n- **By hand in the harness:** run it once without `auto` at 2 players: its frames are your players on this computer, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard. New round, Time up, Remove player. Drive it by script and read the state to see the card, ghosts and results; one screenshot.\\n\\n**Screenshots, at the end, by eye, 1920 × 1080:** title, island, Pose Class HUD, Still Pond with a lure, The Line, Great Tilt wide shot, results, pause, Dev Mode, About. `node x shot labs/game --png` or a Playwright screenshot: both worked here.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 17. Build order\\n\\nDo the risky integration first. The game should be shippable, in a smaller form, at every milestone.\\n\\n1. **Skeleton (first hour's job).** Workspace, one-file build, hub client and tags, `game:round` with one mat, harness passing with the engine truly running, `verify.mjs` skeleton.\\n2. **The verb.** Wobble Model, action maps, mouse lean, Balance Disc, glyphs, one activity (Still Ring), its bot, tests, dev sliders. Tune until holding still is fun by itself. The bot tells you it is winnable; numbers tell you it isn't trivial.\\n3. **The body.** Clip set, scrubbing, Guest and Plumb, Pose Class. Voice system early: it carries the tone.\\n4. **The island.** Terrain, sea, look, stations, roam, flow, Programme, Noticeboard, saves, Morning Check, results, Tare.\\n5. **The joke.** Still Pond. Then Cairn, plaques, day cycle.\\n6. **Water and sand.** The Line, Paddle Glide, Sand Garden.\\n7. **The rest.** Step Class, Reps Hall, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, Island Jog.\\n8. **The finale.** Great Tilt, ending, credits.\\n9. **Together.** Group Class; hub ghosts, own card, full round.\\n10. **The shell.** Attract, full Dev Mode, Settings, Controls, About, touch-only, no-WebGPU page.\\n11. **Polish.** All 200+ lines, songs, ambience, effects, camera moves, rumble, Expert variants, outfits.\\n12. **Finish.** Section 18 and 19.\\n\\n**Cut line, if and only if you run out of budget.** Cut whole things and re-flow `days.ts`, leaving no trace (no greyed-out rows, no mentions), in this order: Great Tilt step 3 → Expert variants and outfits → Hoop Patrol → Reps Hall → Island Jog's race (keep the walk) → Cliff Plunge → Step Class. Never cut: Morning Check, Pose Class, Still Pond, The Line, Sand Garden, Paddle Glide, Great Tilt steps 1, 2, 4 and 5, Group Class, hub ghost round, attract, Dev Mode, About.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 18. Done\\n\\n- Every feature in this plan is real: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. `grep -rEi \\\"todo|fixme|placeholder|stub\\\" labs/game` is clean (except test words).\\n- A new player can reach the credits from the title with a pad alone, and again with keyboard and mouse alone.\\n- `node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs` reports zero failures; `npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the engine is unchanged or its patch is declared.\\n- The single HTML is in the place your TASK.md names, opens from `file://`, and fetches nothing.\\n- `labs/game/README.md` says what the game is and the calls you made. `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` is honest and specific.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 19. Reports\\n\",\"total_lines\":1059}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3baea44a-9878-46d0-972a-4c818a5b7e31-25","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101