= 0) out['game:r'] = clamp(stick.x, 0, 1);\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/scenes/island.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":256,\"content\":\"  const move = intents.move;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/scenes/round.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":68,\"content\":\"export const round = defineScene('game:round', {\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/scenes/events/pond.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":12,\"content\":\"import { actOf, apply, cfgOf, readSeat, rowOf, sweep } from './kit';\"},{\"line_number\":38,\"content\":\"  const seat = readSeat(intents);\"},{\"line_number\":65,\"content\":\"  if (readSeat(intents).both) act.pose = 1;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/scenes/events/kit.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":53,\"content\":\"export function readSeat(intents: Intents, seat = 0): Seat {\"},{\"line_number\":67,\"content\":\"  const mouse = pair(intents, 'game:mouse');\"},{\"line_number\":70,\"content\":\"    x: num(intents, 'game:r') - num(intents, 'game:l'),\"},{\"line_number\":123,\"content\":\"  const seat = readSeat(intents, seatIndex);\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"48205cfc-a419-4ef0-acfd-66c4331bc6ee"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_314","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Reviewing the action map values.\n\nMapping game actions to keys and controllers. Right, left, forward, and back use WASD and arrow keys plus the left stick. Footl and footr map to Q/E and the mouse buttons. Breathing uses Space and both triggers.\n\nSpace is breathe only, not act. This prevents disturbing the pond. Act maps to F and Enter and PadSouth. Gentle maps to ShiftLeft and PadL1. PadSouth is both act and uiAccept, which risks triggering accept while playing.\n\nModal mode drops the `act` action from reaching the simulation, so PadSouth cannot trigger an unwanted act during menus. The menu still receives `uiAccept` directly from input, keeping the accept behavior intact.\n\nWASD moves the island character through intents.move. Activities zero actor intents so WASD does not conflict with them.\n\nMouse lean sends game:mouse each frame from captured deltas. It must be cleared when no delta exists to prevent the axis from sticking.\n\nThe engine samples virtual axes before onFrame runs. Setting the mouse lean axis inside onFrame would apply it to the next frame and leave it stuck at the last value. It must be cleared to zero immediately after sampling, or set every frame before that point.\n\nMouse lean will use a one-frame lag: deltas accumulate each onFrame, then zero out if no new movement occurs. This prevents the axis from sticking.\n\nFor seats, the page reads gamepads and sets game:seats every frame during group activities.\n\nGlyphs are tracked per device type rather than through input.prompt. The capture listeners record the last detected device kind. The glyph set switches based on that device: PlayStation default, Xbox after a settings change, keys after keyboard input, and mouse after a click. Settings stores the icon choice as either 'playstation' or 'xbox'.\n\nAttract mode activates after ten seconds of idle input on front-end menus. It sets game.auto and reseeds the inspector without writing to save. Input exits attract mode and resets the seed again. Touch devices detect coarse pointers and show a controller required prompt with a settings button.\n\nTouch input is disabled in attract mode. Taps on settings do not exit attract or start play.\n\nWebGPU support is checked before engine creation. A missing WebGPU instance shows a message and fails the AIHub.\n\nThe hub strips AIHub keys, forces touch off, sets post look to retreat, and uses chase camera. If AIHub is active it connects, seeds the scene, and waits for the 14000ms watchdog before marking ready. Card HTML is shown for answer entry. T or Triangle selects answers. On start round.go begins progress and ghost updates. During play these update every 100ms. The pause overlay does not pause the simulation. Dev mode is disabled. Store mode memory is considered.\n\nThe hub will use demo store mode to avoid writing to the user's real localStorage. The pause overlay shows Resume, volume, and button icons without pausing the inspector. The group class event is attached to the island scene by default.\n\nGroup Class can run on the island scene. The pavilion scene is reserved for the hub. The plan calls for walking up to the Pavilion or picking it from the title, with all participants playing on mats.\n\nStarting Group Class from the island title triggers game.start with event 'group'. If game.cpu is set to 1, the group simulation handles CPU players automatically. The page sends game:seats for any extra pads.\n\nChecking group.ts to confirm whether the page must send seat data. The save object is stored as JSON under the key 'save' and passed to game.begin. The flow modes are title, roam, event, results, evening, and credits. Page screens include the title menu, touch gate overlay, programme, settings, controls, about, dev, pause, erase, results, evening, credits, play HUD, attract banner, and hub card.\n\nThe global game state object will hold title, screen, attract, lastDevice, glyphSet, volumes, day, event, and focus. Key, mouse, and pad listeners update it in the same frame. The title menu lists six items: Begin, Continue, Free Play, Group Class, Settings, and About. The pause menu splits into two pages of six options each. Settings will be paginated.\n\nSettings will use a two-page menu. Page one holds Music, Effects, Voices, Icons, Assist, and More. Page two holds CPU guests, Erase, and Back.\n\nVolume sliders will cycle through preset levels on pick. The full TUNABLES list of eighteen items will be split into five-item pages with a Next button.\n\nThe dev menu will show four tunables per page with Next and Reset rows. Picking a tunable increments its value, and uiLeft decrements it while the screen is open. Jumps are paginated across days one through five, activities, finale steps, and finish rank.\n\nAttract mode restarts the scene seed and returns to title, then starts the first frame pose. Exiting attract restores the previous screen instead of forcing the title menu. A fake pause panel for pond lures is drawn as a banner rather than a real menu, so it does not count toward the six-item limit; any input still costs composure.\n\nDiscs will render as HTML or SVG at 30vh from wobble x and y. Body text must be at least 28px, set via CSS max(28px, 2.6vh) on [data-ui]. The music track list covers title and roam lobby, morning and pose stillness, line and paddle water, garden rake, tilt storm tilt, dawn and credits dawn, and results stamp once with loop false. Voice announcements trigger say() on line changes. voice.ts is page-side under labs/game/audio so the page can import speakerFor and say without breaking the simulation. The file must stay under the 600-line ESLint limit.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-c88c5b9a-17a3-4361-ba15-3360b5e425a8-114","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"texture:water","glob":"**/*.{ts,js}","head_limit":15}},{"type":"tool_use","id"