{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-78ae9884-8e1b-4ee1-9481-4e93b2301942-12","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Runs the repository checks no standard tool covers, last in `npm run check` (PLAN.md §8.1).\\n *\\n * Plugins: the asset scan, here, and the `check` a module in `tools/cmd/` may export (`export const check:\\n * CheckPlugin`), which is how dependency pins, docs drift and escalation records arrive (WPs 0.3, 0.6, 0.7).\\n *\\n * The asset scan (doctrine: Assets) reads every file git tracks or would track. A binary file (by extension, magic\\n * bytes or a NUL byte) passes only if `data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json` lists it, `{ path, escalation: \\\"ESC-NNNN\\\",\\n * reason }`, with its record in `docs/escalations/`, or if it is a font (WOFF2, TTF, OTF) under `data/fonts/` with\\n10→ * its license beside it. A base64 run or `data:` URI over 1 KB fails too. Else: `x esc open --principle Assets`.\\n * In a game made from the engine (`isGame`, ADR-0024), which has no `x esc` and keeps the engine's files read-only, the\\n * game's own approvals are `APPROVED-BINARIES.json` at its root, `{ path, reason }` (the reason: the owner's answer),\\n * its fonts go anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them, and the engine's approvals stand as its\\n * repository checked them. The one-file playground (`ONE_FILE`, playground.html) is not scanned: a build of the scanned\\n * sources, it carries its npm dependencies' binaries inline (Rapier's WebAssembly, three.js's SMAA textures), as the\\n * owner asked of a self-contained file (ADR-0023 amendment 5). A file deleted between the listing and its read is skipped (`scanAssets`): at gate G6's\\n * review, `x new module --test` wrote and removed its sample in the repository while a parallel `x check` scanned,\\n * which crashed on the read.\\n *\\n20→ * Lane mode is detected: in a linked git worktree (`--git-dir` differs from `--git-common-dir`), an ultracode lane,\\n * a `warnInLane` plugin (docs drift) only warns (§11.3). `X_LANE=0|1` overrides the detection.\\n *\\n * Usage: node x check. Exit 0 when every plugin passes, 1 otherwise.\\n * @see tools/cmd/check.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, lstatSync, readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { basename, dirname, extname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\\nimport { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\\n30→import { ONE_FILE } from '../lib/oneFile';\\nimport type { Finding } from '../lib/report';\\nimport { closest, commandNames, isGame, type Command, type CommandResult } from '../x';\\n\\n/** What a plugin is given: the repository root, and whether this checkout is an ultracode lane. */\\nexport interface CheckContext {\\n  root: string;\\n  lane: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n40→/** What a plugin found. */\\nexport interface CheckOutcome {\\n  failures: Finding[];\\n  warnings?: Finding[];\\n  /** Named numbers for the report, prefixed with the plugin's name by `x check`. */\\n  metrics?: Record<string, number | string | boolean>;\\n  /** One short line for the output, such as `214 files`. */\\n  summary?: string;\\n}\\n\\n50→/** One repository check. A `tools/cmd/<name>.ts` module plugs one in by exporting it as `check`. */\\nexport interface CheckPlugin {\\n  /** Its name in the output and the metrics: `assets`, `deps`, `docs`, `esc`. */\\n  name: string;\\n  /** In an ultracode lane, its failures are warnings: the integrator regenerates what it checks (PLAN.md §11.3). */\\n  warnInLane?: boolean;\\n  run(context: CheckContext): CheckOutcome | Promise<CheckOutcome>;\\n}\\n\\n/** Extensions of binary formats, by name, so a file is caught even when its bytes look like text. */\\n60→const BINARY_EXTENSIONS = new Set(\\n  (\\n    'png jpg jpeg gif webp avif bmp ico tif tiff psd exr hdr ktx ktx2 basis dds glb bin wasm fbx blend ' +\\n    'mp3 wav ogg oga flac m4a aac opus mp4 webm mov avi mkv zip gz tgz bz2 xz 7z rar jar pdf exe dll so dylib ' +\\n    'woff woff2 ttf otf eot npy npz'\\n  ).split(' '),\\n);\\n/** The pre-approved font formats (DOCTRINE.md, Assets). */\\nconst FONT_EXTENSIONS = new Set(['woff2', 'ttf', 'otf']);\\n/** Leading bytes of common binary formats, to name what a file is. */\\n70→const MAGIC: [string, number[]][] = [\\n  ['PNG', [0x89, 0x50, 0x4e, 0x47]],\\n  ['JPEG', [0xff, 0xd8, 0xff]],\\n  ['GIF', [0x47, 0x49, 0x46, 0x38]],\\n  ['RIFF (WAV, WebP, AVI)', [0x52, 0x49, 0x46, 0x46]],\\n  ['Ogg', [0x4f, 0x67, 0x67, 0x53]],\\n  ['FLAC', [0x66, 0x4c, 0x61, 0x43]],\\n  ['MP3', [0x49, 0x44, 0x33]],\\n  ['ZIP', [0x50, 0x4b, 0x03, 0x04]],\\n  ['gzip', [0x1f, 0x8b]],\\n80→  ['PDF', [0x25, 0x50, 0x44, 0x46]],\\n  ['WebAssembly', [0x00, 0x61, 0x73, 0x6d]],\\n  ['glTF binary', [0x67, 0x6c, 0x54, 0x46]],\\n  ['WOFF', [0x77, 0x4f, 0x46, 0x46]],\\n  ['WOFF2', [0x77, 0x4f, 0x46, 0x32]],\\n  ['TrueType', [0x00, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00]],\\n  ['OpenType', [0x4f, 0x54, 0x54, 0x4f]],\\n  ['ELF', [0x7f, 0x45, 0x4c, 0x46]],\\n  ['Matroska/WebM', [0x1a, 0x45, 0xdf, 0xa3]],\\n];\\n90→/** The longest embedded run accepted in text: 1 KB. */\\nconst MAX_EMBEDDED = 1024;\\nconst BASE64_RUN = new RegExp(`[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]{${MAX_EMBEDDED + 1},}={0,2}`, 'g');\\nconst DATA_URI = /data:(?:[a-z]+\\\\/[a-z0-9.+-]+)?(?:;[^,\\\\s\\\"'`]*)?,([^\\\\s\\\"'`)]*)/gi;\\nconst LICENSE_NAME = /licen[cs]e|copying|(^|[._-])ofl([._-]|$)/i;\\nconst APPROVALS = 'data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json';\\nconst APPROVAL_KEYS = ['path', 'escalation', 'reason'];\\nconst ASK =\\n  \\\"needs the owner's approval: x esc open --principle Assets; once approved, list it in data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json with its escalation record. Meanwhile generate it, or keep it as readable text (doctrine: Assets)\\\";\\n/** A game's own approvals, and their keys (see the file comment). */\\n100→const GAME_APPROVALS = 'APPROVED-BINARIES.json';\\nconst GAME_APPROVAL_KEYS = ['path', 'reason'];\\n/** Where a game's fonts go: anywhere in the game's own folder, each with its license beside it. */\\nconst GAME_FONTS = 'labs/game/';\\nconst GAME_ASK =\\n  'needs the owner\\\\'s approval (AGENTS.md): ask them; once approved, list it in APPROVED-BINARIES.json as { \\\"path\\\", \\\"reason\\\" }, the reason their answer, who and when. Meanwhile generate it, or keep it as readable text (doctrine: Assets)';\\n\\n/** Where a repository keeps its approvals and fonts, and what a binary needs: the engine's own, or a game's. */\\ninterface AssetRules {\\n  /** Approval files, each with its keys and whether an entry needs its escalation record. */\\n110→  approvals: { file: string; keys: string[]; record: boolean }[];\\n  /** Where fonts pass, each with its license beside it. */\\n  fonts: string[];\\n  /** Where a new font goes: in a game, its own folder (the engine's fonts in data/fonts/ are the engine's). */\\n  newFonts: string;\\n  ask: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The engine repository's rules; in a game, its approvals stand without their records, which stayed behind. */\\nexport function assetRules(game: boolean): AssetRules {\\n120→  if (!game) return { approvals: [{ file: APPROVALS, keys: APPROVAL_KEYS, record: true }], fonts: ['data/fonts/'], newFonts: 'data/fonts/', ask: ASK }; // prettier-ignore\\n  const approvals = [\\n    { file: APPROVALS, keys: APPROVAL_KEYS, record: false },\\n    { file: GAME_APPROVALS, keys: GAME_APPROVAL_KEYS, record: false },\\n  ];\\n  return { approvals, fonts: ['data/fonts/', GAME_FONTS], newFonts: GAME_FONTS, ask: GAME_ASK };\\n}\\n\\n/** What binary format `bytes` (a file named `path`) is, or undefined for text. */\\nexport function binaryKind(path: string, bytes: Buffer): string | undefined {\\n130→  const magic = MAGIC.find(([, lead]) => lead.every((byte, i) => bytes[i] === byte));\\n  if (magic) return magic[0];\\n  const extension = extname(path).slice(1).toLowerCase();\\n  if (BINARY_EXTENSIONS.has(extension)) return `a .${extension} file`;\\n  if (bytes.subarray(0, 8000).includes(0)) return 'binary (it holds NUL bytes)';\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n\\n/** The files git tracks or would track under `root`: tracked plus untracked, ignored ones left out. */\\nexport function repositoryFiles(root: string): string[] {\\n140→  const out = execFileSync('git', ['ls-files', '-z', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard'], {\\n    cwd: root,\\n    encoding: 'utf8',\\n    maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024,\\n  });\\n  const files = new Set(out.split('\\\\0').filter(Boolean));\\n  return [...files].filter((file) => existsSync(join(root, file)) && lstatSync(join(root, file)).isFile()).sort();\\n}\\n\\n/** The 1-based line of character `index` in `text`. */\\n150→const lineAt = (text: string, index: number) => text.slice(0, index).split('\\\\n').length;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Runs the repository checks no standard tool covers, last in `npm run check` (PLAN.md §8.1).\\n *\\n * Plugins: the asset scan, here, and the `check` a module in `tools/cmd/` may export (`export const check:\\n * CheckPlugin`), which is how dependency pins, docs drift and escalation records arrive (WPs 0.3, 0.6, 0.7).\\n *\\n * The asset scan (doctrine: Assets) reads every file git tracks or would track. A binary file (by extension, magic\\n * bytes or a NUL byte) passes only if `data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json` lists it, `{ path, escalation: \\\"ESC-NNNN\\\",\\n * reason }`, with its record in `docs/escalations/`, or if it is a font (WOFF2, TTF, OTF) under `data/fonts/` with\\n10→ * its license beside it. A base64 run or `data:` URI over 1 KB fails too. Else: `x esc open --principle Assets`.\\n * In a game made from the engine (`isGame`, ADR-0024), which has no `x esc` and keeps the engine's files read-only, the\\n * game's own approvals are `APPROVED-BINARIES.json` at its root, `{ path, reason }` (the reason: the owner's answer),\\n * its fonts go anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them, and the engine's approvals stand as its\\n * repository checked them. The one-file playground (`ONE_FILE`, playground.html) is not scanned: a build of the scanned\\n * sources, it carries its npm dependencies' binaries inline (Rapier's WebAssembly, three.js's SMAA textures), as the\\n * owner asked of a self-contained file (ADR-0023 amendment 5). A file deleted between the listing and its read is skipped (`scanAssets`): at gate G6's\\n * review, `x new module --test` wrote and removed its sample in the repository while a parallel `x check` scanned,\\n * which crashed on the read.\\n *\\n20→ * Lane mode is detected: in a linked git worktree (`--git-dir` differs from `--git-common-dir`), an ultracode lane,\\n * a `warnInLane` plugin (docs drift) only warns (§11.3). `X_LANE=0|1` overrides the detection.\\n *\\n * Usage: node x check. Exit 0 when every plugin passes, 1 otherwise.\\n * @see tools/cmd/check.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, lstatSync, readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { basename, dirname, extname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\\nimport { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\\n30→import { ONE_FILE } from '../lib/oneFile';\\nimport type { Finding } from '../lib/report';\\nimport { closest, commandNames, isGame, type Command, type CommandResult } from '../x';\\n\\n/** What a plugin is given: the repository root, and whether this checkout is an ultracode lane. */\\nexport interface CheckContext {\\n  root: string;\\n  lane: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n40→/** What a plugin found. */\\nexport interface CheckOutcome {\\n  failures: Finding[];\\n  warnings?: Finding[];\\n  /** Named numbers for the report, prefixed with the plugin's name by `x check`. */\\n  metrics?: Record<string, number | string | boolean>;\\n  /** One short line for the output, such as `214 files`. */\\n  summary?: string;\\n}\\n\\n50→/** One repository check. A `tools/cmd/<name>.ts` module plugs one in by exporting it as `check`. */\\nexport interface CheckPlugin {\\n  /** Its name in the output and the metrics: `assets`, `deps`, `docs`, `esc`. */\\n  name: string;\\n  /** In an ultracode lane, its failures are warnings: the integrator regenerates what it checks (PLAN.md §11.3). */\\n  warnInLane?: boolean;\\n  run(context: CheckContext): CheckOutcome | Promise<CheckOutcome>;\\n}\\n\\n/** Extensions of binary formats, by name, so a file is caught even when its bytes look like text. */\\n60→const BINARY_EXTENSIONS = new Set(\\n  (\\n    'png jpg jpeg gif webp avif bmp ico tif tiff psd exr hdr ktx ktx2 basis dds glb bin wasm fbx blend ' +\\n    'mp3 wav ogg oga flac m4a aac opus mp4 webm mov avi mkv zip gz tgz bz2 xz 7z rar jar pdf exe dll so dylib ' +\\n    'woff woff2 ttf otf eot npy npz'\\n  ).split(' '),\\n);\\n/** The pre-approved font formats (DOCTRINE.md, Assets). */\\nconst FONT_EXTENSIONS = new Set(['woff2', 'ttf', 'otf']);\\n/** Leading bytes of common binary formats, to name what a file is. */\\n70→const MAGIC: [string, number[]][] = [\\n  ['PNG', [0x89, 0x50, 0x4e, 0x47]],\\n  ['JPEG', [0xff, 0xd8, 0xff]],\\n  ['GIF', [0x47, 0x49, 0x46, 0x38]],\\n  ['RIFF (WAV, WebP, AVI)', [0x52, 0x49, 0x46, 0x46]],\\n  ['Ogg', [0x4f, 0x67, 0x67, 0x53]],\\n  ['FLAC', [0x66, 0x4c, 0x61, 0x43]],\\n  ['MP3', [0x49, 0x44, 0x33]],\\n  ['ZIP', [0x50, 0x4b, 0x03, 0x04]],\\n  ['gzip', [0x1f, 0x8b]],\\n80→  ['PDF', [0x25, 0x50, 0x44, 0x46]],\\n  ['WebAssembly', [0x00, 0x61, 0x73, 0x6d]],\\n  ['glTF binary', [0x67, 0x6c, 0x54, 0x46]],\\n  ['WOFF', [0x77, 0x4f, 0x46, 0x46]],\\n  ['WOFF2', [0x77, 0x4f, 0x46, 0x32]],\\n  ['TrueType', [0x00, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00]],\\n  ['OpenType', [0x4f, 0x54, 0x54, 0x4f]],\\n  ['ELF', [0x7f, 0x45, 0x4c, 0x46]],\\n  ['Matroska/WebM', [0x1a, 0x45, 0xdf, 0xa3]],\\n];\\n90→/** The longest embedded run accepted in text: 1 KB. */\\nconst MAX_EMBEDDED = 1024;\\nconst BASE64_RUN = new RegExp(`[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]{${MAX_EMBEDDED + 1},}={0,2}`, 'g');\\nconst DATA_URI = /data:(?:[a-z]+\\\\/[a-z0-9.+-]+)?(?:;[^,\\\\s\\\"'`]*)?,([^\\\\s\\\"'`)]*)/gi;\\nconst LICENSE_NAME = /licen[cs]e|copying|(^|[._-])ofl([._-]|$)/i;\\nconst APPROVALS = 'data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json';\\nconst APPROVAL_KEYS = ['path', 'escalation', 'reason'];\\nconst ASK =\\n  \\\"needs the owner's approval: x esc open --principle Assets; once approved, list it in data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json with its escalation record. Meanwhile generate it, or keep it as readable text (doctrine: Assets)\\\";\\n/** A game's own approvals, and their keys (see the file comment). */\\n100→const GAME_APPROVALS = 'APPROVED-BINARIES.json';\\nconst GAME_APPROVAL_KEYS = ['path', 'reason'];\\n/** Where a game's fonts go: anywhere in the game's own folder, each with its license beside it. */\\nconst GAME_FONTS = 'labs/game/';\\nconst GAME_ASK =\\n  'needs the owner\\\\'s approval (AGENTS.md): ask them; once approved, list it in APPROVED-BINARIES.json as { \\\"path\\\", \\\"reason\\\" }, the reason their answer, who and when. Meanwhile generate it, or keep it as readable text (doctrine: Assets)';\\n\\n/** Where a repository keeps its approvals and fonts, and what a binary needs: the engine's own, or a game's. */\\ninterface AssetRules {\\n  /** Approval files, each with its keys and whether an entry needs its escalation record. */\\n110→  approvals: { file: string; keys: string[]; record: boolean }[];\\n  /** Where fonts pass, each with its license beside it. */\\n  fonts: string[];\\n  /** Where a new font goes: in a game, its own folder (the engine's fonts in data/fonts/ are the engine's). */\\n  newFonts: string;\\n  ask: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The engine repository's rules; in a game, its approvals stand without their records, which stayed behind. */\\nexport function assetRules(game: boolean): AssetRules {\\n120→  if (!game) return { approvals: [{ file: APPROVALS, keys: APPROVAL_KEYS, record: true }], fonts: ['data/fonts/'], newFonts: 'data/fonts/', ask: ASK }; // prettier-ignore\\n  const approvals = [\\n    { file: APPROVALS, keys: APPROVAL_KEYS, record: false },\\n    { file: GAME_APPROVALS, keys: GAME_APPROVAL_KEYS, record: false },\\n  ];\\n  return { approvals, fonts: ['data/fonts/', GAME_FONTS], newFonts: GAME_FONTS, ask: GAME_ASK };\\n}\\n\\n/** What binary format `bytes` (a file named `path`) is, or undefined for text. */\\nexport function binaryKind(path: string, bytes: Buffer): string | undefined {\\n130→  const magic = MAGIC.find(([, lead]) => lead.every((byte, i) => bytes[i] === byte));\\n  if (magic) return magic[0];\\n  const extension = extname(path).slice(1).toLowerCase();\\n  if (BINARY_EXTENSIONS.has(extension)) return `a .${extension} file`;\\n  if (bytes.subarray(0, 8000).includes(0)) return 'binary (it holds NUL bytes)';\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n\\n/** The files git tracks or would track under `root`: tracked plus untracked, ignored ones left out. */\\nexport function repositoryFiles(root: string): string[] {\\n140→  const out = execFileSync('git', ['ls-files', '-z', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard'], {\\n    cwd: root,\\n    encoding: 'utf8',\\n    maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024,\\n  });\\n  const files = new Set(out.split('\\\\0').filter(Boolean));\\n  return [...files].filter((file) => existsSync(join(root, file)) && lstatSync(join(root, file)).isFile()).sort();\\n}\\n\\n/** The 1-based line of character `index` in `text`. */\\n150→const lineAt = (text: string, index: number) => text.slice(0, index).split('\\\\n').length;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/tools/cmd/check.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":150,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file Runs the repository checks no standard tool covers, last in `npm run check` (PLAN.md §8.1).\\n *\\n * Plugins: the asset scan, here, and the `check` a module in `tools/cmd/` may export (`export const check:\\n * CheckPlugin`), which is how dependency pins, docs drift and escalation records arrive (WPs 0.3, 0.6, 0.7).\\n *\\n * The asset scan (doctrine: Assets) reads every file git tracks or would track. A binary file (by extension, magic\\n * bytes or a NUL byte) passes only if `data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json` lists it, `{ path, escalation: \\\"ESC-NNNN\\\",\\n * reason }`, with its record in `docs/escalations/`, or if it is a font (WOFF2, TTF, OTF) under `data/fonts/` with\\n * its license beside it. A base64 run or `data:` URI over 1 KB fails too. Else: `x esc open --principle Assets`.\\n * In a game made from the engine (`isGame`, ADR-0024), which has no `x esc` and keeps the engine's files read-only, the\\n * game's own approvals are `APPROVED-BINARIES.json` at its root, `{ path, reason }` (the reason: the owner's answer),\\n * its fonts go anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them, and the engine's approvals stand as its\\n * repository checked them. The one-file playground (`ONE_FILE`, playground.html) is not scanned: a build of the scanned\\n * sources, it carries its npm dependencies' binaries inline (Rapier's WebAssembly, three.js's SMAA textures), as the\\n * owner asked of a self-contained file (ADR-0023 amendment 5). A file deleted between the listing and its read is skipped (`scanAssets`): at gate G6's\\n * review, `x new module --test` wrote and removed its sample in the repository while a parallel `x check` scanned,\\n * which crashed on the read.\\n *\\n * Lane mode is detected: in a linked git worktree (`--git-dir` differs from `--git-common-dir`), an ultracode lane,\\n * a `warnInLane` plugin (docs drift) only warns (§11.3). `X_LANE=0|1` overrides the detection.\\n *\\n * Usage: node x check. Exit 0 when every plugin passes, 1 otherwise.\\n * @see tools/cmd/check.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, lstatSync, readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { basename, dirname, extname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\\nimport { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\\nimport { ONE_FILE } from '../lib/oneFile';\\nimport type { Finding } from '../lib/report';\\nimport { closest, commandNames, isGame, type Command, type CommandResult } from '../x';\\n\\n/** What a plugin is given: the repository root, and whether this checkout is an ultracode lane. */\\nexport interface CheckContext {\\n  root: string;\\n  lane: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n/** What a plugin found. */\\nexport interface CheckOutcome {\\n  failures: Finding[];\\n  warnings?: Finding[];\\n  /** Named numbers for the report, prefixed with the plugin's name by `x check`. */\\n  metrics?: Record<string, number | string | boolean>;\\n  /** One short line for the output, such as `214 files`. */\\n  summary?: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** One repository check. A `tools/cmd/<name>.ts` module plugs one in by exporting it as `check`. */\\nexport interface CheckPlugin {\\n  /** Its name in the output and the metrics: `assets`, `deps`, `docs`, `esc`. */\\n  name: string;\\n  /** In an ultracode lane, its failures are warnings: the integrator regenerates what it checks (PLAN.md §11.3). */\\n  warnInLane?: boolean;\\n  run(context: CheckContext): CheckOutcome | Promise<CheckOutcome>;\\n}\\n\\n/** Extensions of binary formats, by name, so a file is caught even when its bytes look like text. */\\nconst BINARY_EXTENSIONS = new Set(\\n  (\\n    'png jpg jpeg gif webp avif bmp ico tif tiff psd exr hdr ktx ktx2 basis dds glb bin wasm fbx blend ' +\\n    'mp3 wav ogg oga flac m4a aac opus mp4 webm mov avi mkv zip gz tgz bz2 xz 7z rar jar pdf exe dll so dylib ' +\\n    'woff woff2 ttf otf eot npy npz'\\n  ).split(' '),\\n);\\n/** The pre-approved font formats (DOCTRINE.md, Assets). */\\nconst FONT_EXTENSIONS = new Set(['woff2', 'ttf', 'otf']);\\n/** Leading bytes of common binary formats, to name what a file is. */\\nconst MAGIC: [string, number[]][] = [\\n  ['PNG', [0x89, 0x50, 0x4e, 0x47]],\\n  ['JPEG', [0xff, 0xd8, 0xff]],\\n  ['GIF', [0x47, 0x49, 0x46, 0x38]],\\n  ['RIFF (WAV, WebP, AVI)', [0x52, 0x49, 0x46, 0x46]],\\n  ['Ogg', [0x4f, 0x67, 0x67, 0x53]],\\n  ['FLAC', [0x66, 0x4c, 0x61, 0x43]],\\n  ['MP3', [0x49, 0x44, 0x33]],\\n  ['ZIP', [0x50, 0x4b, 0x03, 0x04]],\\n  ['gzip', [0x1f, 0x8b]],\\n  ['PDF', [0x25, 0x50, 0x44, 0x46]],\\n  ['WebAssembly', [0x00, 0x61, 0x73, 0x6d]],\\n  ['glTF binary', [0x67, 0x6c, 0x54, 0x46]],\\n  ['WOFF', [0x77, 0x4f, 0x46, 0x46]],\\n  ['WOFF2', [0x77, 0x4f, 0x46, 0x32]],\\n  ['TrueType', [0x00, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00]],\\n  ['OpenType', [0x4f, 0x54, 0x54, 0x4f]],\\n  ['ELF', [0x7f, 0x45, 0x4c, 0x46]],\\n  ['Matroska/WebM', [0x1a, 0x45, 0xdf, 0xa3]],\\n];\\n/** The longest embedded run accepted in text: 1 KB. */\\nconst MAX_EMBEDDED = 1024;\\nconst BASE64_RUN = new RegExp(`[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]{${MAX_EMBEDDED + 1},}={0,2}`, 'g');\\nconst DATA_URI = /data:(?:[a-z]+\\\\/[a-z0-9.+-]+)?(?:;[^,\\\\s\\\"'`]*)?,([^\\\\s\\\"'`)]*)/gi;\\nconst LICENSE_NAME = /licen[cs]e|copying|(^|[._-])ofl([._-]|$)/i;\\nconst APPROVALS = 'data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json';\\nconst APPROVAL_KEYS = ['path', 'escalation', 'reason'];\\nconst ASK =\\n  \\\"needs the owner's approval: x esc open --principle Assets; once approved, list it in data/APPROVED-BINARIES.json with its escalation record. Meanwhile generate it, or keep it as readable text (doctrine: Assets)\\\";\\n/** A game's own approvals, and their keys (see the file comment). */\\nconst GAME_APPROVALS = 'APPROVED-BINARIES.json';\\nconst GAME_APPROVAL_KEYS = ['path', 'reason'];\\n/** Where a game's fonts go: anywhere in the game's own folder, each with its license beside it. */\\nconst GAME_FONTS = 'labs/game/';\\nconst GAME_ASK =\\n  'needs the owner\\\\'s approval (AGENTS.md): ask them; once approved, list it in APPROVED-BINARIES.json as { \\\"path\\\", \\\"reason\\\" }, the reason their answer, who and when. Meanwhile generate it, or keep it as readable text (doctrine: Assets)';\\n\\n/** Where a repository keeps its approvals and fonts, and what a binary needs: the engine's own, or a game's. */\\ninterface AssetRules {\\n  /** Approval files, each with its keys and whether an entry needs its escalation record. */\\n  approvals: { file: string; keys: string[]; record: boolean }[];\\n  /** Where fonts pass, each with its license beside it. */\\n  fonts: string[];\\n  /** Where a new font goes: in a game, its own folder (the engine's fonts in data/fonts/ are the engine's). */\\n  newFonts: string;\\n  ask: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The engine repository's rules; in a game, its approvals stand without their records, which stayed behind. */\\nexport function assetRules(game: boolean): AssetRules {\\n  if (!game) return { approvals: [{ file: APPROVALS, keys: APPROVAL_KEYS, record: true }], fonts: ['data/fonts/'], newFonts: 'data/fonts/', ask: ASK }; // prettier-ignore\\n  const approvals = [\\n    { file: APPROVALS, keys: APPROVAL_KEYS, record: false },\\n    { file: GAME_APPROVALS, keys: GAME_APPROVAL_KEYS, record: false },\\n  ];\\n  return { approvals, fonts: ['data/fonts/', GAME_FONTS], newFonts: GAME_FONTS, ask: GAME_ASK };\\n}\\n\\n/** What binary format `bytes` (a file named `path`) is, or undefined for text. */\\nexport function binaryKind(path: string, bytes: Buffer): string | undefined {\\n  const magic = MAGIC.find(([, lead]) => lead.every((byte, i) => bytes[i] === byte));\\n  if (magic) return magic[0];\\n  const extension = extname(path).slice(1).toLowerCase();\\n  if (BINARY_EXTENSIONS.has(extension)) return `a .${extension} file`;\\n  if (bytes.subarray(0, 8000).includes(0)) return 'binary (it holds NUL bytes)';\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n\\n/** The files git tracks or would track under `root`: tracked plus untracked, ignored ones left out. */\\nexport function repositoryFiles(root: string): string[] {\\n  const out = execFileSync('git', ['ls-files', '-z', '--cached', '--others', '--exclude-standard'], {\\n    cwd: root,\\n    encoding: 'utf8',\\n    maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024,\\n  });\\n  const files = new Set(out.split('\\\\0').filter(Boolean));\\n  return [...files].filter((file) => existsSync(join(root, file)) && lstatSync(join(root, file)).isFile()).sort();\\n}\\n\\n/** The 1-based line of character `index` in `text`. */\\nconst lineAt = (text: string, index: number) => text.slice(0, index).split('\\\\n').length;\\n\",\"total_lines\":364}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-78ae9884-8e1b-4ee1-9481-4e93b2301942-13","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The public API by area, for docs/GAME-GUIDE.md's last section (PLAN.md WP 12.4, ADR-0024): each area comment\\n * of the barrels, in their order (engine/sim-api.ts with the areas engine/anim/api.ts adds in its place, then\\n * engine/index.ts), with the names the area exports, values first, then types. `x docs --write` puts it between the\\n * guide's `api-map` markers, and `x docs --check` fails while the block differs, so the one file a game agent (or a\\n * model without the repository) reads names every public export and never drifts from the code; it also holds the\\n * whole guide to `GUIDE_BUDGET_BYTES`. The rest of the guide\\n * is hand-written, and Prettier leaves it alone (.prettierignore), so this block is written as it should stay.\\n *\\n10→ * Invariants: the barrels are flat lists of `export { name } from …` and `export type { Name } from …` lines under `//`\\n * area comments (their file comments say so); `export * from './x'` puts x's areas in its place. An area with no names\\n * of its own (a heading whose names live in another file) is left out.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * apiMap(process.cwd()).includes('`defineScene`'); // true\\n * withApiMap('a\\\\n<!-- api-map:start -->\\\\nold\\\\n<!-- api-map:end -->\\\\n', 'new'); // 'a\\\\n<!-- api-map:start -->\\\\nnew\\\\n<!-- api-map:end -->\\\\n'\\n * @see tools/lib/docsGameApi.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { existsSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\n20→import { join, posix } from 'node:path';\\nimport type { Finding } from './report';\\n\\n/** The guide whose last section this module writes. */\\nexport const GUIDE = 'docs/GAME-GUIDE.md';\\n\\n/**\\n * The most the guide may weigh, in bytes (about 15,000 tokens): it is read whole, once, by every game agent, and handed\\n * as one file to models without the repository. It weighed 43 KB when written (ADR-0024).\\n */\\n30→export const GUIDE_BUDGET_BYTES = 60_000;\\n\\n/** The markers around the generated block; the start marker's comment may run on to say what writes the block. */\\nconst START = '<!-- api-map:start';\\nconst END = '<!-- api-map:end -->';\\n\\n/** One area of a barrel: its comment and the names it exports. */\\nexport interface ApiArea {\\n  /** The comment above it, its lines joined. */\\n  text: string;\\n40→  values: string[];\\n  types: string[];\\n}\\n\\n/** The areas of the barrel at `file` (repository path), with `export *` lines replaced by the target's areas. */\\nexport function barrelAreas(root: string, file: string): ApiArea[] {\\n  const areas: ApiArea[] = [];\\n  let area: ApiArea | null = null;\\n  let continuing = false;\\n  for (const line of readFileSync(join(root, file), 'utf8').split('\\\\n')) {\\n50→    const comment = /^\\\\/\\\\/ (.*)$/.exec(line)?.[1];\\n    if (comment !== undefined) {\\n      if (continuing && area) area.text += ` ${comment}`;\\n      else areas.push((area = { text: comment, values: [], types: [] }));\\n      continuing = true;\\n      continue;\\n    }\\n    continuing = false;\\n    const star = /^export \\\\* from '(\\\\.[^']+)';$/.exec(line)?.[1];\\n    if (star) areas.push(...barrelAreas(root, `${posix.join(posix.dirname(file), star)}.ts`));\\n60→    const named = /^export (type )?\\\\{ (\\\\w+) \\\\} from /.exec(line);\\n    if (named && area) (named[1] ? area.types : area.values).push(named[2]);\\n  }\\n  return areas.filter((entry) => entry.values.length + entry.types.length > 0);\\n}\\n\\n/** One area as Markdown: its comment, its title (the words before the first colon) in bold, then its names. */\\nfunction areaMarkdown(area: ApiArea): string {\\n  const colon = area.text.indexOf(':');\\n  const text = colon > 0 && colon < 80 ? `**${area.text.slice(0, colon)}**${area.text.slice(colon)}` : area.text;\\n70→  const names = [\\n    area.values.map((name) => `\\\\`${name}\\\\``).join(', '),\\n    area.types.length ? `types ${area.types.map((name) => `\\\\`${name}\\\\``).join(', ')}` : '',\\n  ].filter(Boolean);\\n  return `${text}\\\\n${names.join('; ')}.`;\\n}\\n\\n/** How the guide introduces the API's two parts: what the sim side imports, then what only pages do. */\\nconst GUIDE_WORDS = {\\n  intro: [\\n80→    'Sim-side code imports these from engine/sim-api.ts; pages get them from engine/index.ts as well. Each name has the',\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The public API by area, for docs/GAME-GUIDE.md's last section (PLAN.md WP 12.4, ADR-0024): each area comment\\n * of the barrels, in their order (engine/sim-api.ts with the areas engine/anim/api.ts adds in its place, then\\n * engine/index.ts), with the names the area exports, values first, then types. `x docs --write` puts it between the\\n * guide's `api-map` markers, and `x docs --check` fails while the block differs, so the one file a game agent (or a\\n * model without the repository) reads names every public export and never drifts from the code; it also holds the\\n * whole guide to `GUIDE_BUDGET_BYTES`. The rest of the guide\\n * is hand-written, and Prettier leaves it alone (.prettierignore), so this block is written as it should stay.\\n *\\n10→ * Invariants: the barrels are flat lists of `export { name } from …` and `export type { Name } from …` lines under `//`\\n * area comments (their file comments say so); `export * from './x'` puts x's areas in its place. An area with no names\\n * of its own (a heading whose names live in another file) is left out.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * apiMap(process.cwd()).includes('`defineScene`'); // true\\n * withApiMap('a\\\\n<!-- api-map:start -->\\\\nold\\\\n<!-- api-map:end -->\\