{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_556","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Resuming work from where I left off.\n\nReading the long-running background tasks skill first. Then checking current file state and preparing to run verify.mjs.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The retreat tests already passed. Next I will write the hand-in records and run the official verifier without retuning the sim."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fb60b557-5e0b-4c2b-9e38-c02faa891089-0","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/.grok/bundled/skills/long-running-background-tasks/SKILL.md","limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fb60b557-5e0b-4c2b-9e38-c02faa891089-1","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/STAGE.md","limit":150}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fb60b557-5e0b-4c2b-9e38-c02faa891089-2","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"## Verification|## Deliverable|exit-interview|PROGRESS|ENGINE-FEEDBACK|Deviations|checklist","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/TASK.md"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":26437,"output_tokens":387,"cache_read_input_tokens":10112,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"5676e9f6-35ba-490d-8e2a-3dc304d1449c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-fb60b557-5e0b-4c2b-9e38-c02faa891089-0","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→---\\nname: long-running-background-tasks\\ndescription: >-\\n  Required reading before you start, watch, or wait on anything that keeps running after you launch it — background jobs, watchers, scheduled loops, CI, pull requests, training runs, dev servers, long builds. Read it before you launch such work and before you report on its state. Saying where a running job stands counts as working on it. Use when: about to launch, supervise, inspect, diagnose, or report on work that keeps running after it is started.\\n---\\n\\n# Working with long-running background tasks\\n\\nOnly submitting or queuing jobs is not sufficient to complete the task. The definition of done is the job has completed successfully and you have verified that it has produced all the expected outputs.\\n10→\\nThis means you must see through the jobs completion by watching the job to ensure that its healthy, progressing, and working as intended.\\n\\n## Tools available\\n\\nThere's two ways to watch jobs: monitor and loop. Decide which of the two you need, sometimes you need both.\\n\\n### monitor — deterministic, actionable events\\n\\n- Use a monitor when the event is a predicate a script can evaluate — an API response, an exit code, a port answering.\\n20→- Typical events: CI turning red, a PR comment or merge conflict, a PR becoming ready to merge, a dev server coming up or going down, a job reaching a terminal state.\\n- The monitor requires a script polls on a fixed cadence and stays silent until the predicate fires, so an idle job costs nothing. More on writing good monitoring scripts below.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Monitoring a PR — wake on CI breaking, merge conflicts, etc.\\n  - Checking if local tooling is up — wake on the service being unavailable, errors, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases can be checked deterministically.\\n\\n### loop — qualitative health that needs reading\\n\\n- Use a loop when judging health requires reading the logs and system information to detect failing, faulty or stalled jobs. It uses a subagent which is more expensive but more thorough.\\n30→- Typical signals: the loss curve going unstable, a filtering pipeline producing garbage, throughput quietly degrading, faulty nodes throwing CUDA errors.\\n- A subagent reads the logs on a fixed cadence (say every 10 min) and reports what changed, so it costs a wakeup every cycle.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Checking on a training or data processing job — the subagent should scan for instability, degradation, faulty nodes, etc.\\n  - Monitoring system deployment health — the subagent should scan for 4xx/5xx, response times, resources, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases are hard to describe deterministically and hard to fully enumerate, so it is better to use a subagent to validate.\\n\\n## Good monitoring scripts\\n\\nThe monitor tool wakes you up every time you log to stdout. Wakeups are expensive, so only log if there is something actionable — but log it as early as possible, since staying silent delays the task. Plan accordingly: minimize the number of wakeups while still firing them as quickly as possible.\\n40→\\n### Planning\\n\\n- Enumerate all terminal conditions.\\n  - For PRs, this includes the PR being merged or closed.\\n  - For long-running jobs, this includes the job completing or unrecoverable failures.\\n- Enumerate all other situations where a wakeup is needed.\\n  - For PRs, this would include when a comment arrives (there are four different types of PR comments), when CI fails, when a merge conflict arises, and when the PR becomes ready to merge (CI green, no conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers).\\n  - For an ML training job, this can be when the loss is unstable or the instance is preempted.\\n- Determine situations where the monitoring has to update what it is tracking. For example, for monitoring PRs, the latest commit hash may change as you push new commits.\\n50→\\n### Writing\\n\\n- The monitoring script should poll every 30s. `watch_pr.py` defaults to\\n  `--poll-interval 30`. Lower intervals cause GitHub rate limits, especially\\n  with more than one watcher on the same token.\\n- Based on the planning, exit immediately on terminal conditions with FAILED, DONE, or CANCELLED, and log ACTION_REQUIRED for wakeups.\\n- Send all debug diagnostics — both your watcher's own trace and the monitored job's output — to a file, so a log line can never be mistaken for DONE, FAILED, or CANCELLED.\\n- The script should fail as early as possible. Emit a FAILED token immediately when any required condition reaches terminal failure. For example, if you are waiting on CI and any of them fail, emit FAILED immediately.\\n- Write the script to a durable location such as ~/.grok and name it something clear such as `ci_watcher.sh`.\\n60→- Key every log file the script writes on the pid or session id of the thing being watched. The paths are otherwise fixed, so a second job of the same kind silently clobbers the first one's log.\\n\\n## Handling wake-ups\\n\\n- Address errors or actionable events immediately. Look through the logs and debug the issue thoroughly before settling on a root cause, as long-running processes are generally expensive. Resubmit the job once it is fixed:\\n  - For PRs, this means repushing the changes and restarting CI (if needed).\\n  - For jobs, requeue or relaunch the job.\\n  - Always restart the monitor or loop so that it points at the new PR or job.\\n- If there are multiple jobs running, start debugging immediately when any jobs fail. Minimize total wall-time with completing tasks.\\n\\n70→## Restarts\\n\\n- Whenever you relaunch a job, always update the monitor.\\n- Monitors have a maximum 10-hour time limit, so be sure to restart them if you hit the time limit and if the task being monitored is still running.\\n- When you spawn a new monitor, make sure to kill any previous ones that the new one replaces. For example, if you fix a training run and respawn it, be sure to kill previous monitors, as irrelevant wakeups are expensive.\\n\\n## Cleanup\\n\\n- If a monitor is in a terminal state, kill the monitor, the loop, and the job. Dangling processes such as unkilled broken training runs are expensive. Then, address all issues if present.\\n- If an outdated monitor wakes you — that is, it is attached to an old or dead run — make sure to kill it.\\n80→- Do not leave detached workers, duplicate pollers, or orphaned watcher processes behind.\\n\\n## Examples\\n\\n### Example: maintaining a dev server\\n\\nSuppose you just made some changes to the user's frontend code and would like to spin it up to test it. While you're testing it, you would like to know if the server goes down for any reason (if you made changes, for example). This is a great use case for the monitor tool. First start a React app in a background terminal, which reports its pid:\\n\\n```\\nrun_terminal_command(\\n90→  command=\\\"echo $$; npm start >~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_$$.log 2>&1\\\",\\n  timeout=0,\\n  description=\\\"Start the dev server on :3000\\\",\\n  background=true\\n) # prints the pid 123456789\\n```\\n\\nThere are no terminal states, but there are two actionable events from the watcher: `http://localhost:3000` coming up and going down.\\n\\nYou want to be notified as soon as it is up so you can use it, and you want to be woken when it stops so you can address it — fix the code, restart the server, free the port. Both down paths quote the tail of the server's log, so the wakeup arrives with the error already in hand. Polling every 5s is a good cadence since you want to quickly fix it if it is down, and it is not expensive to poll.\\n100→\\n```bash\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Log paths are keyed on the server pid so two dev servers cannot clobber each other.\\nPID=$1 URL=http://localhost:3000 DIR=~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks\\nLOG=$DIR/dev_server_$PID.log\\nup() { curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 5 \\\"$URL\\\" 2>>\\\"$DIR/watch_dev_server_$PID.log\\\"; }\\nwhy() { tail -20 \\\"$LOG\\\" | tr -d '\\\\r' | tr '\\\\n' ' '; }\\n\\nuntil up; do\\n110→  kill -0 \\\"$PID\\\" 2>/dev/null || { echo \\\"FAILED: pid $PID exited before $URL came up: $(why)\\\"; exit 1; }\\n  sleep 5\\ndone\\necho \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is up\\\"\\n\\nwhile :; do\\n  sleep 5\\n  up || { echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is down: $(why)\\\"; until up; do sleep 5; done; }\\ndone\\n```\\n120→\\nTo spawn the monitor, point it at the pid `npm start` reported:\\n\\n```\\nmonitor(\\n  command='bash ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_dev_server.sh 123456789',\\n  description=\\\"Watch the dev server on :3000\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n130→On the first wakeup the app is ready to use and you can start testing it. After that, you may be woken up if the server goes down. When you do:\\n- Read `~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_<pid>.log` for the full compile error or port conflict and fix it.\\n- If the dev server survived, hot reload picks the fix up and the watcher recovers on its own, so leave the monitor running.\\n- If the process is dead, restart it with a fresh `run_terminal_command`, then kill this monitor and spawn a new one against the new pid.\\n\\n### Example: PR monitoring\\n\\nPR monitoring should stop when the PR is merged or closed, and it should wake up on:\\n- For comments, you should ask users how they would like to resolve them (which ones to address automatically, whether to respond / resolve, etc.), then store the decision for new future sessions. There are four different types of PR comments.\\n  - Inline review threads\\n140→  - Review bodies\\n  - Conversation comments\\n  - Commit comments\\n- Failing CI - most can be fixed in the code but sometimes they may be flaky. For flaky CI, just rerun them.\\n- Merge conflicts\\n- The PR becoming ready to merge — CI passes, no merge conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers, no unresolved inline threads. That wakeup is `kind: mergeable`. Tell the user they can merge now. It is not terminal; keep watching until merge or close.\\n\\nYou can find the example script bundled with this skill at watch_pr.py. It needs an authenticated `gh` CLI (`gh auth login`) and `python3`. Default poll is 30s; a lower poll rate may hit GitHub rate limits at higher concurrency. Transport errors (timeouts, resets, DNS) retry with backoff and jitter and do not print a line.\\n\\n```\\n150→monitor(\\n  command='python3 ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_pr.py \\\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/pull/123\\\"',\\n  description=\\\"Watch PR 123\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n### Example: training monitoring\\n\\nFor monitoring long training jobs, the terminal conditions are when the job either fails or completes. Further, many issues cannot be caught deterministically, so use a subagent to run a script for most of the high-level update, then scan the logs for errors such as faulty nodes or CUDA warnings.\\n\\n160→The script below is bundled with this skill as watch_training.py. Run it with `--help` for every flag and for the run-directory and log-line layout it expects, since it parses nanoGPT stdout out of a Slurm run dir.\\n\\n```md\\nKeep an eye on the nanoGPT run nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 (slurm job 1234567) and tell me what changed since last time. Worktree ~/worktrees/nanogpt-owt-124m.\\n\\n1. python3 ~/.grok/skills/long-running-background-tasks/watch_training.py --run-name nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 --job-id 1234567 --target-val 3.00\\n\\nHow to report:\\n- Start with the job's state and if it changed, e.g. \\\"still RUNNING\\\" or \\\"PENDING -> RUNNING\\\".\\n- Copy the ISSUES line and the STATUS line from the monitor exactly as it is written, numbers and all. STATUS will include where the run is heading: how far along, iters left, the eta, and whether val loss is still coming down. We want val loss at or under 3.00.\\n170→- If the state didn't change, tell me that in one line and stop.\\n- If the job has finished or died, give me the state, the exit code, and the last ISSUES, and run these two for me:\\n  sacct -j 1234567 -D -X -P -o JobID,State,ExitCode,DerivedExitCode,Elapsed,NodeList,FailedNode\\n  tail -20 ~/train_logs/slurm-nanogpt_owt_124m_r1-1234567.out\\n\\nKeep <=10 lines: state, progress and eta, loss trend, then anything that smells like training going unstable (loss spiking, NaN, val loss creeping back up) or the cluster misbehaving (a drained or failing node, OOM, NCCL errors, a preemption, iters suddenly slower than usual).\\n```\\n\\nLaunch the scheduler on a 10 min interval, and spawn a monitor to be notified when the job crashes or succeeds.\\n\\n180→```\\nscheduler_create(\\n  interval=\\\"10m\\\",\\n  prompt: \\\"...\\\", # Copy from the prompt above.\\n)\\nmonitor(\\n  # Wakes on pending/running/finished changes after the first observation.\\n  # First poll is silent unless the job is already COMPLETED or failed —\\n  # PENDING matches *ING, so treating an empty previous state as a\\n  # transition would wake immediately on a just-submitted job.\\n190→  command='J=1234567; p=; while :; do s=$(sacct -nXP -j $J -o State|head -1); s=${s%% *}; s=${s:-PENDING}; if [ \\\"$s\\\" != \\\"$p\\\" ]; then case $s in COMPLETED) echo \\\"DONE: $J\\\"; exit;; PENDING|RUNNING|CONFIGURING|COMPLETING|REQUEUED|SUSPENDED|RESIZING) [ -n \\\"$p\\\" ] && echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $J $p -> $s\\\";; *) echo \\\"FAILED: $J $s\\\"; exit 1;; esac; fi; p=$s; sleep 30; done',\\n  description=\\\"Watch training run\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nLaunch both, so that we know when the job completes. When the monitor reports that the job has completed, cancel both the monitor and the scheduler, then determine next steps. Then:\\n- If it succeeded, simply present the results to the user.\\n- If it failed, debug the issue thoroughly, then fix and relaunch the job with new monitors attached. Make sure to have a thorough understanding of the issue before resubmitting, since training jobs are expensive. But always relaunch jobs so you don't waste the user's time.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→---\\nname: long-running-background-tasks\\ndescription: >-\\n  Required reading before you start, watch, or wait on anything that keeps running after you launch it — background jobs, watchers, scheduled loops, CI, pull requests, training runs, dev servers, long builds. Read it before you launch such work and before you report on its state. Saying where a running job stands counts as working on it. Use when: about to launch, supervise, inspect, diagnose, or report on work that keeps running after it is started.\\n---\\n\\n# Working with long-running background tasks\\n\\nOnly submitting or queuing jobs is not sufficient to complete the task. The definition of done is the job has completed successfully and you have verified that it has produced all the expected outputs.\\n10→\\nThis means you must see through the jobs completion by watching the job to ensure that its healthy, progressing, and working as intended.\\n\\n## Tools available\\n\\nThere's two ways to watch jobs: monitor and loop. Decide which of the two you need, sometimes you need both.\\n\\n### monitor — deterministic, actionable events\\n\\n- Use a monitor when the event is a predicate a script can evaluate — an API response, an exit code, a port answering.\\n20→- Typical events: CI turning red, a PR comment or merge conflict, a PR becoming ready to merge, a dev server coming up or going down, a job reaching a terminal state.\\n- The monitor requires a script polls on a fixed cadence and stays silent until the predicate fires, so an idle job costs nothing. More on writing good monitoring scripts below.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Monitoring a PR — wake on CI breaking, merge conflicts, etc.\\n  - Checking if local tooling is up — wake on the service being unavailable, errors, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases can be checked deterministically.\\n\\n### loop — qualitative health that needs reading\\n\\n- Use a loop when judging health requires reading the logs and system information to detect failing, faulty or stalled jobs. It uses a subagent which is more expensive but more thorough.\\n30→- Typical signals: the loss curve going unstable, a filtering pipeline producing garbage, throughput quietly degrading, faulty nodes throwing CUDA errors.\\n- A subagent reads the logs on a fixed cadence (say every 10 min) and reports what changed, so it costs a wakeup every cycle.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Checking on a training or data processing job — the subagent should scan for instability, degradation, faulty nodes, etc.\\n  - Monitoring system deployment health — the subagent should scan for 4xx/5xx, response times, resources, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases are hard to describe deterministically and hard to fully enumerate, so it is better to use a subagent to validate.\\n\\n## Good monitoring scripts\\n\\nThe monitor tool wakes you up every time you log to stdout. Wakeups are expensive, so only log if there is something actionable — but log it as early as possible, since staying silent delays the task. Plan accordingly: minimize the number of wakeups while still firing them as quickly as possible.\\n40→\\n### Planning\\n\\n- Enumerate all terminal conditions.\\n  - For PRs, this includes the PR being merged or closed.\\n  - For long-running jobs, this includes the job completing or unrecoverable failures.\\n- Enumerate all other situations where a wakeup is needed.\\n  - For PRs, this would include when a comment arrives (there are four different types of PR comments), when CI fails, when a merge conflict arises, and when the PR becomes ready to merge (CI green, no conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers).\\n  - For an ML training job, this can be when the loss is unstable or the instance is preempted.\\n- Determine situations where the monitoring has to update what it is tracking. For example, for monitoring PRs, the latest commit hash may change as you push new commits.\\n50→\\n### Writing\\n\\n- The monitoring script should poll every 30s. `watch_pr.py` defaults to\\n  `--poll-interval 30`. Lower intervals cause GitHub rate limits, especially\\n  with more than one watcher on the same token.\\n- Based on the planning, exit immediately on terminal conditions with FAILED, DONE, or CANCELLED, and log ACTION_REQUIRED for wakeups.\\n- Send all debug diagnostics — both your watcher's own trace and the monitored job's output — to a file, so a log line can never be mistaken for DONE, FAILED, or CANCELLED.\\n- The script should fail as early as possible. Emit a FAILED token immediately when any required condition reaches terminal failure. For example, if you are waiting on CI and any of them fail, emit FAILED immediately.\\n- Write the script to a durable location such as ~/.grok and name it something clear such as `ci_watcher.sh`.\\n60→- Key every log file the script writes on the pid or session id of the thing being watched. The paths are otherwise fixed, so a second job of the same kind silently clobbers the first one's log.\\n\\n## Handling wake-ups\\n\\n- Address errors or actionable events immediately. Look through the logs and debug the issue thoroughly before settling on a root cause, as long-running processes are generally expensive. Resubmit the job once it is fixed:\\n  - For PRs, this means repushing the changes and restarting CI (if needed).\\n  - For jobs, requeue or relaunch the job.\\n  - Always restart the monitor or loop so that it points at the new PR or job.\\n- If there are multiple jobs running, start debugging immediately when any jobs fail. Minimize total wall-time with completing tasks.\\n\\n70→## Restarts\\n\\n- Whenever you relaunch a job, always update the monitor.\\n- Monitors have a maximum 10-hour time limit, so be sure to restart them if you hit the time limit and if the task being monitored is still running.\\n- When you spawn a new monitor, make sure to kill any previous ones that the new one replaces. For example, if you fix a training run and respawn it, be sure to kill previous monitors, as irrelevant wakeups are expensive.\\n\\n## Cleanup\\n\\n- If a monitor is in a terminal state, kill the monitor, the loop, and the job. Dangling processes such as unkilled broken training runs are expensive. Then, address all issues if present.\\n- If an outdated monitor wakes you — that is, it is attached to an old or dead run — make sure to kill it.\\n80→- Do not leave detached workers, duplicate pollers, or orphaned watcher processes behind.\\n\\n## Examples\\n\\n### Example: maintaining a dev server\\n\\nSuppose you just made some changes to the user's frontend code and would like to spin it up to test it. While you're testing it, you would like to know if the server goes down for any reason (if you made changes, for example). This is a great use case for the monitor tool. First start a React app in a background terminal, which reports its pid:\\n\\n```\\nrun_terminal_command(\\n90→  command=\\\"echo $$; npm start >~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_$$.log 2>&1\\\",\\n  timeout=0,\\n  description=\\\"Start the dev server on :3000\\\",\\n  background=true\\n) # prints the pid 123456789\\n```\\n\\nThere are no terminal states, but there are two actionable events from the watcher: `http://localhost:3000` coming up and going down.\\n\\nYou want to be notified as soon as it is up so you can use it, and you want to be woken when it stops so you can address it — fix the code, restart the server, free the port. Both down paths quote the tail of the server's log, so the wakeup arrives with the error already in hand. Polling every 5s is a good cadence since you want to quickly fix it if it is down, and it is not expensive to poll.\\n100→\\n```bash\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Log paths are keyed on the server pid so two dev servers cannot clobber each other.\\nPID=$1 URL=http://localhost:3000 DIR=~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks\\nLOG=$DIR/dev_server_$PID.log\\nup() { curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 5 \\\"$URL\\\" 2>>\\\"$DIR/watch_dev_server_$PID.log\\\"; }\\nwhy() { tail -20 \\\"$LOG\\\" | tr -d '\\\\r' | tr '\\\\n' ' '; }\\n\\nuntil up; do\\n110→  kill -0 \\\"$PID\\\" 2>/dev/null || { echo \\\"FAILED: pid $PID exited before $URL came up: $(why)\\\"; exit 1; }\\n  sleep 5\\ndone\\necho \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is up\\\"\\n\\nwhile :; do\\n  sleep 5\\n  up || { echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is down: $(why)\\\"; until up; do sleep 5; done; }\\ndone\\n```\\n120→\\nTo spawn the monitor, point it at the pid `npm start` reported:\\n\\n```\\nmonitor(\\n  command='bash ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_dev_server.sh 123456789',\\n  description=\\\"Watch the dev server on :3000\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n130→On the first wakeup the app is ready to use and you can start testing it. After that, you may be woken up if the server goes down. When you do:\\n- Read `~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_<pid>.log` for the full compile error or port conflict and fix it.\\n- If the dev server survived, hot reload picks the fix up and the watcher recovers on its own, so leave the monitor running.\\n- If the process is dead, restart it with a fresh `run_terminal_command`, then kill this monitor and spawn a new one against the new pid.\\n\\n### Example: PR monitoring\\n\\nPR monitoring should stop when the PR is merged or closed, and it should wake up on:\\n- For comments, you should ask users how they would like to resolve them (which ones to address automatically, whether to respond / resolve, etc.), then store the decision for new future sessions. There are four different types of PR comments.\\n  - Inline review threads\\n140→  - Review bodies\\n  - Conversation comments\\n  - Commit comments\\n- Failing CI - most can be fixed in the code but sometimes they may be flaky. For flaky CI, just rerun them.\\n- Merge conflicts\\n- The PR becoming ready to merge — CI passes, no merge conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers, no unresolved inline threads. That wakeup is `kind: mergeable`. Tell the user they can merge now. It is not terminal; keep watching until merge or close.\\n\\nYou can find the example script bundled with this skill at watch_pr.py. It needs an authenticated `gh` CLI (`gh auth login`) and `python3`. Default poll is 30s; a lower poll rate may hit GitHub rate limits at higher concurrency. Transport errors (timeouts, resets, DNS) retry with backoff and jitter and do not print a line.\\n\\n```\\n150→monitor(\\n  command='python3 ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_pr.py \\\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/pull/123\\\"',\\n  description=\\\"Watch PR 123\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n### Example: training monitoring\\n\\nFor monitoring long training jobs, the terminal conditions are when the job either fails or completes. Further, many issues cannot be caught deterministically, so use a subagent to run a script for most of the high-level update, then scan the logs for errors such as faulty nodes or CUDA warnings.\\n\\n160→The script below is bundled with this skill as watch_training.py. Run it with `--help` for every flag and for the run-directory and log-line layout it expects, since it parses nanoGPT stdout out of a Slurm run dir.\\n\\n```md\\nKeep an eye on the nanoGPT run nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 (slurm job 1234567) and tell me what changed since last time. Worktree ~/worktrees/nanogpt-owt-124m.\\n\\n1. python3 ~/.grok/skills/long-running-background-tasks/watch_training.py --run-name nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 --job-id 1234567 --target-val 3.00\\n\\nHow to report:\\n- Start with the job's state and if it changed, e.g. \\\"still RUNNING\\\" or \\\"PENDING -> RUNNING\\\".\\n- Copy the ISSUES line and the STATUS line from the monitor exactly as it is written, numbers and all. STATUS will include where the run is heading: how far along, iters left, the eta, and whether val loss is still coming down. We want val loss at or under 3.00.\\n170→- If the state didn't change, tell me that in one line and stop.\\n- If the job has finished or died, give me the state, the exit code, and the last ISSUES, and run these two for me:\\n  sacct -j 1234567 -D -X -P -o JobID,State,ExitCode,DerivedExitCode,Elapsed,NodeList,FailedNode\\n  tail -20 ~/train_logs/slurm-nanogpt_owt_124m_r1-1234567.out\\n\\nKeep <=10 lines: state, progress and eta, loss trend, then anything that smells like training going unstable (loss spiking, NaN, val loss creeping back up) or the cluster misbehaving (a drained or failing node, OOM, NCCL errors, a preemption, iters suddenly slower than usual).\\n```\\n\\nLaunch the scheduler on a 10 min interval, and spawn a monitor to be notified when the job crashes or succeeds.\\n\\n180→```\\nscheduler_create(\\n  interval=\\\"10m\\\",\\n  prompt: \\\"...\\\", # Copy from the prompt above.\\n)\\nmonitor(\\n  # Wakes on pending/running/finished changes after the first observation.\\n  # First poll is silent unless the job is already COMPLETED or failed —\\n  # PENDING matches *ING, so treating an empty previous state as a\\n  # transition would wake immediately on a just-submitted job.\\n190→  command='J=1234567; p=; while :; do s=$(sacct -nXP -j $J -o State|head -1); s=${s%% *}; s=${s:-PENDING}; if [ \\\"$s\\\" != \\\"$p\\\" ]; then case $s in COMPLETED) echo \\\"DONE: $J\\\"; exit;; PENDING|RUNNING|CONFIGURING|COMPLETING|REQUEUED|SUSPENDED|RESIZING) [ -n \\\"$p\\\" ] && echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $J $p -> $s\\\";; *) echo \\\"FAILED: $J $s\\\"; exit 1;; esac; fi; p=$s; sleep 30; done',\\n  description=\\\"Watch training run\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nLaunch both, so that we know when the job completes. When the monitor reports that the job has completed, cancel both the monitor and the scheduler, then determine next steps. Then:\\n- If it succeeded, simply present the results to the user.\\n- If it failed, debug the issue thoroughly, then fix and relaunch the job with new monitors attached. Make sure to have a thorough understanding of the issue before resubmitting, since training jobs are expensive. But always relaunch jobs so you don't waste the user's time.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/bundled/skills/long-running-background-tasks/SKILL.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"---\\nname: long-running-background-tasks\\ndescription: >-\\n  Required reading before you start, watch, or wait on anything that keeps running after you launch it — background jobs, watchers, scheduled loops, CI, pull requests, training runs, dev servers, long builds. Read it before you launch such work and before you report on its state. Saying where a running job stands counts as working on it. Use when: about to launch, supervise, inspect, diagnose, or report on work that keeps running after it is started.\\n---\\n\\n# Working with long-running background tasks\\n\\nOnly submitting or queuing jobs is not sufficient to complete the task. The definition of done is the job has completed successfully and you have verified that it has produced all the expected outputs.\\n\\nThis means you must see through the jobs completion by watching the job to ensure that its healthy, progressing, and working as intended.\\n\\n## Tools available\\n\\nThere's two ways to watch jobs: monitor and loop. Decide which of the two you need, sometimes you need both.\\n\\n### monitor — deterministic, actionable events\\n\\n- Use a monitor when the event is a predicate a script can evaluate — an API response, an exit code, a port answering.\\n- Typical events: CI turning red, a PR comment or merge conflict, a PR becoming ready to merge, a dev server coming up or going down, a job reaching a terminal state.\\n- The monitor requires a script polls on a fixed cadence and stays silent until the predicate fires, so an idle job costs nothing. More on writing good monitoring scripts below.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Monitoring a PR — wake on CI breaking, merge conflicts, etc.\\n  - Checking if local tooling is up — wake on the service being unavailable, errors, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases can be checked deterministically.\\n\\n### loop — qualitative health that needs reading\\n\\n- Use a loop when judging health requires reading the logs and system information to detect failing, faulty or stalled jobs. It uses a subagent which is more expensive but more thorough.\\n- Typical signals: the loss curve going unstable, a filtering pipeline producing garbage, throughput quietly degrading, faulty nodes throwing CUDA errors.\\n- A subagent reads the logs on a fixed cadence (say every 10 min) and reports what changed, so it costs a wakeup every cycle.\\n- Examples of when to use:\\n  - Checking on a training or data processing job — the subagent should scan for instability, degradation, faulty nodes, etc.\\n  - Monitoring system deployment health — the subagent should scan for 4xx/5xx, response times, resources, etc.\\n  - All of these wakeup cases are hard to describe deterministically and hard to fully enumerate, so it is better to use a subagent to validate.\\n\\n## Good monitoring scripts\\n\\nThe monitor tool wakes you up every time you log to stdout. Wakeups are expensive, so only log if there is something actionable — but log it as early as possible, since staying silent delays the task. Plan accordingly: minimize the number of wakeups while still firing them as quickly as possible.\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\n- Enumerate all terminal conditions.\\n  - For PRs, this includes the PR being merged or closed.\\n  - For long-running jobs, this includes the job completing or unrecoverable failures.\\n- Enumerate all other situations where a wakeup is needed.\\n  - For PRs, this would include when a comment arrives (there are four different types of PR comments), when CI fails, when a merge conflict arises, and when the PR becomes ready to merge (CI green, no conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers).\\n  - For an ML training job, this can be when the loss is unstable or the instance is preempted.\\n- Determine situations where the monitoring has to update what it is tracking. For example, for monitoring PRs, the latest commit hash may change as you push new commits.\\n\\n### Writing\\n\\n- The monitoring script should poll every 30s. `watch_pr.py` defaults to\\n  `--poll-interval 30`. Lower intervals cause GitHub rate limits, especially\\n  with more than one watcher on the same token.\\n- Based on the planning, exit immediately on terminal conditions with FAILED, DONE, or CANCELLED, and log ACTION_REQUIRED for wakeups.\\n- Send all debug diagnostics — both your watcher's own trace and the monitored job's output — to a file, so a log line can never be mistaken for DONE, FAILED, or CANCELLED.\\n- The script should fail as early as possible. Emit a FAILED token immediately when any required condition reaches terminal failure. For example, if you are waiting on CI and any of them fail, emit FAILED immediately.\\n- Write the script to a durable location such as ~/.grok and name it something clear such as `ci_watcher.sh`.\\n- Key every log file the script writes on the pid or session id of the thing being watched. The paths are otherwise fixed, so a second job of the same kind silently clobbers the first one's log.\\n\\n## Handling wake-ups\\n\\n- Address errors or actionable events immediately. Look through the logs and debug the issue thoroughly before settling on a root cause, as long-running processes are generally expensive. Resubmit the job once it is fixed:\\n  - For PRs, this means repushing the changes and restarting CI (if needed).\\n  - For jobs, requeue or relaunch the job.\\n  - Always restart the monitor or loop so that it points at the new PR or job.\\n- If there are multiple jobs running, start debugging immediately when any jobs fail. Minimize total wall-time with completing tasks.\\n\\n## Restarts\\n\\n- Whenever you relaunch a job, always update the monitor.\\n- Monitors have a maximum 10-hour time limit, so be sure to restart them if you hit the time limit and if the task being monitored is still running.\\n- When you spawn a new monitor, make sure to kill any previous ones that the new one replaces. For example, if you fix a training run and respawn it, be sure to kill previous monitors, as irrelevant wakeups are expensive.\\n\\n## Cleanup\\n\\n- If a monitor is in a terminal state, kill the monitor, the loop, and the job. Dangling processes such as unkilled broken training runs are expensive. Then, address all issues if present.\\n- If an outdated monitor wakes you — that is, it is attached to an old or dead run — make sure to kill it.\\n- Do not leave detached workers, duplicate pollers, or orphaned watcher processes behind.\\n\\n## Examples\\n\\n### Example: maintaining a dev server\\n\\nSuppose you just made some changes to the user's frontend code and would like to spin it up to test it. While you're testing it, you would like to know if the server goes down for any reason (if you made changes, for example). This is a great use case for the monitor tool. First start a React app in a background terminal, which reports its pid:\\n\\n```\\nrun_terminal_command(\\n  command=\\\"echo $$; npm start >~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_$$.log 2>&1\\\",\\n  timeout=0,\\n  description=\\\"Start the dev server on :3000\\\",\\n  background=true\\n) # prints the pid 123456789\\n```\\n\\nThere are no terminal states, but there are two actionable events from the watcher: `http://localhost:3000` coming up and going down.\\n\\nYou want to be notified as soon as it is up so you can use it, and you want to be woken when it stops so you can address it — fix the code, restart the server, free the port. Both down paths quote the tail of the server's log, so the wakeup arrives with the error already in hand. Polling every 5s is a good cadence since you want to quickly fix it if it is down, and it is not expensive to poll.\\n\\n```bash\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Log paths are keyed on the server pid so two dev servers cannot clobber each other.\\nPID=$1 URL=http://localhost:3000 DIR=~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks\\nLOG=$DIR/dev_server_$PID.log\\nup() { curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 5 \\\"$URL\\\" 2>>\\\"$DIR/watch_dev_server_$PID.log\\\"; }\\nwhy() { tail -20 \\\"$LOG\\\" | tr -d '\\\\r' | tr '\\\\n' ' '; }\\n\\nuntil up; do\\n  kill -0 \\\"$PID\\\" 2>/dev/null || { echo \\\"FAILED: pid $PID exited before $URL came up: $(why)\\\"; exit 1; }\\n  sleep 5\\ndone\\necho \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is up\\\"\\n\\nwhile :; do\\n  sleep 5\\n  up || { echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $URL is down: $(why)\\\"; until up; do sleep 5; done; }\\ndone\\n```\\n\\nTo spawn the monitor, point it at the pid `npm start` reported:\\n\\n```\\nmonitor(\\n  command='bash ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_dev_server.sh 123456789',\\n  description=\\\"Watch the dev server on :3000\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nOn the first wakeup the app is ready to use and you can start testing it. After that, you may be woken up if the server goes down. When you do:\\n- Read `~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/dev_server_<pid>.log` for the full compile error or port conflict and fix it.\\n- If the dev server survived, hot reload picks the fix up and the watcher recovers on its own, so leave the monitor running.\\n- If the process is dead, restart it with a fresh `run_terminal_command`, then kill this monitor and spawn a new one against the new pid.\\n\\n### Example: PR monitoring\\n\\nPR monitoring should stop when the PR is merged or closed, and it should wake up on:\\n- For comments, you should ask users how they would like to resolve them (which ones to address automatically, whether to respond / resolve, etc.), then store the decision for new future sessions. There are four different types of PR comments.\\n  - Inline review threads\\n  - Review bodies\\n  - Conversation comments\\n  - Commit comments\\n- Failing CI - most can be fixed in the code but sometimes they may be flaky. For flaky CI, just rerun them.\\n- Merge conflicts\\n- The PR becoming ready to merge — CI passes, no merge conflicts, reviews complete, no blockers, no unresolved inline threads. That wakeup is `kind: mergeable`. Tell the user they can merge now. It is not terminal; keep watching until merge or close.\\n\\nYou can find the example script bundled with this skill at watch_pr.py. It needs an authenticated `gh` CLI (`gh auth login`) and `python3`. Default poll is 30s; a lower poll rate may hit GitHub rate limits at higher concurrency. Transport errors (timeouts, resets, DNS) retry with backoff and jitter and do not print a line.\\n\\n```\\nmonitor(\\n  command='python3 ~/.grok/long-running-background-tasks/watch_pr.py \\\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/pull/123\\\"',\\n  description=\\\"Watch PR 123\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\n### Example: training monitoring\\n\\nFor monitoring long training jobs, the terminal conditions are when the job either fails or completes. Further, many issues cannot be caught deterministically, so use a subagent to run a script for most of the high-level update, then scan the logs for errors such as faulty nodes or CUDA warnings.\\n\\nThe script below is bundled with this skill as watch_training.py. Run it with `--help` for every flag and for the run-directory and log-line layout it expects, since it parses nanoGPT stdout out of a Slurm run dir.\\n\\n```md\\nKeep an eye on the nanoGPT run nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 (slurm job 1234567) and tell me what changed since last time. Worktree ~/worktrees/nanogpt-owt-124m.\\n\\n1. python3 ~/.grok/skills/long-running-background-tasks/watch_training.py --run-name nanogpt_owt_124m_r1 --job-id 1234567 --target-val 3.00\\n\\nHow to report:\\n- Start with the job's state and if it changed, e.g. \\\"still RUNNING\\\" or \\\"PENDING -> RUNNING\\\".\\n- Copy the ISSUES line and the STATUS line from the monitor exactly as it is written, numbers and all. STATUS will include where the run is heading: how far along, iters left, the eta, and whether val loss is still coming down. We want val loss at or under 3.00.\\n- If the state didn't change, tell me that in one line and stop.\\n- If the job has finished or died, give me the state, the exit code, and the last ISSUES, and run these two for me:\\n  sacct -j 1234567 -D -X -P -o JobID,State,ExitCode,DerivedExitCode,Elapsed,NodeList,FailedNode\\n  tail -20 ~/train_logs/slurm-nanogpt_owt_124m_r1-1234567.out\\n\\nKeep <=10 lines: state, progress and eta, loss trend, then anything that smells like training going unstable (loss spiking, NaN, val loss creeping back up) or the cluster misbehaving (a drained or failing node, OOM, NCCL errors, a preemption, iters suddenly slower than usual).\\n```\\n\\nLaunch the scheduler on a 10 min interval, and spawn a monitor to be notified when the job crashes or succeeds.\\n\\n```\\nscheduler_create(\\n  interval=\\\"10m\\\",\\n  prompt: \\\"...\\\", # Copy from the prompt above.\\n)\\nmonitor(\\n  # Wakes on pending/running/finished changes after the first observation.\\n  # First poll is silent unless the job is already COMPLETED or failed —\\n  # PENDING matches *ING, so treating an empty previous state as a\\n  # transition would wake immediately on a just-submitted job.\\n  command='J=1234567; p=; while :; do s=$(sacct -nXP -j $J -o State|head -1); s=${s%% *}; s=${s:-PENDING}; if [ \\\"$s\\\" != \\\"$p\\\" ]; then case $s in COMPLETED) echo \\\"DONE: $J\\\"; exit;; PENDING|RUNNING|CONFIGURING|COMPLETING|REQUEUED|SUSPENDED|RESIZING) [ -n \\\"$p\\\" ] && echo \\\"ACTION_REQUIRED: $J $p -> $s\\\";; *) echo \\\"FAILED: $J $s\\\"; exit 1;; esac; fi; p=$s; sleep 30; done',\\n  description=\\\"Watch training run\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nLaunch both, so that we know when the job completes. When the monitor reports that the job has completed, cancel both the monitor and the scheduler, then determine next steps. Then:\\n- If it succeeded, simply present the results to the user.\\n- If it failed, debug the issue thoroughly, then fix and relaunch the job with new monitors attached. Make sure to have a thorough understanding of the issue before resubmitting, since training jobs are expensive. But always relaunch jobs so you don't waste the user's time.\\n\",\"total_lines\":197}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-fb60b557-5e0b-4c2b-9e38-c02faa891089-1","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/STAGE.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":150,\"raw_output\":\"Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-fb60b557-5e0b-4c2b-9e38-c02faa891089-2","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,95,95,120,53,52,102,97,99,99,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,49,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,51,54,53,54,95,95,120,53,52,102,97,99,99,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,65,83,75,46,109,100,10,49,48,57,58,69,120,105,116,32,105,110,116,101,114,118,105,101,119,58,32,98,101,102,111,114,101,32,121,111,117,32,102,105,110,105,115,104,44,32,97,108,115,111,32,115,97,118,101,32,116,104,101,115,101,32,97,110,115,119,101,114,115,32,97,115,32,101,120,105,116,45,105,110,116,101,114,118,105,101,119,46,106,115,111,110,32,105,110,32,116,104,105,115,32,102,111,108,100,101,114,39,115,32,114,111,111,116,44,32,98,101,115,105,100,101,32,116,104,101,32,103,97,109,101,39,115,32,46,104,116,109,108,58,32,111,110,101,32,74,83,79,78,32,111,98,106,101,99,116,32,105,110,32,101,120,97,99,116,108,121,32,116,104,101,32,115,104,97,112,101,32,98,101,108,111,119,44,32,101,118,101,114,121,32,118,97,108,117,101,32,102,105,108,108,101,100,32,105,110,32,40,34,117,110,107,110,111,119,110,34,32,102,111,114,32,97,110,121,116,104,105,110,103,32,121,111,117,32,99,97,110,39,116,32,99,111,110,102,105,114,109,41,46,32,84,111,111,108,115,32,114,101,97,100,32,105,116,32,40,116,104,101,32,103,97,109,101,39,115,32,114,117,110,32,114,101,99,111,114,100,32,97,110,100,32,116,104,101,32,65,73,32,72,117,98,32,99,97,116,97,108,111,103,117,101,41,44,32,115,111,32,119,114,105,116,101,32,112,108,97,105,110,32,116,101,120,116,44,32,97,110,100,32,99,104,101,99,107,32,116,104,97,116,32,116,104,101,32,102,105,108,101,32,112,97,114,115,101,115,32,97,115,32,74,83,79,78,46,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/TASK.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":109,\"content\":\"Exit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"8c30b7fc-274e-41ba-a315-dfc8bcef24c9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_557","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-510e40e2-8a20-4d6f-81b9-c4eb18666c9f-3","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/TASK.md","offset":1,"limit":250}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-510e40e2-8a20-4d6f-81b9-c4eb18666c9f-4","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"^## |^### |exit-interview|PROGRESS|ENGINE|README|Deviations|- \\[ \\]","path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc%2Fwork/01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc/goal/plan.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-510e40e2-8a20-4d6f-81b9-c4eb18666c9f-5","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"export const EXIT|TESTING|ENGINE|PROMPT|AI_HUB|usage","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work/Thumb Yoga-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656/labs/game/ui/about-data.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4023,"output_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":36480,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"d142d297-fb37-4cef-ae16-2c0d55d90d7e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-510e40e2-8a20-4d6f-81b9-c4eb18666c9f-3","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:36:56\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Wii Fit\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-223656].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.