ass\\\"]:\\n        print(\\\"FAIL\\\", c[\\\"name\\\"], \\\"—\\\", c[\\\"detail\\\"][:400])\\nprint(\\\"--- ghosts ---\\\")\\nfor i,g in enumerate(data[\\\"ghosts\\\"]):\\n    print(i, \\\"ready\\\", g[\\\"ready\\\"], \\\"errors\\\", g[\\\"errors\\\"], \\\"ghosts\\\", g[\\\"ghosts\\\"], \\\"trace_nonzero\\\", sum(1 for n in g[\\\"trace\\\"] if n))\\nprint(\\\"aihub\\\", data[\\\"aihub\\\"][:3])\\nPY\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Parse the 4-player hub failure details\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-223656__x54facc%2Fwork/01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc/terminal/call-dc7052cb-49cd-4ef5-8893-ab97ab43b92b-69.log\",\"total_bytes\":294}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"bc339d21-9f93-4ed8-9a7d-39fcecdebcc3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_875","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The Enter failure detail is empty rather than containing parameter names.\n\nThe empty detail means every player answered ready. The check failed because not every player had a card sent within two seconds.\n\nOnly p0 called ready() within the 20-second window. The other three engines finished createEngine after that deadline. All remaining engines are now ready, but p3's iframe was removed because n>=3 removes the last cell.\n\nEnter is poked only once and never retried. Players who had not called ready() by the two-second card-sent deadline never received cards.\n\nThe `sent` flag is captured from the `until()` call at that exact moment. Cards may have arrived after the two-second window, but Enter was still poked unconditionally. Players answered after the deadline, so `sent` remained false while `answered` became true.\n\nThe parser only printed failing checks, so sends, progress, and round end all passed. The round likely began while some engines were still booting.\n\nCalling ready() before createEngine finishes would let Enter answer immediately, but the round would start before engines finish looping. Sends could then drop to zero if the play window elapses during initialization.\n\nPreviously, the watchdog passed at 14 seconds while sends remained zero and engines never became ready. Now, without the watchdog, engines do become ready by the end because cheap graphics work. The trade-off is that three of four players miss the 20-second ready window.\n\nThe timeline shows all four engines start createEngine at t=0. p0 finishes and calls ready() before the 20-second deadline. The ready check fails for p1, p2, and p3, yet the harness continues regardless. The card-sent check also fails at t=20-22, Enter is still poked once, and the round proceeds even though not every engine was ready.\n\nThe round starts even when not all engines are ready. The late iframes finish initialization during the 20-second wait and play window, so sends and progress pass by the time they are checked. The fix is to call ready() earlier while keeping engine initialization fast enough for sends to occur.\n\nCalling ready() before createEngine would let all four players pass the ready check immediately. Cards would be sent and Enter answered within two seconds. The round would start while the slow copies are still initializing. If those copies need twenty-five seconds to finish, sends could drop to zero by the time they are checked.\n\nThe exact timing of the late engines is uncertain. They likely finished within about thirty-five seconds, but the harness continues through the full play and grace period regardless.\n\nA better approach would be to signal readiness as soon as the page can answer Enter, while delaying the actual gameplay sends. The harness starts the round immediately upon ready() and cannot pause it afterward without failing the ready check or the send count.\n\nThe send check occurs roughly countdown plus five seconds after ready(). An engine must therefore be looping within that window. Calling ready() at t=0 would require the engine to be ready by about t=10, which p0 might manage but p1 through p3 likely could not. Waiting until t=18 to call ready() gives the engine more time, yet a watchdog at that point risks passing the ready check while the engine is still initializing, causing sends to fail.\n\nA 12-second ready deadline would give the slowest engines roughly six seconds to finish after the round starts. That should leave enough time for sends. The coordinator might also wait for all players to signal readiness before starting.\n\nThe round-start check passed, so all copies were ready before the round began. The slow engines finished initialization within about fifteen seconds after releaseRound. They likely became ready around t=25 and the round started shortly after.\n\nThe round-start check confirms every engine finished createEngine and called ready() before the round began. All copies became ready within fifteen seconds after releaseRound, which occurred around t=25. The slowest engines therefore missed the twenty-second deadline by up to twenty seconds, yet sends still passed.\n\nA 16-second ready deadline would let every engine pass the twenty-second window. Cards would be sent and Enter answered immediately, because the keydown handler does not need the engine to finish initializing first.\n\nThe round-start check passes as soon as the coordinator begins, because it only verifies phase and readiness rather than engine initialization. If ready() is called at sixteen seconds, the round starts around eighteen seconds while some engines are still initializing. The send check occurs roughly twenty-seven seconds after the round begins, giving those late engines enough time to finish before sends are evaluated.\n\nEngines finishing at thirty to forty seconds would miss the send check. The previous zero-send failure occurred before cheap graphics were applied; now engines finish and sends work only because initialization is not forced to start early. The best fix is to make four-player initialization complete within twenty seconds. The bottleneck is concurrent SwiftShader WebGPU initializations, with p0 finishing first and the others queuing behind it. A better approach is to use even cheaper rendering so each engine initializes in about four seconds.\n\nHalving the rendering workload could let all four engines finish within twenty seconds. Staggering the other iframes is not possible. The current look still compiles grade, outline, and toon shaders even at low quality, so a simpler style with no filters might make warmup instant.\n\nA new `look:pavilion` style with flat daylight and no filters will be added to `retreat.ts` and set as the session post look in `main.ts`. The phone test can keep `look:retreat` because its single engine already finished within sixty seconds. Only the hub four-player test still exceeds the twenty-second ready deadline.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-54b4d59e-0fa7-440e-a621-df63ef698b56-70","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"shadowMap|shadows:","glob":"**/engine/**/*settings*"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-54b4d59e-0fa7-440e-a621-df63ef698b56-71","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"'shadows'|shadows: \\{","glob":"**/engine/**/*.ts","head_limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":446,"output_tokens":3021,"cache_read_input_tokens":114560,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa35-d4ff-74f0-9f18-3308a1dd51dc","uuid":"9e3209b8-d9c9-4e98-82a5-efe3cdcf4ee1"}
