{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_1111","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Source files were edited around 04:39–04:40.\n\nHTML rebuilt at 04:47, after the source edits. The test log finished at 04:58, yet `island.test.ts` was modified at 04:59:00, making it newer than the test run. This suggests the test suite may have touched the file, or the timing is inconsistent.\n\nThe 04:59 modification to `island.test.ts` suggests the tests may have been edited after the 04:58 run. Prettier check does not write files, so the implementer likely edited the test file afterward and then ran `npm run check`, which does not re-run tests. This would mean the captured test results reflect an older version of the test file.\n\nThe check log confirms Prettier did not rewrite the file. The 04:59 modification to `island.test.ts` therefore remains unexplained.\n\nThe 04:59 file write occurred before `npm run check` began, leaving a 40-second window where the test file could have been altered after the passing tests. The current `island.test.ts` may therefore differ from the version that produced the captured results.\n\nGit status or implementer transcripts could reveal what touched the test file. A full re-run of the 211-second suite is too expensive; a targeted spot-check of the new tests may suffice if the post-test edit introduced regressions.\n\nThe verifier should not build or re-run the suite themselves. They must inspect the actual test file content to see if it matches the captured passing run, or request the implementer produce fresh evidence.\n\nStatic inspection of the current tests shows they match the code and would pass. The post-test file modification does not invalidate the captured results. The HTML contains the required age strings, game.tilt.rate, pebble identifiers, and plaque.want. RETREAT/Chair may be constructed dynamically rather than stored as a literal string.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_us